_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned FAIL\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] summary: counterexample trace: reg_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_44_60 at rvfi_reg_check.sv:44.6-44.59 step 20\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\n\\nFull run log: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-luna-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260710-001-r1s0/formal/last_run-53490.log\\nPer-check logs in: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-luna-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260710-001-r1s0/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-53490/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): A zero-bubble EX/MEM load-data bypass must keep the extracted value, pipeline valid bits, and RVFI metadata aligned while `dmemReady` is low; merely holding the memory stage and suppressing redirects lets dependent instructions observe or retire mismatched register state.\"}\n5:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Share the RV32M multiplier datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current combinational ALU builds three independent 32x32 products for\\nMUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU on every cycle, although each instruction needs\\nonly one product interpretation. This is costly on the Tang Nano 20K, where\\nthe current predictor-enhanced fixture is already above the LUT target and\\nmultiplier routing can also limit Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current combinational ALU builds three independent 32x32 products for\\nMUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU on every cycle, although each instruction needs\\nonly one product interpretation. This is costly on the Tang Nano 20K, where\\nthe current predictor-enhanced fixture is already above the LUT target and\\nmultiplier routing can also limit Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel multiplier expressions with one shared signed\\n33x33 product. Select sign-extension versus zero-extension independently for\\neach operand from the decoded M operation, then use the low or high 32 bits\\nfor the corresponding MUL-family result. A 33-bit sign/zero representation\\npreserves the exact RV32M low and high halves, including MULHSU, while\\nkeeping the existing one-cycle EX latency, divider behavior, forwarding, and\\nRVFI timing unchanged. The reduced multiplier replication should lower LUT\\nusage and routing pressure, improving the product of CoreMark IPC and Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a single sign-controlled 33x33 RV32M multiplier, select its low/high halves for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU, and retain the ALTOPS substitutions plus exact divider semantics.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a single sign-controlled 33x33 RV32M multiplier, select its low/high halves for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU, and retain the ALTOPS substitutions plus exact divider semantics.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 286.85, \"delta_pct\": -7.63, \"vs_baseline\": 1.42, \"fmax_mhz\": 122.19, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9983, \"ff\": 1898, \"seeds\": [114.12, 134.72, 122.19], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T20:22:24.214983Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 2}\n8:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Share a native-width RV32M multiplier\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor-enhanced bench core has already recovered the important\\nCoreMark control-flow bubbles, but it uses 10554 LUT4s and its ALU still\\ninstantiates three full-width multiplier expressions for the MUL family.\\nThe previous sharing attempt used a 33x33 signed multiplier and reduced\\nFmax substantially, so the next optimization should preserve the FPGA's\\nnative 32x32 mapping rather than widen the arithmetic cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The predictor-enhanced bench core has already recovered the important\\nCoreMark control-flow bubbles, but it uses 10554 LUT4s and its ALU still\\ninstantiates three full-width multiplier expressions for the MUL family.\\nThe previous sharing attempt used a 33x33 signed multiplier and reduced\\nFmax substantially, so the next optimization should preserve the FPGA's\\nnative 32x32 mapping rather than widen the arithmetic cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the parallel signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned\\nproducts with one native 32x32 unsigned product. Use sign-dependent high\\nhalf correction terms to implement MULH and MULHSU exactly, while taking\\nthe unchanged low half for MUL and the unsigned high half for MULHU. Keep\\nthe existing one-cycle EX interface, divider behavior, RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\\nsubstitutions, and RVFI timing unchanged. This should remove redundant\\nmultiplier hardware and routing pressure without the critical-path penalty\\nof the prior 33x33 implementation, improving Fmax and/or fitting margin\\nwhile preserving CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Use one native-width unsigned 32x32 product and sign-correction arithmetic for exact MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU results; retain ALTOPS and divider semantics.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Use one native-width unsigned 32x32 product and sign-correction arithmetic for exact MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU results; retain ALTOPS and divider semantics.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 317.32, \"delta_pct\": 2.18, \"vs_baseline\": 12.2, \"fmax_mhz\": 135.17, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10283, \"ff\": 1895, \"seeds\": [135.17, 123.08, 138.24], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T20:49:37.121156Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (improvement, +2.18%): One native-width unsigned `a * b` plus sign-dependent high-half correction terms implements RV32M `MUL`, `MULH`, `MULHU`, and `MULHSU` without the timing penalty of a widened multiplier, preserving CoreMark throughput while reducing redundant multiply hardware.\"}\n11:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Reuse the EX adder for control-flow targets\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage computes the normal ALU result and also builds separate\\nadders for branch targets, JAL targets, and JALR targets. Those duplicated\\n32-bit additions increase LUT use and routing on the Tang Nano 20K, even\\nthough control-transfer instructions already select an ADD-like operation.\\nThe best fixture is near both the timing and LUT limits, so a localized\\ndatapath-sharing change is preferable to adding predictor or pipeline state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage computes the normal ALU result and also builds separate\\nadders for branch targets, JAL targets, and JALR targets. Those duplicated\\n32-bit additions increase LUT use and routing on the Tang Nano 20K, even\\nthough control-transfer instructions already select an ADD-like operation.\\nThe best fixture is near both the timing and LUT limits, so a localized\\ndatapath-sharing change is preferable to adding predictor or pipeline state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Retarget the EX ALU operand selection so branches and direct jumps use the\\nexisting ADD datapath for pc plus immediate, while JALR uses that same path\\nfor rs1 plus immediate. Use the resulting ALU sum for branch and jump target\\nresolution, retaining the bit-zero clear for JALR and the separate PC+4 link\\nvalue for register writeback. This should remove redundant target adders and\\ntheir routing without adding a cycle, changing forwarding, or altering the\\nexisting static prediction, trap, memory, and RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Reuse the ALU ADD result for branch, JAL, and JALR target generation; remove redundant target adders while preserving alignment traps, prediction comparison, PC+4 link writeback, and RVFI metadata.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Reuse the ALU ADD result for branch, JAL, and JALR target generation; remove redundant target adders while preserving alignment traps, prediction comparison, PC+4 link writeback, and RVFI metadata.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 257.74, \"delta_pct\": -18.78, \"vs_baseline\": -8.87, \"fmax_mhz\": 109.79, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10096, \"ff\": 1919, \"seeds\": [111.11, 109.79, 106.81], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation notes\\n\\n- Updated `cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv` so branches and direct jumps select\\n  `pc` plus their immediate on the existing ALU ADD path, while JALR selects\\n  the forwarded `rs1` plus its immediate on that same path.\\n- Branch and jump target resolution now consumes `alu_result`; JALR still\\n  clears bit 0. The EX/MEM link value for JAL/JALR remains `pc + 4`, and the\\n  existing prediction, redirect, misalignment-trap, forwarding, and RVFI\\n  paths were otherwise unchanged.\\n- No deviations from the hypothesis were needed.\\n- Lint passed:\\n  `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`\\n- Local formal passed with 53 checks and 0 failures using the unchanged\\n  `formal/run_all.sh` against a writable copy of the vendored formal tree.\\n  The direct in-tree invocation was blocked because the sandbox mounts\\n  `formal/riscv-formal/cores/` non-writable (`Operation not permitted`).\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T21:12:05.940539Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (regression, -18.78%): Reusing the EX ALU sum for branch/JAL/JALR targets can make control-flow resolution depend on added operand-selection muxing, so removing target adders is not worthwhile if it lengthens the fetch redirect path and costs CoreMark throughput.\"}\n14:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Zero-bubble zero-wait load-use bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The five-stage pipeline currently inserts a load-use bubble even when the\\ndata bus is zero-wait, which is the normal FPGA bench case and a substantial\\npart of the CoreMark memory/arithmetic stream. A prior unrestricted bypass\\nattempt was broken because it allowed a dependent instruction to advance\\nwhile dmemReady was low. The optimization should therefore be qualified by\\nthe actual ready signal and leave the existing stalled-bus sequencing intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The five-stage pipeline currently inserts a load-use bubble even when the\\ndata bus is zero-wait, which is the normal FPGA bench case and a substantial\\npart of the CoreMark memory/arithmetic stream. A prior unrestricted bypass\\nattempt was broken because it allowed a dependent instruction to advance\\nwhile dmemReady was low. The optimization should therefore be qualified by\\nthe actual ready signal and leave the existing stalled-bus sequencing intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a combinational EX/MEM load-result bypass using the memory stage's\\nalready sign- or zero-extended load value. Suppress the load-use bubble only\\nwhen io_dmemReady is high; in that case the dependent instruction advances\\nimmediately and selects the load value ahead of the normal ALU/MEM-WB\\nforwarding paths. When dmemReady is low, retain the current load-use bubble,\\nEX/MEM hold, MEM/WB valid suppression, and redirect behavior so no dependent\\ninstruction, register write, or RVFI record can observe incomplete memory\\ndata. This preserves the arbitrary-stall correctness contract while removing\\none cycle from each zero-wait load dependency.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify load-use stalling with dmemReady so only zero-wait loads use the new bypass, while preserving the existing dmem-stall and flush precedence.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the current load's extracted sign/zero-extended value as a combinational forwarding source without changing MEM/WB capture or RVFI metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an EX/MEM load-result forwarding selection with priority over the normal EX/MEM ALU and MEM/WB selections.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the ready-qualified memory load bypass into EX while retaining the unchanged external IO and single-channel RVFI contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the forwarded load value for dependent ALU, address, and branch operands, with no bypass use when the memory request is stalled.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify load-use stalling with dmemReady so only zero-wait loads use the new bypass, while preserving the existing dmem-stall and flush precedence.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the current load's extracted sign/zero-extended value as a combinational forwarding source without changing MEM/WB capture or RVFI metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an EX/MEM load-result forwarding selection with priority over the normal EX/MEM ALU and MEM/WB selections.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the ready-qualified memory load bypass into EX while retaining the unchanged external IO and single-channel RVFI contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the forwarded load value for dependent ALU, address, and branch operands, with no bypass use when the memory request is stalled.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 252.64, \"delta_pct\": -20.38, \"vs_baseline\": -10.67, \"fmax_mhz\": 103.34, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4090325, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10254, \"ff\": 1923, \"seeds\": [99.84, 103.34, 105.02], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T21:37:31.609002Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -20.38%): A zero-wait load-use bypass that drives sign/zero-extended `dmem_rdata` through the EX forwarding mux can lengthen the critical path enough to outweigh the bubble it removes, so its CPI win must be measured against the added MEM-to-EX timing cost.\"}\n17:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Share the combinational divide and remainder datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU currently spells out signed and unsigned DIV and REM as separate\\noperators. On the FPGA this can leave redundant quotient/remainder divider\\ncones and wide result-mux routing in the already timing-sensitive EX2\\nstage. The previous iterative-divider experiment showed that adding a\\nmulti-cycle stall is too expensive, so the useful opportunity is to reduce\\nhardware while retaining the existing single-cycle ALU contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU currently spells out signed and unsigned DIV and REM as separate\\noperators. On the FPGA this can leave redundant quotient/remainder divider\\ncones and wide result-mux routing in the already timing-sensitive EX2\\nstage. The previous iterative-divider experiment showed that adding a\\nmulti-cycle stall is too expensive, so the useful opportunity is to reduce\\nhardware while retaining the existing single-cycle ALU contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rewrite the real RV32M division logic around one combinational unsigned\\nmagnitude divider that produces both quotient and remainder, then apply\\noperand-sign correction and the RISC-V divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 rules\\nto select DIV, DIVU, REM, or REMU. Keep the ALTOPS formal substitutions\\nunchanged and preserve combinational latency, so dependent instructions,\\nRVFI ordering, and dmem-stall behavior do not acquire new bubbles. Sharing\\nthe expensive divide work should reduce LUT/routing load and improve Fmax\\nwithout the throughput loss of a globally held iterative unit.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the independent real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU operators with one shared quotient-and-remainder datapath, including exact RV32M signedness, zero-divisor, and overflow handling while retaining the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend ALU coverage for shared-divider signed and unsigned quotient/remainder pairs, divide-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1 overflow, and back-to-back operations to guard the refactor.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the independent real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU operators with one shared quotient-and-remainder datapath, including exact RV32M signedness, zero-divisor, and overflow handling while retaining the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend ALU coverage for shared-divider signed and unsigned quotient/remainder pairs, divide-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1 overflow, and back-to-back operations to guard the refactor.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"build_failed: yosys synth: ERROR: Latch inferred for signal `\\\\alu.\\\\div_bit' from always_comb process `\\\\alu.$proc$cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv:61$9'.\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (broken, n/a): In Yosys `always_comb`, a module-scoped integer used as a restoring-divider loop iterator is a synthesizable signal, so paths that skip the loop (such as divide-by-zero) must still assign it or the tool infers a latch; keep loop indices local/automatic or fully assigned.\"}\n20:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Remove redundant MEM/WB forwarding leg\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The retimed core already captures forwarded operands in EX1, but its\\nforwarding network still performs a third age comparison and selects a\\nMEM/WB value.  The register file has an explicit write-first WB bypass, and\\nEX1 re-reads the held instruction's registers, so this MEM/WB leg duplicates\\na value that is already available at the register-file read ports.  Removing\\nthe duplicate compare and mux should reduce EX1 capture delay and routing\\nwithout changing the arithmetic or memory pipeline.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The retimed core already captures forwarded operands in EX1, but its\\nforwarding network still performs a third age comparison and selects a\\nMEM/WB value.  The register file has an explicit write-first WB bypass, and\\nEX1 re-reads the held instruction's registers, so this MEM/WB leg duplicates\\na value that is already available at the register-file read ports.  Removing\\nthe duplicate compare and mux should reduce EX1 capture delay and routing\\nwithout changing the arithmetic or memory pipeline.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Eliminate the MEM/WB source from the EX1 forwarding priority, retaining only\\nthe freshest EX2 result, the EX/MEM result or load value, and the\\nwrite-first register-file read as the fallback.  Simplify the forwarding\\ninterfaces and wiring so the WB value is supplied solely by the existing\\nregister-file bypass.  Because WB writes are visible combinationally and\\nEX1 re-reads operands when an instruction is held, dependent ALU, load-use,\\nbus-stall, redirect, RVFI, and x0 behavior should remain unchanged while\\nthe operand-capture path becomes smaller and faster.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the MEM/WB equality comparison and forwarding selection while preserving EX2-over-EX/MEM age priority and x0 suppression.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Simplify the EX1 operand mux inputs to use EX2, EX/MEM, or the write-first register-file read, with no functional change to load forwarding or stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Update forwarding wiring to drop the redundant MEM/WB forwarding source while retaining the existing WB write-first regfile path and top-level RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the MEM/WB equality comparison and forwarding selection while preserving EX2-over-EX/MEM age priority and x0 suppression.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Simplify the EX1 operand mux inputs to use EX2, EX/MEM, or the write-first register-file read, with no functional change to load forwarding or stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Update forwarding wiring to drop the redundant MEM/WB forwarding source while retaining the existing WB write-first regfile path and top-level RVFI contract.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 480.9, \"delta_pct\": 21.33, \"vs_baseline\": 70.04, \"fmax_mhz\": 208.72, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4340155, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10135, \"ff\": 2076, \"seeds\": [214.55, 208.72, 203.96], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T22:14:26.680923Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (improvement, +21.33%): Removing a redundant MEM/WB forwarding compare and mux improved CoreMark because EX1's write-first register-file reread already exposes WB data, so forwarding should retain only younger values unavailable at the read ports while preserving EX2-over-EX/MEM priority.\"}\n23:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Qualify load-use hazard source comparisons\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock compares an older load destination against\\nboth raw register fields of every younger instruction. For immediate,\\nload, jump, and upper-immediate instructions, one or both fields are not\\nregister operands, so coincidental matches create avoidable pipeline\\nbubbles in CoreMark. These bubbles reduce iterations per cycle without\\nproviding any correctness benefit.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock compares an older load destination against\\nboth raw register fields of every younger instruction. For immediate,\\nload, jump, and upper-immediate instructions, one or both fields are not\\nregister operands, so coincidental matches create avoidable pipeline\\nbubbles in CoreMark. These bubbles reduce iterations per cycle without\\nproviding any correctness benefit.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add explicit uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 control bits during decode and carry\\nthem through the existing ID/EX control bundle. Make the hazard unit gate\\neach load-use comparison with the corresponding use bit, while retaining\\nthe existing x0 suppression, EX1 timing, forwarding priority, dmem-stall,\\nredirect, and RVFI behavior. Eliminating false interlocks should improve\\nCoreMark IPC with only a small decode/control-width cost and no new\\ncritical datapath mux.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend ctrl_t with explicit rs1/rs2 operand-use bits carried through the pipeline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Set uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 only for validated RV32I/RV32M encodings that actually read those source registers.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the decoder's operand-use bits into the ID/EX control bundle without changing the top-level interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify load-use comparisons independently for rs1 and rs2 so immediate and non-register fields cannot cause false stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend ctrl_t with explicit rs1/rs2 operand-use bits carried through the pipeline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Set uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 only for validated RV32I/RV32M encodings that actually read those source registers.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the decoder's operand-use bits into the ID/EX control bundle without changing the top-level interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify load-use comparisons independently for rs1 and rs2 so immediate and non-register fields cannot cause false stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 (broken, n/a): Source-use bits for load-use interlocks must remain hazard-only; zeroing unused `rs1`/`rs2` addresses or data in RVFI changes the cosim-visible raw instruction-field trace and breaks selftest.\"}\n26:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Opcode-qualified load-use interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The retimed pipeline still compares a load destination against both raw\\nregister fields of every younger instruction. Immediate, upper-immediate,\\njump, and other non-register-source instructions can therefore incur a\\nneedless load-use bubble. CoreMark's tight integer code makes these false\\nstalls a direct IPC loss, while the previous source-use attempt showed that\\nchanging the architectural trace fields is unsafe.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The retimed pipeline still compares a load destination against both raw\\nregister fields of every younger instruction. Immediate, upper-immediate,\\njump, and other non-register-source instructions can therefore incur a\\nneedless load-use bubble. CoreMark's tight integer code makes these false\\nstalls a direct IPC loss, while the previous source-use attempt showed that\\nchanging the architectural trace fields is unsafe.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Qualify the load-use hazard entirely inside the hazard unit using the raw\\nID/EX instruction opcode and funct3 where needed. Compare the older load's\\nrd only against source fields that the younger encoding actually reads, but\\nkeep the original rs1/rs2 addresses and data unchanged for RVFI and cosim.\\nThis removes false interlocks without adding a forwarding mux or changing\\npipeline timing, while retaining the existing dmem-stall, redirect, and\\nEX2-over-EX/MEM ordering rules.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive hazard-only uses_rs1/uses_rs2 qualifiers from the raw younger instruction encoding and gate each load-use comparison without altering pipeline payload fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass the existing ID/EX instruction word into the hazard unit so source-use qualification does not modify decoder outputs, register addresses, or RVFI metadata.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive hazard-only uses_rs1/uses_rs2 qualifiers from the raw younger instruction encoding and gate each load-use comparison without altering pipeline payload fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass the existing ID/EX instruction word into the hazard unit so source-use qualification does not modify decoder outputs, register addresses, or RVFI metadata.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\nfinished (returncode=0)\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_srai_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_srai_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:02 (2)\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_srai_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:02 (2)\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_srai_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_srai_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_srai_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_sw_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:02  Status: passed\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_sw_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=0)\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_sw_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_sw_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:02 (2)\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_sw_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:02 (2)\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_sw_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_sw_ch0] summ