{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rg -n \"micro_opt|hyp-20260710-.*r1[45]|forward|hazard|ALU|decode|pipeline\" cores/bench/LESSONS.md cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -n 160'","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/LESSONS.md:1:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): A zero-bubble EX/MEM load-data bypass must keep the extracted value, pipeline valid bits, and RVFI metadata aligned while `dmemReady` is low; merely holding the memory stage and suppressing redirects lets dependent instructions observe or retire mismatched register state.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:3:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (regression, -13.79%): A radix-2 iterative divider that holds the in-order pipeline for its full multi-cycle latency can lose more CoreMark throughput than removing divide operators from the EX cone gains in timing, so divider frequency and stall cost must be measured before coupling DIV/REM to a global EX hold.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:4:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, -9.22%): A 16-entry BHT indexed only by `pc[5:2]` aliases CoreMark conditional branches, and weak-taken reset can turn the tuned static forward-not-taken behavior into costly early mispredictions, so tiny dynamic predictors need an aliasing-aware initialization and benchmark profile check.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:7:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r3s2 (regression, -14.14%): Even a four-entry RAS can regress CoreMark when its combinational 4-way `ras_top` mux and return-form decode sit directly on the IF next-PC path, so return-prediction savings must be weighed against fetch timing before adding predictor state.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:8:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (regression, -18.78%): Reusing the EX ALU sum for branch/JAL/JALR targets can make control-flow resolution depend on added operand-selection muxing, so removing target adders is not worthwhile if it lengthens the fetch redirect path and costs CoreMark throughput.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:11:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -20.38%): A zero-wait load-use bypass that drives sign/zero-extended `dmem_rdata` through the EX forwarding mux can lengthen the critical path enough to outweigh the bubble it removes, so its CPI win must be measured against the added MEM-to-EX timing cost.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:12:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r5s1 (improvement, +24.91%): Splitting execute into an operand/forwarding EX1 and arithmetic/control EX2 stage can materially improve CoreMark fitness by removing the forwarding muxes from the ALU cone, provided EX2-to-EX1 age-ordered bypasses and precise bubble/hold control preserve dependent ALU, load-use, and dmem-stall ordering.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:15:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (regression, -5.28%): A writeback-fed x1/x5 shadow predictor can lose CoreMark throughput when return decode, valid compares, and a 32-bit link-plus-immediate adder enter the IF next-PC cone, so return-bubble savings must be weighed against fetch timing and stale-link mispredict redirects.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:16:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (improvement, +21.33%): Removing a redundant MEM/WB forwarding compare and mux improved CoreMark because EX1's write-first register-file reread already exposes WB data, so forwarding should retain only younger values unavailable at the read ports while preserving EX2-over-EX/MEM priority.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:17:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Moving the retirement boundary behind an elastic completion queue can make the causal RVFI/dmem assumptions unsatisfiable at a shallow step and cascade into every instruction check, so queue issue, memory-handshake, forwarding, and in-order retirement metadata must remain formally coherent before measuring stall hiding.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:18:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r7s2 (regression, +1.74%): Even a weakly-not-taken, forward-only 32-entry gshare can regress CoreMark when the combinational `forward_bht[prediction_index][1]` lookup and global-history update add IF-path/state cost that the benchmark's relatively few useful forward-taken predictions do not repay.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:19:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 (broken, n/a): Source-use bits for load-use interlocks must remain hazard-only; zeroing unused `rs1`/`rs2` addresses or data in RVFI changes the cosim-visible raw instruction-field trace and breaks selftest.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:20:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r8s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Registering the dmem request/response boundary is not free on this FPGA: carrying full `mem_req_t`/`mem_resp_t` payloads plus request-stall and extra load-forwarding control can make placement fail, so isolate the memory read/format cone with compact metadata and localized backpressure rather than a wide retimed backend.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:21:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r8s2 (regression, -18.69%): Even a one-entry committed x1 shadow that recognizes only `jalr x0, 0(x1)` can regress CoreMark when IF adds canonical-return decode and validity gating to the next-PC cone, so narrowing target arithmetic does not remove the critical-path cost of return prediction.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:22:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r9s0 (broken, n/a): Opcode-qualified load-use comparisons in the hazard unit still failed the RVFI register check, so removing bubbles requires proving the raw-instruction source map remains aligned with valid, held, and redirected ID/EX entries across the full instruction stream.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:23:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r9s1 (regression, -56.76%): Replacing EX1's dedicated register-file reread with stall-gated ID/EX snapshot refresh shifted complexity into decode-address muxing and an added WB forwarding compare/mux, so removing read ports can severely hurt CoreMark unless the operand-path timing and stall behavior are measured together.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:24:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r9s2 (regression, -59.91%): Sharing B/J target generation through a selected-immediate mux and an unconditional `pc + selected_imm` adder left more decode logic on the IF next-PC cone, showing that arithmetic sharing can trade area for severe CoreMark timing loss.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:25:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r10s0 (regression, -55.42%): Replacing the load-stage variable shift with explicit byte-lane and halfword muxes did not improve CoreMark and likely lengthened the load-response/forwarding cone, so FPGA resource-oriented mux simplification must be validated against the implemented timing path.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:27:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r10s2 (regression, -38.60%): Even a one-entry exact-PC forward-branch predictor can severely regress CoreMark when its fetched-branch decode, tag compare, and counter state feed the IF next-PC cone, so residual forward-redirect savings do not justify adding predictor logic to the fetch critical path.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:28:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r11s0 (regression, -77.60%): A latch-free shared 32-iteration restoring divider can catastrophically regress CoreMark by putting a long compare/subtract loop in the combinational ALU path, so divider sharing must be evaluated for timing depth rather than area reduction alone.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:30:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r11s2 (regression, -31.90%): Even a stateless five-bit `rs1 == rs2` check plus funct3 decode can regress CoreMark when added to the IF next-PC cone, so architecturally certain branch identities are not worth predicting unless their decode cost is timing-neutral.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:32:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r12s1 (regression, -38.18%): Banking one 32x32 register file into four 8-entry arrays did not guarantee shallower FPGA read muxes, while four combinational ports still paid bank-select, decode, and routing cost, so physical isolation must be confirmed before trading a simple array for banked RTL.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:33:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r12s2 (regression, -38.59%): Eliding the IF predictor's `funct3` legality check still regressed CoreMark, showing that removing one decode predicate is insufficient when the remaining opcode/sign recognition and backward-target arithmetic stay on the fetch next-PC path.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:34:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r13s0 (regression, -43.56%): Removing architecturally dead fields from packed `ctrl_t` and `ex_mem_t` records can still perturb FPGA synthesis/placement enough to hurt CoreMark, so pipeline-sideband trimming needs post-implementation timing evidence rather than an assumed routing win.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:37:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r14s0 (regression, -54.44%): A registered IF `pc_plus4` sidecar can hurt CoreMark despite removing the sequential adder from the next-PC mux, because its extra state, fanout, and `pc + 8` maintenance path still perturb FPGA timing; fetch arithmetic retiming needs post-implementation evidence.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:38:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r14s1 (regression, -34.29%): Even a compact registered dmem response can regress CoreMark when the added raw-word/metadata token changes load-use forwarding and stall control, so memory retiming must preserve one-cycle load throughput and atomic response/retirement alignment rather than merely narrow the payload.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:39:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r14s2 (regression, -54.29%): Filtering backward-branch prediction with a wide `branch_imm[31:8]` equality added enough decode and immediate logic to the IF next-PC path that saved long-exit flushes could not repay the fetch timing cost, so stateless predictor refinements must be timing-neutral.\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:1:{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-4ddfa22\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 282.82, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9563, \"ff\": 1866, \"seeds\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T19:51:27.181163Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Zero-bubble load-use forwarding\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline unconditionally stalls one cycle for every\\nload-use dependency, even though the Tang Nano bench presents combinational\\nzero-wait read data. CoreMark has frequent load-dependent address, compare,\\nand arithmetic instructions, so this bubble directly reduces iterations per\\ncycle. The existing P&R report also shows the EX/MEM-to-EX forwarding path as\\nthe dominant timing path, making a dedicated load-data bypass a focused\\nhigh-value datapath optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline unconditionally stalls one cycle for every\\nload-use dependency, even though the Tang Nano bench presents combinational\\nzero-wait read data. CoreMark has frequent load-dependent address, compare,\\nand arithmetic instructions, so this bubble directly reduces iterations per\\ncycle. The existing P&R report also shows the EX/MEM-to-EX forwarding path as\\nthe dominant timing path, making a dedicated load-data bypass a focused\\nhigh-value datapath optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a dedicated EX/MEM load-data bypass from the memory stage's extracted\\nbyte/half/word value into the EX operand mux, and remove the unconditional\\nload-use bubble. When dmemReady is low, hold the dependent instruction and\\nsuppress redirects or side effects until the load completes; when it is high,\\nforward the load value directly and retire the dependent instruction without\\na bubble. This should preserve arbitrary bus-stall correctness while\\nimproving CoreMark IPC on the zero-wait FPGA path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the unconditional load-use bubble with replay-safe holding on an outstanding dmem stall, and provide control to block redirects while the dependent instruction waits.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a highest-priority EX/MEM load-result forwarding selection, with normal ALU and MEM/WB forwarding retained as fallbacks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the already sign/zero-extended load value as a combinational forwarding source while retaining the existing RVFI extraction and stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the load-data bypass and ensure a stalled dependent instruction cannot generate a redirect or architectural side effect before its operand is valid.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the load-result bypass and dmem-stall qualification through the pipeline without changing the top-level IO or RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the unconditional load-use bubble with replay-safe holding on an outstanding dmem stall, and provide control to block redirects while the dependent instruction waits.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a highest-priority EX/MEM load-result forwarding selection, with normal ALU and MEM/WB forwarding retained as fallbacks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the already sign/zero-extended load value as a combinational forwarding source while retaining the existing RVFI extraction and stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the load-data bypass and ensure a stalled dependent instruction cannot generate a redirect or architectural side effect before its operand is valid.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the load-result bypass and dmem-stall qualification through the pipeline without changing the top-level IO or RVFI contract.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\nvcd\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:20  Writing trace to Verilog testbench: engine_0/trace_tb.v\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:20  Writing trace to constraints file: engine_0/trace.smtc\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:20  Writing trace to Yosys witness file: engine_0/trace.yw\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:20  Status: failed\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=1)\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: FAIL\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:21 (21)\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:12 (12)\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned FAIL\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] summary: counterexample trace: reg_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_44_60 at rvfi_reg_check.sv:44.6-44.59 step 20\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\n\\nFormal: 52 passed, 1 failed\\nFailed: reg_ch0\\n\\n--- reg_ch0/logfile.txt (last 30 lines) ---\\nSBY 17:03:54 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 9..\\nSBY 17:03:54 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 10..\\nSBY 17:03:54 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 11..\\nSBY 17:03:54 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 12..\\nSBY 17:03:54 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 13..\\nSBY 17:03:54 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 14..\\nSBY 17:03:54 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00 