: reg_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_44_60 at rvfi_reg_check.sv:44.6-44.59 step 20\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\n\\nFull run log: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-luna-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260710-001-r1s0/formal/last_run-53490.log\\nPer-check logs in: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-luna-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260710-001-r1s0/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-53490/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): A zero-bubble EX/MEM load-data bypass must keep the extracted value, pipeline valid bits, and RVFI metadata aligned while `dmemReady` is low; merely holding the memory stage and suppressing redirects lets dependent instructions observe or retire mismatched register state.\"}\nhyp-20260710-001-r2s0 | Share the RV32M multiplier datapath | regression | 286.85 | -7.63\nhyp-20260710-001-r3s0 | Share a native-width RV32M multiplier | improvement | 317.32 | 2.18\nhyp-20260710-001-r4s0 | Reuse the EX adder for control-flow targets | regression | 257.74 | -18.78\nhyp-20260710-001-r5s0 | Zero-bubble zero-wait load-use bypass | regression | 252.64 | -20.38\n17:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Share the combinational divide and remainder datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU currently spells out signed and unsigned DIV and REM as separate\\noperators. On the FPGA this can leave redundant quotient/remainder divider\\ncones and wide result-mux routing in the already timing-sensitive EX2\\nstage. The previous iterative-divider experiment showed that adding a\\nmulti-cycle stall is too expensive, so the useful opportunity is to reduce\\nhardware while retaining the existing single-cycle ALU contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU currently spells out signed and unsigned DIV and REM as separate\\noperators. On the FPGA this can leave redundant quotient/remainder divider\\ncones and wide result-mux routing in the already timing-sensitive EX2\\nstage. The previous iterative-divider experiment showed that adding a\\nmulti-cycle stall is too expensive, so the useful opportunity is to reduce\\nhardware while retaining the existing single-cycle ALU contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rewrite the real RV32M division logic around one combinational unsigned\\nmagnitude divider that produces both quotient and remainder, then apply\\noperand-sign correction and the RISC-V divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 rules\\nto select DIV, DIVU, REM, or REMU. Keep the ALTOPS formal substitutions\\nunchanged and preserve combinational latency, so dependent instructions,\\nRVFI ordering, and dmem-stall behavior do not acquire new bubbles. Sharing\\nthe expensive divide work should reduce LUT/routing load and improve Fmax\\nwithout the throughput loss of a globally held iterative unit.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the independent real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU operators with one shared quotient-and-remainder datapath, including exact RV32M signedness, zero-divisor, and overflow handling while retaining the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend ALU coverage for shared-divider signed and unsigned quotient/remainder pairs, divide-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1 overflow, and back-to-back operations to guard the refactor.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the independent real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU operators with one shared quotient-and-remainder datapath, including exact RV32M signedness, zero-divisor, and overflow handling while retaining the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend ALU coverage for shared-divider signed and unsigned quotient/remainder pairs, divide-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1 overflow, and back-to-back operations to guard the refactor.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"build_failed: yosys synth: ERROR: Latch inferred for signal `\\\\alu.\\\\div_bit' from always_comb process `\\\\alu.$proc$cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv:61$9'.\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (broken, n/a): In Yosys `always_comb`, a module-scoped integer used as a restoring-divider loop iterator is a synthesizable signal, so paths that skip the loop (such as divide-by-zero) must still assign it or the tool infers a latch; keep loop indices local/automatic or fully assigned.\"}\nhyp-20260710-001-r7s0 | Remove redundant MEM/WB forwarding leg | improvement | 480.9 | 21.33\n23:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Qualify load-use hazard source comparisons\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock compares an older load destination against\\nboth raw register fields of every younger instruction. For immediate,\\nload, jump, and upper-immediate instructions, one or both fields are not\\nregister operands, so coincidental matches create avoidable pipeline\\nbubbles in CoreMark. These bubbles reduce iterations per cycle without\\nproviding any correctness benefit.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock compares an older load destination against\\nboth raw register fields of every younger instruction. For immediate,\\nload, jump, and upper-immediate instructions, one or both fields are not\\nregister operands, so coincidental matches create avoidable pipeline\\nbubbles in CoreMark. These bubbles reduce iterations per cycle without\\nproviding any correctness benefit.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add explicit uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 control bits during decode and carry\\nthem through the existing ID/EX control bundle. Make the hazard unit gate\\neach load-use comparison with the corresponding use bit, while retaining\\nthe existing x0 suppression, EX1 timing, forwarding priority, dmem-stall,\\nredirect, and RVFI behavior. Eliminating false interlocks should improve\\nCoreMark IPC with only a small decode/control-width cost and no new\\ncritical datapath mux.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend ctrl_t with explicit rs1/rs2 operand-use bits carried through the pipeline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Set uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 only for validated RV32I/RV32M encodings that actually read those source registers.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the decoder's operand-use bits into the ID/EX control bundle without changing the top-level interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify load-use comparisons independently for rs1 and rs2 so immediate and non-register fields cannot cause false stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend ctrl_t with explicit rs1/rs2 operand-use bits carried through the pipeline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Set uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 only for validated RV32I/RV32M encodings that actually read those source registers.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the decoder's operand-use bits into the ID/EX control bundle without changing the top-level interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify load-use comparisons independently for rs1 and rs2 so immediate and non-register fields cannot cause false stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 (broken, n/a): Source-use bits for load-use interlocks must remain hazard-only; zeroing unused `rs1`/`rs2` addresses or data in RVFI changes the cosim-visible raw instruction-field trace and breaks selftest.\"}\n26:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Opcode-qualified load-use interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The retimed pipeline still compares a load destination against both raw\\nregister fields of every younger instruction. Immediate, upper-immediate,\\njump, and other non-register-source instructions can therefore incur a\\nneedless load-use bubble. CoreMark's tight integer code makes these false\\nstalls a direct IPC loss, while the previous source-use attempt showed that\\nchanging the architectural trace fields is unsafe.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The retimed pipeline still compares a load destination against both raw\\nregister fields of every younger instruction. Immediate, upper-immediate,\\njump, and other non-register-source instructions can therefore incur a\\nneedless load-use bubble. CoreMark's tight integer code makes these false\\nstalls a direct IPC loss, while the previous source-use attempt showed that\\nchanging the architectural trace fields is unsafe.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Qualify the load-use hazard entirely inside the hazard unit using the raw\\nID/EX instruction opcode and funct3 where needed. Compare the older load's\\nrd only against source fields that the younger encoding actually reads, but\\nkeep the original rs1/rs2 addresses and data unchanged for RVFI and cosim.\\nThis removes false interlocks without adding a forwarding mux or changing\\npipeline timing, while retaining the existing dmem-stall, redirect, and\\nEX2-over-EX/MEM ordering rules.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"f