{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,200p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-001-r15s0.yaml && git status --short -- cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-001-r15s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260710-001-r15s0\ntitle: \"Share the native RV32M divide datapath\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The ALU currently exposes separate native signed and unsigned division and\n  remainder operators.  That can synthesize several large divider cones even\n  though quotient and remainder are mathematically coupled, contributing LUT\n  pressure and routing congestion in the already timing-sensitive EX2 stage.\n  Prior multi-cycle divider changes hurt throughput, so this hypothesis keeps\n  the existing single-cycle execution contract.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the four real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU operators with one unsigned\n  magnitude quotient datapath.  Convert signed operands to magnitudes, derive\n  the remainder as dividend magnitude minus quotient times divisor magnitude,\n  and apply the RISC-V sign, divide-by-zero, and INT_MIN/-1 corrections at the\n  result boundary.  Keep the formal ALTOPS substitutions unchanged and retain\n  the existing shared multiplier for MUL and high-half operations.  Sharing\n  the expensive divider while preserving combinational latency should reduce\n  implementation pressure and improve Fmax without adding pipeline bubbles.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Implement one unsigned-magnitude quotient path with reconstructed remainder and exact signed/unsigned RV32M result correction, while preserving ALTOPS and all divide edge cases.\"\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: \"Add paired DIV/REM and DIVU/REMU vectors covering signed magnitudes, zero divisors, INT_MIN/-1 overflow, and boundary quotients to verify the shared datapath.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
