{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-001-r2s0.yaml && git diff --check -- cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-001-r2s0.yaml && ruby -e 'require \\\"yaml\\\"; p YAML.load_file(ARGV[0])' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-001-r2s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260710-001-r2s0\ntitle: \"Share the RV32M multiplier datapath\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current combinational ALU builds three independent 32x32 products for\n  MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU on every cycle, although each instruction needs\n  only one product interpretation. This is costly on the Tang Nano 20K, where\n  the current predictor-enhanced fixture is already above the LUT target and\n  multiplier routing can also limit Fmax.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the three parallel multiplier expressions with one shared signed\n  33x33 product. Select sign-extension versus zero-extension independently for\n  each operand from the decoded M operation, then use the low or high 32 bits\n  for the corresponding MUL-family result. A 33-bit sign/zero representation\n  preserves the exact RV32M low and high halves, including MULHSU, while\n  keeping the existing one-cycle EX latency, divider behavior, forwarding, and\n  RVFI timing unchanged. The reduced multiplier replication should lower LUT\n  usage and routing pressure, improving the product of CoreMark IPC and Fmax.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Implement a single sign-controlled 33x33 RV32M multiplier, select its low/high halves for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU, and retain the ALTOPS substitutions plus exact divider semantics.\"\n{\"id\"=>\"hyp-20260710-001-r2s0\", \"title\"=>\"Share the RV32M multiplier datapath\", \"category\"=>\"micro_opt\", \"motivation\"=>\"The current combinational ALU builds three independent 32x32 products for\\nMUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU on every cycle, although each instruction needs\\nonly one product interpretation. This is costly on the Tang Nano 20K, where\\nthe current predictor-enhanced fixture is already above the LUT target and\\nmultiplier routing can also limit Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\"=>\"Replace the three parallel multiplier expressions with one shared signed\\n33x33 product. Select sign-extension versus zero-extension independently for\\neach operand from the decoded M operation, then use the low or high 32 bits\\nfor the corresponding MUL-family result. A 33-bit sign/zero representation\\npreserves the exact RV32M low and high halves, including MULHSU, while\\nkeeping the existing one-cycle EX latency, divider behavior, forwarding, and\\nRVFI timing unchanged. The reduced multiplier replication should lower LUT\\nusage and routing pressure, improving the product of CoreMark IPC and Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\"=>{\"fitness_delta_pct\"=>3, \"confidence\"=>\"medium\"}, \"changes\"=>[{\"file\"=>\"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\"=>\"Implement a single sign-controlled 33x33 RV32M multiplier, select its low/high halves for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU, and retain the ALTOPS substitutions plus exact divider semantics.\"}]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
