{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-001-r8s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260710-001-r8s0\ntitle: \"Qualify load-use hazard source comparisons\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current load-use interlock compares an older load destination against\n  both raw register fields of every younger instruction. For immediate,\n  load, jump, and upper-immediate instructions, one or both fields are not\n  register operands, so coincidental matches create avoidable pipeline\n  bubbles in CoreMark. These bubbles reduce iterations per cycle without\n  providing any correctness benefit.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add explicit uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 control bits during decode and carry\n  them through the existing ID/EX control bundle. Make the hazard unit gate\n  each load-use comparison with the corresponding use bit, while retaining\n  the existing x0 suppression, EX1 timing, forwarding priority, dmem-stall,\n  redirect, and RVFI behavior. Eliminating false interlocks should improve\n  CoreMark IPC with only a small decode/control-width cost and no new\n  critical datapath mux.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Extend ctrl_t with explicit rs1/rs2 operand-use bits carried through the pipeline.\"\n  - file: rtl/decoder.sv\n    description: \"Set uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 only for validated RV32I/RV32M encodings that actually read those source registers.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Wire the decoder's operand-use bits into the ID/EX control bundle without changing the top-level interface.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Qualify load-use comparisons independently for rs1 and rs2 so immediate and non-register fields cannot cause false stalls.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
