mful weak-taken startup bias, while larger tagged predictors hurt\\nthe IF timing path. A predictor that targets only the remaining forward\\nbranches can add useful direction information without disturbing the proven\\nloop prediction policy.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current static predictor is effective for CoreMark's backward loops and\\ndirect JALs, but it leaves every forward conditional branch not-taken. The\\nearlier PC-only 16-entry BHT regressed because it aliased branch sites and\\nused a harmful weak-taken startup bias, while larger tagged predictors hurt\\nthe IF timing path. A predictor that targets only the remaining forward\\nbranches can add useful direction information without disturbing the proven\\nloop prediction policy.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small 32-entry, 2-bit gshare direction table for forward conditional\\nbranches. Index the table with a low PC slice XORed with a short global\\nconditional-branch history, initialize counters weakly not-taken, and update\\nthe counter from the precise EX branch outcome. Use the table only when the\\nbranch immediate is forward; retain the existing static backward-taken and\\ndirect-JAL predictions for all other control flow. Carry the existing\\nprediction metadata and redirect on a resolved next-PC mismatch, so cold\\nentries and history aliasing recover precisely without changing RVFI or\\narchitectural behavior. The XOR history should separate recurring CoreMark\\nbranch contexts better than the failed PC-only BHT while adding only a small\\ncounter array and narrow update state.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the small gshare history/counter state, use it only to choose taken predictions for forward conditional branches, preserve the existing backward-branch and direct-JAL static cases, and update state from resolved branches.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose a precise conditional-branch update event containing the resolved branch PC, direction, and validity while leaving redirect, trap, and RVFI next-PC behavior unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX branch-resolution update event into the IF predictor without changing the external bus or single-channel RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the small gshare history/counter state, use it only to choose taken predictions for forward conditional branches, preserve the existing backward-branch and direct-JAL static cases, and update state from resolved branches.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose a precise conditional-branch update event containing the resolved branch PC, direction, and validity while leaving redirect, trap, and RVFI next-PC behavior unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX branch-resolution update event into the IF predictor without changing the external bus or single-channel RVFI contract.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 403.25, \"delta_pct\": 1.74, \"vs_baseline\": 42.58, \"fmax_mhz\": 174.67, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4331511, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10400, \"ff\": 2145, \"seeds\": [184.77, 164.07, 174.67], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T22:18:02.863966Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r7s2 (regression, +1.74%): Even a weakly-not-taken, forward-only 32-entry gshare can regress CoreMark when the combinational `forward_bht[prediction_index][1]` lookup and global-history update add IF-path/state cost that the benchmark's relatively few useful forward-taken predictions do not repay.\"}\n23:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Qualify load-use hazard source comparisons\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock compares an older load destination against\\nboth raw register fields of every younger instruction. For immediate,\\nload, jump, and upper-immediate instructions, one or both fields are not\\nregister operands, so coincidental matches create avoidable pipeline\\nbubbles in CoreMark. These bubbles reduce iterations per cycle without\\nproviding any correctness benefit.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use interlock compares an older load destination against\\nboth raw register fields of every younger instruction. For immediate,\\nload, jump, and upper-immediate instructions, one or both fields are not\\nregister operands, so coincidental matches create avoidable pipeline\\nbubbles in CoreMark. These bubbles reduce iterations per cycle without\\nproviding any correctness benefit.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add explicit uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 control bits during decode and carry\\nthem through the existing ID/EX control bundle. Make the hazard unit gate\\neach load-use comparison with the corresponding use bit, while retaining\\nthe existing x0 suppression, EX1 timing, forwarding priority, dmem-stall,\\nredirect, and RVFI behavior. Eliminating false interlocks should improve\\nCoreMark IPC with only a small decode/control-width cost and no new\\ncritical datapath mux.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend ctrl_t with explicit rs1/rs2 operand-use bits carried through the pipeline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Set uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 only for validated RV32I/RV32M encodings that actually read those source registers.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the decoder's operand-use bits into the ID/EX control bundle without changing the top-level interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify load-use comparisons independently for rs1 and rs2 so immediate and non-register fields cannot cause false stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend ctrl_t with explicit rs1/rs2 operand-use bits carried through the pipeline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Set uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 only for validated RV32I/RV32M encodings that actually read those source registers.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the decoder's operand-use bits into the ID/EX control bundle without changing the top-level interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify load-use comparisons independently for rs1 and rs2 so immediate and non-register fields cannot cause false stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 (broken, n/a): Source-use bits for load-use interlocks must remain hazard-only; zeroing unused `rs1`/`rs2` addresses or data in RVFI changes the cosim-visible raw instruction-field trace and breaks selftest.\"}\n24:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Retimed two-phase memory response\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The retimed EX1/EX2 core has already removed the forwarding muxes from the\\narithmetic critical path, and the failed queue experiments show that broad\\nbackend decoupling is too risky.  The remaining memory-stage path combines\\nthe FPGA data-memory read, byte extraction, sign extension, and MEM/WB\\ncapture in one timing interval.  CoreMark uses enough loads for this path to\\naffect both Fmax and the load-use schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The retimed EX1/EX2 core has already removed the forwarding muxes from the\\narithmetic critical path, and the failed queue experiments show that broad\\nbackend decoupling is too risky.  The remaining memory-stage path combines\\nthe FPGA data-memory read, byte extraction, sign extension, and MEM/WB\\ncapture in one timing interval.  CoreMark uses enough loads for this path to\\naffect both Fmax and the load-use schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the memory stage into a registered request stage and a registered\\nresponse/format stage.  The request stage drives the existing dmem address,\\nwrite data, and byte enables and holds precisely when io_dmemReady is low;\\nthe response stage first captures raw read data and memory metadata, then\\nperforms byte-lane extraction and sign extension into the existing writeback\\npayload.  Pipeline the stages at one request per cycle so zero-wait memory\\nretains throughput while the added register isolates the FPGA memory output\\nfrom the load-formatting cone.  Update hazards and valid/hold sequencing so\\nstores, loads, load-use forwarding, RVFI metadata, and rvfi_order remain\\nage-correct under both ready and stalled data buses.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add request/response pipeline payload fields needed to carry memory operation metadata and raw read data across the retimed backend stages.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single memory combinational path with a ready-aware request register and a response register that performs load extraction and writeback payload formation.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Recompute data-bus stall, load-use, and pipeline hold/bubble controls for the extra memory-response latency without allowing duplicate memory effects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the retimed memory-stage controls and forwarding timing while preserving the existing external buses and single-channel RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add request/response pipeline payload fields needed to carry memory operation metadata and raw read data across the retimed backend stages.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single memory combinational path with a ready-aware request register and a response register that performs load extraction and writeback payload formation.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Recompute data-bus stall, load-use, and pipeline hold/bubble controls for the extra memory-response latency without allowing duplicate memory effects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the retimed memory-stage controls and forwarding timing while preserving the existing external buses and single-channel RVFI contract.\"}], \"outcome\": \"placement_failed\", \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": \"placement_failed\", \"seeds\": [null, null, null], \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r8s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Registering the dmem request/response boundary is not free on this FPGA: carrying full `mem_req_t`/`mem_resp_t` payloads plus request-stall and extra load-forwarding control can make placement fail, so isolate the memory read/format cone with compact metadata and localized backpressure rather than a wide retimed backend.\"}\n25:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Narrow zero-offset return predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The proven static predictor already removes most backward-loop and direct-JAL\\nredirects. Broader return predictors have regressed because their IF logic\\nadded a return decode, validity compares, target arithmetic, or multiway\\nlookup to the fetch critical path. CoreMark's common ABI return form is the\\ncanonical zero-offset JALR through x1, so a narrower predictor can target the\\nrecurring penalty while avoiding the expensive general case.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The proven static predictor already removes most backward-loop and direct-JAL\\nredirects. Broader return predictors have regressed because their IF logic\\nadded a return decode, validity compares, target arithmetic, or multiway\\nlookup to the fetch critical path. CoreMark's common ABI return form is the\\ncanonical zero-offset JALR through x1, so a narrower predictor can target the\\nrecurring penalty while avoiding the expensive general case.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Track only the committed value of x1 in one valid shadow register and predict\\nonly `jalr x0, 0(x1)` in IF. The prediction target is the shadow value itself,\\nwith no link-plus-immediate adder, tag array, history table, or x5 selection\\non the fetch path. Update the shadow from the existing writeback stream and\\ncarry the existing prediction metadata so EX remains authoritative and\\nredirects on a cold, stale, or noncanonical prediction. This should recover\\nrepeated ABI return bubbles at substantially lower Fmax risk than the prior\\ngeneral x1/x5 shadow predictor.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single valid x1 shadow target and predict only canonical zero-offset jalr x0,0(x1), preserving the existing static branch and direct-JAL predictions and precise redirect fallback.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Feed the committed writeback destination/data into IF so the x1 shadow tracks architectural state without changing the external buses or RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single valid x1 shadow target and predict only canonical zero-offset jalr x0,0(x1), preserving the existing static branch and direct-JAL predictions and precise redirect fallback.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Feed the committed writeback destination/data into IF so the x1 shadow tracks architectural state without changing the external buses or RVFI contract.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 391.01, \"delta_pct\": -18.69, \"vs_baseline\": 38.25, \"fmax_mhz\": 168.63, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4312687, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10162, \"ff\": 2105, \"seeds\": [168.63, 175.56, 167.64], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T22:37:24.834917Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r8s2 (regression, -18.69%): Even a one-entry committed x1 shadow that recognizes only `jalr x0, 0(x1)` can regress CoreMark when IF adds canonical-return decode and validity gating to the next-PC cone, so narrowing target arithmetic does not remove the critical-path cost of return prediction.\"}\n26:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Opcode-qualified load-use interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The retimed pipeline still compares a load destination against both raw\\nregister fields of every younger instruction. Immediate, upper-immediate,\\njump, and other non-register-source instructions can therefore incur a\\nneedless load-use bubble. CoreMark's tight integer code makes these false\\nstalls a direct IPC loss, while the previous source-use attempt showed that\\nchanging the architectural trace fields is unsafe.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The retimed pipeline still compares a load destination against both raw\\nregister fields of every younger instruction. Immediate, upper-immediate,\\njump, and other non-register-source instructions can therefore incur a\\nneedless load-use bubble. CoreMark's tight integer code makes these false\\nstalls a direct IPC loss, while the previous source-use attempt showed that\\nchanging the architectural trace fields is unsafe.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Qualify the load-use hazard entirely inside the hazard unit using the raw\\nID/EX instruction opcode and funct3 where needed. Compare the older load's\\nrd only against source fields that the younger encoding actually reads, but\\nkeep the original rs1/rs2 addresses and data unchanged for RVFI and cosim.\\nThis removes false interlocks without adding a forwarding mux or changing\\npipeline timing, while retaining the existing dmem-stall, redirect, and\\nEX2-over-EX/MEM ordering rules.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive hazard-only uses_rs1/uses_rs2 qualifiers from the raw younger instruction encoding and gate each load-use comparison without altering pipeline payload fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass the existing ID/EX instruction word into the hazard unit so source-use qualification does not modify decoder outputs, register addresses, or RVFI metadata.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive hazard-only uses_rs1/uses_rs2 qualifiers from the raw younger instruction encoding and gate each load-use comparison without altering pipeline payload fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass the existing ID/EX instruction word into the hazard unit so source-use qualification does not modify decoder outputs, register addresses, or RVFI metadata.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\nfinished (returncode=0)\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_srai_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_srai_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:02 (2)\\nSBY 19:47:26 [insn_srai_ch0] s