{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|structural|EX1|pipeline|retim|queue|cache|memory' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -n 120\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): A zero-bubble EX/MEM load-data bypass must keep the extracted value, pipeline valid bits, and RVFI metadata aligned while `dmemReady` is low; merely holding the memory stage and suppressing redirects lets dependent instructions observe or retire mismatched register state.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (improvement, +9.81%): A small IF-stage predictor for backward conditional branches and direct JALs, with prediction metadata carried through ID/EX and EX redirects only on next-PC mismatch, removes most CoreMark loop redirect bubbles without the area cost of a BTB/BHT.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (regression, -13.79%): A radix-2 iterative divider that holds the in-order pipeline for its full multi-cycle latency can lose more CoreMark throughput than removing divide operators from the EX cone gains in timing, so divider frequency and stall cost must be measured before coupling DIV/REM to a global EX hold.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, -9.22%): A 16-entry BHT indexed only by `pc[5:2]` aliases CoreMark conditional branches, and weak-taken reset can turn the tuned static forward-not-taken behavior into costly early mispredictions, so tiny dynamic predictors need an aliasing-aware initialization and benchmark profile check.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (improvement, +2.18%): One native-width unsigned `a * b` plus sign-dependent high-half correction terms implements RV32M `MUL`, `MULH`, `MULHU`, and `MULHSU` without the timing penalty of a widened multiplier, preserving CoreMark throughput while reducing redundant multiply hardware.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (regression, -10.46%): A registered one-entry IF skid buffer must preserve empty-slot or simultaneous pop/push bypass behavior and redirect refill latency, or its added fetch bubbles outweigh the isolated imem-stall cycles it hides.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r3s2 (regression, -14.14%): Even a four-entry RAS can regress CoreMark when its combinational 4-way `ras_top` mux and return-form decode sit directly on the IF next-PC path, so return-prediction savings must be weighed against fetch timing before adding predictor state.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (regression, -18.78%): Reusing the EX ALU sum for branch/JAL/JALR targets can make control-flow resolution depend on added operand-selection muxing, so removing target adders is not worthwhile if it lengthens the fetch redirect path and costs CoreMark throughput.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): Moving branch/JALR resolution into ID requires age-ordered operand bypasses and synchronized `resolved_valid`/redirect/flush/RVFI metadata, because a partial early redirect path can make the retired instruction stream, PC-next values, and register checks disagree even when the EX fallback remains.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r4s2 (regression, -22.88%): A tagged loop predictor that adds IF-stage tag/state lookup to suppress only final-iteration backward-branch redirects can cost more fetch performance than the occasional exit flush it removes, even when exact tags avoid BHT aliasing.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -20.38%): A zero-wait load-use bypass that drives sign/zero-extended `dmem_rdata` through the EX forwarding mux can lengthen the critical path enough to outweigh the bubble it removes, so its CPI win must be measured against the added MEM-to-EX timing cost.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r5s1 (improvement, +24.91%): Splitting execute into an operand/forwarding EX1 and arithmetic/control EX2 stage can materially improve CoreMark fitness by removing the forwarding muxes from the ALU cone, provided EX2-to-EX1 age-ordered bypasses and precise bubble/hold control preserve dependent ALU, load-use, and dmem-stall ordering.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (broken, n/a): In Yosys `always_comb`, a module-scoped integer used as a restoring-divider loop iterator is a synthesizable signal, so paths that skip the loop (such as divide-by-zero) must still assign it or the tool infers a latch; keep loop indices local/automatic or fully assigned.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (regression, -46.71%): A one-entry store buffer does not hide dmem stalls if queue occupancy still feeds the global `dmem_stall` and a combinational `store_dmem_valid` priority mux arbitrates the external bus, so capture/drain handshakes must be decoupled without lengthening the memory timing path or serializing later accesses.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (regression, -5.28%): A writeback-fed x1/x5 shadow predictor can lose CoreMark throughput when return decode, valid compares, and a 32-bit link-plus-immediate adder enter the IF next-PC cone, so return-bubble savings must be weighed against fetch timing and stale-link mispredict redirects.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (improvement, +21.33%): Removing a redundant MEM/WB forwarding compare and mux improved CoreMark because EX1's write-first register-file reread already exposes WB data, so forwarding should retain only younger values unavailable at the read ports while preserving EX2-over-EX/MEM priority.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r7s1 (broken, n/a): Moving the retirement boundary behind an elastic completion queue can make the causal RVFI/dmem assumptions unsatisfiable at a shallow step and cascade into every instruction check, so queue issue, memory-handshake, forwarding, and in-order retirement metadata must remain formally coherent before measuring stall hiding.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r7s2 (regression, +1.74%): Even a weakly-not-taken, forward-only 32-entry gshare can regress CoreMark when the combinational `forward_bht[prediction_index][1]` lookup and global-history update add IF-path/state cost that the benchmark's relatively few useful forward-taken predictions do not repay.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 (broken, n/a): Source-use bits for load-use interlocks must remain hazard-only; zeroing unused `rs1`/`rs2` addresses or data in RVFI changes the cosim-visible raw instruction-field trace and breaks selftest.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r8s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Registering the dmem request/response boundary is not free on this FPGA: carrying full `mem_req_t`/`mem_resp_t` payloads plus request-stall and extra load-forwarding control can make placement fail, so isolate the memory read/format cone with compact metadata and localized backpressure rather than a wide retimed backend.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r8s2 (regression, -18.69%): Even a one-entry committed x1 shadow that recognizes only `jalr x0, 0(x1)` can regress CoreMark when IF adds canonical-return decode and validity gating to the next-PC cone, so narrowing target arithmetic does not remove the critical-path cost of return prediction.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r9s0 (broken, n/a): Opcode-qualified load-use comparisons in the hazard unit still failed the RVFI register check, so removing bubbles requires proving the raw-instruction source map remains aligned with valid, held, and redirected ID/EX entries across the full instruction stream.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r9s1 (regression, -56.76%): Replacing EX1's dedicated register-file reread with stall-gated ID/EX snapshot refresh shifted complexity into decode-address muxing and an added WB forwarding compare/mux, so removing read ports can severely hurt CoreMark unless the operand-path timing and stall behavior are measured together.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r9s2 (regression, -59.91%): Sharing B/J target generation through a selected-immediate mux and an unconditional `pc + selected_imm` adder left more decode logic on the IF next-PC cone, showing that arithmetic sharing can trade area for severe CoreMark timing loss.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r10s0 (regression, -55.42%): Replacing the load-stage variable shift with explicit byte-lane and halfword muxes did not improve CoreMark and likely lengthened the load-response/forwarding cone, so FPGA resource-oriented mux simplification must be validated against the implemented timing path.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r10s1 (regression, -52.32%): Replicating the full 32x32 register file for ID and EX1 preserves logical read timing but can make FPGA resource, reset, and write-fanout costs overwhelm locality gains, so bank replication needs post-place evidence that the shared read mux is the binding constraint.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r10s2 (regression, -38.60%): Even a one-entry exact-PC forward-branch predictor can severely regress CoreMark when its fetched-branch decode, tag compare, and counter state feed the IF next-PC cone, so residual forward-redirect savings do not justify adding predictor logic to the fetch critical path.\n2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Zero-bubble load-use forwarding\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline unconditionally stalls one cycle for every\\nload-use dependency, even though the Tang Nano bench presents combinational\\nzero-wait read data. CoreMark has frequent load-dependent address, compare,\\nand arithmetic instructions, so this bubble directly reduces iterations per\\ncycle. The existing P&R report also shows the EX/MEM-to-EX forwarding path as\\nthe dominant timing path, making a dedicated load-data bypass a focused\\nhigh-value datapath optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage pipeline unconditionally stalls one cycle for every\\nload-use dependency, even though the Tang Nano bench presents combinational\\nzero-wait read data. CoreMark has frequent load-dependent address, compare,\\nand arithmetic instructions, so this bubble directly reduces iterations per\\ncycle. The existing P&R report also shows the EX/MEM-to-EX forwarding path as\\nthe dominant timing path, making a dedicated load-data bypass a focused\\nhigh-value datapath optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a dedicated EX/MEM load-data bypass from the memory stage's extracted\\nbyte/half/word value into the EX operand mux, and remove the unconditional\\nload-use bubble. When dmemReady is low, hold the dependent instruction and\\nsuppress redirects or side effects until the load completes; when it is high,\\nforward the load value directly and retire the dependent instruction without\\na bubble. This should preserve arbitrary bus-stall correctness while\\nimproving CoreMark IPC on the zero-wait FPGA path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the unconditional load-use bubble with replay-safe holding on an outstanding dmem stall, and provide control to block redirects while the dependent instruction waits.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a highest-priority EX/MEM load-result forwarding selection, with normal ALU and MEM/WB forwarding retained as fallbacks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the already sign/zero-extended load value as a combinational forwarding source while retaining the existing RVFI extraction and stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the load-data bypass and ensure a stalled dependent instruction cannot generate a redirect or architectural side effect before its operand is valid.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the load-result bypass and dmem-stall qualification through the pipeline without changing the top-level IO or RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"c