{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-002-r5s1.yaml && ruby -e 'require \\\"yaml\\\"; p YAML.load_file(ARGV[0])' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-002-r5s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260710-002-r5s1\ntitle: \"Retimed two-phase execute pipeline\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current critical path starts at the ID/EX register and traverses the\n  EX forwarding muxes, ALU operand selection, and the 32-bit arithmetic cone\n  before reaching EX/MEM.  The successful static predictor already removes\n  most loop redirect bubbles, while the earlier fetch-buffer and decode-\n  redirect structures lost more Fmax than they gained in IPC.  A physical\n  execute-stage retiming therefore offers a more direct way to improve the\n  fitness product without adding predictor state or widening the interface.\nhypothesis: |\n  Split execute into an operand/forwarding EX1 stage and an arithmetic/control\n  EX2 stage, with a pipeline register between them.  EX1 captures the selected\n  forwarded operands, immediate, PC, control, and prediction metadata; EX2\n  performs the ALU operation, memory-address calculation, branch or jump\n  resolution, and alignment checks, then drives the existing EX/MEM contract.\n  Add age-correct EX2-to-EX2 and MEM/WB bypasses and stall/flush control so\n  dependent ALU instructions still sustain one instruction per cycle, while\n  load-use and dmem stalls retain precise RVFI validity and ordering.  The\n  extra register should remove the forwarding/operand mux delay from the\n  arithmetic timing path and raise Fmax, with only the already-predicted\n  control-flow cases avoiding an added redirect penalty.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add the internal EX1 pipeline bundle needed to carry forwarded operands, control, PC, and static-prediction metadata across the new execute-stage boundary while retaining the existing EX/MEM and RVFI bundle fields.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Retiming the execute logic into operand-capture EX1 and arithmetic/control EX2 registers, with precise forwarding, branch prediction comparison, misalignment traps, dmem-stall holds, and the unchanged EX/MEM output contract.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Recompute load-use, redirect flush, and dmem backpressure arbitration for the additional execute stage so bubbles and holds preserve age order and do not duplicate or skip RVFI retirements.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the retimed execute-stage controls and forwarding sources through the existing five-stage top-level datapath without changing the external buses or single-channel RVFI port set.\"\n{\"id\"=>\"hyp-20260710-002-r5s1\", \"title\"=>\"Retimed two-phase execute pipeline\", \"category\"=>\"structural\", \"motivation\"=>\"The current critical path starts at the ID/EX register and traverses the\\nEX forwarding muxes, ALU operand selection, and the 32-bit arithmetic cone\\nbefore reaching EX/MEM.  The successful static predictor already removes\\nmost loop redirect bubbles, while the earlier fetch-buffer and decode-\\nredirect structures lost more Fmax than they gained in IPC.  A physical\\nexecute-stage retiming therefore offers a more direct way to improve the\\nfitness product without adding predictor state or widening the interface.\\n\", \"hypothesis\"=>\"Split execute into an operand/forwarding EX1 stage and an arithmetic/control\\nEX2 stage, with a pipeline register between them.  EX1 captures the selected\\nforwarded operands, immediate, PC, control, and prediction metadata; EX2\\nperforms the ALU operation, memory-address calculation, branch or jump\\nresolution, and alignment checks, then drives the existing EX/MEM contract.\\nAdd age-correct EX2-to-EX2 and MEM/WB bypasses and stall/flush control so\\ndependent ALU instructions still sustain one instruction per cycle, while\\nload-use and dmem stalls retain precise RVFI validity and ordering.  The\\nextra register should remove the forwarding/operand mux delay from the\\narithmetic timing path and raise Fmax, with only the already-predicted\\ncontrol-flow cases avoiding an added redirect penalty.\\n\", \"expected_impact\"=>{\"fitness_delta_pct\"=>6, \"confidence\"=>\"medium\"}, \"changes\"=>[{\"file\"=>\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\"=>\"Add the internal EX1 pipeline bundle needed to carry forwarded operands, control, PC, and static-prediction metadata across the new execute-stage boundary while retaining the existing EX/MEM and RVFI bundle fields.\"}, {\"file\"=>\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\"=>\"Retiming the execute logic into operand-capture EX1 and arithmetic/control EX2 registers, with precise forwarding, branch prediction comparison, misalignment traps, dmem-stall holds, and the unchanged EX/MEM output contract.\"}, {\"file\"=>\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\"=>\"Recompute load-use, redirect flush, and dmem backpressure arbitration for the additional execute stage so bubbles and holds preserve age order and do not duplicate or skip RVFI retirements.\"}, {\"file\"=>\"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\"=>\"Wire the retimed execute-stage controls and forwarding sources through the existing five-stage top-level datapath without changing the external buses or single-channel RVFI port set.\"}]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
