{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-002-r6s1.yaml && git status --short -- cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-002-r6s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260710-002-r6s1\ntitle: \"Add a one-entry nonblocking store buffer\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current MEM stage freezes the entire in-order pipeline whenever an\n  EX/MEM load or store sees io_dmemReady low.  The benchmark deliberately\n  injects about 22% data-bus stalls, and a store has no result dependency that\n  requires younger arithmetic instructions to wait for the bus.  Decoupling\n  the write side can recover useful work during those stalls without putting\n  another mux on the instruction-fetch path or adding a load-data bypass to\n  the already timing-sensitive EX path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Insert a one-entry store queue between EX/MEM and the dmem interface.  A\n  store is captured with its aligned address, replicated data, byte mask, and\n  RVFI metadata, then the core may continue issuing non-memory instructions\n  while the queue holds the request until io_dmemReady accepts it.  Preserve\n  program-order memory semantics by blocking a second memory operation while\n  the queue is occupied (or forwarding an exact same-address store only if the\n  existing bus contract makes that safe), and leave loads on the current\n  precise wait path.  The queue must retire each store once, drain before a\n  younger load can observe memory, and flush/hold consistently with redirects\n  and reset.  This should hide a portion of random store stalls while keeping\n  load-use and RVFI ordering behavior unchanged.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/store_buffer.sv\n    description: \"Add a one-entry ready/valid store queue that retains address, write data, byte enables, and ordering state until the dmem request is accepted.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Split store request capture and bus draining from load response handling, and expose precise store-queue full/drained controls without duplicating RVFI memory effects.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Allow younger non-memory instructions to advance behind a queued store while stalling later memory operations until the queue drains, with dmem-stall and redirect precedence preserved.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Integrate the store buffer into the existing MEM interface and pipeline control while preserving the unchanged top-level IO and single-channel RVFI contract.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
