{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"structural\\\") | [.id,.outcome,(.delta_pct // \\\"n/a\\\"),.title,((.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis) | gsub(\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\";\\\" \\\"))] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260710-002-r1s1\tregression\t-4.46\tAdd a decoupled instruction prefetch FIFO\tReplace the single PC/IF-ID coupling with a small redirect-aware two-entry instruction prefetch FIFO. Fetch into the FIFO whenever imemReady is high, consume one entry per cycle into ID, and invalidate both entries on an EX redirect while restarting the fetch pointer at the resolved target. This should absorb isolated imem stalls and remove front-end bubbles without changing retirement order or the external bus contract. \nhyp-20260710-002-r2s1\tregression\t-13.79\tDecouple long-latency M execution\tReplace the combinational divide/remainder portion of the ALU with a shared iterative radix-2 M execution unit. A DIV, DIVU, REM, or REMU entering EX holds the in-order pipeline while the unit runs, then writes one RV32M-correct result through the existing EX/MEM and RVFI paths; ordinary ALU, load/store, branch, and multiply operations retain their single-cycle path. Add explicit busy/done control and operand/result registers so the divider is removed from the normal EX critical path, while preserving the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS behavior and all trap, memory-stall, and retirement ordering invariants. \nhyp-20260710-002-r3s1\tregression\t-10.46\tAdd a minimal elastic fetch skid buffer\tInsert a single-entry registered fetch skid buffer between the instruction bus and ID. Capture the fetched instruction, PC, and existing static prediction metadata whenever the bus handshakes, consume the buffered entry when ID can advance, and hold it across an imem stall instead of injecting a front-end bubble. On an EX redirect, invalidate the entry and restart the fetch address at the resolved target; preserve the existing load-use, dmem-stall, RVFI ordering, and predictor-metadata rules. The one-entry elastic path should recover isolated iStall cycles while avoiding the pointer and mux overhead that hurt the prior two-entry FIFO's Fmax. \nhyp-20260710-002-r4s1\tbroken\tn/a\tResolve control flow in decode\tMove branch-condition, branch-target, and JALR-target resolution into ID, using narrow bypasses from EX/MEM and MEM/WB plus the existing write-first register-file behavior. Feed the resolved target into the current redirect and prediction-metadata machinery so correctly predicted transfers do not redirect twice, while retaining an EX fallback for misalignment and any unresolved operand case. This should reduce the wrong-path window for forward branches and eliminate most of the remaining JALR return penalty at single-issue throughput, with less IF timing cost than a dynamic return predictor. \nhyp-20260710-002-r5s1\timprovement\t24.91\tRetimed two-phase execute pipeline\tSplit execute into an operand/forwarding EX1 stage and an arithmetic/control EX2 stage, with a pipeline register between them.  EX1 captures the selected forwarded operands, immediate, PC, control, and prediction metadata; EX2 performs the ALU operation, memory-address calculation, branch or jump resolution, and alignment checks, then drives the existing EX/MEM contract. Add age-correct EX2-to-EX2 and MEM/WB bypasses and stall/flush control so dependent ALU instructions still sustain one instruction per cycle, while load-use and dmem stalls retain precise RVFI validity and ordering.  The extra register should remove the forwarding/operand mux delay from the arithmetic timing path and raise Fmax, with only the already-predicted control-flow cases avoiding an added redirect penalty. \nhyp-20260710-002-r6s1\tregression\t-46.71\tAdd a one-entry nonblocking store buffer\tInsert a one-entry store queue between EX/MEM and the dmem interface.  A store is captured with its aligned address, replicated data, byte mask, and RVFI metadata, then the core may continue issuing non-memory instructions while the queue holds the request until io_dmemReady accepts it.  Preserve program-order memory semantics by blocking a second memory operation while the queue is occupied (or forwarding an exact same-address store only if the existing bus contract makes that safe), and leave loads on the current precise wait path.  The queue must retire each store once, drain before a younger load can observe memory, and flush/hold consistently with redirects and reset.  This should hide a portion of random store stalls while keeping load-use and RVFI ordering behavior unchanged. \nhyp-20260710-002-r7s1\tbroken\tn/a\tElastic in-order memory backend\tAdd a small age-ordered completion queue after EX2 together with a dedicated memory-request slot.  A load or store occupies the memory slot until its dmem handshake completes, while younger non-memory instructions can execute and wait in the completion queue instead of freezing the whole backend. Block younger memory operations and consumers of a pending load, and apply backpressure only when the queue is full.  Retire and expose RVFI strictly from the oldest queue entry, with queue-aware forwarding for values that are complete but not yet retired.  This should hide a meaningful fraction of random dmem stalls without adding logic to the IF critical path or changing the external bus and single-channel RVFI contracts. \nhyp-20260710-002-r8s1\tplacement_failed\tn/a\tRetimed two-phase memory response\tSplit the memory stage into a registered request stage and a registered response/format stage.  The request stage drives the existing dmem address, write data, and byte enables and holds precisely when io_dmemReady is low; the response stage first captures raw read data and memory metadata, then performs byte-lane extraction and sign extension into the existing writeback payload.  Pipeline the stages at one request per cycle so zero-wait memory retains throughput while the added register isolates the FPGA memory output from the load-formatting cone.  Update hazards and valid/hold sequencing so stores, loads, load-use forwarding, RVFI metadata, and rvfi_order remain age-correct under both ready and stalled data buses. \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"The strongest remaining structural opportunity is to collapse the redundant four-read-port register-file topology. The ID/EX bundle already snapshots operands; the extra EX1 reread ports exist mainly to recover values after a stall. Refreshing those snapshots during ID/EX holds preserves the required stall behavior while removing two read ports, their muxing, and their fanout from the execute path."}}
