{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category == \\\"predictor\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.delta_pct // \\\"\\\"),(.fitness // \\\"\\\"),(.fmax_mhz // \\\"\\\"),(.lesson // \\\"\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260710-003-r1s2\tEarly static backward-branch prediction\timprovement\t9.81\t310.56\t132.29\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (improvement, +9.81%): A small IF-stage predictor for backward conditional branches and direct JALs, with prediction metadata carried through ID/EX and EX redirects only on next-PC mismatch, removes most CoreMark loop redirect bubbles without the area cost of a BTB/BHT.\nhyp-20260710-003-r2s2\tAdd a tiny dynamic branch history predictor\tregression\t-9.22\t281.94\t118.96\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, -9.22%): A 16-entry BHT indexed only by `pc[5:2]` aliases CoreMark conditional branches, and weak-taken reset can turn the tuned static forward-not-taken behavior into costly early mispredictions, so tiny dynamic predictors need an aliasing-aware initialization and benchmark profile check.\nhyp-20260710-003-r3s2\tAdd a compact return-address stack predictor\tregression\t-14.14\t266.64\t113.21\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r3s2 (regression, -14.14%): Even a four-entry RAS can regress CoreMark when its combinational 4-way `ras_top` mux and return-form decode sit directly on the IF next-PC path, so return-prediction savings must be weighed against fetch timing before adding predictor state.\nhyp-20260710-003-r4s2\tAdd a tagged loop-exit predictor\tregression\t-22.88\t244.72\t104.48\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r4s2 (regression, -22.88%): A tagged loop predictor that adds IF-stage tag/state lookup to suppress only final-iteration backward-branch redirects can cost more fetch performance than the occasional exit flush it removes, even when exact tags avoid BHT aliasing.\nhyp-20260710-003-r5s2\tAdd a tiny exact-tag return target cache\tregression\t-21.52\t249.03\t105.82\t\nhyp-20260710-003-r6s2\tShadow-link return predictor\tregression\t-5.28\t375.45\t161.92\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (regression, -5.28%): A writeback-fed x1/x5 shadow predictor can lose CoreMark throughput when return decode, valid compares, and a 32-bit link-plus-immediate adder enter the IF next-PC cone, so return-bubble savings must be weighed against fetch timing and stale-link mispredict redirects.\nhyp-20260710-003-r7s2\tSelective gshare forward-branch predictor\tregression\t1.74\t403.25\t174.67\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r7s2 (regression, +1.74%): Even a weakly-not-taken, forward-only 32-entry gshare can regress CoreMark when the combinational `forward_bht[prediction_index][1]` lookup and global-history update add IF-path/state cost that the benchmark's relatively few useful forward-taken predictions do not repay.\nhyp-20260710-003-r8s2\tNarrow zero-offset return predictor\tregression\t-18.69\t391.01\t168.63\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r8s2 (regression, -18.69%): Even a one-entry committed x1 shadow that recognizes only `jalr x0, 0(x1)` can regress CoreMark when IF adds canonical-return decode and validity gating to the next-PC cone, so narrowing target arithmetic does not remove the critical-path cost of return prediction.\nhyp-20260710-003-r9s2\tSingle-adder static branch predictor\tregression\t-59.91\t192.8\t83.68\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r9s2 (regression, -59.91%): Sharing B/J target generation through a selected-immediate mux and an unconditional `pc + selected_imm` adder left more decode logic on the IF next-PC cone, showing that arithmetic sharing can trade area for severe CoreMark timing loss.\nhyp-20260710-003-r10s2\tExact one-entry forward branch predictor\tregression\t-38.6\t295.29\t128.16\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r10s2 (regression, -38.60%): Even a one-entry exact-PC forward-branch predictor can severely regress CoreMark when its fetched-branch decode, tag compare, and counter state feed the IF next-PC cone, so residual forward-redirect savings do not justify adding predictor logic to the fetch critical path.\nhyp-20260710-003-r11s2\tStatic operand-identity branch prediction\tregression\t-31.9\t327.48\t142.13\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r11s2 (regression, -31.90%): Even a stateless five-bit `rs1 == rs2` check plus funct3 decode can regress CoreMark when added to the IF next-PC cone, so architecturally certain branch identities are not worth predicting unless their decode cost is timing-neutral.\nhyp-20260710-003-r12s2\tDecode-elided backward branch prediction\tregression\t-38.59\t295.31\t128.17\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r12s2 (regression, -38.59%): Eliding the IF predictor's `funct3` legality check still regressed CoreMark, showing that removing one decode predicate is insufficient when the remaining opcode/sign recognition and backward-target arithmetic stay on the fetch next-PC path.\nhyp-20260710-003-r13s2\tSingle-entry JALR target cache\tregression\t-34.55\t314.75\t135.98\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r13s2 (regression, -34.55%): Even a one-entry EX-trained JALR target cache with only an exact-PC compare and target mux on the IF next-PC path can cost more CoreMark timing than recurring return bubbles save, so minimal indirect prediction must still be timing-neutral in fetch.\nhyp-20260710-003-r14s2\tShort-loop filtered backward prediction\tregression\t-54.29\t219.81\t95.54\t- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r14s2 (regression, -54.29%): Filtering backward-branch prediction with a wide `branch_imm[31:8]` equality added enough decode and immediate logic to the IF next-PC path that saved long-exit flushes could not repay the fetch timing cost, so stateless predictor refinements must be timing-neutral.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
