es.append((pc, target, direction))\\nimport collections\\ncnt = collections.Counter([b[2] for b in branches])\\nprint(cnt)\\nprint('Total branches:', len(branches))\\n\\\"\\n\",\"description\":\"Classify branches as forward/backward\"},\"output\":\"Traceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 12, in <module>\\n    insn = int(parts[1].split('\\\\t')[0].strip(), 16)\\nValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 16: ''\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"Traceback (most recent call last):\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 12, in <module>\\n    insn = int(parts[1].split('\\\\t')[0].strip(), 16)\\nValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 16: ''\\n\",\"exit\":1,\"description\":\"Classify branches as forward/backward\",\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"Classify branches as forward/backward\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778747893169,\"end\":1778747893171}},\"id\":\"prt_e25a2cd940015eooiHLo4eACg6\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1da682b06ffenZMz5sHSWkRx4V\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e25a2b1e6001sDFupzMn55n9rm\"}}\nbench/kimi-k2_6/rep2/agent.log:919:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778747909984,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1da682b06ffenZMz5sHSWkRx4V\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"bash:14\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"python3 -c \\\"\\nimport subprocess, re\\nelf = '/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/kimi-k2_6-rep2/bench/programs/coremark.elf'\\nlines = subprocess.check_output(['riscv64-unknown-elf-objdump', '-d', elf]).decode().split('\\\\n')\\nbranches = []\\nfor line in lines:\\n    if any(b in line for b in ['beq','bne','blt','bge','bltu','bgeu']):\\n        m = re.match(r'\\\\s+([0-9a-f]+):\\\\s+([0-9a-f]+)', line)\\n        if m:\\n            pc = int(m.group(1), 16)\\n            insn = int(m.group(2), 16)\\n            imm12 = ((insn >> 31) & 1) << 12\\n            imm12 |= ((insn >> 7) & 1) << 11\\n            imm12 |= ((insn >> 25) & 0x3f) << 5\\n            imm12 |= ((insn >> 8) & 0xf) << 1\\n            if imm12 & 0x1000:\\n                imm12 = imm12 - 0x2000\\n            target = pc + imm12\\n            direction = 'backward' if target < pc else 'forward'\\n            branches.append((pc, target, direction, line.strip()))\\nimport collections\\ncnt = collections.Counter([b[2] for b in branches])\\nprint('Forward:', cnt.get('forward', 0))\\nprint('Backward:', cnt.get('backward', 0))\\nprint('Total:', len(branches))\\n# Show first 10 forward branches\\nfor b in branches[:10]:\\n    if b[2] == 'forward':\\n        print(hex(b[0]), '->', hex(b[1]), b[3])\\n\\\"\\n\",\"description\":\"Classify branches forward/backward\"},\"output\":\"Forward: 246\\nBackward: 126\\nTotal: 372\\n0x28 -> 0x34 28:\\t00078663          \\tbeqz\\ta5,34 <calc_func+0x18>\\n0x68 -> 0x104 68:\\t08068e63          \\tbeqz\\ta3,104 <calc_func+0xe8>\\n0x70 -> 0xf4 70:\\t08c69263          \\tbne\\ta3,a2,f4 <calc_func+0xd8>\\n0xa0 -> 0x148 a0:\\t0a079463          \\tbnez\\ta5,148 <calc_func+0x12c>\\n0x108 -> 0x110 108:\\t00c6e463          \\tbltu\\ta3,a2,110 <calc_func+0xf4>\\n0x140 -> 0x148 140:\\t00079463          \\tbnez\\ta5,148 <calc_func+0x12c>\\n0x178 -> 0x218 178:\\t0a079063          \\tbnez\\ta5,218 <cmp_complex+0xc8>\\n0x1a0 -> 0x360 1a0:\\t1c068063          \\tbeqz\\ta3,360 <cmp_complex+0x210>\\n0x1a8 -> 0x2f4 1a8:\\t14c69663          \\tbne\\ta3,a2,2f4 <cmp_complex+0x1a4>\\n0x1d8 -> 0x3a4 1d8:\\t1c079663          \\tbnez\\ta5,3a4 <cmp_complex+0x254>\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"Forward: 246\\nBackward: 126\\nTotal: 372\\n0x28 -> 0x34 28:\\t00078663          \\tbeqz\\ta5,34 <calc_func+0x18>\\n0x68 -> 0x104 68:\\t08068e63          \\tbeqz\\ta3,104 <calc_func+0xe8>\\n0x70 -> 0xf4 70:\\t08c69263          \\tbne\\ta3,a2,f4 <calc_func+0xd8>\\n0xa0 -> 0x148 a0:\\t0a079463          \\tbnez\\ta5,148 <calc_func+0x12c>\\n0x108 -> 0x110 108:\\t00c6e463          \\tbltu\\ta3,a2,110 <calc_func+0xf4>\\n0x140 -> 0x148 140:\\t00079463          \\tbnez\\ta5,148 <calc_func+0x12c>\\n0x178 -> 0x218 178:\\t0a079063          \\tbnez\\ta5,218 <cmp_complex+0xc8>\\n0x1a0 -> 0x360 1a0:\\t1c068063          \\tbeqz\\ta3,360 <cmp_complex+0x210>\\n0x1a8 -> 0x2f4 1a8:\\t14c69663          \\tbne\\ta3,a2,2f4 <cmp_complex+0x1a4>\\n0x1d8 -> 0x3a4 1d8:\\t1c079663          \\tbnez\\ta5,3a4 <cmp_complex+0x254>\\n\",\"exit\":0,\"description\":\"Classify branches forward/backward\",\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"Classify branches forward/backward\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778747909980,\"end\":1778747909984}},\"id\":\"prt_e25a30b590014GTrLuGvokroaA\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1da682b06ffenZMz5sHSWkRx4V\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e25a2ef48001AoBF1d3Hur495L\"}}\nbench/kimi-k2_6/rep2/agent.log:922:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778747932239,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1da682b06ffenZMz5sHSWkRx4V\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"bash:15\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"riscv64-unknown-elf-objdump -d /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/kimi-k2_6-rep2/bench/programs/coremark.elf | grep -E 'jal\\\\s+[a-z0-9]' | wc -l\",\"description\":\"Count JAL instructions\"},\"output\":\"      96\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"      96\\n\",\"exit\":0,\"description\":\"Count JAL instructions\",\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"Count JAL instructions\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778747932237,\"end\":1778747932239}},\"id\":\"prt_e25a37911001NkSwL58750tQb3\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1da682b06ffenZMz5sHSWkRx4V\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e25a330d6001mcCF7P3csFh8tg\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep1/log.jsonl:13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny RAS return predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior branch predictors regressed because they put target adders, tag compares,\\nor predicted-PC metadata on already-sensitive frontend and EX paths. CoreMark\\nstill executes call/return control flow around its benchmark kernels, and\\nreturns are predictable with much less IF logic than conditional branches or a\\nBTB: an exact `ret` instruction compare plus a registered return-stack top.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior branch predictors regressed because they put target adders, tag compares,\\nor predicted-PC metadata on already-sensitive frontend and EX paths. CoreMark\\nstill executes call/return control flow around its benchmark kernels, and\\nreturns are predictable with much less IF logic than conditional branches or a\\nBTB: an exact `ret` instruction compare plus a registered return-stack top.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny return-address-stack predictor for canonical RISC-V returns only.\\nTrain the stack when EX resolves link-register JAL/JALR calls, and when IF\\nfetches `jalr x0, 0(x1)` or `jalr x0, 0(x5)` with a valid stack entry, use the\\nstored top as the next PC. Carry only return-prediction metadata to EX so a\\ncorrectly predicted return suppresses the normal JALR redirect, while a bad or\\nempty-stack prediction redirects to the architecturally resolved target. This\\nshould recover return redirect bubbles with far less timing cost than the\\nfailed static branch, BTB, and loop-target predictors because IF does not\\ncompute branch immediates, add targets, or compare fetch PCs against tags.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact return-prediction metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for EX redirect suppression and recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny registered return-address stack, exact canonical-ret detection, and EX-trained push/pop inputs without adding branch target arithmetic to IF.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Detect link-register calls and canonical returns, provide RAS update signals, and suppress JALR redirects only when the carried return prediction matches the resolved target.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire RAS prediction/update signals between IF and EX while preserving the nret=1 RVFI port contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact return-prediction metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for EX redirect suppression and recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny registered return-address stack, exact canonical-ret detection, and EX-trained push/pop inputs without adding branch target arithmetic to IF.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Detect link-register calls and canonical returns, provide RAS update signals, and suppress JALR redirects only when the carried return prediction matches the resolved target.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire RAS prediction/update signals between IF and EX while preserving the nret=1 RVFI port contract.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 368.56, \"delta_pct\": -3.19, \"vs_baseline\": 30.32, \"fmax_mhz\": 165.04, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4478022, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6399, \"ff\": 2512, \"seeds\": [157.55, 169.55, 165.04], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-12T15:55:52.894682Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-05-12 hyp-20260512-003-r4s2 (regression, -3.19%): Even canonical-ret-only RAS prediction can regress CoreMark because the IF `imem_data` return compare, RAS top/index read, and next-PC mux cost more timing than saved JALR redirect bubbles.\"}\nbench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep1/log.jsonl:37:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Decode return target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor experiments on this core repeatedly lost Fmax by putting\\nbranch target adders, tag compares, BTB/RAS reads, or replay muxing on the\\nfetch next-PC path. The least costly remaining control-flow opportunity is\\ncanonical function returns: CoreMark still has call/return structure, and a\\nreturn target is already available as the decoded rs1 register value for\\nwell-spaced `jalr x0, 0(x1/x5)` instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor experiments on this core repeatedly lost Fmax by putting\\nbranch target adders, tag compares, BTB/RAS reads, or replay muxing on the\\nfetch next-PC path. The least costly remaining control-flow opportunity is\\ncanonical function returns: CoreMark still has call/return structure, and a\\nreturn target is already available as the decoded rs1 register value for\\nwell-spaced `jalr x0, 0(x1/x5)` instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a decode-stage exact return target predictor for canonical returns only.\\nWhen ID sees `jalr x0, 0(x1)` or `jalr x0, 0(x5)`, the instruction is valid,\\nID is not stalled/flushed, the rs1 value does not need EX/MEM forwarding, and\\nthe cleared JALR target is word-aligned, redirect IF to that register target\\none cycle before the existing EX JALR redirect. Carry a compact predicted-ret\\nbit and target in ID/EX so EX suppresses the normal redirect only when the\\nresolved target matches; all forwarded, misaligned, or mismatching cases fall\\nback to the current EX redirect/trap behavior. This targets return bubbles\\nwith much less timing risk than the failed IF-side RAS, BTB, JAL, loop, and\\nreplay predictors because there is no fetch-stage instruction decode, target\\naddition, stack lookup, or tag compare on the common fall-through path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact predicted-return metadata to id_ex_t so EX can suppress only matching early return redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Detect aligned canonical returns in decode, gate them on valid/non-stalled state and no required EX/MEM forwarding, emit an early IF redirect target, and latch the prediction metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare predicted return targets against resolved JALR targets and suppress the normal redirect only on exact matches, preserving misalignment traps and RVFI pc_next.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the ID-stage early return redirect to IF with EX redirect priority and without changing the nret=1 RVFI port contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept a gated decode-stage predicted redirect as a lower-priority PC update than EX redirect while keeping ordinary fall-through fetch unchanged.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact predicted-return metadata to id_ex_t so EX can suppress only matching early return redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Detect aligned canonical returns in decode, gate them on valid/non-stalled state and no required EX/MEM forwarding, emit an early IF redirect target, and latch the prediction metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare predicted return targets against resolved JALR targets and suppress the normal redirect only on exact matches, preserving misalignment traps and RVFI pc_next.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the ID-stage early return redirect to IF with EX redirect priority and without changing the nret=1 RVFI port contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept a gated decode-stage predicted redirect as a lower-priority PC update than EX redirect while keeping ordinary fall-through fetch unchanged.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 345.09, \"delta_pct\": -13.26, \"vs_baseline\": 22.02, \"fmax_mhz\": 157.8, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4572742, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6160, \"ff\": 2360, \"seeds\": [157.8, 152.79, 160.82], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-12T20:02:38.360237Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 12, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-05-12 hyp-20260512-003-r12s2 (regression, -13.26%): Decode-stage canonical-return prediction still regressed because ID regfile/forwarding/alignment checks feed an IF next-PC redirect path and add ID/EX `pred_ret` suppression state, so avoiding IF-side RAS/tag lookup was not enough to make return prediction cheap for CoreMark.\"}\nbench/gpt-5_5_xhigh/rep2/log.jsonl:13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260512-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Return-address stack predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts on this core regressed because they put too much\\ntarget-generation or tag-lookup logic on the shallow fetch critical path.\\nCoreMark still contains repeated call/return control flow in addition to\\nloops, and returns are the one dynamic-control case where a tiny predictor\\ncan provide a target without a BTB target array or branch-immediate adder.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts on this core regressed because they put too much\\ntarget-generation or tag-lookup logic on the shallow fetch critical path.\\nCoreMark still contains repeated call/return control flow in addition to\\nloops, and returns are the one dynamic-control case where a tiny predictor\\ncan provide a target without a BTB target array or branch-immediate adder.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small return-address stack for JALR return prediction only. Push\\npc+4 when a legal link-register JAL/JALR call resolves, pop when a legal\\nreturn resolves, and in IF predict a fetched `jalr x0, x1/x5, 0` to the\\ncurrent stack top. Carry predicted-next-PC metadata to EX so a correctly\\npredicted return does not flush, while any mismatch redirects and repairs\\nthe stack precisely.\\n\\nThis intentionally does not predict conditional branches and does not add\\na PC-indexed BTB/tag compare. The IF critical-path addition should be only\\na narrow opcode/rd/rs1/imm-zero return recognizer plus a mux to the stack\\ntop, so Fmax risk is lower than the failed static branch, BTB, and hot\\nredirect predictor variants. The cycle gain is expected to be modest but\\npositive if CoreMark's timed call/return traffic is frequent enough.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC and return-predicted metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for precise EX validation.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a tiny RAS and predict only canonical return JALR instructions to the stack top, with EX repair/update inputs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the return prediction metadata through the ID/EX register without changing decode legality rules.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Classify resolved calls and returns, compare actual next PC against the carried prediction, and emit RAS push/pop/repair updates.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire prediction validation and RAS update signals between IF and EX while preserving the nret=1 RVFI port contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC and return-predicted metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX bundles for precise EX validation.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a tiny RAS and predict only canonical return JALR instructions to the stack top, with EX repair/update inputs.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the return prediction metadata through the ID/EX register without changing decode legality rules.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Classify resolved calls and returns, compare actual next PC against the carried prediction, and emit RAS push/pop/repair updates.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire prediction validation and RAS update signals between IF and EX while preserving the nret=1 RVFI port contract.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 202.01, \"delta_pct\": -49.57, \"vs_baseline\": -28.57, \"fmax_mhz\": 90.46, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4478102, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6089, \"ff\": 2382, \"seeds\": [85.19, 90.46, 90.69], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-12T16:01:08.076378Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-05-12 hyp-20260512-003-r4s2 (regression, -49.57%): Return-only RAS prediction still adds imem_data return decode, stack-top next_pc muxing, and predicted-next-PC repair plumbing to the shallow fetch/EX control path, while CoreMark call/return traffic was too sparse to amortize the timing cost.\"}\nbench/kimi-k2_6/rep3/log.jsonl:25:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260514-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"2-entry return-address stack for JALR prediction\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline pays a 2-cycle flush bubble for every taken JALR (function return).\\nCoreMark is call-heavy, so returns are a significant fraction of dynamic control flow.\\nPrior BTB attempts either thrashed (8-entry) or were synthesized away (implementation bugs),\\nand static BTFN predictors destroyed Fmax by adding an imem_data->adder->next_pc critical path.\\nA tiny 2-entry RAS predicts returns with near-perfect accuracy, avoids an IF-stage adder entirely,\\nand targets a different instruction class (returns vs branches) than all previous predictor attempts.\\nCorrectly predicted returns eliminate the flush bubble, directly improving IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline pays a 2-cycle flush bubble for every taken JALR (function return).\\nCoreMark is call-heavy, so returns are a significant fraction of dynamic control flow.\\nPrior BTB attempts either thrashed (8-entry) or were synthesized away (implementation bugs),\\nand static BTFN predictors destroyed Fmax by adding an imem_data->adder->next_pc critical path.\\nA tiny 2-entry RAS predicts returns with near-perfect accuracy, avoids an IF-stage adder entirely,\\nand targets a different instruction class (returns vs branches) than all previous predictor attempts.\\nCorrectly predicted returns eliminate the flush bubble, directly improving IPC.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 2-entry Return Address Stack (RAS) in core.sv and change EX-stage redirect to\\nmispredict-only so that correct return predictions do not flush the pipeline.\\n\\n1. RAS logic in core.sv (top level, no new module):\\n   - Detect push:  jal with rd==ra  (opcode 1101111, instr[11:7]==1).\\n   - Detect pop/predict: jalr with rs1==ra (opcode 1100111, instr[19:15]==1).\\n   - Two 32-bit registers (ras_top, ras_next) plus pointer implied by the swap.\\n   - On push: ras_next <= ras_top; ras_top <= io_imemAddr + 32'd4.\\n   - On pop:  ras_top <= ras_next.\\n   - Updates are gated by !ex_redirect && !flush_if && !stall_if so wrong-path\\n     instructions during flushes do not corrupt the stack.\\n   - ras_predict = is_jalr_ra && !ex_redirect && !flush_if && !stall_if.\\n   - ras_target  = ras_top.\\n\\n2. Redirect muxing in core.sv:\\n   - Keep the raw ex_redirect / ex_redirect_target wires from ex_stage.\\n   - Drive if_stage inputs with combined signals:\\n       if_redirect       = ex_redirect || ras_predict;\\n       if_redirect_target = ras_predict ? ras_target : ex_redirect_target;\\n   - Hazard unit MUST continue to receive the raw ex_redirect (not the combined\\n     signal) so that correct predictions do not spuriously flush IF/ID.\\n   - Create an intermediate if_id_t signal from if_stage output, override\\n     predict_taken = ras_predict and predicted_target = ras_target, then feed\\n     that to id_stage.  This keeps the RAS out of the timing-critical if_stage\\n     next-pc path and avoids pc_fwd violations.\\n\\n3. Pipeline-bundle extension in core_pkg.sv:\\n   - Add `logic predict_taken;` and `logic [31:0] predicted_target;` to both\\n     `if_id_t` and `id_ex_t`.\\n\\n4. ID stage capture in id_stage.sv:\\n   - In the ID/EX register always_ff, capture:\\n       reg_q.predict_taken    <= in.predict_taken;\\n       reg_q.predicted_target <= in.predicted_target;\\n\\n5. EX-stage mispredict logic in ex_stage.sv:\\n   - Keep the existing redirect_target and pc_next assignments UNCHANGED.\\n     (This is the #1 source of prior pc_fwd failures; do not touch them.)\\n   - Add:\\n       logic actual_taken = in.ctrl.is_jump || branch_taken;\\n       logic target_mismatch = in.ctrl.is_jump &&\\n                               ({jalr_sum[31:1], 1'b0} != in.predicted_target);\\n       assign redirect = ((actual_taken != in.predict_taken) ||\\n                          (in.predict_taken && target_mismatch)) && !misalign_fault;\\n   - For baseline behavior (predict_taken=0, predicted_target=0) this collapses\\n     to the original redirect expression.\\n   - On a correctly predicted return, redirect stays low and the pipeline\\n     continues fetching from the return address without a bubble.\\n   - On a RAS miss, EX asserts redirect to the true jump target and the\\n     hazard unit flushes IF/ID as usual.\\n\\nCost estimate: ~65 LUTs (32 for the redirect_target mux, ~31 for the 32-bit\\nequality comparator in EX, plus decode/control).  Previous cores with ~10200\\nLUTs still evaluated successfully, so the modest area increase is acceptable\\nfor the IPC gain.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add `logic predict_taken;` and `logic [31:0] predicted_target;`\\nto both `if_id_t` and `id_ex_t` pipeline bundles.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the 2-entry RAS (ras_top, ras_next), push/pop decode from io_imemData,\\nras_predict / ras_target generation, combined redirect mux\\n(if_redirect = ex_redirect || ras_predict; if_redirect_target muxed),\\nfeed raw ex_redirect to hazard_unit.redirect, and override predict_taken\\n/ predicted_target on the if_id_t path to id_stage.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture in.predict_taken and in.predicted_target into the ID/EX register.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 32-bit equality comparator between the resolved jump target\\n({jalr_sum[31:1],1'b0}) and in.predicted_target.  Change redirect to\\nmispredict-only: ((actual_taken != predict_taken) ||\\n(predict_taken && target_mismatch)) && !misalign_fault.\\nDO NOT modify pc_next, redirect_target, or any other EX logic.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add `logic predict_taken;` and `logic [31:0] predicted_target;`\\nto both `if_id_t` and `id_ex_t` pipeline bundles.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the 2-entry RAS (ras_top, ras_next), push/pop decode from io_imemData,\\nras_predict / ras_target generation, combined redirect mux\\n(if_redirect = ex_redirect || ras_predict; if_redirect_target muxed),\\nfeed raw ex_redirect to hazard_unit.redirect, and override predict_taken\\n/ predicted_target on the if_id_t path to id_stage.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture in.predict_taken and in.predicted_target into the ID/EX register.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 32-bit equality comparator between the resolved jump target\\n({jalr_sum[31:1],1'b0}) and in.predicted_target.  Change redirect to\\nmispredict-only: ((actual_taken != predict_taken) ||\\n(predict_taken && target_mismatch)) && !misalign_fault.\\nDO NOT modify pc_next, redirect_target, or any other EX logic.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 169.08, \"delta_pct\": -49.43, \"vs_baseline\": -40.22, \"fmax_mhz\": 70.4, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4163814, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10352, \"ff\": 1959, \"seeds\": [70.4, 63.32, 73.15], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-14T16:17:13.429883Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 8, \"scribe_skipped\": \"TimeoutError: scribe timed out after 240s\"}\nbench/gemini-3_1-pro/rep1/agent.log:58:{\"type\":\"text\",\"timestamp\":1778768750587,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d9206836ffeyHPq7xKh4Z90ue\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_e26dfe488001bgkB5cKrFbTOGO\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e26dfd354002oVLVJCBYIYgYyr\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d9206836ffeyHPq7xKh4Z90ue\",\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"evaluating with [ thought ]\\nAh, so `hyp-20260514-001-r12s0` scored 354.73 fitness (Fmax 149.7 * IPC). Cycles: 4220145.\\nIn baseline, the best fitness is 354.73 (from `r5s2`, which added passing predicted PC to EX).\\nWait, if `r12s0` scored exactly 354.73, and Fmax=149.7, it means its micro-opt changes were basically a NO-OP or just matched `r5s2`.\\nLet's check the current core RTL in `cores/bench/rtl/`.\\nWait, the `cores/bench/rtl/` already has the predicted PC stuff in EX from `r5s2`. Let me check `ex_stage.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic pred_branch;\\n  logic pred_pc4;\\n  logic pred_jump;\\n\\n  assign pred_branch = (in.pc_next_fetched == branch_target);\\n  assign pred_pc4    = (in.pc_next_fetched == (in.pc + 32'd4));\\n  assign pred_jump   = (in.pc_next_fetched == jump_target);\\n\\n  logic is_mispredicted;\\n  always_comb begin\\n    if (misalign_fault)      is_mispredicted = !pred_pc4;\\n    else if (in.ctrl.is_jump)    is_mispredicted = !pred_jump;\\n    else if (in.ctrl.is_branch)  is_mispredicted = branch_taken ? !pred_branch : !pred_pc4;\\n    else                         is_mispredicted = !pred_pc4;\\n  end\\n```\\nSo `cores/bench/` is *at* the r5s2 state! The baseline fitness of `cores/bench/` is 354.73. Wait, the prompt says:\\n\\\"Current best fitness: 354.73\\nBaseline fitness: 282.82\\\"\\nYes! The core has already incorporated the BTB predicted PC forwarding!\\n\\nNow, I need to find another `micro_opt` that improves IPC or Fmax.\\nLet's see what else could improve IPC:\\n1. \\\"Precise Hazard Decode\\\": Stalling only when `rs1` and `rs2` are actually read.\\nWait, `r12s0` tried this! \\\"Decoding the opcode in the hazard unit to only stall when registers are actually read\\\". But its delta_pct was 0.\\nLet's check `r8s1`. It did the same thing: Precise Hazard Unit & Store Bypass. Cycles went from 4220145 down to 4218963. IPC improved very slightly (4220145 -> 4218963 is a 0.03% difference). But Fmax dropped from 149.7 to 137.17, leading to a fitness regression.\\nSo precise hazard decode barely improves IPC (CoreMark probably doesn't have many false load-use stalls).\\n\\nWait! What about the ALU? Unifying multiplier into a 33x33 multiplier didn't improve Fmax in `r12s0` (it got 149.7, exactly the same).\\n\\nWhat other `micro_opt` can we do?\\nWhat if we optimize the ALU adder or branch resolver?\\nLet's check `ex_stage.sv`.\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic [31:0] branch_target;\\n  logic [31:0] jump_target;\\n  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */\\n  logic [31:0] jalr_sum;  // bit 0 deliberately dropped per RV JALR spec\\n  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */\\n\\n  always_comb begin\\n    // ...\\n    branch_target = in.pc + in.imm;\\n    // JALR clears bit 0 (RV spec); JAL uses imm directly.\\n    jalr_sum    = rs1 + in.imm;\\n    jump_target = in.ctrl.is_jalr ? {jalr_sum[31:1], 1'b0}\\n                                  : (in.pc + in.imm);\\n  end\\n```\\nWe have three adders here!\\n1. `alu_result = alu_a + alu_b`\\n2. `branch_target = in.pc + in.imm`\\n3. `jalr_sum = rs1 + in.imm`\\nActually, `jump_target` uses `in.pc + in.imm` for JAL, which could share `branch_target`.\\nAnd `branch_target` and `jump_target` could reuse the main ALU? But `alu_result` is already computing `in.pc + in.imm` for AUIPC/JAL, and `rs1 + in.imm` for JALR!\\nWait, let's look at `decoder.sv` and `ex_stage.sv`:\\nFor JAL: `is_jump=1, reg_write=1`. `alu_src` is 0 (default). So `alu_a = rs1, alu_b = rs2`. But `alu_result` is computed but ignored for JAL!\\nFor JALR: `is_jump=1, is_jalr=1, alu_src=1`. `alu_a = rs1, alu_b = imm`. `alu_result = rs1 + imm`. This is exactly `jalr_sum`!\\nFor BRANCH: `is_branch=1`. `alu_src=0`. `alu_a = rs1, alu_b = rs2`. `alu_result = rs1 - rs2` (if `alu_op == ALU_SUB`, wait, `alu_op` is default `ALU_ADD` for branch).\\nWait, branches do a comparison, they don't need `alu_result` from the adder!\\nSo we could reuse the ALU adder for `branch_target`? If we do, we need to mux `alu_a = in.pc` and `alu_b = in.imm`. But branches also need to compare `rs1` and `rs2`. The comparison in `ex_stage` is currently done with `<` and `==`:\\n```systemverilog\\n    case (in.ctrl.branch_op)\\n      BR_BEQ:  branch_cond = (rs1 == rs2);\\n...\\n```\\nThis is separate from the ALU. So the ALU adder is idle during branches!\\nIf we reuse the ALU adder for `branch_target = in.pc + in.imm`, we can eliminate the dedicated `branch_target` adder.\\nFor JAL, `alu_a = in.pc, alu_b = in.imm`, so `alu_result = in.pc + in.imm`.\\nFor JALR, `alu_a = rs1, alu_b = in.imm`, so `alu_result = rs1 + in.imm`.\\nLet's see if we can do this.\\n`alu_a = (in.ctrl.is_branch || (in.ctrl.is_jump && !in.ctrl.is_jalr)) ? in.pc : (in.ctrl.is_auipc ? in.pc : rs1);`\\n`alu_b = (in.ctrl.is_branch || in.ctrl.is_jump) ? in.imm : (in.ctrl.alu_src ? in.imm : rs2);`\\nWait, JALR already sets `alu_src=1`, so `alu_b` is `in.imm`.\\nIf we do this, `alu_result` will hold `in.pc + in.imm` for BRANCH and JAL, and `rs1 + in.imm` for JALR.\\nThen:\\n```systemverilog\\n    branch_target = alu_result;\\n    jump_target = in.ctrl.is_jalr ? {alu_result[31:1], 1'b0} : alu_result;\\n```\\nThis removes TWO 32-bit adders!\\nDoes this improve Fmax? `r11s1` tried this! Let's see:\\nIn `r11s1`: \\\"Eliminate the redundant 32-bit adders `branch_target = in.pc + in.imm;` and `jalr_sum = rs1 + in.imm;`. Change the `alu_a` and `alu_b` mux logic to reuse the main ALU for jumps/branches... Update `branch_target` and `jump_target` to consume `alu_result`\\\"\\nWait, `r11s1` was `broken` due to \\\"hypothesis agent modified off-limits paths... 'test_fmax.sv'\\\". It didn't fail verification, it failed generation!\\nLet's check if there is any other `micro_opt` that could improve IPC.\\n\\nWait, what about the forwarding logic?\\nCurrently, if `EX/MEM` is a LOAD, and `ID/EX` needs it, there is a load-use hazard, pipeline stalls.\\nWhat if `MEM/WB` is a LOAD, and `ID/EX` needs it? Forwarding handles it.\\nWait, look at `hazard_unit.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n    load_use_hazard = id_ex_mem_read\\n                   && (id_ex_rd == if_id_rs1 || id_ex_rd == if_id_rs2)\\n                   && (id_ex_rd != 5'b0);\\n```\\nWait! What if the instruction in ID is a STORE, and its `rs2` is the data being stored.\\n```assembly\\nlw x5, 0(x6)\\nsw x5, 4(x7)\\n```\\nIn this case, `id_ex_rd` is `x5`. `if_id_rs2` is `x5`.\\nThe `hazard_unit` will stall because `id_ex_mem_read` is true and `if_id_rs2 == x5`.\\nBut a STORE does not need `rs2` in the EX stage! It only needs `rs2` in the MEM stage.\\nIf we bypass `rs2` from the `MEM/WB` stage (which holds the loaded data) directly to the `MEM` stage for the STORE, we don't need to stall the STORE in ID!\\nWait, `r8s1` tried this (\\\"Store Bypass\\\"). It achieved 4218963 cycles (a tiny IPC gain) but Fmax tanked, so it regressed fitness.\\nWhy did Fmax tank? Because `store_data = (wb_w_en && wb_w_addr == in.rs2_addr) ? wb_w_data : in.write_data;` adds a 32-bit mux in the MEM stage (which also does memory byte masking, shifting, etc.) and it extended the critical path.\\n\\nLet's rethink IPC.\\nCoreMark branches: many loops.\\nCan we optimize the branch penalty?\\n\\\"CoreMark contains many tight loops. The current 1-bit BTB clears its valid bit when a loop exits (not taken).\\\"\\nWait, this is a predictor optimization, but the category must be `micro_opt`! The prompt says \\\"Focus this hypothesis on the category: micro_opt.\\\"\\nSo I must propose a `micro_opt`.\\n\\nAre there any other structural / micro_opt things we can do?\\nLook at `alu.sv` `ALU_SLT` and `ALU_SLTU`:\\n```systemverilog\\n      ALU_SLT:    out = {31'b0, $signed(a) < $signed(b)};\\n      ALU_SLTU:   out = {31'b0, a < b};\\n```\\nLook at `ex_stage.sv` branch conditions:\\n```systemverilog\\n    case (in.ctrl.branch_op)\\n      BR_BEQ:  branch_cond = (rs1 == rs2);\\n      BR_BNE:  branch_cond = (rs1 != rs2);\\n      BR_BLT:  branch_cond = ($signed(rs1) <  $signed(rs2));\\n      BR_BGE:  branch_cond = ($signed(rs1) >= $signed(rs2));\\n      BR_BLTU: branch_cond = (rs1 <  rs2);\\n      BR_BGEU: branch_cond = (rs1 >= rs2);\\n```\\nNotice that `ex_stage` duplicates the `<` and `< unsigned` comparators!\\nThe ALU already computes `a < b` and `$signed(a) < $signed(b)`.\\nBut wait, for branches, `alu_a` is `rs1` and `alu_b` is `rs2`.\\nCould we reuse the ALU's comparators?\\nIf we do, `branch_cond` would depend on `alu_result`, which is a 32-bit value. But for branches, `alu_op` is default `ALU_ADD`. We would need the decoder to set `alu_op = ALU_SUB` or something?\\nWait, if we compute `branch_cond` in parallel, it's just a few LUTs. Is it really hurting Fmax? Maybe.\\n\\nLet's review the critical path for Fmax.\\nFmax is currently 149.7 MHz.\\nWhat is the critical path?\\n`ex_stage` has:\\n`jalr_sum = rs1 + in.imm;` -> 32-bit adder.\\n`jump_target = is_jalr ? {jalr_sum[31:1], 1'b0} : (in.pc + in.imm)` -> 32-bit adder (`in.pc + in.imm`) + Mux.\\n`branch_target = in.pc + in.imm` -> Wait, `in.pc + in.imm` is duplicated!\\nAh, look at `ex_stage.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n    branch_target = in.pc + in.imm;\\n    // JALR clears bit 0 (RV spec); JAL uses imm directly.\\n    jalr_sum    = rs1 + in.imm;\\n    jump_target = in.ctrl.is_jalr ? {jalr_sum[31:1], 1'b0}\\n                                  : (in.pc + in.imm);\\n```\\n`in.pc + in.imm` is computed TWICE! (Synthesis might merge it, though, Yosys is pretty good at that).\\nBut `jalr_sum` uses `rs1`, which comes from the forwarding mux:\\n`rs1 = (fwd_rs1_sel == 1) ? fwd_ex_mem : (fwd_rs1_sel == 2) ? fwd_mem_wb : in.rs1_val;`\\nSo the path is:\\n`in.rs1_val` (from ID/EX) -> fwd mux -> `rs1` -> `rs1 + in.imm` -> `jalr_sum` -> `jump_target` -> `correct_next_pc` -> `is_mispredicted` -> `redirect_target` -> `redirect`.\\nWait! `redirect` and `redirect_target` go back to `if_stage` to update `pc`:\\n```systemverilog\\nalways_ff @(posedge clock) begin\\n    if (redirect) pc <= redirect_target;\\n```\\nThis is a long combinational path:\\nID/EX flop -> fwd mux -> adder (`jalr_sum` / `alu_result`) -> `correct_next_pc` -> `is_mispredicted` -> `redirect` -> IF PC flop.\\nCan we optimize this?\\nWhat if `is_mispredicted` is calculated faster?\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic pred_branch;\\n  logic pred_pc4;\\n  logic pred_jump;\\n\\n  assign pred_branch = (in.pc_next_fetched == branch_target);\\n  assign pred_pc4    = (in.pc_next_fetched == (in.pc + 32'd4));\\n  assign pred_jump   = (in.pc_next_fetched == jump_target);\\n```\\n`pred_jump` checks `in.pc_next_fetched == jump_target`.\\n`jump_target` for JALR is `{jalr_sum[31:1], 1'b0}`.\\n`jalr_sum = rs1 + in.imm`.\\nSo the comparator `pred_jump` depends on `jalr_sum`, which depends on `rs1`, which depends on forwarding mux.\\nCan we compute `pred_jump` without the full 32-bit addition? No, because we need the exact target.\\nIs there any other path?\\nLook at `alu_result`. `alu_result` for JAL/JALR is not used for the branch target.\\nWait, `alu_a = in.ctrl.is_auipc ? in.pc : rs1;`\\n`alu_b = in.ctrl.alu_src ? in.imm : rs2;`\\nFor JALR, `alu_a = rs1, alu_b = in.imm`.\\nSo `alu_result` (with `ALU_ADD`) is `rs1 + in.imm`.\\nBut `alu.sv` has a big mux for `alu_op`.\\nIf we just use `alu_result` for `jalr_sum`, we add the ALU delay. Currently, `jalr_sum` is a separate adder, which is faster than the ALU (which has a big mux). So keeping `jalr_sum` separate is actually good for Fmax!\\n\\nWait, `mul_ss` and friends in `alu.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n    mul_ss = $signed({{32{a[31]}}, a}) * $signed({{32{b[31]}}, b});\\n    mul_uu = {32'b0, a} * {32'b0, b};\\n    mul_su = $signed({{32{a[31]}}, a}) * $signed({32'b0, b});\\n```\\nThis infers three 64-bit multipliers! This is a classic FPGA synthesis Fmax killer.\\nLet's see what `r12s0` did. It unified them into a single 33x33 multiplier:\\n```systemverilog\\nlogic a_is_signed, b_is_signed; logic signed [32:0] mul_a, mul_b; logic signed [65:0] mul_res;\\nAssign a_is_signed = (op == ALU_MULH || op == ALU_MULHSU);\\nAssign b_is_signed = (op == ALU_MULH);\\nAssign mul_a = $signed({a_is_signed & a[31], a});\\nAssign mul_b = $signed({b_is_signed & b[31], b});\\nAssign mul_res = mul_a * mul_b;\\n```\\nThis is a standard way to implement all 4 RISC-V multiplications with one DSP block setup.\\nBut `r12s0` had `delta_pct: 0` because the baseline was already at 354.73, and Fmax remained 149.7.\\nWait, if it was 149.7, maybe the multiplier wasn't the critical path?\\nWhat IS the critical path?\\nIn Tang Nano 20K, Fmax is bounded by LUTs and routing.\\nIf unifying the multiplier doesn't improve Fmax, maybe the divider does?\\n```systemverilog\\n      ALU_DIV: begin\\n        if (b == 32'b0)\\n          out = 32'hFFFFFFFF;\\n...\\n```\\nThere are 4 dividers! `/` and `%` for signed and unsigned.\\nYosys implements `/` and `%` using a generic division state machine or unrolled divider? For Tang Nano 20K (Gowin), `/` operator without `ALTOPS` will be synthesized into a huge combinational divider! Wait! No, `ALTOPS` is defined during `make formal`, but for the FPGA build, `ALTOPS` is UNDEFINED.\\nWait, `fpga/core_bench.sv` is synthesized for FPGA. Does it define `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`? No, the comment says: \\\"The Verilator/cocotb/cosim builds leave ALTOPS undefined and run the real arithmetic.\\\"\\nAh! Combinational 32-bit division is insanely slow! It unrolls to a 32-stage ripple borrow subtractor.\\nWait, if it unrolls to a 32-stage subtractor, the Fmax would be like 10 MHz!\\nWhy is the Fmax 149.7 MHz??\\nBecause `test/cosim/main.cpp` and `fpga/synth.tcl` might not be exercising the divider if CoreMark doesn't use it?\\nWait, `yosys` synthesizes it. If it synthesizes a combinational 32-bit divider, Fmax would be terrible. But wait, `fpga/synth.tcl` uses `yosys` and Gowin primitives. Maybe Yosys maps it to a DSP-based divider or just unrolled. But Fmax is 149.7!\\nHow is 149.7 MHz possible with a combinational 32-bit divider?\\nMaybe CoreMark doesn't use `DIV` and Yosys optimizes it out? No, the ALU is fully connected.\\nWait, is the Fmax reported 149.7 MHz the actual Fmax? Yes, 149.7 MHz.\\n\\nWhat else could be optimized?\\nLet's check the pipeline hazard stall logic.\\n```systemverilog\\n// hazard_unit.sv\\n    load_use_hazard = id_ex_mem_read\\n                   && (id_ex_rd == if_id_rs1 || id_ex_rd == if_id_rs2)\\n                   && (id_ex_rd != 5'b0);\\n```\\nWait, if `id_ex_rd` is the destination of a LOAD, and the NEXT instruction has `if_id_rs1` or `if_id_rs2` matching it, it stalls.\\nWhat if `if_id_rs1` or `rs2` is NOT used by the next instruction?\\nFor example, `LUI x5, 0x1234` does not read `rs1` or `rs2`. But its instruction encoding has `19:15` as 0 and `24:20` as 0. Since `id_ex_rd != 0`, it won't match `x0`.\\nWhat about `JAL x5, 0x1234`? `19:15` and `24:20` could be anything! But wait, `JAL` has `rd` in `11:7`, and the rest is immediate. So `rs1` and `rs2` could randomly match `id_ex_rd`!\\nYes, JAL or AUIPC or U-type or J-type or even OP-IMM (which doesn't use `rs2`) could have garbage in `rs2` field that matches the LOAD's `rd`, causing a SPURIOUS STALL!\\nThis was addressed in `r8s1` and `r12s0`!\\nWhy did `r12s0` have `delta_pct: 0`?\\nLet's look at `r12s0`'s exact changes:\\n```systemverilog\\nReplace if_id_rs1 and if_id_rs2 inputs with input logic [31:0] if_id_instr.\\nExtract logic [4:0] if_id_rs1 = if_id_instr[19:15]; and logic [4:0] if_id_rs2 = if_id_instr[24:20]; internally.\\nDecode if_id_instr[6:0] to rs1_ren (true for 7'b0110011, 7'b0010011, 7'b0000011, 7'b0100011, 7'b1100011, 7'b1100111) and rs2_ren (true for 7'b0110011, 7'b0100011, 7'b1100011).\\nUpdate load_use_hazard to require rs1_ren when matching if_id_rs1, and rs2_ren when matching if_id_rs2.\\n```\\nThis is correct. But the impact was 0%. Wait, why?\\nBecause CoreMark might not have many spurious load-use stalls!\\nThe coremark binary is compiled with `-O3`. The register allocator is pretty good at not generating random bits in unused fields, or perhaps the assembler clears them to 0!\\nAh! In RISC-V, unused register fields in U-type and J-type instructions are actually part of the immediate!\\nWait, U-type (LUI, AUIPC) has immediate in bits 31:12. So bits 19:15 and 24:20 ARE part of the immediate! They are not zeroed by the assembler.\\nBUT, maybe they don't frequently match the load's `rd`? There's a 1/31 chance of a match. CoreMark does have LUI/AUIPC, but maybe not immediately following a LOAD.\\nWhat about I-type (ADDI)? It doesn't use `rs2`. Its `rs2` field is bits 24:20, which is the lower 5 bits of the immediate! So `ADDI` could have a random `rs2` match.\\nBut wait! If the IPC gain is so small (4220145 -> 4218963 in `r8s1`, which is 1182 cycles out of 4 million, i.e., 0.02%), it means spurious load-use stalls are VERY RARE.\\nSo precise hazard decode is NOT a good optimization for this benchmark.\\n\\nWhat else?\\nLet's consider memory operations.\\n`MEM_WB` forwarding.\\n`forward_unit` handles `EX/MEM` and `MEM/WB` forwarding to `EX`.\\nWhat about `MEM/WB` forwarding to `MEM`?\\nWait! Does a STORE instruction need forwarding?\\n```assembly\\nadd x5, x1, x2\\nsw x5, 0(x3)\\n```\\nIn ID stage: `ADD` is decoded.\\nNext cycle: `ADD` is in EX, `SW` is in ID.\\nNext cycle: `ADD` is in MEM, `SW` is in EX.\\nIn EX, `SW` calculates address: `rs1 + imm`. `rs1` is `x3`.\\n`rs2` is `x5`, which is the data to store. `SW` forwards `x5` from `EX/MEM` (`ADD`'s output).\\nSo `SW` gets the correct data into `ex_mem_t.write_data`.\\nNext cycle: `ADD` is in WB, `SW` is in MEM.\\nIn MEM, `SW` writes `in.write_data` to memory.\\nThis works perfectly!\\n\\nWhat if it's a LOAD followed by a STORE?\\n```assembly\\nlw x5, 0(x1)\\nsw x5, 0(x2)\\n```\\nCycle 1: LW in ID\\nCycle 2: LW in EX, SW in ID\\nCycle 3: LW in MEM, SW in EX.\\nWait! `LW` produces its data at the END of the MEM stage.\\nIn Cycle 3, `SW` is in EX, it needs `x5` for `rs2`. But `LW` hasn't produced it yet!\\nSo `hazard_unit` detects load-use hazard in Cycle 2, and stalls `SW` in ID.\\nCycle 2: LW in EX, SW in ID (stalled).\\nCycle 3: LW in MEM, SW in ID (unstalled).\\nCycle 4: LW in WB, SW in EX.\\nIn EX, `SW` forwards `x5` from `MEM/WB`.\\nCycle 5: SW in MEM.\\nThis takes 5 cycles.\\nCould we avoid the stall?\\nA STORE does not need its data (`rs2`) in the EX stage! It only needs it in the MEM stage!\\nIf we didn't stall, in Cycle 3: LW in MEM, SW in EX. `SW` computes its address. Its `write_data` gets garbage.\\nCycle 4: LW in WB, SW in MEM.\\nIn MEM, `SW` can forward the LOAD data directly from the WB stage (or `LW`'s MEM/WB register)!\\nThis is exactly the \\\"Store Bypass\\\" that `r8s1` tried!\\n`r8s1` changes:\\n```systemverilog\\nstore_data = (wb_w_en && wb_w_addr != 5'b0 && wb_w_addr == in.rs2_addr) ? wb_w_data : in.write_data;\\n```\\nAnd modified `hazard_unit` to NOT stall if the instruction is a STORE and the matched register is `rs2`.\\nWhy did `r8s1` regress?\\nBecause Fmax dropped from 149.7 to 137.17 MHz.\\nThe `store_data` mux was in `mem_stage.sv`.\\n```systemverilog\\n    case (in.ctrl.mem_width)\\n      2'd0:    wdata_rep = {4{store_data[7:0]}};\\n      2'd1:    wdata_rep = {2{store_data[15:0]}};\\n      default: wdata_rep = store_data;\\n    endcase\\n```\\n`wb_w_data` comes from `wb_stage`, which is:\\n```systemverilog\\nw_data = in.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? in.read_data : in.alu_result;\\n```\\nThis is heavily combinational. `load_data` -> `w_data` -> `store_data` -> `wdata_rep` -> `dmem_wdata`.\\nThis path is: SRAM read (`dmem_rdata`) -> Shift & Extend (`load_data`) -> WB Mux (`w_data`) -> Store Mux (`store_data`) -> Byte Replication (`wdata_rep`) -> `dmem_wdata`.\\nThis path goes entirely through the combinational logic from `dmem_rdata` to `dmem_wdata` within a single cycle!\\nWait! The memory is synchronous. The load data comes out of the BRAM `dmem_rdata` early in the cycle, then it goes through the MEM stage logic to `load_data`, then to `MEM/WB` flop!\\nWait, no! `dmem_rdata` in Cycle 4 is the result of the LOAD that was in MEM in Cycle 3.\\nIn Cycle 4, the LOAD is in the WB stage. The data it loaded is already sitting in the `MEM/WB` pipeline register as `in.read_data` (wait, no, `mem_wb_t.read_data` is latched!)\\nLet's check `mem_stage.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n      reg_q.read_data  <= load_data;\\n```\\nSo in Cycle 4, the LOAD instruction is in the `MEM/WB` register. `mem_wb_w.read_data` is just a register output!\\nIt does NOT depend on `dmem_rdata` anymore!\\nSo the path for `wb_w_data` is: `mem_wb_w.read_data` (flop) -> WB Mux -> `wb_w_data` -> Store Mux -> `wdata_rep` -> `dmem_wdata`.\\nThis path should be extremely short! Flop -> Mux -> Mux -> replication -> SRAM setup.\\nWhy did it drop Fmax to 137 MHz?\\nMaybe the comparator `wb_w_addr == in.rs2_addr`? Both are pipeline registers (`mem_wb_w.rd` and `ex_mem_w.rs2_addr`).\\nMaybe the forwarding in `forward_unit` is a better place to do this?\\nNo, the data is needed in `mem_stage`, so it must be forwarded there.\\n\\nWhat if we optimize the branch predictor instead? The prompt says category: `micro_opt`, not `predictor`. We should stick to `micro_opt`.\\n\\nLet's look at `forward_unit.sv`.\\n```systemverilog\\n  always_comb begin\\n    if      (ex_mem_w_en && ex_mem_rd != 5'b0 && ex_mem_rd == id_ex_rs1) fwd_rs1 = 2'd1;\\n    else if (mem_wb_w_en && mem_wb_rd != 5'b0 && mem_wb_rd == id_ex_rs1) fwd_rs1 = 2'd2;\\n    else                                                                  fwd_rs1 = 2'd0;\\n  end\\n```\\nThis is standard.\\n\\nWhat about `alu.sv`?\\nIs there a way to optimize the ALU and branch comparators?\\n`alu_result` is computed as:\\n```systemverilog\\n      ALU_SLT:    out = {31'b0, $signed(a) < $signed(b)};\\n      ALU_SLTU:   out = {31'b0, a < b};\\n```\\nIn `ex_stage.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n      BR_BLT:  branch_cond = ($signed(rs1) <  $signed(rs2));\\n      BR_BGE:  branch_cond = ($signed(rs1) >= $signed(rs2));\\n      BR_BLTU: branch_cond = (rs1 <  rs2);\\n      BR_BGEU: branch_cond = (rs1 >= rs2);\\n```\\nCan we merge these?\\nYes! If we add `ALU_BGE` and `ALU_BGEU` or just reuse the comparisons.\\nWait, `branch_cond` is just evaluating the branch. It doesn't use `alu_result`.\\nIf we merge them, we can tell the ALU to output the comparison result, and use `alu_result[0]` as the branch condition.\\nBut wait, the ALU also needs to compute the branch target? No, `branch_target` is `in.pc + in.imm`, which is a separate adder!\\nIf we use the ALU for the comparison, then the ALU cannot be used for `in.pc + in.imm`.\\nSince `branch_target` is a separate adder anyway, the ALU is currently evaluating `rs1 + rs2` (because `alu_op` defaults to `ALU_ADD` for branch).\\nWait, if we set `alu_op` in `decoder.sv` to `ALU_SLT` for `BLT`, then `alu_result[0]` would be the condition!\\nLet's see:\\n```systemverilog\\n    case (in.ctrl.branch_op)\\n      BR_BEQ:  branch_cond = (rs1 == rs2);\\n      BR_BNE:  branch_cond = (rs1 != rs2);\\n      BR_BLT:  branch_cond = alu_result[0]; // if we set alu_op = ALU_SLT\\n...\\n```\\nThis saves two 32-bit comparators (`<` and `< unsigned`) in `ex_stage`, reusing the ones in the ALU.\\nBut wait, how many LUTs does a 32-bit comparator take? About 10-16 LUTs. Saving 30 LUTs is insignificant. It won't noticeably improve Fmax or fitness.\\n\\nLet's look for a better `micro_opt`.\\nWhat if we optimize the `imm_gen`? No, it's trivial.\\n\\nLook at `core.sv` - the stalling logic.\\n```systemverilog\\n  // dmem bus backpressure. Drive 1 for zero-wait. Drive 0 to model\\n  // dStall — when there is a memory op in EX/MEM, the entire pipeline\\n  // freezes back to MEM; MEM/WB captures a bubble; the LOAD/STORE waits\\n  // until the bus delivers.\\n```\\nIn `hazard_unit.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n    dmem_stall = !dmem_ready && ex_mem_mem_op;\\n    stall_if      = load_use_hazard || imem_stall || dmem_stall;\\n```\\nWait. If `dmem_stall` is active, the PC register is stalled, IF/ID is stalled, ID/EX is stalled, EX/MEM is stalled.\\nIs it possible to decouple this?\\n\\\"dmem stall only matters if there's actually a memory op in EX/MEM... MEM/WB clears valid only; data fields stay (for fwd)\\\"\\nWait, if `dmem_ready` is low, the pipeline freezes. Could the pipeline continue fetching?\\nYes! `r9s1` tried to add a Skid Buffer.\\n\\\"Adding a 2-entry fall-through instruction queue (skid buffer) in IF allows fetching ahead during downstream pipeline stalls, hiding transient imem latency.\\\"\\nWait, `r9s1` was a `structural` hypothesis, but it was `regression`. It got 0% delta. Why did it get 0%?\\nWait! `r9s1` cycle count was 4220145. Delta = 0.\\nThis means the skid buffer did NOT reduce cycle count at all!\\nWhy? Because `io_imemReady` and `io_dmemReady` stalls in `test/cosim/main.cpp` are injected *together* maybe? Or maybe the 22% stalls don't cause many bubbles if the pipeline is already stalled?\\nWait, if `cycles` didn't change, the skid buffer was probably incorrectly implemented, or the testbench doesn't use it the way we thought.\\nWait, `r9s1` changes for Skid buffer were not fully detailed in the log, we can't see the exact implementation, maybe it was bypassed.\\n\\nLet's find a micro-opt that WILL improve IPC.\\nCoreMark branches: what if we implement early branch resolution?\\nCurrently, branches are resolved in EX.\\nCan we resolve them in ID?\\nIf we resolve branches in ID, the mispredict penalty is 1 cycle instead of 2.\\nTo resolve branches in ID, we need:\\n1. `rs1` and `rs2` available in ID.\\n2. A comparator in ID.\\n3. Target calculation in ID (`in.pc + imm`).\\nAre `rs1` and `rs2` available in ID?\\nThey are read from the regfile in ID.\\nBUT what if they are produced by the immediately preceding instruction?\\nThen they are in the EX/MEM register (from EX stage's ALU).\\nWe would need forwarding to ID!\\nForwarding to ID requires:\\n`fwd_id_rs1 = (ex_mem_w_en && ex_mem_rd == rs1) ? alu_result : (mem_wb_w_en ... )`\\nWait, the immediately preceding instruction is in EX. Its result is NOT ready yet! (It's being computed in the ALU).\\nSo if we resolve in ID, and the branch depends on the instruction in EX, we must STALL the branch in ID for 1 cycle (a load-use-like hazard, but for ALU-use!).\\nIn RV32, branches frequently depend on the immediately preceding instruction.\\n```assembly\\nsub x5, x1, x2\\nbeq x5, x0, target\\n```\\nIf we resolve in ID, `sub` is in EX, `beq` is in ID. `beq` needs `x5`, which is coming out of the ALU in EX. We can't forward it to ID early enough (unless we do an asynchronous ALU -> ID forwarding, which kills Fmax).\\nSo we'd have to stall `beq` for 1 cycle. This loses the 1-cycle advantage!\\nTherefore, early branch resolution is typically a trade-off. In a 5-stage pipeline, EX is the best place to resolve branches.\\n\\nWhat else?\\nWait! Look at the `btb_update` logic.\\n```systemverilog\\n  assign btb_update        = in.valid && (in.ctrl.is_branch || in.ctrl.is_jump) && !stall;\\n```\\nIf the BTB update happens in EX, the penalty for a BTB miss is 2 cycles (redirect flushes IF and ID).\\nWait, `is_jump` (JAL) has a fixed target and NO condition!\\n`JAL` does not depend on any registers!\\nWe could resolve `JAL` in ID!\\nIf we resolve `JAL` in ID, we save 1 cycle on every `JAL` mispredict!\\nDoes `JAL` mispredict?\\nWait, `JAL` targets are unconditional. The BTB will cache them after the first execution. But the first time, it misses and costs 2 cycles.\\nIf we resolve `JAL` in ID, the first time it costs 1 cycle.\\nBut is `JAL` frequent enough in CoreMark to matter?\\nMostly function calls. Maybe a few thousands. Not as impactful as loops.\\n\\nWait, what about the BTB predictor?\\nThe prompt says: \\\"Focus this hypothesis on the category: micro_opt.\\\"\\nCan I do a BTB optimization and call it `micro_opt`? No, the category is checked and the tournament slot wants a diverse micro_opt.\\n\\nLet's look at `hazard_unit.sv`.\\n```systemverilog\\n    // PC reg holds on any stall reason.\\n    stall_if      = load_use_hazard || imem_stall || dmem_stall;\\n```\\nWait. If `dmem_stall` is active, the whole pipeline stalls.\\nBut what if `imem_stall` is active?\\n```systemverilog\\n    imem_stall = !imem_ready;\\n    stall_if      = load_use_hazard || imem_stall || dmem_stall;\\n    flush_if      = redirect || imem_stall;\\n```\\nIf `imem_stall` is active, `stall_if` is 1, so `pc` holds. `flush_if` is 1, so the `if_id_w` output becomes a NOP.\\nThis means an imem stall injects a NOP into the ID stage!\\nWait. If the imem is stalled, it means the instruction memory couldn't provide the instruction this cycle. So we MUST inject a NOP, because there is no instruction.\\nBut wait! What if the ID stage is ALSO stalled?\\nIf `dmem_stall` is active, `stall_id` is 1. The ID/EX register holds its value.\\nIn this case, it doesn't matter what IF emits, because ID/EX is holding.\\nBut what if ONLY `imem_stall` is active? Then ID/EX gets a NOP. The pipeline bubble propagates.\\nIs there any inefficiency here?\\nIf `imem_ready` drops randomly, it takes 1 cycle to fetch the instruction once it returns. The pipeline naturally bubbles.\\n\\nLet's look at `reg_file.sv`.\\n```systemverilog\\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\\n    if (reset) begin\\n      for (int i = 0; i < 32; i++) regs[i] <= 32'b0;\\n```\\nResetting all 32 registers in a loop forces them into flops instead of LUTRAM/BRAM.\\nActually, Yosys can map initialized RAM to BRAM, but resetting every cycle prevents it.\\nDoes the RegFile reset matter for Fmax?\\nWait, if it's 32x32 flops, it's 1024 flops.\\nCurrent FF count is 2036. So the regfile is half the flops in the design!\\nIf we remove the reset from the reg_file, what happens?\\nThe RISC-V specification does NOT require registers to be initialized to 0 on reset!\\nIf we remove the reset, Yosys can map `reg_file` to Distributed RAM (LUTRAM) or Block RAM.\\nWait, `reg_file` has 2 read ports and 1 write port. In Gowin, Distributed RAM is 16x4 SDP (Simple Dual Port). So we can do 2 read ports by duplicating the RAM!\\nThis would save 1024 FFs and a ton of LUT muxes!\\nCould this significantly improve Fmax and Area?\\nLet's check LUT4: 10242. FF: 2036. Fmax: 149.7.\\nIf we remove the reset loop in `reg_file.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n// rtl/reg_file.sv\\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\\n    if (w_en && w_addr != 5'b0) begin\\n      regs[w_addr] <= w_data;\\n    end\\n  end\\n```\\nWait! In `CLAUDE.md`, does `riscv-formal` require registers to be 0 at reset?\\nriscv-formal typically assumes uninitialized registers are symbolic.\\nWait, `CLAUDE.md` invariant says \\\"EBREAK is the only SYSTEM instruction...\\\". It doesn't say anything about regfile reset.\\nBUT, `bench/programs/selftest.S` or `crt0.S` might rely on registers being 0?\\nUsually, `crt0.S` manually zeroes the registers if needed.\\nLet's check `test/cosim/reference.py` or formal setup.\\nIf `make formal` checks it, does it fail?\\nriscv-formal `insn_*.v` might assume something? No, formal handles uninitialized state fine.\\nWait, if I remove the reset from the regfile, does that classify as a `micro_opt`?\\nYes, removing reset to infer LUTRAM is a classic micro-architecture optimization.\\nBut wait! `test/cosim/main.cpp` uses the VexRiscv Python model. The Python model initializes its registers to 0. If the DUT has X (or random) in registers, and an instruction writes them to memory, the cosim will mismatch!\\nLet's check `crt0.S`. I can use the read tool to check `bench/programs/crt0.S`.\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778768667784,\"end\":1778768750585}}}\nbench/gemini-3_1-pro/rep1/agent.log:415:{\"type\":\"text\",\"timestamp\":1778770962606,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d900ab3ffferM2r3uJpjSZnxO\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_e26ffbdcc001pP1SriYDxpDYCu\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e26ff93c9001aKczLJRXapAMME\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d900ab3ffferM2r3uJpjSZnxO\",\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The current state of `cores/bench/rtl/` already HAS the BTB from `hyp-r5s2`.\\nWait, I see the BTB is present in `if_stage.sv` and `ex_stage.sv` in the prompt's source.\\nNow, the previous failures tried:\\n- Pipelined 2-bit Bimodal Predictor (broken due to modified off-limits paths). Wait, that was categorized as `structural` but the title says Predictor. The prompt says \\\"Focus this hypothesis on the category: structural.\\\"\\n- ALU Consolidation (hyp-r13s1): \\\"Replace three 64x64 multipliers and four dividers with a single signed 33x33 multiplier and a single signed 33-bit divider to save LUTs and DSPs, improving Fmax.\\\" It failed formal: `Failed: reg_ch0`. Oh! \\\"Value for anyconst rvfi_testbench.register_index ... 22\\\". This is a register checking assertion failure. Ah, the bypass logic they added for `store_data` must have broken it, or maybe the ALU consolidation broke it because `M` extension results were wrong or something. Wait, \\\"failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_44_62\\\". This is usually when a register gets the wrong value.\\nWait, let's look at `test/test_alu.py` failure, did it fail? The error says `formal_failed: reg_ch0`. The bypass logic `store_data` was added to `mem_stage` but they didn't bypass `rs1`, or maybe they bypassed the WRONG data for store.\\nWait, the ALU consolidation also removed ALTOPS `ifdefs` maybe?\\nLet's see what else we could do structurally.\\n\\nWe can try precise hazard decode AND store bypass, but correctly.\\nWait, look at `hyp-20260514-002-r5s1`: `Precise Hazard & Store Data Bypass`. It passed cosim and formal! But it was a `regression` because Fmax dropped or IPC dropped. `delta_pct: 8.04` vs `vs_baseline: 8.04`, wait, current best fitness is 354.73 (+25.43% from baseline). If we only get +8.04%, it's a regression relative to the CURRENT BEST. The BTB gave +25.43%.\\nOh, wait! The current best is the BTB. If we apply Precise Hazard Decode to the current code, we might get an ADDITIONAL +8% over the current best, getting us to +35%.\\nWait, in round 9, `hyp-20260514-002-r9s1` tried `Skid Buffer & Precise Hazard Decode` without BTB? No, it was on top of baseline, wait. The rounds are sequential but they branch. Since `r5s2` was an improvement, the orchestrator applied it to `bench` branch. So now `bench` has the BTB.\\nLet's verify what `cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv` has. The prompt shows `cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv` HAS the BTB!\\nSo we just need to add the precise hazard decode + store bypass on top of the BTB, OR we can add the Skid Buffer on top of the BTB.\\n\\nWait, `hyp-20260514-002-r9s1` added a skid buffer and precise hazard decode, and passed formal, getting `354.73` fitness! Wait, `354.73` is EXACTLY the current best fitness! Oh, look at the log: `hyp-20260514-002-r9s1` says `fitness: 354.73`, `delta_pct: 0`. It was a regression relative to `r5s2` (which also had 354.73). Wait, no, `r5s2` had 354.73. And `r9s1` had 354.73. Did `r9s1` just re-run the exact same code? No, `r9s1` had different changes but ended up with the exact same IPC and Fmax? No, `r9s1` had `ff: 2036`, `lut4: 10242`, `fmax_mhz: 149.7`. `r5s2` had `ff: 2036`, `lut4: 10242`, `fmax_mhz: 149.7`. It looks like `r9s1`'s changes didn't actually synthesize or somehow reverted to the same state? No, `r9s1` says `delta_pct: 0` vs `r5s2`. Let me read `r9s1`'s `error`: null.\\n\\nWhat if we consolidate the ALU? In `hyp-20260514-002-r13s1`, ALU consolidation was attempted with Store Bypass, but failed formal. What if we JUST do ALU consolidation?\\nThe current ALU uses three 64x64 multipliers:\\n`mul_ss = $signed({{32{a[31]}}, a}) * $signed({{32{b[31]}}, b});`\\n`mul_uu = {32'b0, a} * {32'b0, b};`\\n`mul_su = $signed({{32{a[31]}}, a}) * $signed({32'b0, b});`\\nThis is huge and wastes DSPs/LUTs. Fmax is 149.7 MHz. Can we improve Fmax by making it a single 33x33 multiplier?\\n`logic signed [32:0] mul_a = { (op == ALU_MULH || op == ALU_MULHSU) ? a[31] : 1'b0, a };`\\n`logic signed [32:0] mul_b = { (op == ALU_MULH) ? b[31] : 1'b0, b };`\\n`logic signed [65:0] mul_res = mul_a * mul_b;`\\n\\nLet's do ALU consolidation properly.\\nWe should wrap it in `ifndef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` properly, so ALTOPS works.\\nIn `alu.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic        [4:0]  shamt;\\n  shamt = b[4:0];\\n\\n`ifndef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\\n  logic a_is_signed, b_is_signed;\\n  logic signed [32:0] mul_a, mul_b;\\n  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */\\n  logic signed [65:0] mul_res;\\n  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */\\n\\n  always_comb begin\\n    a_is_signed = (op == ALU_MULH) || (op == ALU_MULHSU);\\n    b_is_signed = (op == ALU_MULH);\\n    mul_a = $signed({a_is_signed & a[31], a});\\n    mul_b = $signed({b_is_signed & b[31], b});\\n    mul_res = mul_a * mul_b;\\n  end\\n\\n  logic div_is_signed;\\n  logic signed [32:0] div_a, div_b;\\n  logic signed [32:0] div_q, div_r;\\n  always_comb begin\\n    div_is_signed = (op == ALU_DIV) || (op == ALU_REM);\\n    div_a = $signed({div_is_signed & a[31], a});\\n    div_b = $signed({div_is_signed & b[31], b});\\n    if (b == 32'b0) begin\\n      div_q = -33'sd1;\\n      div_r = div_a;\\n    end else if (div_is_signed && a == 32'h80000000 && b == 32'hFFFFFFFF) begin\\n      div_q = div_a;\\n      div_r = 33'sb0;\\n    end else begin\\n      div_q = div_a / div_b;\\n      div_r = div_a % div_b;\\n    end\\n  end\\n`endif\\n```\\n\\nWait, Yosys and formal can get mad if we put divider in always_comb outside ALTOPS if we don't guard the divider by `ifndef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`.\\nYes, we should place the divider and multiplier logic inside `ifndef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`.\\nWait, the ALU consolidation alone should increase Fmax. But does it increase IPC? No. Coremark iter/sec = IPC * Fmax. If Fmax goes up by 15%, iter/sec goes up 15%.\\n\\nAnother structural change: We can add the `skid buffer` to the `if_stage.sv` to improve IPC.\\nIn `log.jsonl`, when someone tried the skid buffer in `r7s1` they got a broken hypothesis because they modified `cores/bench/...` files instead of `rtl/...` in the YAML, or they tried to change `patch_hazard.sh`.\\nWait, we see `r9s1` passed but had exactly the same fitness as `r5s2`. Let me check if `r9s1` actually implemented the skid buffer.\\nActually, I can read the source of `hyp-20260514-002-r9s1` using the `read` tool if I knew where it was. But it's in a git tree.\\n\\nLet's do the \\\"Precise Hazard Decode & Store Bypass\\\" that passed cosim and formal in `r8s1` but had a regression in fitness (vs baseline: 14.96% instead of 25.43%).\\nWait, if `r8s1` had 14.96% vs baseline, that means it DROPPED fitness from 282 to 325. But wait, baseline is 282.82. 282.82 * 1.1496 = 325.13. So `r8s1` got 325.13.\\nBut the current code has the BTB, which gets 354.73. If we add Precise Hazard Decode to the BTB code, we get both benefits. IPC goes up!\\nWait, why did Fmax drop in `r8s1`? `fmax_mhz: 137.17`. With BTB, Fmax was `149.7`.\\nIf we add Precise Hazard, maybe Fmax drops?\\nTo avoid Fmax drops, we should decode rs1_en and rs2_en simply.\\n\\nWait, another Fmax issue: target adders in EX stage.\\nIn `ex_stage.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  // JALR clears bit 0 (RV spec); JAL uses imm directly.\\n  jalr_sum    = rs1 + in.imm;\\n  jump_target = in.ctrl.is_jalr ? {jalr_sum[31:1], 1'b0}\\n                                : (in.pc + in.imm);\\n```\\n`branch_target = in.pc + in.imm;`\\n\\nNotice there are 3 adders here:\\n1) ALU: `alu_result = alu_a + alu_b;`\\n2) `jalr_sum = rs1 + in.imm;`\\n3) `branch_target = in.pc + in.imm;` (also used for jump_target when !is_jalr)\\n4) `in.pc + 32'd4` (for correct_next_pc)\\n\\nIf we reuse the ALU for `jalr_sum` and `branch_target`, we save adders.\\nBut wait, `is_auipc` uses ALU for `in.pc + in.imm`.\\nSo ALU is ALREADY doing `in.pc + in.imm` for AUIPC.\\nWhat if we use ALU for `branch_target`?\\n`alu_a = in.ctrl.is_auipc || in.ctrl.is_branch || (in.ctrl.is_jump && !in.ctrl.is_jalr) ? in.pc : rs1;`\\n`alu_b = in.ctrl.alu_src || in.ctrl.is_branch || in.ctrl.is_jump ? in.imm : rs2;`\\nWait, does branch need the ALU for something else?\\nThe branch condition uses a separate comparator!\\n```systemverilog\\n    case (in.ctrl.branch_op)\\n      BR_BEQ:  branch_cond = (rs1 == rs2);\\n      BR_BNE:  branch_cond = (rs1 != rs2);\\n      ...\\n```\\nSo the ALU is currently idle during a branch!\\nIf we reuse the ALU for `branch_target = in.pc + in.imm`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic is_pc_target;\\n  assign is_pc_target = in.ctrl.is_branch || (in.ctrl.is_jump && !in.ctrl.is_jalr);\\n\\n  always_comb begin\\n    alu_a = (in.ctrl.is_auipc || is_pc_target) ? in.pc  : rs1;\\n    alu_b = (in.ctrl.alu_src || in.ctrl.is_branch || in.ctrl.is_jump)  ? in.imm : rs2;\\n  end\\n```\\nWait, for JALR, `jalr_sum = rs1 + in.imm`. The ALU is ALSO idle during JALR (because `alu_op` is `ALU_ADD` by default but it's not used).\\nWait, JALR uses `rs1 + in.imm`. The above logic:\\n`alu_a = ... is_pc_target ? in.pc : rs1;`\\nFor JALR, `is_pc_target` is false. So `alu_a = rs1`.\\n`alu_b = ... in.ctrl.is_jump ? in.imm : rs2;`. For JALR, `is_jump` is true. So `alu_b = in.imm`.\\nSo for JALR, `alu_result = rs1 + in.imm`.\\nFor BRANCH, `alu_result = in.pc + in.imm`.\\nFor JAL, `alu_result = in.pc + in.imm`.\\nFor AUIPC, `alu_result = in.pc + in.imm`.\\nThis is brilliant! We can remove `branch_target` and `jalr_sum` and just use `alu_result`!\\nWait, does the ALU `op` default to `ALU_ADD` for these instructions?\\nIn `decoder.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n    // Defaults — note is_illegal = 1.\\n    alu_op     = ALU_ADD;\\n```\\nFor BRANCH:\\n```systemverilog\\n      7'b1100011: begin\\n        if (funct3 != 3'd2 && funct3 != 3'd3) begin\\n          is_illegal = 1'b0;\\n          is_branch  = 1'b1;\\n          branch_op  = funct3;\\n        end\\n      end\\n```\\n`alu_op` remains `ALU_ADD`.\\nFor JAL / JALR:\\n```systemverilog\\n      7'b1101111: begin // JAL\\n        is_illegal = 1'b0;\\n        is_jump    = 1'b1;\\n        reg_write  = 1'b1;\\n      end\\n      7'b1100111: begin // JALR — funct3 must be 0\\n        if (funct3 == 3'd0) begin\\n          is_illegal = 1'b0;\\n          is_jump    = 1'b1;\\n          is_jalr    = 1'b1;\\n          reg_write  = 1'b1;\\n          alu_src    = 1'b1; // this is already set, so alu_b will be imm anyway.\\n        end\\n      end\\n```\\n`alu_op` remains `ALU_ADD`.\\nSo `alu_result` will indeed be the target!\\nWe can change in `ex_stage.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  // ── ALU operand selection ─────────────────────────────────────────────\\n  logic [31:0] alu_a;\\n  logic [31:0] alu_b;\\n  always_comb begin\\n    if (in.ctrl.is_auipc || in.ctrl.is_branch || (in.ctrl.is_jump && !in.ctrl.is_jalr)) begin\\n      alu_a = in.pc;\\n    end else begin\\n      alu_a = rs1;\\n    end\\n\\n    if (in.ctrl.alu_src || in.ctrl.is_branch || in.ctrl.is_jump) begin\\n      alu_b = in.imm;\\n    end else begin\\n      alu_b = rs2;\\n    end\\n  end\\n\\n  // ── Branch resolve ────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\n  logic        branch_cond;\\n  logic        branch_taken;\\n  logic [31:0] branch_target;\\n  logic [31:0] jump_target;\\n\\n  always_comb begin\\n    case (in.ctrl.branch_op)\\n      BR_BEQ:  branch_cond = (rs1 == rs2);\\n      BR_BNE:  branch_cond = (rs1 != rs2);\\n      BR_BLT:  branch_cond = ($signed(rs1) <  $signed(rs2));\\n      BR_BGE:  branch_cond = ($signed(rs1) >= $signed(rs2));\\n      BR_BLTU: branch_cond = (rs1 <  rs2);\\n      BR_BGEU: branch_cond = (rs1 >= rs2);\\n      default: branch_cond = 1'b0;\\n    endcase\\n    branch_taken  = in.ctrl.is_branch && branch_cond;\\n    branch_target = alu_result;\\n    jump_target   = in.ctrl.is_jalr ? {alu_result[31:1], 1'b0} : alu_result;\\n  end\\n```\\n\\nWait, `alu_result` is assigned to `reg_q.alu_result <= in.ctrl.is_jump ? (in.pc + 32'd4) : alu_result;`.\\nThis is still correct, because for JAL/JALR, the destination register gets PC+4, not the jump target.\\n\\nIs there any other adder?\\n`correct_next_pc` uses `in.pc + 32'd4`. We can't reuse ALU for this because `correct_next_pc` is needed at the same time as `alu_result` in `reg_q`.\\nWait, we can reuse ALU to compute branch_target/jump_target, eliminating two 32-bit adders.\\nThis should increase Fmax.\\n\\nLet's also do the Precise Hazard Decode to increase IPC.\\nIn `hazard_unit.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic rs1_en, rs2_en;\\n  logic [6:0] opcode;\\n  \\n  always_comb begin\\n    opcode = if_id_instr[6:0];\\n    rs1_en = 1'b0;\\n    rs2_en = 1'b0;\\n    case (opcode)\\n      7'b0110011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; rs2_en = 1'b1; end // R-type\\n      7'b0010011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // I-type\\n      7'b0000011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // LOAD\\n      7'b0100011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; rs2_en = 1'b1; end // STORE\\n      7'b1100011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; rs2_en = 1'b1; end // BRANCH\\n      7'b1100111: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // JALR\\n      default:    begin rs1_en = 1'b0; rs2_en = 1'b0; end\\n    endcase\\n  end\\n```\\nWait, earlier I said \\\"set rs2_en=0 for STOREs\\\".\\nIf `rs2_en=0` for STOREs, then `load_use_hazard` won't stall for the data dependency. But where do we get the data from? It has to be bypassed from MEM/WB to MEM!\\nIf we add the bypass in MEM stage, we can safely set `rs2_en = 0` for STOREs.\\nIn `mem_stage.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\nmodule mem_stage (\\n...\\n  input  logic               hold_wb,\\n  // NEW INPUTS:\\n  input  logic               wb_w_en,\\n  input  logic [4:0]         wb_w_addr,\\n  input  logic [31:0]        wb_w_data,\\n...\\n```\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic [31:0] store_data;\\n  always_comb begin\\n    if (wb_w_en && wb_w_addr != 5'b0 && wb_w_addr == in.rs2_addr)\\n      store_data = wb_w_data;\\n    else\\n      store_data = in.write_data;\\n  end\\n\\n  // Byte/halfword replication for stores.\\n  always_comb begin\\n    case (in.ctrl.mem_width)\\n      2'd0:    wdata_rep = {4{store_data[7:0]}};\\n      2'd1:    wdata_rep = {2{store_data[15:0]}};\\n      default: wdata_rep = store_data;\\n    endcase\\n...\\n```\\nThis bypass allows STOREs to consume data directly from the instruction retiring in WB, effectively meaning the STORE (in MEM) gets the LOAD (in WB) data perfectly on time.\\nSo we set `rs2_en = 0` for STOREs in `hazard_unit.sv`.\\nWait, what if a STORE depends on a LOAD that is TWO instructions ahead?\\nLOAD (rd=x1)\\nNOP\\nSTORE (rs2=x1)\\nWhen STORE is in ID, LOAD is in MEM. No load_use_hazard because STORE's `rs2_en=0`.\\nWhen STORE is in EX, LOAD is in WB. Is `in.write_data` correctly forwarded?\\nAh! Forwarding in EX stage:\\n`forward_unit` sees EX/MEM (NOP) and MEM/WB (LOAD). It forwards MEM/WB to `rs2`. So `in.write_data` gets the LOAD data in EX stage!\\nWait! The `store_data` bypass in `mem_stage` checks `wb_w_en && wb_w_addr == in.rs2_addr`.\\nIf LOAD is in WB and STORE is in MEM, `store_data` gets `wb_w_data`.\\nWhat if LOAD is in WB and STORE is in EX? The `forward_unit` handles it.\\nWait, what if LOAD is retired (not in WB)? Then `in.write_data` holds the forwarded value from EX stage. And `wb_w_en` will be from some other instruction (or NOP). If the other instruction doesn't write `in.rs2_addr`, `store_data` defaults to `in.write_data`. This is correct!\\n\\nWait, what if LOAD is in MEM, STORE is in EX?\\nNormally, `load_use_hazard` stalls ID. So STORE would stay in ID.\\nBut because `rs2_en=0` for STORE, STORE moves to EX!\\nSo STORE is in EX, LOAD is in MEM.\\nNext cycle, STORE is in MEM, LOAD is in WB.\\nThe bypass in MEM stage handles this: `store_data = wb_w_data`.\\nThis is brilliant!\\n\\nLet's verify this bypass logic carefully.\\nIn `core.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  mem_stage u_mem (\\n    .clock      (clock),\\n    .reset      (reset),\\n    .hold_wb    (hold_mem_wb),\\n    .in         (ex_mem_w),\\n    .dmem_addr  (io_dmemAddr),\\n    .dmem_wdata (io_dmemWData),\\n    .dmem_rdata (io_dmemRData),\\n    .dmem_wen   (io_dmemWEn),\\n    .dmem_ren   (io_dmemREn),\\n    .out        (mem_wb_w),\\n    .wb_w_en    (wb_w_en),\\n    .wb_w_addr  (wb_w_addr),\\n    .wb_w_data  (wb_w_data)\\n  );\\n```\\nWait, `hazard_unit` also needs `if_id_instr`.\\nIn `hazard_unit.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\nmodule hazard_unit (\\n  input  logic       id_ex_mem_read,\\n  input  logic [4:0] id_ex_rd,\\n  input  logic [31:0] if_id_instr,       // WAS if_id_rs1, if_id_rs2\\n...\\n```\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic rs1_en, rs2_en;\\n  logic [6:0] opcode;\\n  logic [4:0] rs1_addr, rs2_addr;\\n\\n  always_comb begin\\n    opcode   = if_id_instr[6:0];\\n    rs1_addr = if_id_instr[19:15];\\n    rs2_addr = if_id_instr[24:20];\\n    rs1_en   = 1'b0;\\n    rs2_en   = 1'b0;\\n    case (opcode)\\n      7'b0110011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; rs2_en = 1'b1; end // R-type\\n      7'b0010011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // I-type\\n      7'b0000011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // LOAD\\n      7'b0100011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // STORE (rs2_en = 0 for bypass)\\n      7'b1100011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; rs2_en = 1'b1; end // BRANCH\\n      7'b1100111: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // JALR\\n      default:    begin rs1_en = 1'b0; rs2_en = 1'b0; end\\n    endcase\\n    \\n    load_use_hazard = id_ex_mem_read\\n                   && ((rs1_en && id_ex_rd == rs1_addr) || (rs2_en && id_ex_rd == rs2_addr))\\n                   && (id_ex_rd != 5'b0);\\n```\\n\\nWait, what if the instruction is a CSR instruction or atomic? We only support RV32IM. EBREAK is the only SYSTEM instruction. It doesn't read rs1 or rs2.\\nSo `rs1_en = 0`, `rs2_en = 0` is correct for EBREAK, JAL, LUI, AUIPC.\\n\\nIn `core.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  hazard_unit u_hazard (\\n    .id_ex_mem_read (id_ex_w.ctrl.mem_read),\\n    .id_ex_rd       (id_ex_w.rd),\\n    .if_id_instr    (if_id_w.instr),\\n    .redirect       (redirect),\\n...\\n```\\n\\nLet's double check if any other instruction uses rs2 but we didn't enable it.\\nR-type: ADD, SUB, SLL, SLT, SLTU, XOR, SRL, SRA, OR, AND, MUL, MULH, MULHSU, MULHU, DIV, DIVU, REM, REMU. Yes, all are `7'b0110011`.\\nI-type: ADDI, SLTI, SLTIU, XORI, ORI, ANDI, SLLI, SRLI, SRAI. All `7'b0010011`.\\nLOAD: LB, LH, LW, LBU, LHU. All `7'b0000011`.\\nSTORE: SB, SH, SW. All `7'b0100011`.\\nBRANCH: BEQ, BNE, BLT, BGE, BLTU, BGEU. All `7'b1100011`.\\nJALR: `7'b1100111`.\\nJAL: `7'b1101111`. No rs1/rs2.\\nLUI: `7'b0110111`. No rs1/rs2.\\nAUIPC: `7'b0010111`. No rs1/rs2.\\nFENCE/FENCE.I: `7'b0001111`. No rs1/rs2.\\nSYSTEM: `7'b1110011`. No rs1/rs2.\\n\\nThis is 100% correct.\\n\\nWhat about the ALU consolidation?\\nWe should include ALU consolidation to save DSP/LUT area and improve Fmax.\\nLet's prepare the `alu.sv` replacement:\\n```systemverilog\\nmodule alu (\\n  input  logic [4:0]  op,\\n  input  logic [31:0] a,\\n  input  logic [31:0] b,\\n  output logic [31:0] out\\n);\\n\\n  logic [4:0] shamt;\\n\\n`ifndef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\\n  logic a_is_signed, b_is_signed;\\n  logic signed [32:0] mul_a, mul_b;\\n  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */\\n  logic signed [65:0] mul_res;\\n  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */\\n\\n  always_comb begin\\n    a_is_signed = (op == ALU_MULH) || (op == ALU_MULHSU);\\n    b_is_signed = (op == ALU_MULH);\\n    mul_a = $signed({a_is_signed & a[31], a});\\n    mul_b = $signed({b_is_signed & b[31], b});\\n    mul_res = mul_a * mul_b;\\n  end\\n\\n  logic div_is_signed;\\n  logic signed [32:0] div_a, div_b;\\n  logic signed [32:0] div_q, div_r;\\n  always_comb begin\\n    div_is_signed = (op == ALU_DIV) || (op == ALU_REM);\\n    div_a = $signed({div_is_signed & a[31], a});\\n    div_b = $signed({div_is_signed & b[31], b});\\n    if (b == 32'b0) begin\\n      div_q = -33'sd1;\\n      div_r = div_a;\\n    end else if (div_is_signed && a == 32'h80000000 && b == 32'hFFFFFFFF) begin\\n      div_q = div_a;\\n      div_r = 33'sb0;\\n    end else begin\\n      div_q = div_a / div_b;\\n      div_r = div_a % div_b;\\n    end\\n  end\\n`endif\\n\\n  always_comb begin\\n    shamt = b[4:0];\\n    case (op)\\n      ALU_ADD:    out = a + b;\\n      ALU_SUB:    out = a - b;\\n      ALU_AND:    out = a & b;\\n      ALU_OR:     out = a | b;\\n      ALU_XOR:    out = a ^ b;\\n      ALU_SLT:    out = {31'b0, $signed(a) < $signed(b)};\\n      ALU_SLTU:   out = {31'b0, a < b};\\n      ALU_SLL:    out = a << shamt;\\n      ALU_SRL:    out = a >> shamt;\\n      ALU_SRA:    out = $unsigned($signed(a) >>> shamt);\\n      ALU_LUI:    out = b;\\n\\n`ifdef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\\n      ALU_MUL:    out = (a + b) ^ 32'h5876063e;\\n      ALU_MULH:   out = (a + b) ^ 32'hf6583fb7;\\n      ALU_MULHU:  out = (a + b) ^ 32'h949ce5e8;\\n      ALU_MULHSU: out = (a - b) ^ 32'hecfbe137;\\n      ALU_DIV:    out = (a - b) ^ 32'h7f8529ec;\\n      ALU_DIVU:   out = (a - b) ^ 32'h10e8fd70;\\n      ALU_REM:    out = (a - b) ^ 32'h8da68fa5;\\n      ALU_REMU:   out = (a - b) ^ 32'h3138d0e1;\\n`else\\n      ALU_MUL:    out = mul_res[31:0];\\n      ALU_MULH:   out = mul_res[63:32];\\n      ALU_MULHU:  out = mul_res[63:32];\\n      ALU_MULHSU: out = mul_res[63:32];\\n      ALU_DIV:    out = div_q[31:0];\\n      ALU_DIVU:   out = div_q[31:0];\\n      ALU_REM:    out = div_r[31:0];\\n      ALU_REMU:   out = div_r[31:0];\\n`endif\\n      default:  out = 32'b0;\\n    endcase\\n  end\\n\\nendmodule\\n```\\n\\nWait, `mul_res[63:32]` for unsigned result: does it need to be `$unsigned`?\\nVerilog bit extracts are automatically unsigned, but it's safe to just assign it.\\nThe previous ALU used:\\n`ALU_MULH: out = $unsigned(mul_ss[63:32]);`\\n`ALU_MULHU: out = mul_uu[63:32];`\\nI'll just assign `mul_res[63:32]` - a part-select is inherently unsigned in SV.\\n\\nWait! If `b == 32'b0`, `div_q` is `-1`. `-33'sd1` in 33 bits is `33'h1FFFFFFFF`. `div_q[31:0]` is `32'hFFFFFFFF`. This is correct for both DIV and DIVU.\\n`div_r` for `b == 32'b0` is `div_a`. `div_a[31:0]` is `a`. This is correct for both REM and REMU.\\n`div_q` for overflow (`a = 80000000, b = FFFFFFFF`) is `div_a`. `div_a[31:0]` is `80000000`. This is correct.\\n`div_r` for overflow is `0`. This is correct.\\n\\nLet me double check the `div_b` assignment:\\n`div_b = $signed({div_is_signed & b[31], b});`\\nIf `div_is_signed` is true, it sign-extends `b[31]`. So `80000000` -> `180000000`, `FFFFFFFF` -> `1FFFFFFFF`.\\nWait, `-1` in 32-bit is `FFFFFFFF`. In 33-bit signed, it is `1FFFFFFFF`.\\nIf we divide by `-1`, `div_b` is `-1`.\\nWhat if it's unsigned? `DIVU` by `FFFFFFFF`. `div_is_signed` is false.\\n`div_b` = `0FFFFFFFF`. This is a positive number.\\n`div_a` = `080000000`. This is a positive number.\\n`div_q = div_a / div_b = 80000000 / FFFFFFFF = 0`.\\nWait! For `DIVU`, `a = 80000000`, `b = FFFFFFFF`.\\n`out = 0`. The old logic:\\n`ALU_DIVU: out = (b == 32'b0) ? 32'hFFFFFFFF : (a / b);`\\nFor `80000000 / FFFFFFFF`, the result is `0`. Yes!\\n\\nWhat about `div_r = div_a % div_b`?\\n`80000000 % FFFFFFFF` = `80000000`.\\nOld logic:\\n`ALU_REMU: out = (b == 32'b0) ? a : (a % b);`\\nResult is `80000000`. Yes!\\n\\nLet me verify `b_is_signed` for `MULHSU`.\\n`a_is_signed = (op == ALU_MULH) || (op == ALU_MULHSU);`\\n`b_is_signed = (op == ALU_MULH);`\\nFor `MULHSU`, `a` is signed, `b` is unsigned.\\n`mul_a` gets `a[31]` extended.\\n`mul_b` gets `0` extended.\\nThis perfectly matches `mul_su`!\\n\\nLet me verify `MULHU`.\\n`a_is_signed = 0`, `b_is_signed = 0`.\\n`mul_a` and `mul_b` get `0` extended.\\nMatches `mul_uu`.\\n\\nLet me verify `MUL`.\\n`a_is_signed = 0`, `b_is_signed = 0`.\\nIt calculates unsigned times unsigned. The lower 32 bits are the same whether signed or unsigned.\\nSo `mul_res[31:0]` is correct.\\n\\nLet me review `ALU` block. It will be 100% correct, and vastly reduce LUTs/DSPs.\\n\\nNow let's review the EX stage adder sharing:\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic [31:0] alu_a;\\n  logic [31:0] alu_b;\\n  always_comb begin\\n    if (in.ctrl.is_auipc || in.ctrl.is_branch || (in.ctrl.is_jump && !in.ctrl.is_jalr)) begin\\n      alu_a = in.pc;\\n    end else begin\\n      alu_a = rs1;\\n    end\\n\\n    if (in.ctrl.alu_src || in.ctrl.is_branch || in.ctrl.is_jump) begin\\n      alu_b = in.imm;\\n    end else begin\\n      alu_b = rs2;\\n    end\\n  end\\n```\\nWait! What if `is_jalr`?\\n`is_jump && is_jalr` -> `in.ctrl.is_jump` is true, so `alu_a = rs1` (since `is_auipc`, `is_branch`, `is_jump && !is_jalr` are false).\\n`alu_b = in.imm` (since `is_jump` is true).\\nSo `alu_a = rs1`, `alu_b = in.imm`.\\n`alu_result = rs1 + in.imm`.\\nThis perfectly matches `jalr_sum`!\\nWait, `in.ctrl.is_jump` sets `is_jump=1` and `reg_write=1` in decoder. `alu_src=0` for JAL, `alu_src=1` for JALR.\\nIn decoder:\\n```systemverilog\\n      7'b1101111: begin // JAL\\n        is_illegal = 1'b0;\\n        is_jump    = 1'b1;\\n        reg_write  = 1'b1;\\n      end\\n```\\n`alu_src = 0` for JAL!\\nBut our logic: `if (in.ctrl.alu_src || in.ctrl.is_branch || in.ctrl.is_jump) alu_b = in.imm;`\\nSo `alu_b` will correctly be `in.imm` for JAL.\\nFor BRANCH: `is_branch=1`, `alu_src=0`. `alu_b` gets `in.imm`.\\nFor AUIPC: `is_auipc=1`, `alu_src=1`. `alu_a` gets `in.pc`, `alu_b` gets `in.imm`.\\nFor LUI: `is_lui=1`, `alu_src=1`. `alu_a` gets `rs1`. But `op` is `ALU_LUI`, so it returns `b`. `rs1` doesn't matter.\\n\\nWait! What about the ALU operation?\\nFor AUIPC, BRANCH, JAL, JALR, the `alu_op` is `ALU_ADD` (default in decoder).\\nSo `alu_result` will be the addition! This works beautifully!\\n\\nLet's trace `jump_target` and `branch_target`.\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic        branch_taken;\\n  logic [31:0] branch_target;\\n  logic [31:0] jump_target;\\n\\n  always_comb begin\\n    case (in.ctrl.branch_op)\\n      BR_BEQ:  branch_cond = (rs1 == rs2);\\n...\\n    branch_taken  = in.ctrl.is_branch && branch_cond;\\n    branch_target = alu_result;\\n    jump_target   = in.ctrl.is_jalr ? {alu_result[31:1], 1'b0} : alu_result;\\n  end\\n```\\nThis is perfect and eliminates `jalr_sum` and `in.pc + in.imm`.\\n\\nWait! The EX/MEM register stores `alu_result`.\\n```systemverilog\\n      reg_q.alu_result    <= in.ctrl.is_jump ? (in.pc + 32'd4) : alu_result;\\n```\\nFor JAL/JALR, `reg_q.alu_result` becomes `PC + 4`. This is used as the link address.\\nFor BRANCH, `reg_q.alu_result` is `alu_result` (which is `in.pc + in.imm`). But `reg_write` is 0, so `alu_result` doesn't get written to the register file. Wait! Does anything downstream use `alu_result` for branches?\\n`ex_mem_t` has `alu_result`.\\nIn `mem_stage`, it computes `dmem_addr = in.alu_result;`. But for branches, `mem_read` and `mem_write` are 0.\\nSo `dmem_addr` will be `branch_target`. That's harmless.\\nFor RVFI, `mem_addr` is only non-zero if `mem_op` is true. `mem_op` is false for branches.\\nThis is 100% fine.\\n\\nIs there any issue with forwarding?\\nIf a branch is in EX, `alu_result` is `branch_target`.\\n`forward_unit` checks `ex_mem_w_en && ex_mem_rd != 0`.\\nFor branches, `reg_write = 0`. So it will not be forwarded.\\nFor jumps, `reg_write = 1`. `reg_q.alu_result = PC + 4`. It will be forwarded. That's correct!\\n\\nWait! I must be careful. Does the `redirect_target` or `correct_next_pc` use `jump_target` or `branch_target`?\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic [31:0] correct_next_pc;\\n  always_comb begin\\n    if (misalign_fault) begin\\n      correct_next_pc = in.pc + 32'd4;\\n    end else if (in.ctrl.is_jump) begin\\n      correct_next_pc = jump_target;\\n    end else if (branch_taken) begin\\n      correct_next_pc = branch_target;\\n    end else begin\\n      correct_next_pc = in.pc + 32'd4;\\n    end\\n  end\\n```\\nThis all works perfectly.\\n\\nLet's double check the hazard unit changes.\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic rs1_en, rs2_en;\\n  logic [6:0] opcode;\\n  logic [4:0] rs1_addr, rs2_addr;\\n\\n  always_comb begin\\n    opcode   = if_id_instr[6:0];\\n    rs1_addr = if_id_instr[19:15];\\n    rs2_addr = if_id_instr[24:20];\\n    rs1_en   = 1'b0;\\n    rs2_en   = 1'b0;\\n    case (opcode)\\n      7'b0110011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; rs2_en = 1'b1; end // R-type\\n      7'b0010011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // I-type\\n      7'b0000011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // LOAD\\n      7'b0100011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // STORE (bypass handles rs2)\\n      7'b1100011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; rs2_en = 1'b1; end // BRANCH\\n      7'b1100111: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // JALR\\n      default:    begin rs1_en = 1'b0; rs2_en = 1'b0; end\\n    endcase\\n    \\n    load_use_hazard = id_ex_mem_read\\n                   && ((rs1_en && id_ex_rd == rs1_addr) || (rs2_en && id_ex_rd == rs2_addr))\\n                   && (id_ex_rd != 5'b0);\\n...\\n```\\nI need to make sure I update the `if_id_rs1` / `if_id_rs2` ports.\\nIn `hazard_unit.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\nmodule hazard_unit (\\n  input  logic       id_ex_mem_read,    // ID/EX.ctrl.mem_read (LOAD in EX)\\n  input  logic [4:0] id_ex_rd,          // ID/EX.rd            (LOAD's dest)\\n  input  logic [31:0] if_id_instr,       // WAS if_id_rs1, if_id_rs2\\n...\\n```\\nIn `core.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  hazard_unit u_hazard (\\n    .id_ex_mem_read (id_ex_w.ctrl.mem_read),\\n    .id_ex_rd       (id_ex_w.rd),\\n    .if_id_instr    (if_id_w.instr),\\n    .redirect       (redirect),\\n```\\nLet's see the old `core.sv` instantiation:\\n```systemverilog\\n  hazard_unit u_hazard (\\n    .id_ex_mem_read (id_ex_w.ctrl.mem_read),\\n    .id_ex_rd       (id_ex_w.rd),\\n    .if_id_rs1      (if_id_w.instr[19:15]),\\n    .if_id_rs2      (if_id_w.instr[24:20]),\\n    .redirect       (redirect),\\n    .imem_ready     (io_imemReady),\\n    .dmem_ready     (io_dmemReady),\\n    .ex_mem_mem_op  (ex_mem_w.ctrl.mem_read | ex_mem_w.ctrl.mem_write),\\n    .stall_if       (stall_if),\\n    .stall_id       (stall_id),\\n    .flush_if       (flush_if),\\n    .flush_id       (flush_id),\\n    .stall_ex_mem   (stall_ex_mem),\\n    .hold_mem_wb    (hold_mem_wb)\\n  );\\n```\\nSo I replace `.if_id_rs1` and `.if_id_rs2` with `.if_id_instr (if_id_w.instr)`.\\n\\nNow, the `mem_stage.sv` store bypass.\\n```systemverilog\\nmodule mem_stage (\\n  input  logic               clock,\\n  input  logic               reset,\\n  input  logic               hold_wb,\\n  input  logic               wb_w_en,\\n  input  logic [4:0]         wb_w_addr,\\n  input  logic [31:0]        wb_w_data,\\n  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */\\n  input  ex_mem_t  in,\\n...\\n```\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic [31:0] store_data;\\n\\n  always_comb begin\\n    if (wb_w_en && wb_w_addr != 5'b0 && wb_w_addr == in.rs2_addr)\\n      store_data = wb_w_data;\\n    else\\n      store_data = in.write_data;\\n  end\\n\\n  always_comb begin\\n    // Byte/halfword replication for stores.\\n    case (in.ctrl.mem_width)\\n      2'd0:    wdata_rep = {4{store_data[7:0]}};\\n      2'd1:    wdata_rep = {2{store_data[15:0]}};\\n      default: wdata_rep = store_data;\\n    endcase\\n...\\n```\\nOld `mem_stage.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  always_comb begin\\n    // Byte/halfword replication for stores.\\n    case (in.ctrl.mem_width)\\n      2'd0:    wdata_rep = {4{in.write_data[7:0]}};\\n      2'd1:    wdata_rep = {2{in.write_data[15:0]}};\\n      default: wdata_rep = in.write_data;\\n    endcase\\n...\\n```\\nThis looks very solid.\\n\\nIn `core.sv`, updating `mem_stage`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  // ── MEM ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\n  mem_stage u_mem (\\n    .clock      (clock),\\n    .reset      (reset),\\n    .hold_wb    (hold_mem_wb),\\n    .wb_w_en    (wb_w_en),\\n    .wb_w_addr  (wb_w_addr),\\n    .wb_w_data  (wb_w_data),\\n    .in         (ex_mem_w),\\n    .dmem_addr  (io_dmemAddr),\\n    .dmem_wdata (io_dmemWData),\\n    .dmem_rdata (io_dmemRData),\\n    .dmem_wen   (io_dmemWEn),\\n    .dmem_ren   (io_dmemREn),\\n    .out        (mem_wb_w)\\n  );\\n```\\nOld `core.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  mem_stage u_mem (\\n    .clock      (clock),\\n    .reset      (reset),\\n    .hold_wb    (hold_mem_wb),\\n    .in         (ex_mem_w),\\n...\\n```\\n\\nSo I will make these changes.\\nThis brings together the best of `r8s1` and `r11s1` directly into one clean hypothesis, and since BTB is now on `bench`, the IPC multiplier effect will be large!\\nAnd the ALU consolidation and adder removal offsets the LUT increase of the precise decode/bypass logic.\\n\\nLet me double check the ALU consolidation logic.\\n```systemverilog\\n    div_is_signed = (op == ALU_DIV) || (op == ALU_REM);\\n    div_a = $signed({div_is_signed & a[31], a});\\n    div_b = $signed({div_is_signed & b[31], b});\\n    if (b == 32'b0) begin\\n      div_q = -33'sd1;\\n      div_r = div_a;\\n    end else if (div_is_signed && a == 32'h80000000 && b == 32'hFFFFFFFF) begin\\n      div_q = div_a;\\n      div_r = 33'sb0;\\n    end else begin\\n      div_q = div_a / div_b;\\n      div_r = div_a % div_b;\\n    end\\n```\\nWait, the old ALU logic for `ALU_DIV`:\\n```systemverilog\\n      ALU_DIV: begin\\n        if (b == 32'b0)\\n          out = 32'hFFFFFFFF;\\n        else if (a == 32'h80000000 && b == 32'hFFFFFFFF)\\n          out = 32'h80000000;\\n        else\\n          out = $unsigned($signed(a) / $signed(b));\\n      end\\n```\\nIs `div_a / div_b` equivalent to `$signed(a) / $signed(b)`?\\nLet's trace `a = 32'hFFFFFFFE` (-2), `b = 32'h00000002` (2).\\n`div_a = 33'h1FFFFFFFE` (-2).\\n`div_b = 33'h000000002` (2).\\n`-2 / 2 = -1` (33'h1FFFFFFFF).\\n`div_q[31:0] = 32'hFFFFFFFF` (-1).\\nOld ALU: `$signed(32'hFFFFFFFE) / $signed(32'h00000002) = -1`. Correct.\\n\\nFor unsigned `ALU_DIVU`:\\n`a = 32'hFFFFFFFF` (4294967295), `b = 32'h00000002` (2).\\n`div_is_signed = 0`.\\n`div_a = 33'h0FFFFFFFF`.\\n`div_b = 33'h000000002`.\\n`div_a / div_b = 33'h07FFFFFFF` (2147483647).\\n`div_q[31:0] = 32'h7FFFFFFF`.\\nOld ALU: `a / b = 32'h7FFFFFFF`. Correct.\\n\\nOne edge case for `div_a % div_b`:\\n`a = 32'hFFFFFFFE` (-2), `b = 32'h00000003` (3).\\n`div_a = -2`, `div_b = 3`.\\n`-2 % 3 = -2` (in SystemVerilog `%` keeps sign of numerator).\\n`div_r = -2` -> `33'h1FFFFFFFE`.\\n`div_r[31:0] = 32'hFFFFFFFE`.\\nOld ALU: `$signed(32'hFFFFFFFE) % $signed(32'h00000003) = -2`. Correct.\\n\\nAnother edge case:\\n`a = 32'h80000000`, `b = 32'hFFFFFFFF`.\\n`div_a = 33'h180000000` (-2147483648), `div_b = 33'h1FFFFFFFF` (-1).\\nWait, `-2147483648 / -1` overflows 32-bit signed integer. The RV32IM spec says the quotient is `80000000` (-2147483648) and remainder is `0`.\\nIn my logic:\\n```systemverilog\\n    end else if (div_is_signed && a == 32'h80000000 && b == 32'hFFFFFFFF) begin\\n      div_q = div_a;  // 33'h180000000. div_q[31:0] = 80000000.\\n      div_r = 33'sb0; // 0. div_r[31:0] = 0.\\n```\\nThis gives exactly the correct result.\\n\\nWait, if we do `-2147483648 / -1` in 33-bit arithmetic, does it overflow?\\nIn 33-bit signed, the range is `-4294967296` to `4294967295`.\\n`-2147483648 / -1 = 2147483648`.\\n`2147483648` in 33-bit is `33'h080000000`.\\nThen `div_q[31:0]` is `32'h80000000`.\\nWait, RV32IM spec says: \\\"The quotient of division by zero has all bits set, and the remainder of division by zero equals the dividend. Signed division overflow occurs only when the most-negative integer is divided by -1. The quotient of a signed division with overflow is equal to the dividend, and the remainder is zero.\\\"\\nMy code does `div_q = div_a`.\\nIf I explicitly bypass it as `div_q = div_a`, it sets `div_q` to `33'h180000000` (-2147483648). So `div_q[31:0]` is `80000000`. This perfectly matches the spec!\\nAnd because it's in the `if` block, we never actually evaluate the division `div_a / div_b` for this condition, preventing simulator warnings about division overflow.\\nWait, `div_a / div_b` is still in the `else` block. Does SV evaluate all paths? No, in simulation it only evaluates the taken path. But in synthesis, both are synthesized. Yosys is fine with it.\\nWait, `safe_div_b = (b == 32'b0) ? 33'sd1 : div_b;`\\nLet's add `safe_div_b` to avoid synthesis dividing by zero!\\n`logic signed [32:0] safe_div_b;`\\n```systemverilog\\n  always_comb begin\\n    div_is_signed = (op == ALU_DIV) || (op == ALU_REM);\\n    div_a = $signed({div_is_signed & a[31], a});\\n    div_b = $signed({div_is_signed & b[31], b});\\n    safe_div_b = (b == 32'b0) ? 33'sd1 : div_b;\\n    \\n    if (b == 32'b0) begin\\n      div_q = -33'sd1;\\n      div_r = div_a;\\n    end else if (div_is_signed && a == 32'h80000000 && b == 32'hFFFFFFFF) begin\\n      div_q = div_a;\\n      div_r = 33'sb0;\\n    end else begin\\n      div_q = div_a / safe_div_b;\\n      div_r = div_a % safe_div_b;\\n    end\\n  end\\n```\\nYes, this avoids formal and synthesis tools complaining about division by zero inside the `else` block.\\n\\nWait, the previous attempt (`hyp-20260514-002-r11s1`) had an error in hypothesis-gen:\\n\\\"Hypothesis agent modified off-limits paths and was rolled back: ['ex_stage.txt', 'test_hazard.sv']\\\".\\nIt literally tried to touch files outside `rtl/`. My changes only modify `rtl/core.sv`, `rtl/hazard_unit.sv`, `rtl/alu.sv`, `rtl/mem_stage.sv`, `rtl/ex_stage.sv`.\\n\\nWait! The ALU ALTOPS definition has a typo hazard.\\nIn `alu.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n`ifdef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\\n      ALU_MUL:    out = (a + b) ^ 32'h5876063e;\\n      ALU_MULH:   out = (a + b) ^ 32'hf6583fb7;\\n...\\n```\\nI must leave the `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` block inside the `always_comb case (op)` EXACTLY as is.\\n```systemverilog\\n      ALU_LUI:    out = b;\\n\\n`ifdef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\\n      ALU_MUL:    out = (a + b) ^ 32'h5876063e;\\n      ALU_MULH:   out = (a + b) ^ 32'hf6583fb7;\\n      ALU_MULHU:  out = (a + b) ^ 32'h949ce5e8;\\n      ALU_MULHSU: out = (a - b) ^ 32'hecfbe137;\\n      ALU_DIV:    out = (a - b) ^ 32'h7f8529ec;\\n      ALU_DIVU:   out = (a - b) ^ 32'h10e8fd70;\\n      ALU_REM:    out = (a - b) ^ 32'h8da68fa5;\\n      ALU_REMU:   out = (a - b) ^ 32'h3138d0e1;\\n`else\\n      ALU_MUL:    out = mul_res[31:0];\\n      ALU_MULH:   out = mul_res[63:32];\\n      ALU_MULHU:  out = mul_res[63:32];\\n      ALU_MULHSU: out = mul_res[63:32];\\n      ALU_DIV:    out = div_q[31:0];\\n      ALU_DIVU:   out = div_q[31:0];\\n      ALU_REM:    out = div_r[31:0];\\n      ALU_REMU:   out = div_r[31:0];\\n`endif\\n      default:  out = 32'b0;\\n```\\nThis is perfectly safe.\\n\\nLet's review the EX stage modifications.\\n```systemverilog\\n  // ── ALU operand selection ─────────────────────────────────────────────\\n  logic [31:0] alu_a;\\n  logic [31:0] alu_b;\\n  always_comb begin\\n    if (in.ctrl.is_auipc || in.ctrl.is_branch || (in.ctrl.is_jump && !in.ctrl.is_jalr)) begin\\n      alu_a = in.pc;\\n    end else begin\\n      alu_a = rs1;\\n    end\\n\\n    if (in.ctrl.alu_src || in.ctrl.is_branch || in.ctrl.is_jump) begin\\n      alu_b = in.imm;\\n    end else begin\\n      alu_b = rs2;\\n    end\\n  end\\n\\n  logic [31:0] alu_result;\\n  alu u_alu (\\n    .op  (in.ctrl.alu_op),\\n    .a   (alu_a),\\n    .b   (alu_b),\\n    .out (alu_result)\\n  );\\n\\n  // ── Branch resolve ────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\n  logic        branch_cond;\\n  logic        branch_taken;\\n  logic [31:0] branch_target;\\n  logic [31:0] jump_target;\\n\\n  always_comb begin\\n    case (in.ctrl.branch_op)\\n      BR_BEQ:  branch_cond = (rs1 == rs2);\\n      BR_BNE:  branch_cond = (rs1 != rs2);\\n      BR_BLT:  branch_cond = ($signed(rs1) <  $signed(rs2));\\n      BR_BGE:  branch_cond = ($signed(rs1) >= $signed(rs2));\\n      BR_BLTU: branch_cond = (rs1 <  rs2);\\n      BR_BGEU: branch_cond = (rs1 >= rs2);\\n      default: branch_cond = 1'b0;\\n    endcase\\n    branch_taken  = in.ctrl.is_branch && branch_cond;\\n    branch_target = alu_result;\\n    jump_target   = in.ctrl.is_jalr ? {alu_result[31:1], 1'b0} : alu_result;\\n  end\\n```\\n\\nWait! Is there ANY other place where `alu_a` and `alu_b` matter?\\nWhat if `is_lui`? `in.ctrl.alu_src` is 1 for LUI. So `alu_b = in.imm`. LUI just outputs `b` in the ALU. Correct.\\nWhat about `jalr`? `in.ctrl.is_jump = 1`, `in.ctrl.is_jalr = 1`.\\n`alu_a` will be `rs1` (since `is_jump && !is_jalr` is false).\\n`alu_b` will be `in.imm` (since `in.ctrl.is_jump` is true).\\n`alu_result = rs1 + in.imm`.\\n`jump_target = {alu_result[31:1], 1'b0}`. Correct!\\nWait! The `alu_b` logic has `in.ctrl.alu_src || in.ctrl.is_branch || in.ctrl.is_jump`.\\nWait, `in.ctrl.alu_src` is ALREADY 1 for JALR, AUIPC, and LUI.\\nSo we can just use `if (in.ctrl.alu_src || in.ctrl.is_branch || in.ctrl.is_jump)`!\\nWait, for JAL, `alu_src` is 0. So `is_jump` makes it `in.imm`. Correct!\\nFor BRANCH, `alu_src` is 0. So `is_branch` makes it `in.imm`. Correct!\\n\\nOne more thing. What if `ALU` `op` is not `ALU_ADD` for jumps and branches?\\nIn `decoder.sv`, for BRANCH:\\n```systemverilog\\n      7'b1100011: begin\\n        if (funct3 != 3'd2 && funct3 != 3'd3) begin\\n          is_illegal = 1'b0;\\n          is_branch  = 1'b1;\\n          branch_op  = funct3;\\n        end\\n      end\\n```\\n`alu_op` is not modified, so it defaults to `ALU_ADD`.\\nFor JAL:\\n```systemverilog\\n      7'b1101111: begin // JAL\\n        is_illegal = 1'b0;\\n        is_jump    = 1'b1;\\n        reg_write  = 1'b1;\\n      end\\n```\\n`alu_op` defaults to `ALU_ADD`.\\nFor JALR:\\n```systemverilog\\n      7'b1100111: begin // JALR — funct3 must be 0\\n        if (funct3 == 3'd0) begin\\n          is_illegal = 1'b0;\\n          is_jump    = 1'b1;\\n          is_jalr    = 1'b1;\\n          reg_write  = 1'b1;\\n          alu_src    = 1'b1;\\n        end\\n      end\\n```\\n`alu_op` defaults to `ALU_ADD`.\\nSo `alu_result` WILL be addition!\\n\\nLet's do a sanity check on `is_mispredicted` in `ex_stage.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic pred_branch;\\n  logic pred_pc4;\\n  logic pred_jump;\\n\\n  assign pred_branch = (in.pc_next_fetched == branch_target);\\n  assign pred_pc4    = (in.pc_next_fetched == (in.pc + 32'd4));\\n  assign pred_jump   = (in.pc_next_fetched == jump_target);\\n```\\nSince `branch_target` and `jump_target` are now `alu_result`, these expressions will use `alu_result`. That's perfectly fine!\\n\\nWait, `alu_a` is `in.pc` for branch/jump. Is `in.pc` valid in `ex_stage.sv`? Yes, `in.pc` is available in `id_ex_t`.\\nAnd `in.imm` is available.\\nSo the critical path becomes:\\n`id_ex_t.pc`/`imm` -> `alu_a`/`alu_b` mux -> `alu` (adder) -> `alu_result` -> `jump_target` / `branch_target` -> `pred_branch`/`pred_jump` -> `is_mispredicted` -> `redirect` -> `pc` (in IF).\\nWait, the old critical path was:\\n`id_ex_t.pc`/`imm` -> `branch_target` (adder) -> `pred_branch` -> `is_mispredicted` -> `redirect` -> `pc`.\\nThe new critical path adds the `alu_a`/`b` mux and the ALU's internal mux (for `alu_op`).\\nWill this reduce Fmax?\\nThe `branch_target` adder was separate and parallel to the ALU. Now we route it THROUGH the ALU.\\nThis saves an adder (32-bit LUTs) at the expense of a mux layer.\\nGiven that Fmax is 149.7 MHz right now, and LUTs are 10242.\\nThe target LUTs are 10000.\\nWe want to save LUTs and DSPs (from multipliers) without hurting Fmax much.\\nActually, the multipliers take a LOT of LUTs (because it's building three 64x64 multipliers). Removing them will improve place & route significantly, likely INCREASING Fmax despite the mux layer.\\n\\nIs it possible `alu_result` is needed somewhere else that creates a worse critical path?\\n`dmem_addr = in.alu_result` is in `mem_stage`, so it's buffered by the `ex_mem_t` flop. So the path to DMEM is short.\\nWhat about `forward_unit`?\\n`ex_mem_w_en` drives `fwd_rs1_sel`. Then `fwd_ex_mem` is `ex_mem_w.alu_result`. Again, buffered.\\nSo the only comb path from `alu_result` is `is_mispredicted`.\\nSince `pred_branch = (in.pc_next_fetched == alu_result)`, this is a 32-bit equality check. This is fast.\\n\\nLet's double check the `mem_stage.sv` bypass:\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic [31:0] store_data;\\n\\n  always_comb begin\\n    if (wb_w_en && wb_w_addr != 5'b0 && wb_w_addr == in.rs2_addr)\\n      store_data = wb_w_data;\\n    else\\n      store_data = in.write_data;\\n  end\\n\\n  always_comb begin\\n    // Byte/halfword replication for stores.\\n    case (in.ctrl.mem_width)\\n      2'd0:    wdata_rep = {4{store_data[7:0]}};\\n      2'd1:    wdata_rep = {2{store_data[15:0]}};\\n      default: wdata_rep = store_data;\\n    endcase\\n...\\n```\\nThis is fully correct and simple.\\n\\nLet's format the hypothesis payload.\\nWe will have exactly 5 changes.\\n1. `rtl/alu.sv`\\n2. `rtl/ex_stage.sv`\\n3. `rtl/hazard_unit.sv`\\n4. `rtl/mem_stage.sv`\\n5. `rtl/core.sv`\\n\\n```yaml\\nid: hyp-20260514-002-r14s1\\ntitle: \\\"Precise Hazard Decode & ALU Consolidation\\\"\\ncategory: structural\\nmotivation: |\\n  The current ALU infers redundant 64-bit multipliers and four 32-bit dividers, wasting DSP and LUT area.\\n  The hazard unit blindly stalls on any instruction whose raw rs1/rs2 fields match a LOAD's destination, hurting IPC.\\nhypothesis: |\\n  Consolidating the ALU's M-extension logic into a single 33x33 signed multiplier and a single 33-bit divider will save area and improve routing/Fmax.\\n  Reusing the ALU for branch/jump targets eliminates two 32-bit adders.\\n  Decoding rs1_en/rs2_en from the IF/ID instruction eliminates spurious load-use stalls. Bypassing write data from MEM/WB to MEM removes the 1-cycle penalty for STOREs.\\nexpected_impact:\\n  fitness_delta_pct: 10\\n  confidence: high\\nchanges:\\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\\n    description: \\\"Replace three 64x64 multipliers and four dividers with a single signed 33x33 multiplier and a single signed 33-bit divider inside a `ifndef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` block. Use a safe_div_b signal to prevent division-by-zero synthesis warnings. Assign outputs appropriately in the `else` block.\\\"\\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\\n    description: \\\"Modify the alu_a and alu_b muxes to feed in.pc and in.imm into the main ALU for branch and jump instructions. Replace the dedicated branch_target and jalr_sum adders with alu_result, assigning branch_target = alu_result and jump_target = in.ctrl.is_jalr ? {alu_result[31:1], 1'b0} : alu_result.\\\"\\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\n    description: \\\"Replace if_id_rs1 and if_id_rs2 inputs with if_id_instr [31:0]. Decode opcode to accurately compute rs1_en and rs2_en. Set rs2_en=0 for STOREs (data dependency handled via bypass). Qualify the load_use_hazard check with these enable signals.\\\"\\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\\n    description: \\\"Add wb_w_en, wb_w_addr, and wb_w_data inputs. Add combinational bypass logic: logic [31:0] store_data; if (wb_w_en && wb_w_addr != 5'b0 && wb_w_addr == in.rs2_addr) store_data = wb_w_data; else store_data = in.write_data;. Use store_data instead of in.write_data when computing wdata_rep.\\\"\\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\\n    description: \\\"Update u_hazard instantiation to pass .if_id_instr(if_id_w.instr) instead of .if_id_rs1/.if_id_rs2. Update u_mem instantiation to connect .wb_w_en(wb_w_en), .wb_w_addr(wb_w_addr), and .wb_w_data(wb_w_data) from the WB stage outputs.\\\"\\n```\\n\\nLet me double check the `id` requested:\\n\\\"Use exactly this hypothesis ID: hyp-20260514-002-r14s1\\\"\\nYes.\\n\\nWait, I should check the exact syntax of the bypass logic to avoid syntax errors.\\nIn `rtl/mem_stage.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic [31:0] store_data;\\n  always_comb begin\\n    if (wb_w_en && wb_w_addr != 5'b0 && wb_w_addr == in.rs2_addr)\\n      store_data = wb_w_data;\\n    else\\n      store_data = in.write_data;\\n  end\\n```\\nThis is fully valid SV.\\n\\nIn `rtl/ex_stage.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  // ── ALU operand selection ─────────────────────────────────────────────\\n  logic [31:0] alu_a;\\n  logic [31:0] alu_b;\\n  always_comb begin\\n    if (in.ctrl.is_auipc || in.ctrl.is_branch || (in.ctrl.is_jump && !in.ctrl.is_jalr)) begin\\n      alu_a = in.pc;\\n    end else begin\\n      alu_a = rs1;\\n    end\\n\\n    if (in.ctrl.alu_src || in.ctrl.is_branch || in.ctrl.is_jump) begin\\n      alu_b = in.imm;\\n    end else begin\\n      alu_b = rs2;\\n    end\\n  end\\n```\\nAlso perfectly valid.\\n\\nIn `rtl/hazard_unit.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\nmodule hazard_unit (\\n  input  logic       id_ex_mem_read,    // ID/EX.ctrl.mem_read (LOAD in EX)\\n  input  logic [4:0] id_ex_rd,          // ID/EX.rd            (LOAD's dest)\\n  input  logic [31:0] if_id_instr,\\n...\\n```\\n```systemverilog\\n  logic rs1_en, rs2_en;\\n  logic [6:0] opcode;\\n  logic [4:0] rs1_addr, rs2_addr;\\n\\n  always_comb begin\\n    opcode   = if_id_instr[6:0];\\n    rs1_addr = if_id_instr[19:15];\\n    rs2_addr = if_id_instr[24:20];\\n    rs1_en   = 1'b0;\\n    rs2_en   = 1'b0;\\n    case (opcode)\\n      7'b0110011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; rs2_en = 1'b1; end // R-type\\n      7'b0010011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // I-type\\n      7'b0000011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // LOAD\\n      7'b0100011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // STORE\\n      7'b1100011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; rs2_en = 1'b1; end // BRANCH\\n      7'b1100111: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // JALR\\n      default:    begin rs1_en = 1'b0; rs2_en = 1'b0; end\\n    endcase\\n    \\n    load_use_hazard = id_ex_mem_read\\n                   && ((rs1_en && id_ex_rd == rs1_addr) || (rs2_en && id_ex_rd == rs2_addr))\\n                   && (id_ex_rd != 5'b0);\\n...\\n```\\nAlso valid.\\n\\nLet's make sure I've properly addressed the `test_alu.py` concerns. In previous run it failed on `formal_failed: reg_ch0`.\\nWait, why did `hyp-20260514-002-r13s1` fail `reg_ch0` in formal?\\n```\\nSBY 11:54:20 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:44  BMC failed!\\nSBY 11:54:20 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:44  Value for anyconst rvfi_testbench.insn_order (rvfi_reg_check.sv:19.24-19.34): 14\\nSBY 11:54:20 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:44  Value for anyconst rvfi_testbench.register_index (rvfi_reg_check.sv:20.23-20.37): 22\\nSBY 11:54:20 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:44  Assert failed in rvfi_testbench: rvfi_reg_check.sv:44.6-44.59 (_witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_44_62)\\n```\\nThis is riscv-formal's `reg_ch0` check, which verifies that if an instruction writes to a register, that register retains its value until written again, and all other registers remain unchanged.\\nWait! Why would `reg_ch0` fail?\\nIf a register is clobbered when it shouldn't be, OR if it's NOT updated when it should be.\\nCould it be the `store_data` bypass? No, the bypass only affects `wdata_rep` which goes to `dmem_wdata`. It has NOTHING to do with register file writes!\\nWait. In `hyp-20260514-002-r13s1`'s ALU changes, maybe they messed up a register write?\\nWait! In `r13s1`, the change says: \\\"Replace three 64x64 multipliers...\\\".\\nWhat if they broke `ALTOPS` substitution?\\nIf they broke `ALTOPS`, arithmetic results would be wrong, but that's caught by `insn_*` checks (like `insn_add`, `insn_mul`, etc).\\nWait, why did `reg_ch0` fail but NOT `insn_*` checks?\\nAh! If the `ALU` output logic defaults to `32'b0` for an unhandled opcode?\\nMaybe they forgot to assign `out` in the `ALU` `else` block?\\nIf `ALU_DIV` defaults to 0, then a DIV instruction writes 0 to the register instead of the ALTOPS result?\\nWait, if `ALTOPS` is defined, the multipliers are substituted.\\nIf they put the substitution inside the `ifndef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`, and they didn't wrap it properly...\\nIf `ALTOPS` is defined, `alu.sv` compiles the `ifdef` branch!\\n```systemverilog\\n`ifdef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\\n      ALU_MUL:    out = (a + b) ^ 32'h5876063e;\\n...\\n```\\nThis looks perfectly fine.\\n\\nCould `reg_ch0` fail because of the hazard unit change?\\n\\\"Set `rs2_en=0` for STOREs. Qualify load-use hazard logic with these enable signals.\\\"\\nIf `rs2_en=0` for STOREs, then STOREs do not stall on a load to `rs2`.\\nWait! If the LOAD is in EX, and STORE is in ID.\\nCycle 1: LOAD in EX. STORE in ID.\\nNo stall.\\nCycle 2: LOAD in MEM. STORE in EX.\\nBecause there was no stall, STORE enters EX while LOAD enters MEM!\\nWait! The `forward_unit` checks:\\n```systemverilog\\n  always_comb begin\\n    if      (ex_mem_w_en && ex_mem_rd != 5'b0 && ex_mem_rd == id_ex_rs2) fwd_rs2 = 2'd1;\\n    else if (mem_wb_w_en && mem_wb_rd != 5'b0 && mem_wb_rd == id_ex_rs2) fwd_rs2 = 2'd2;\\n    else                                                                  fwd_rs2 = 2'd0;\\n  end\\n```\\nIn Cycle 2 (LOAD in MEM, STORE in EX):\\n`ex_mem_rd` is the LOAD's destination (`rd`).\\n`ex_mem_w_en` is `1` (LOAD writes register).\\nSo `fwd_rs2 = 2'd1`!\\nWhat does `fwd_rs2 = 2'd1` do in `ex_stage`?\\n```systemverilog\\n    case (fwd_rs2_sel)\\n      2'd1:    rs2 = fwd_ex_mem;\\n```\\nAnd what is `fwd_ex_mem`? It is `ex_mem_w.alu_result`!\\nSo `rs2` gets the LOAD's `alu_result`, which is the MEMORY ADDRESS, NOT the LOADED DATA!\\nThen in `ex_stage`, `reg_q.write_data <= rs2;`!\\nSo `write_data` in EX/MEM register gets the LOAD'S MEMORY ADDRESS!\\nThen in Cycle 3 (LOAD in WB, STORE in MEM):\\n`store_data` bypass in `mem_stage`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  always_comb begin\\n    if (wb_w_en && wb_w_addr != 5'b0 && wb_w_addr == in.rs2_addr)\\n      store_data = wb_w_data;\\n    else\\n      store_data = in.write_data;\\n  end\\n```\\nWait, if LOAD is in WB, `wb_w_addr` is LOAD's `rd`, which matches `in.rs2_addr`!\\nSo `store_data` gets `wb_w_data`, which is the LOADED DATA!\\nThis works perfectly!\\n\\nBut what if the STORE was using `rs1` (the address)?\\nWait, `rs1_en = 1` for STORE. So if STORE's `rs1` matches LOAD's `rd`, it WILL STALL.\\nSo STORE only advances if the dependency is `rs2`.\\n\\nWait! What if there's an ADD instruction that writes `rd` = 22, and then a STORE that uses `rs2` = 22.\\nCycle 1: ADD in EX, STORE in ID.\\nDoes it stall? No, `id_ex_mem_read` is 0 for ADD. So no load-use stall anyway!\\nCycle 2: ADD in MEM, STORE in EX.\\n`fwd_rs2 = 2'd1`. `rs2` gets `fwd_ex_mem` (ADD's `alu_result`).\\n`reg_q.write_data` gets the ADD result!\\nCycle 3: ADD in WB, STORE in MEM.\\nBypass checks `wb_w_en && wb_w_addr == in.rs2_addr`.\\n`store_data` gets `wb_w_data` (which is ADD result).\\nSo it bypasses AGAIN! That's harmless.\\n\\nWait, what if there is an instruction between LOAD and STORE?\\nCycle 1: LOAD in EX. NOP in ID. (STORE is not in ID).\\nCycle 2: LOAD in MEM. NOP in EX. STORE in ID.\\nCycle 3: LOAD in WB. NOP in MEM. STORE in EX.\\nIn EX stage for STORE, `fwd_rs2` logic:\\n`ex_mem_w_en` is 0 (NOP in MEM).\\n`mem_wb_w_en` is 1 (LOAD in WB). `mem_wb_rd` matches `id_ex_rs2` (STORE's rs2).\\nSo `fwd_rs2 = 2'd2`!\\n`rs2` gets `fwd_mem_wb`. What is `fwd_mem_wb`?\\nIn `core.sv`:\\n```systemverilog\\n  ex_stage u_ex (\\n    .fwd_mem_wb      (wb_w_data),            // WB-stage's write-mux output\\n```\\nSo `rs2` gets `wb_w_data`, which is the LOADED DATA!\\nThen `reg_q.write_data <= rs2;` (STORE's EX/MEM register gets the LOADED DATA).\\nCycle 4: LOAD retires. NOP in WB. STORE in MEM.\\nIn MEM stage for STORE:\\n`wb_w_en` is 0 (NOP in WB).\\nSo `store_data = in.write_data`.\\nAnd `in.write_data` is the LOADED DATA (captured correctly in EX stage)!\\nThis works perfectly!\\n\\nWait! What if the dependency was:\\nCycle 1: LOAD in EX.\\nCycle 2: LOAD in MEM. STORE in EX.\\nWait, if LOAD is in MEM, it's not in WB!\\nWhat if there is a stall in DMEM?\\nIf DMEM stalls, `hold_mem_wb` is 1. The LOAD stays in MEM? No, LOAD is in MEM, but if DMEM stalls, `stall_ex_mem` is 1. The EX/MEM register holds.\\nWhat if LOAD is in WB, and DMEM stalls?\\nWait, DMEM is accessed in MEM stage. So LOAD is in MEM when DMEM is accessed!\\nIf DMEM stalls, LOAD stays in MEM! STORE stays in EX!\\nCycle 2: LOAD in MEM. STORE in EX.\\nDMEM stalls!\\nCycle 3: LOAD in MEM. STORE in EX.\\nCycle 4: DMEM finishes. LOAD goes to WB. STORE goes to MEM.\\nCycle 5: LOAD in WB? No, LOAD retires. STORE in MEM accesses DMEM.\\nWait, if LOAD goes to WB, STORE goes to MEM. In Cycle 4 (the cycle after DMEM finishes), STORE is in MEM, LOAD is in WB!\\nAnd the bypass `store_data = wb_w_data` will pick up the LOAD data!\\nSo the bypass works even with DMEM stalls!\\n\\nWait, why did `reg_ch0` fail in `hyp-20260514-002-r13s1`?\\nLet's look at the failing signal.\\n\\\"Value for anyconst rvfi_testbench.register_index: 22\\\".\\nRegister 22 was corrupted!\\nCould the store bypass corrupt a register?\\nNo, the store bypass only modifies `wdata_rep` which goes to `dmem_wdata`. It never goes to the register file!\\nSo the store bypass CANNOT cause `reg_ch0` to fail!\\n\\nWait. In `hyp-20260514-002-r13s1`, the user wrote:\\n```systemverilog\\n    case (opcode)\\n      7'b0110011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; rs2_en = 1'b1; end // R-type\\n      7'b0010011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // I-type\\n      7'b0000011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // LOAD\\n      7'b0100011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; end                // STORE\\n```\\nNotice they did not explicitly set `rs2_en = 1` for BRANCH!\\n`7'b1100011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; rs2_en = 1'b1; end // BRANCH`\\nIf they forgot BRANCH, `rs2_en = 0` for BRANCH!\\nIf a BRANCH depends on a LOAD, it wouldn't stall!\\nIf a BRANCH does not stall, it will enter EX while LOAD is in MEM.\\nIt will evaluate the branch condition using `rs2 = alu_result` of the LOAD (the memory address)!\\nIt might take the wrong branch!\\nIf it takes the wrong branch, it executes random code.\\nIf it executes random code, maybe it writes to register 22 incorrectly!\\nYES! That explains the failure! A wrong branch prediction causes it to execute an instruction that corrupts a register, or misses a register update.\\nIn fact, the `r13s1` prompt text from log:\\n```\\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\n    description: \\\"Replace if_id_rs1 and if_id_rs2 inputs with if_id_instr. Decode the opcode to compute rs1_en and rs2_en. Set rs2_en=0 for STOREs. Qualify load-use hazard logic with these enable signals.\\\"\\n```\\nIt says \\\"Decode the opcode...\\\". The AI likely forgot to set `rs2_en = 1` for BRANCH!\\nIn my explicit logic above, I have:\\n```systemverilog\\n      7'b1100011: begin rs1_en = 1'b1; rs2_en = 1'b1; end // BRANCH\\n```\\nThis is crucial! I must make sure the AI knows EXACTLY what opcodes set `rs1_en` and `rs2_en`.\\n\\nLet's double check if there's any other hazard I might be introducing with the ALU adder sharing.\\n`alu_a` and `alu_b` are muxed.\\nFor `is_jump && !is_jalr` (i.e. JAL):\\n`alu_a = in.pc`, `alu_b = in.imm`. `alu_result = in.pc + in.imm`.\\n`jump_target = alu_result`. Correct.\\nFor `is_jalr`:\\n`alu_a = rs1`, `alu_b = in.imm`. `alu_result = rs1 + in.imm`.\\n`jump_target = {alu_result[31:1], 1'b0}`. Correct.\\nFor `is_branch`:\\n`alu_a = in.pc`, `alu_b = in.imm`. `alu_result = in.pc + in.imm`.\\n`branch_target = alu_result`. Correct.\\n\\nWait! What if `in.ctrl.alu_src` is true for branches/jumps?\\nIn `decoder.sv`, for BRANCH:\\n`alu_src = 1'b0`.\\nBut my logic says:\\n```systemverilog\\n    if (in.ctrl.alu_src || in.ctrl.is_branch || in.ctrl.is_jump) begin\\n      alu_b = in.imm;\\n    end else begin\\n      alu_b = rs2;\\n    end\\n```\\nSo `alu_b` correctly gets `in.imm`.\\nThis logic is rock solid.\\n\\nLet me just write the hypothesis using the `write` tool.\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778770755020,\"end\":1778770962602}}}\nbench/gemini-3_1-pro/rep1/agent.log:492:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778757458798,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d9cfaa07ffeeiMUzJqoF3uaNk\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"tool_bash_wjCwM563Isno1QOUQJJD\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"description\":\"Write the hypothesis YAML file\",\"command\":\"cat << 'EOF' > experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260514-002-r3s1.yaml\\nid: hyp-20260514-002-r3s1\\ntitle: \\\"Static Branch/JAL Predictor in ID\\\"\\ncategory: structural\\nmotivation: |\\n  CoreMark execution is heavily dominated by loops (backward branches) and function calls (JAL).\\n  Currently, all branches and jumps resolve in EX, incurring a 2-cycle penalty. A static predictor\\n  in the ID stage can achieve a 0-cycle penalty for correctly predicted backward branches and JALs,\\n  significantly boosting IPC without impacting Fmax like complex dynamic predictors.\\nhypothesis: |\\n  We will predict JAL and backward branches (imm < 0) as Taken during the ID stage, immediately\\n  redirecting the IF stage. Forward branches and JALR will be predicted Not Taken. The EX stage\\n  will verify the prediction against the actual condition and redirect if a misprediction occurs.\\n  This structural change requires adding the prediction state to the ID/EX register and updating\\n  the redirect priority logic.\\nexpected_impact:\\n  fitness_delta_pct: 12\\n  confidence: high\\nchanges:\\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\\n    description: \\\"Add `predicted_taken` bit to `id_ex_t`.\\\"\\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\\n    description: \\\"Detect JAL and backward branches, compute target, and output `id_predict_taken`/`id_predict_target`. Guard prediction latching with `if_stalled` to prevent desync during bus stalls.\\\"\\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\\n    description: \\\"Accept ID prediction signals and route them to `next_pc` with lower priority than EX redirect.\\\"\\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\\n    description: \\\"Compute `mispredict` by comparing actual branch outcome with `predicted_taken`. Suppress EX redirect on correct predictions, and enforce `misalign_fault` corrections.\\\"\\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\\n    description: \\\"Wire `id_predict_taken`, `id_predict_target`, and `if_stalled` between ID, IF, and hazard unit.\\\"\\nEOF\"},\"output\":\"zsh:1: no such file or directory: experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260514-002-r3s1.yaml\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"zsh:1: no such file or directory: experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260514-002-r3s1.yaml\\n\",\"exit\":1,\"description\":\"Write the hypothesis YAML file\",\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"Write the hypothesis YAML file\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778757458792,\"end\":1778757458797}},\"metadata\":{\"openrouter\":{\"reasoning_details\":[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"**Proposing Structural Architectural Idea**\\n\\nI'm currently focusing on identifying a structural architectural hypothesis to enhance the `bench` core's `CoreMark iter/sec` performance. My current exploration centers around optimizing the existing 5-stage in-order pipeline (IF, ID, EX, MEM, WB).\\n\\n**Exploring Pipeline Enhancements**\\n\\nI'm investigating ways to improve the `bench` core's performance by refining the existing 5-stage pipeline. Specifically, I'm considering relocating branch resolution to the ID stage to shave off cycles on taken branches. I'm also evaluating the impact of pipelining the combinational multiplier, currently a bottleneck in the EX stage.\\n\\n**Optimizing Execution Units**\\n\\nI'm analyzing the critical path, particularly the combinational multiplier and dividers. My current direction is to transition the multiplier/divider into a multi-cycle execution unit. This structural change should significantly boost Fmax, even with potential minor IPC trade-offs, especially considering CoreMark's reliance on multiplication.\\n\\n**Improving Instruction Fetch**\\n\\nMy focus has shifted to instruction fetch stalls, which are currently impacting performance. I'm exploring the potential of a 1-entry prefetch buffer to mitigate bus stalls by decoupling the IF stage from instruction memory, aiming to hide latency.\\n\\n**Implementing Store Buffer Logic**\\n\\nI'm integrating a 1-entry store buffer into the memory stage to handle store operations more efficiently. This should mitigate bus stalls, especially given CoreMark's high store frequency, by allowing the pipeline to proceed while a store is pending and the bus is occupied.\\n\\n**Clarifying Formal and Cosim Interactions**\\n\\nI'm currently grappling with how a store buffer interacts with formal verification versus cycle-accurate simulation. Formal checks, it seems, are satisfied as long as the `rvfi` signals correctly reflect the state at retirement, regardless of the actual memory write timing. However, cycle-accurate simulation, specifically with a cosim setup, necessitates that loads correctly read the most up-to-date data, even from a buffered store, to maintain ISA semantics. This implies a forwarding mechanism from the store buffer to loads is essential.\\n\\n**Refining Branch Logic**\\n\\nI'm re-evaluating the idea of resolving branches in the ID stage. While initially appealing for performance gains by reducing taken branch penalties, the requirement to combinatorially forward ALU results from EX to the ID stage comparator introduces a critical path that is likely unsustainable in a 5-stage pipeline.\\n\\n**Resolving Branch Dependencies**\\n\\nI'm reconsidering resolving branches in the ID stage. It seems forwarding ALU results combinatorially to the ID comparator creates an unsustainable critical path, likely necessitating a stall anyway. This complexity appears to outweigh potential gains, especially given the possibility of branch dependencies.\\n\\n**Analyzing Structural Enhancements**\\n\\nI'm evaluating the feasibility of a two-stage instruction fetch (IF1 and IF2) to decouple instruction memory from the ID stage registers. This structural modification aims to mitigate stalls by providing a dedicated flop for the IF/ID payload, offering more flexibility in pipeline timing.\\n\\n**Analyzing Fetch Latency**\\n\\nMy current thoughts are on the BRAM synchronous read mechanism. I'm questioning how `imemData` is being supplied combinational if BRAMs have synchronous reads, potentially indicating a one-cycle latency that's being masked. This affects the critical path from PC clock to instruction decoding.\\n\\n**Investigating Fetch Data Timing**\\n\\nI'm questioning how instruction data reaches the pipeline. If BRAMs have synchronous reads, yielding data in the cycle after the address is presented, then the instruction fetched for the current PC is actually from the previous cycle. This timing mismatch could be the source of a hidden cycle of latency, impacting the critical path from PC update to instruction decode.\\n\\n**Investigating Fetch Data Mismatch**\\n\\nI've identified a critical mismatch: synchronous BRAM reads mean `imem_data` is from the previous PC. With PC updating every cycle, this creates a data-PC misalignment, suggesting the testbench might be using asynchronous LUTRAM, not synchronous BRAM as expected for the target FPGA.\\n\\n**Clarifying Pipeline Stages**\\n\\nI've realized the apparent \\\"5-stage\\\" pipeline is actually effectively 4 stages. The Instruction Fetch and Decode are combinatorially coupled, with the first flop being the ID/EX register. This means a significant portion of the core's logic operates without intermediate registers between IF and the EX stage's input.\\n\\n**Adding IF/ID Register**\\n\\nI've discovered my core isn't truly 5-stage; IF and ID are combinatorially linked. Inserting an IF/ID register is the next logical step. This will split IF and ID, significantly shortening the critical path and boosting Fmax, even if branch penalties increase slightly.\\n\\n**Inserting IF/ID Register**\\n\\nI've realized my pipeline is effectively four stages due to a combinatorial link between IF and ID. My next step is to insert an IF/ID register to break this dependency, which should improve Fmax by shortening the critical path. This also means the instruction data I'm seeing might not be from the current PC due to BRAM's synchronous nature.\\n\\n**Updating Hazard Unit**\\n\\nI'm now focusing on adapting the `hazard_unit` to correctly manage the newly inserted `IF/ID` flop. This involves implementing appropriate flushing and stalling mechanisms. The `redirect` logic also needs to be updated to ensure both the `IF/ID` and `ID/EX` registers are flushed when a branch is taken.\\n\\n**Refining Hardware Divider Logic**\\n\\nI'm re-examining the 32-bit combinational hardware divider as a significant Fmax bottleneck. I suspect that despite my expectations, synthesis tools are either optimizing it aggressively or the reported Fmax of 127 MHz isn't accurately reflecting the true critical path for this component on the Tang Nano 20K, especially if DSPs aren't being used for division.\\n\\n**Updating Hazard Unit Logic**\\n\\nI'm now focused on updating the hazard unit to correctly manage the newly inserted IF/ID flop. This requires implementing appropriate flushing and stalling mechanisms. The redirect logic must also be adjusted to ensure both the IF/ID and ID/EX registers are flushed when a branch is taken.\\n\\n**Buffering Against Stalls**\\n\\nI'm focusing on how a prefetch buffer can hide instruction memory stalls. By buffering instructions, I can keep the pipeline fed even when the instruction memory bus is backpressured, which is crucial for CoreMark's performance. This also prevents data memory stalls from unnecessarily halting instruction fetches.\\n\\n**Implementing Instruction Queue**\\n\\nI am implementing a 2-entry instruction queue in the `if_stage` to decouple instruction fetch from memory. The queue will buffer instructions, allowing the pipeline to continue while instruction memory is busy, effectively hiding fetch stalls and preventing data memory stalls from impacting instruction fetches.\\n\\n**Evaluating Branch Prediction Strategies**\\n\\nI'm exploring branch prediction as a structural enhancement. The previous attempt with a next-line predictor (BTB + 2-bit BHT) yielded no improvement, likely due to an Fmax drop on the Tang Nano 20K or an implementation issue. I need to revisit this to ensure correct IPC gains are achieved, or find a different approach.\\n\\n**Analyzing Execution Unit Pipelining**\\n\\nI'm considering pipelining the ALU and divider, or alternatively, implementing a serial divider. The serial approach would stall the EX stage for 32 cycles, potentially impacting IPC, although CoreMark's division usage might be limited. I need to quantify this trade-off.\\n\\n**Building Multi-Cycle Divider**\\n\\nI've realized CoreMark's division occurs outside the timed loop, making a multi-cycle divider ideal for boosting Fmax without hurting IPC. I'm now focused on implementing this in `alu.sv`, as the combinational divider is the main Fmax bottleneck on the Tang Nano 20K, unlike multipliers which Yosys maps to DSPs.\\n\\n**Implementing Clocked ALU Module**\\n\\nI've identified that adding a multi-cycle divider to `alu.sv` requires clock signals. This necessitates significant structural modifications to introduce clock, reset, start, and ready signals into `alu.sv` and propagate them through `ex_stage.sv` and the `hazard_unit`.\\n\\n**Adding ID Stage Hazard Detection**\\n\\nI'm now focused on implementing hazard detection within the ID stage itself to manage potential forwarding conflicts for branches. This involves creating logic to stall the ID stage if the source operands for a branch instruction are still being produced by an instruction in the EX stage, effectively handling data dependencies that arise from earlier pipeline stages.\\n\\n**Updating Hazard Unit Logic**\\n\\nI am currently updating the hazard unit to manage the new IF/ID flop, implementing flushing and stalling. The redirect logic is also being adjusted to flush IF/ID and ID/EX on taken branches. This involves creating logic to stall the ID stage for branch dependencies on registers produced in EX or MEM stages.\\n\\n**Resolving Dependency Issues**\\n\\nI'm analyzing back-to-back dependencies, specifically `addi` followed by `blt`. My current understanding is that forwarding ALU results from MEM to ID is necessary to avoid excessive stalls, preventing a 3-cycle penalty and keeping it at 2 cycles. If the producer is in ID/EX, a stall is unavoidable.\\n\\n**Analyzing Branch Resolution Impact**\\n\\nI'm re-evaluating the branch resolution timing. Moving it to the ID stage offers a mere 1-cycle advantage over EX resolution when instructions are directly back-to-back with their producer. If separated by one instruction, the benefit increases. The key is preventing stalls by forwarding ALU results from MEM to ID.\\n\\n**Implementing Store Buffer**\\n\\nI am implementing a 1-entry store buffer in the `mem_stage` to handle write operations. This buffer allows the pipeline to continue executing while a store operation is pending. However, it will stall the pipeline if another memory operation occurs while the buffer is full and the external memory is not ready, ensuring data integrity.\\n\\n**Addressing Store Buffer Conflicts**\\n\\nI'm clarifying how the store buffer interacts with loads and formal verification. While `rvfi` signals align with retirement, loads must access the most up-to-date data, requiring forwarding from the store buffer to prevent stale reads, especially if CoreMark creates such dependencies.\\n\\n**Predicting Branch Direction**\\n\\nI'm considering static branch prediction to mitigate branch penalties. By predicting backward branches as taken in the ID stage, I can redirect instruction fetch. This initially incurs a 1-cycle penalty for a correct prediction, but a 2-cycle penalty if the prediction is wrong and requires a second redirection in the EX stage.\\n\\n**Implementing Static Branch Prediction**\\n\\nI'm now implementing static branch prediction to reduce loop penalties. By predicting backward branches as taken in the ID stage, I aim to minimize misprediction costs and significantly improve performance for common loop constructs. This involves modifying the ID, IF, and hazard unit logic to handle the prediction and redirection.\\n\\n**Predicting Branch Behavior**\\n\\nI'm refining branch prediction by identifying that unconditional jumps like JAL can be predicted taken in the ID stage, reducing their penalty to a single cycle. This also simplifies the EX stage by removing the need for it to handle redirection for JAL instructions.\\n\\n**Refining Branch Redirection**\\n\\nI'm optimizing branch redirection by predicting unconditional jumps like `JAL` as taken in the ID stage. This reduces their penalty to a single cycle and streamlines the EX stage, eliminating its need to handle `JAL` redirection. My focus is now on ensuring the `hazard_unit` correctly manages the `flush_if` signal for these predicted branches.\\n\\n**Predicting Branch Targets**\\n\\nI'm refining branch prediction, particularly for unconditional jumps like `JAL`. By predicting these as taken in the ID stage, their penalty is reduced to one cycle, and the EX stage's redirection handling is simplified. The next step is ensuring the `hazard_unit` correctly manages the `flush_if` signal for these predicted branches.\\n\\n**Predicting Branch Behavior**\\n\\nI'm refining branch prediction by identifying that unconditional jumps like JAL can be predicted taken in the ID stage, reducing their penalty to a single cycle. This also simplifies the EX stage by removing the need for it to handle redirection for JAL instructions. My focus is now on ensuring the `hazard_unit` correctly manages the `flush_if` signal for these predicted branches.\\n\\n**Analyzing Branch Prediction Penalties**\\n\\nI'm analyzing the penalty of mispredicting a backward branch. My current understanding is that a correctly predicted backward branch incurs a 1-cycle penalty, while a misprediction results in a 2-cycle penalty. This changes the baseline of a 0-cycle penalty for a not-taken branch, making prediction beneficial.\\n\\n**Optimizing Branch Predictions**\\n\\nI've analyzed loop behavior, finding that static prediction of backward branches as taken significantly reduces total penalties. Even with a 2-cycle cost for mispredictions, the overall improvement is substantial compared to no prediction. I've verified that `rvfi` signals, specifically `pc_next`, remain correct irrespective of prediction outcomes. I'm now integrating this static prediction logic into the ID stage.\\n\\n**Refining Branch Prediction Implementation**\\n\\nI've integrated static branch prediction for unconditional jumps and backward branches directly into the ID stage. This change reduces penalties and simplifies redirection logic. I'm now updating the `hazard_unit` to correctly manage the `flush_if` signal based on these predictions, ensuring `rvfi` signals remain accurate even with mispredictions.\\n\\n**Prioritizing Redirect Logic**\\n\\nI'm refining the hazard unit's redirect logic. When the EX stage resolves a branch, its `redirect` signal takes precedence over any `id_redirect` prediction from the ID stage. This ensures that older, more definitive branch resolutions correctly flush the instruction fetch, preventing outdated predictions from causing issues.\\n\\n**Analyzing Hazard Unit Behavior**\\n\\nI'm examining how hazard detection interacts with branch prediction. Specifically, I'm questioning if `flush_id` is correctly handled when `id_redirect` is true but `stall_id` is also active due to a `load_use_hazard`. My current understanding is that `id_redirect` should only flush IF, not ID, as the branch instruction itself needs to proceed to EX.\\n\\n**Clarifying PC Behavior**\\n\\nI'm analyzing the interaction between `stall_if`, `flush_if`, and PC updates. My current understanding is that when `stall_if` is active, the PC doesn't update, but `flush_if` can still be asserted. This means the IF stage outputs a NOP while the PC remains pointing at the original instruction, leading to potential re-fetching upon stall clearance.\\n\\n**Analyzing PC Updates During Stalls**\\n\\nI'm clarifying PC behavior during stalls. My current understanding is that even if the PC holds during an `stall_if`, an asserted `flush_if` still causes the `if_stage` to output a NOP. This means when the stall clears, the PC will finally update to the `id_redirect_target`, but the previous cycle's NOP output from `if_stage` could lead to unexpected behavior if not handled correctly by subsequent stages.\\n\\n**Detecting Combinational Loop**\\n\\nI've discovered a critical combinational loop involving the instruction fetch and decode stages. My prediction logic for taken branches, when driving a NOP into the instruction stream, directly impacts the decoder's input in the same cycle due to the absence of an IF/ID register. This creates an unstable feedback path that needs immediate resolution by inserting the missing register.\\n\\n**Refining Fetch and Redirect Logic**\\n\\nI'm clarifying how predicted branches interact with the pipeline's instruction fetch and subsequent stages. Specifically, I've realized that when a branch is predicted as taken in the ID stage, and the branch instruction itself is the current one being processed, no flushing of the IF stage is required. The PC correctly updates to the predicted target in the next cycle, and the branch instruction proceeds to the ID/EX register as intended.\\n\\n**Clarifying PC Updates**\\n\\nI'm analyzing PC behavior during stalls and predictions. My current understanding is that when a branch is predicted taken in ID, and the branch instruction is current, no IF flush is needed. The PC correctly updates to the target, and the branch proceeds to ID/EX. This avoids unnecessary stalls and potential re-fetches.\\n\\n**Analyzing Branch Resolution Logic**\\n\\nI'm analyzing the branch resolution timing and penalties. A correctly predicted backward branch incurs a 1-cycle penalty, while a misprediction costs 2 cycles, which is a significant improvement over no prediction. I've verified `rvfi` signals remain correct and am integrating static prediction into the ID stage.\\n\\n**Analyzing PC Transitions**\\n\\nMy current focus is on PC transitions during stalls and branch predictions. I've realized that when a stall clears and a branch was predicted taken, the PC correctly updates to the branch target, effectively re-fetching the branch instruction until the stall clears. This ensures `rvfi` signals remain accurate.\\n\\n**Clarifying Redirect Priority**\\n\\nI'm analyzing the interaction between pipeline stalls and branch redirection. I've realized that explicit `redirect` signals from later stages, like EX, always take precedence over any predicted targets from earlier stages, such as the ID stage's `id_predict_target`. This ensures that definitive branch resolutions correctly control instruction fetching, even when stalls are active.\\n\\n**Analyzing PC Transitions**\\n\\nI've realized that when a stall clears and a branch was predicted taken, the PC correctly updates to the branch target. This means the PC will re-fetch the branch instruction if it's still stalled, ensuring `rvfi` signals remain accurate. The priority of EX stage redirects over ID predictions is also confirmed.\\n\\n**Analyzing Redirect Priority**\\n\\nI've clarified that explicit redirects from later pipeline stages, like the EX stage, always override predictions from earlier stages, such as the ID stage. This ensures that definitive branch resolutions correctly control instruction fetching, even when stalls are present. I've also identified a potential issue where `redirect` is forced to 0 if a `misalign_fault` occurs, preventing the IF stage from redirecting, which seems counterintuitive if the misaligned instruction is itself a branch. This requires further investigation into how formal verification handles such scenarios without a trap handler.\\n\\n**Clarifying Misaligned Fetch**\\n\\nI've identified a critical issue where a misaligned fault in the EX stage incorrectly sets `pc_next` to `in.pc + 4`. This means instruction fetch continues from the misaligned target, causing a failure in the `pc_fwd` check during formal verification if the instruction that trapped was a branch. I need to ensure `pc_next` is correctly set to the original instruction's aligned target when a fault occurs.\\n\\n**Fixing PC Alignment Faults**\\n\\nI've uncovered a critical PC alignment fault during misaligned instruction fetches that are also branches. My current fix ensures that when a misaligned fault occurs and a branch was predicted taken, the instruction fetch is correctly redirected to PC+4 to match formal verification expectations, preventing mismatches in `rvfi` signals.\\n\\n**Fixing PC Alignment Faults**\\n\\nI've uncovered a critical PC alignment fault during misaligned instruction fetches that are also branches. My current fix ensures that when a misaligned fault occurs and a branch was predicted taken, the instruction fetch is correctly redirected to PC+4 to match formal verification expectations, preventing mismatches in `rvfi` signals.\\n\\n**Refining Branch Target Logic**\\n\\nI've reviewed the branch target logic and confirmed it correctly handles taken branches by selecting `branch_target` when `branch_taken` is asserted. I've also identified necessary additions to the `id_ex_t` struct in `core_pkg.sv` to accommodate `predicted_taken` and `instr`, and added wiring for `id_predict_taken` and `id_predict_target` in `core.sv`.\\n\\n**Clarifying PC Updates**\\n\\nI've realized that when a stall clears and a branch was predicted taken, the PC correctly updates to the branch target. This means the PC will re-fetch the branch instruction if it's still stalled, ensuring `rvfi` signals remain accurate. The priority of EX stage redirects over ID predictions is also confirmed.\\n\\n**Refining Branch Prediction Logic**\\n\\nI've clarified PC behavior during stalls and predictions. When a stall clears and a branch was predicted taken, the PC correctly updates to the branch target. This ensures that the PC will re-fetch the branch instruction if it's still stalled, guaranteeing `rvfi` signals remain accurate. The priority of EX stage redirects over ID predictions is also confirmed.\\n\\n**Refining Branch Logic**\\n\\nI'm refining the branch prediction logic. I've noticed that the decoder's branch signals (`dec_is_jump`, `dec_is_jalr`, `dec_is_branch`) are crucial inputs to the prediction calculation in the ID stage. Additionally, the EX stage logic for `actual_taken`, `mispredict_branch`, and `mispredict_jump` is now more clearly defined, impacting how predictions are evaluated.\\n\\n**Fixing Branch Target Logic**\\n\\nI've identified and fixed an issue where the `misalign_fault` signal was incorrectly asserted. This fix ensures that the PC correctly updates to PC+4, aligning with formal verification expectations and preventing `rvfi` signal mismatches, especially when branches encounter misaligned faults.\\n\\n**Refining Branch Prediction Logic**\\n\\nI'm refining the branch prediction logic. I've noticed that the decoder's branch signals (`dec_is_jump`, `dec_is_jalr`, `dec_is_branch`) are crucial inputs to the prediction calculation in the ID stage. Additionally, the EX stage logic for `actual_taken`, `mispredict_branch`, and `mispredict_jump` is now more clearly defined, impacting how predictions are evaluated.\\n\\n**Refining Branch Logic**\\n\\nI'm refining the branch prediction logic. I've noticed that the decoder's branch signals (`dec_is_jump`, `dec_is_jalr`, `dec_is_branch`) are crucial inputs to the prediction calculation in the ID stage. Additionally, the EX stage logic for `actual_taken`, `mispredict_branch`, and `mispredict_jump` is now more clearly defined, impacting how predictions are evaluated.\\n\\n**Clarifying Redirect Behavior**\\n\\nI'm analyzing the interaction between mispredictions and instruction flushing. I've realized that asserting `flush_if` when `id_predict_taken` is true would cause the branch instruction to become a NOP, which is incorrect. Therefore, `id_predict_taken` should not trigger `flush_if`.\\n\\n**Refining Fetch and Redirect Logic**\\n\\nI've clarified PC behavior during stalls and predictions, ensuring the PC correctly updates to the branch target when a stall clears after a prediction. Explicit redirects from later stages, like EX, always override earlier predictions, guaranteeing accurate instruction fetching. I'm also refining the `hazard_unit` to ensure `flush_if` is not incorrectly asserted by ID predictions, preventing invalid NOPs from entering the pipeline.\\n\\n**Simplifying Fetch and Redirect**\\n\\nI've realized my initial approach to modifying the hazard unit for branch prediction was overly complex. The existing logic, when combined with the new prediction signals, correctly handles zero-bubble correct predictions and maintains the same misprediction penalties. The `id_predict_taken` signal now solely influences the `if_stage`'s `next_pc` calculation, simplifying the overall design and ensuring correctness.\\n\\n**Refining Prediction Logic**\\n\\nI've confirmed that explicit redirects from the EX stage correctly override any predictions from the ID stage, ensuring fetch accuracy. My recent work involves meticulously documenting the exact code changes needed to implement this priority, particularly for the `next_pc` calculation and the `always_ff` block.\\n\\n**Refining Prediction Priority**\\n\\nI've confirmed that explicit redirects from the EX stage correctly override any predictions from the ID stage, ensuring fetch accuracy. My recent work involves meticulously documenting the exact code changes needed to implement this priority, particularly for the `next_pc` calculation and the `always_ff` block.\\n\\n*