itions.\\n\\n**Refining Branch Prediction Logic**\\n\\nI've confirmed that EX stage redirects correctly override ID stage predictions for fetch accuracy. My focus is documenting code modifications for `next_pc` and the `always_ff` block. The ALU's branch resolution logic and target calculations are also being finalized, ensuring correct behavior for branch instructions and fault conditions.\\n\\n**Finalizing Branch Redirect Logic**\\n\\nI've confirmed that EX stage redirects correctly override ID stage predictions for fetch accuracy. My focus is documenting code modifications for `next_pc` and the `always_ff` block. The ALU's branch resolution logic and target calculations are also being finalized, ensuring correct behavior for branch instructions and fault conditions.\\n\\n**Clarifying PC Updates**\\n\\nI've realized that when a stall clears and a branch was predicted taken, the PC correctly updates to the branch target. This means the PC will re-fetch the branch instruction if it's still stalled, ensuring `rvfi` signals remain accurate. The priority of EX stage redirects over ID predictions is also confirmed.\\n\\n**Correcting Branch Misprediction Logic**\\n\\nI've uncovered a critical bug where a branch predicted as taken and then later confirmed as taken by the EX stage is not correctly redirecting instruction fetch when an `imem_stall` occurs. This leads to the PC updating to the wrong path after the stall clears, causing fetches from incorrect instructions. I'm implementing a fix to ensure the EX stage's `redirect` signal correctly flushes the IF stage, forcing it to re-fetch from the correct branch target even after a stall.\\n\\n**Fixing Branch Stall Mishandling**\\n\\nI've discovered a critical bug where a correctly taken branch isn't redirecting fetch after an `imem_stall`. This causes the PC to follow the not-taken path, breaking architectural correctness. I'm fixing it by ensuring the EX stage's `redirect` signal forces a flush and re-fetch from the correct target even after a stall.\\n\\n**Updating Branch Prediction Logic**\\n\\nI've identified a critical issue where branch predictions are incorrectly handled during instruction memory stalls. My current focus is on ensuring that when `stall_if` is active, the `predicted_taken` flag is correctly reset to `0` before being latched. This prevents the EX stage from misinterpreting a stalled prediction as a non-taken branch, guaranteeing correct redirection upon stall clearance and maintaining architectural fidelity.\\n\\n**Resetting Prediction on Stall**\\n\\nI've identified a crucial bug where branch predictions are lost during instruction memory stalls. My fix ensures that the `predicted_taken` flag is reset to `0` when `stall_if` is active. This prevents the EX stage from misinterpreting stalled predictions, guaranteeing correct branch redirection and maintaining architectural fidelity.\\n\\n**Clarifying Redirect Priority**\\n\\nI've confirmed that explicit redirects from the EX stage always override ID stage predictions for fetch accuracy. My focus is documenting code modifications for `next_pc` and the `always_ff` block. The ALU's branch resolution logic and target calculations are also being finalized, ensuring correct behavior for branch instructions and fault conditions.\\n\\n**Clarifying Stall and Redirect Interaction**\\n\\nI've realized that when `dmem_stall` is active, instruction fetch stalls, but the EX stage's `redirect` signal remains valid and can still assert `flush_if`. This causes the IF stage to output a NOP while the PC holds, and the ID/EX register retains the branch instruction. The IF PC will update to the `redirect_target` only after the stall clears. My `if_stalled` logic correctly handles this IF/ID decoupling.\\n\\n**Clarifying Stall and Redirect Interaction**\\n\\nI've realized that when instruction fetch stalls, the PC holds, but the EX stage's redirect signal remains valid. This allows it to assert `flush_if` even while the IF stage is stalled, correctly decoupling the stages and ensuring the PC updates to the redirect target once the stall clears, maintaining architectural fidelity.\\n\\n**Finalizing Redirect and Stall Logic**\\n\\nI've confirmed that EX stage redirects correctly override ID stage predictions for fetch accuracy. My focus is documenting code modifications for `next_pc` and the `always_ff` block. The ALU's branch resolution logic and target calculations are also being finalized, ensuring correct behavior for branch instructions and fault conditions.\\n\\n\",\"format\":\"google-gemini-v1\",\"index\":0},{\"type\":\"reasoning.encrypted\",\"data\":\" as already redirected before EX, plus any resolved next-PC metadata needed to suppress duplicate redirects later.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JALs in decode, compute `pc + imm`, and...\\n  Line 25: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r8s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Redirect landing-pad replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The existing `bench` predictor win came from the tiny sequential replay path,\\\\nwhile heavier control speculation, wider replay tables, and IF-side target\\\\ndecode all regressed. The remaining frontend blind spot is the first fetch\\\\nafter a taken redirect: hot loop headers and helper-call targets are revisited\\\\noften, but the current replay candidate is cleared exactly when those PCs\\\\nbecome useful again.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The existing `bench` predictor win came from the tiny sequential replay path,\\\\nwhile heavier control speculation, wider replay tables, and IF-side target\\\\ndecode all regressed. The remaining frontend blind spot is the first fetch\\\\nafter a taken redirect: hot loop headers and helper-call targets are revisited\\\\noften, but the current replay candidate is cleared exactly when those PCs\\\\nbecome useful again.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny redirect-target landing-pad cache inside `if_stage`, trained only\\\\nfrom successful post-redirect fetches. When EX redirects, remember the target\\\\nPC for one cycle; if the first fetch at that target later sees `io_imemReady=0`\\\\nand the landing-pad cache has a matching `{pc, instr}` entry, serve that\\\\ncached instruction instead of bubbling. Keep the existing sequential replay\\\\ntable unchanged for the common `pc+4` case so the new logic only touches the\\\\nnarrow post-redirect path, and let the normal replay candidate take over again\\\\nafter the landing-pad instruction issues. Compared with the prior redirect-\\\\ntarget replay attempt, this avoids an always-on lookup into the general replay\\\\ntable and removes dependence on that table still holding the target line,\\\\nwhich should make the control-side replay win cheaper in both timing and\\\\nrouting.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a tiny redirect-tar...\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve canonical RET in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing ID redirect path to cover canonical aligned returns,\\\\ne.g. `jalr x0, x1, 0` and `jalr x0, x5, 0`, when the return-address\\\\nregister is already architecturally available from the regfile/WB path. If\\\\nan older in-flight instruction still owns that source register, simply fall\\\\nback to the current EX-stage JALR path instead of adding the full ID-stage\\\\nbypass/interlock machinery that regressed earlier. This reuses the existing\\\\n`id_redirect` plumbing, carries an \\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through\\\\nthe pipeline so EX suppresses the duplicate redirect, and should cut\\\\nanother hot function-return bubble in CoreMark with much lower timing risk\\\\nthan general early JALR/BRANCH resolution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the existing `jal_early_redirect` pipeline bit into an ID-resolved jump marker that can cover both direct `JAL` and narrow early-return `JALR` cases.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"descr...\\n  Line 28: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Canonical return-address stack predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` already found its best predictor win in the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay path, and the recent structural JAL improvement removed the cheapest\\\\nunconditional jump bubble. The remaining hot control penalty is canonical\\\\nfunction return traffic: `ret`-shaped `JALR` instructions still wait for EX,\\\\nwhile broader IF-side branch prediction, wider replay tables, and redirect\\\\nlanding-pad schemes all failed to repay their timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` already found its best predictor win in the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay path, and the recent structural JAL improvement removed the cheapest\\\\nunconditional jump bubble. The remaining hot control penalty is canonical\\\\nfunction return traffic: `ret`-shaped `JALR` instructions still wait for EX,\\\\nwhile broader IF-side branch prediction, wider replay tables, and redirect\\\\nlanding-pad schemes all failed to repay their timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a very small return-address stack that predicts only the common ABI call\\\\nand return pattern instead of general branch targets. Push `pc+4` for direct\\\\ncalls that write `x1`, use the top entry to early-redirect only canonical\\\\n`ret`/`jalr x0, 0(x1)` in ID, and carry the predicted target forward so EX\\\\ncan suppress the duplicate redirect on a hit or repair the path on a rare\\\\nmismatch. This stays much narrower than the failed branch/JAL predictors: it\\\\ndoes not widen the replay table, does not add BTB-style target RAM, and only\\\\nadds prediction metadata to the small subset of return instructions. CoreMark\\\\nis call/return heavy enough that shaving the return bubble should improve IPC\\\\nwhile keeping Fmax risk materially lower than earlier full-control schemes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the ID/EX bundle with narrow early...\\n  Line 30: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drain-and-replace posted store slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing single posted-store slot, but let it dequeue the older\\\\nbuffered store to dmem and capture one younger postable store in the same\\\\ncycle. In practice this means teaching `mem_stage` to drain and replace the\\\\nslot when `dmem_ready` comes back, and teaching `hazard_unit` not to stall\\\\nan EX/MEM store that can retire into the just-freed slot while still keeping\\\\nyounger loads and non-postable stores conservative. This should recover some\\\\nof the store-burst benefit the two-entry FIFO targeted, without adding\\\\nanother queue entry, alias-compare logic, or front-end timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Allow the one-entry posted store buffer to drain an older buffered RAM store and capture a younger postable store in the same cycle, ...\\n  Line 31: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r10s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Seed replay from ID-resolved JAL targets\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest `bench` predictor result is still the tiny registered replay\\\\ntable, and predictor attempts that widened IF control, added target decode,\\\\nor carried prediction metadata into later stages consistently lost fitness.\\\\nAfter the recent ID-stage `JAL` win, the next control-side blind spot is the\\\\nvery first fetch at a direct-call target: the target PC is now known exactly\\\\none cycle early, but `if_stage` still drops replay state on `id_redirect`, so\\\\na warmed callee entry point bubbles whenever `io_imemReady=0` on that first\\\\ntarget cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest `bench` predictor result is still the tiny registered replay\\\\ntable, and predictor attempts that widened IF control, added target decode,\\\\nor carried prediction metadata into later stages consistently lost fitness.\\\\nAfter the recent ID-stage `JAL` win, the next control-side blind spot is the\\\\nvery first fetch at a direct-call target: the target PC is now known exactly\\\\none cycle early, but `if_stage` still drops replay state on `id_redirect`, so\\\\na warmed callee entry point bubbles whenever `io_imemReady=0` on that first\\\\ntarget cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the existing `{pc,instr}` replay table to seed the next-cycle replay\\\\ncandidate from an exact `id_redirect_target` when decode resolves a legal\\\\naligned `JAL`. On a live ID redirect, `if_stage` would probe the existing\\\\nreplay table with the JAL target and, on a hit, load `replay_valid_q`,\\\\n`replay_pc_q`, and `replay_instr_q` for the upcoming target fetch instead of\\\\nunconditionally clearing them. Keep EX-stage redirect handling, sequential\\\\nreplay fill, and `fetch_available = imem_ready || replay_hit` unchanged, so\\\\nthis stays in the proven replay-family design space: no IF-side immediate\\\\ndecode, no new predicted-PC metadata through ID/EX, and no broader branch or\\\\nreturn speculation. The pay...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":16,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"lookahead|prefetch|queue|replay_hit|candidate|pc\\\\+4|sequential replay|skid|consume replay\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778829667636,\"end\":1778829667652}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0064979cee843ad3016a06c9637958819b9b6383cc3f5cfb5d\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2a82ad2e0018wPnAthd4443Cf\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d57fcb60ffe5aBU7P0ONDjhJZ\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2a807498001hvAbRjMrugpYgR\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:922:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778831089968,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d56809b4ffeXcDEq9kevYdM7a\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_YerMOve0CEVvDPpufHt1UrQI\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":260},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Moving DIV/REM into a multicycle EX micro-unit is not a local ALU swap; if stall/freeze logic does not hold the full EX instruction context and suppress side effects until completion, riscv-formal will start failing even simple ADD/SUB/reg checks from corrupted EX/MEM retirement state.\\n2: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r1s1 (regression, -3.89%): A tiny sequential fetch queue is not enough by itself to beat random `io_imemReady` stalls on this front end; with only 1-instruction-per-cycle fetch bandwidth and frequent redirect flushes, the queue rarely gets far enough ahead of decode to repay its extra control.\\n3: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r1s2 (regression, -1.94%): On `bench`, a zero-table IF predictor for backward B-type branches and direct JALs still loses overall fitness when it adds immediate-decode, target-add, and `predicted_taken`/`predicted_target` bookkeeping to the fetch path, because the front-end cost outweighs the CoreMark loop/call bubbles it removes.\\n4: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r2s0 (regression, +7.86%): On `bench`, moving cold DIV/REM ops off the ALU can still lose when the precise fix adds hot-path interlock machinery like `ex_long_busy`, full EX-context shadow registers, and EX/MEM bubble control, because CoreMark rarely executes divides and does not repay the added control cost.\\n5: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r2s1 (broken, n/a): Stage-aware load/store interlocks must move the architectural operand snapshot with the true consumer stage too; if STORE `rs2` is satisfied by a late WB->MEM bypass but retirement still reports the old EX-captured `rs2_val`, riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs2_rdata` on load->store pairs.\\n6: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r2s2 (improvement, +11.75%): On `bench`, a registered `{pc,instr}` replay table that banks the next `pc+4` hit can hide random `io_imemReady` stalls better than a plain fetch queue or decode-time branch predictor, because CoreMark revisits a small hot sequential PC set and the replay path stays off the IF critical path.\\n7: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r3s0 (improvement, +5.14%): On `bench`, a MEM-to-EX load bypass is worth more than a mandatory load-use bubble when `io_dmemReady` commonly returns zero-wait data and CoreMark presents many short load-consumer chains, provided the forwarded value still flows through the normal EX path so RVFI operand snapshots stay aligned.\\n8: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): Exact `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2_*` load-use gating must retime RVFI source snapshots to the operand's true consumer stage for both sources, not just STORE data, or riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs1_rdata`/`rvfi_rs2_rdata` when forwarding changes the architecturally consumed value.\\n9: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r3s2 (broken, n/a): Extending the successful replay table into a learned next-PC predictor can exhaust `riscv-formal`'s wall-clock budget even without IF-side decode, because threading full `predicted_next_pc`/`predicted_valid` metadata and per-entry successor state through IF/ID/EX forces every instruction proof to reason about extra 32-bit control-flow state and EX mispredict comparisons.\\n10: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.78%): On `bench`, parallel `mul_ss`/`mul_uu`/`mul_su` datapaths are wasted hot-path area for RV32M; collapsing `MUL*` onto one sign-selectable 33x33 product preserves single-cycle behavior while reducing ALU routing pressure enough to lift Tang Nano 20K fitness.\\n11: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (regression, -59.23%): On `bench`, exact ID-stage BRANCH/JAL/JALR resolution is too timing-expensive; pulling compare/target-add plus EX/MEM/MEM/WB operand-bypass and load-interlock logic into decode collapsed Fmax to 52 MHz, so CoreMark's taken-control bubbles are cheaper than widening the front-end critical path.\\n12: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (regression, +0.61%): On `bench`, replaying warmed EX `redirect_target` instructions from the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` table is too narrow a win to pay for extra redirect-hit lookup/arming logic, because it only helps when `io_imemReady` stalls on the first post-redirect cycle while the original `pc+4` replay already captures the hotter sequential locality.\\n13: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r5s0 (regression, +9.14%): On `bench`, replacing the shared sign-selectable 33x33 `MUL*` datapath with a 32x32 product plus `MULH`/`MULHSU` sign-correction subtractors loses on Tang Nano 20K, because the extra 32-bit correction/mux logic on the ALU hot path costs more than carrying the multiplier sign bit through the DSP.\\n14: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r5s1 (improvement, +12.75%): On `bench`, a one-entry posted store buffer that only posts main-memory stores and blocks only younger memory ops turns random `io_dmemReady` store backpressure into background drain time, recovering CoreMark IPC without paying the IF/ID timing cost of front-end control changes.\\n15: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r5s2 (regression, +0.93%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table already has enough capacity for CoreMark's hot sequential PCs; adding 2-way set associativity with dual tag checks, way select, and MRU replacement costs more than the low-bit conflict misses it removes.\\n16: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r6s0 (improvement, +3.63%): On `bench`, carving ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and load/store address generation onto a dedicated EX carry-chain path pays because CoreMark is dominated by simple add/address ops, so keeping them out of the ALU's wide logic/shift/RV32M mux and fanout improves timing without front-end control changes.\\n17: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r6s1 (regression, -11.69%): On `bench`, extending the posted store buffer with MEM-stage aligned-address/byte-mask overlap checks, load forwarding, and load-vs-drain arbitration lost overall fitness because the extra compare/mask/mux logic sat on the hot memory path, dropping Fmax from 151.3 MHz to 133.4 MHz while CoreMark did not expose enough independent load-behind-store overlap to pay it back.\\n18: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r6s2 (regression, +1.63%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table should stay a single sequential `pc+4` candidate; adding a second JAL/backward-branch control-target slot only helps on rare first-post-redirect `io_imemReady` stalls, while the extra immediate-decode, target-add, and second replay-hit path in `if_stage` costs more than it saves.\\n19: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r7s0 (regression, -19.89%): On `bench`, the profitable EX split stops at the add/address fast path; adding op-class gating like `use_alu_result`, zeroed ALU operands for BRANCH/JAL/JALR, and `is_m_op ? a/b : 0` around the shared RV32M cone puts extra decode/mux logic back on the hot EX path and drops Fmax far more than the cold-cone isolation helps.\\n20: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r7s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On `bench`, the posted-store win appears depth-sensitive on Tang Nano 20K: growing the one-entry main-memory store buffer into a two-entry in-order FIFO with `store_buf_full` backpressure and queued-drain muxing pushed MEM/backpressure routing past place-and-route limits before any CoreMark gain could be measured.\\n21: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r7s2 (regression, +0.00%): On `bench`, low-bit conflict misses in the 8-entry sequential `{pc,instr}` replay table are not a meaningful limiter; even a one-entry fully-associative victim spill/check in `if_stage` failed to beat the plain direct-mapped design, so extra replay-hit selection state is already past CoreMark's payoff point.\\n22: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r8s0 (regression, -14.36%): On `bench`, peeling aligned `LW`/`SW` onto a dedicated MEM fast path is not worthwhile when the generic width logic already makes word accesses the default identity/`4'b1111` case; hoisting `mem_mask`/store formatting into EX and threading `mem_word_op` through the pipe adds ALU-result fanout and stage metadata without enough hot-path relief to repay it.\\n23: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r8s1 (improvement, +31.31%): On `bench`, decode-stage control fixes should isolate the cheapest unconditional case: resolving legal aligned `JAL` in ID and carrying a `jal_early_redirect` bit to suppress the duplicate EX redirect removes CoreMark's hot direct-call/jump bubble without dragging BRANCH/JALR compare, bypass, and load-interlock logic onto the front-end critical path.\\n24: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r8s2 (regression, -4.11%): On `bench`, redirect-target replay is opportunity-limited rather than lookup-limited; even a separate one-cycle landing-pad `{pc,instr}` cache in `if_stage` only pays on repeated redirects whose very first fetch later stalls on `io_imemReady=0`, which is rarer than the locality already captured by the sequential `pc+4` replay.\\n25: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r9s0 (regression, -11.24%): On `bench`, precomputing and registering MEM/WB `wb_data` is not a free timing win if the change merely shifts that value's fanout into same-cycle WB-to-regfile/ID read-bypass compare+mux logic; backend writeback cleanup must reduce total hot-path routing, not just move the `mem_to_reg` select earlier.\\n26: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r9s1 (regression, -13.86%): On `bench`, decode-time redirect wins stop at plain `JAL`; even a narrow canonical `ret` fast path (`jalr x0, x1/x5, 0`) with `rs1_busy` fallback adds enough decode-side dependency compare and target-select logic to cost more Fmax than CoreMark's return bubbles repay.\\n27: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r9s2 (regression, -64.82%): On `bench`, the canonical-return loss is not just an `rs1_busy` decode hazard: even a tiny 4-entry `ras_stack` that predicts only `JAL x1`/`ret` and carries `early_redirect_target` into EX for repair adds enough speculative call/return bookkeeping to lose more Fmax than CoreMark's return bubbles repay.\\n28: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r10s0 (broken, n/a): On `bench`, the `reg_file` write-first/WB-to-ID same-cycle read path is part of the backend correctness contract, not free timing junk; moving that bypass behind decode-gated `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` logic can miss real dependencies and break selftest.\\n29: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r10s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On `bench`, the posted-store budget is not just buffer depth: feeding `dmem_ready` into same-cycle drain-and-replace control in both `mem_stage` and `hazard_unit` was enough to blow Tang Nano 20K placement, so future store-burst work must avoid combinational refill/backpressure feedback.\\n30: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r10s2 (regression, -35.73%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table should stay dedicated to the sequential `pc+4` stream; repointing `replay_valid_q`/`replay_pc_q` at an exact ID-resolved `JAL` target burns the hotter fallthrough reuse and adds redirect-target selection to `if_stage` for a first-target-stall corner CoreMark rarely needs.\\n31: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r11s0 (broken, n/a): On `bench`, unused-source cleanup must not rewrite the shared `id_stage` `rs*_addr`/regfile-read path, because this repo's cosim/RVFI contract snapshots raw instruction `rs1`/`rs2` fields and pre-write register contents even for operands the ISA ignores, so `uses_rs*` normalization to `x0` needs a separate trace view or breaks selftest.\\n32: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r11s2 (regression, -51.23%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table only pays when a hit is pre-banked into the one-cycle replay candidate; adding same-cycle current-PC tag-compare and fallback muxing to salvage warm entries after flush/redirect clears puts too much logic back on the fetch critical path for a rare stall corner.\\n\\n(End of file - total 32 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Moving DIV/REM into a multicycle EX micro-unit is not a local ALU swap; if stall/freeze logic does not hold the full EX instruction context and suppress side effects until completion, riscv-formal will start failing even simple ADD/SUB/reg checks from corrupted EX/MEM retirement state.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r1s1 (regression, -3.89%): A tiny sequential fetch queue is not enough by itself to beat random `io_imemReady` stalls on this front end; with only 1-instruction-per-cycle fetch bandwidth and frequent redirect flushes, the queue rarely gets far enough ahead of decode to repay its extra control.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r1s2 (regression, -1.94%): On `bench`, a zero-table IF predictor for backward B-type branches and direct JALs still loses overall fitness when it adds immediate-decode, target-add, and `predicted_taken`/`predicted_target` bookkeeping to the fetch path, because the front-end cost outweighs the CoreMark loop/call bubbles it removes.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r2s0 (regression, +7.86%): On `bench`, moving cold DIV/REM ops off the ALU can still lose when the precise fix adds hot-path interlock machinery like `ex_long_busy`, full EX-context shadow registers, and EX/MEM bubble control, because CoreMark rarely executes divides and does not repay the added control cost.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r2s1 (broken, n/a): Stage-aware load/store interlocks must move the architectural operand snapshot with the true consumer stage too; if STORE `rs2` is satisfied by a late WB->MEM bypass but retirement still reports the old EX-captured `rs2_val`, riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs2_rdata` on load->store pairs.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r2s2 (improvement, +11.75%): On `bench`, a registered `{pc,instr}` replay table that banks the next `pc+4` hit can hide random `io_imemReady` stalls better than a plain fetch queue or decode-time branch predictor, because CoreMark revisits a small hot sequential PC set and the replay path stays off the IF critical path.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r3s0 (improvement, +5.14%): On `bench`, a MEM-to-EX load bypass is worth more than a mandatory load-use bubble when `io_dmemReady` commonly returns zero-wait data and CoreMark presents many short load-consumer chains, provided the forwarded value still flows through the normal EX path so RVFI operand snapshots stay aligned.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): Exact `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2_*` load-use gating must retime RVFI source snapshots to the operand's true consumer stage for both sources, not just STORE data, or riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs1_rdata`/`rvfi_rs2_rdata` when forwarding changes the architecturally consumed value.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r3s2 (broken, n/a): Extending the successful replay table into a learned next-PC predictor can exhaust `riscv-formal`'s wall-clock budget even without IF-side decode, because threading full `predicted_next_pc`/`predicted_valid` metadata and per-entry successor state through IF/ID/EX forces every instruction proof to reason about extra 32-bit control-flow state and EX mispredict comparisons.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.78%): On `bench`, parallel `mul_ss`/`mul_uu`/`mul_su` datapaths are wasted hot-path area for RV32M; collapsing `MUL*` onto one sign-selectable 33x33 product preserves single-cycle behavior while reducing ALU routing pressure enough to lift Tang Nano 20K fitness.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (regression, -59.23%): On `bench`, exact ID-stage BRANCH/JAL/JALR resolution is too timing-expensive; pulling compare/target-add plus EX/MEM/MEM/WB operand-bypass and load-interlock logic into decode collapsed Fmax to 52 MHz, so CoreMark's taken-control bubbles are cheaper than widening the front-end critical path.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (regression, +0.61%): On `bench`, replaying warmed EX `redirect_target` instructions from the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` table is too narrow a win to pay for extra redirect-hit lookup/arming logic, because it only helps when `io_imemReady` stalls on the first post-redirect cycle while the original `pc+4` replay already captures the hotter sequential locality.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r5s0 (regression, +9.14%): On `bench`, replacing the shared sign-selectable 33x33 `MUL*` datapath with a 32x32 product plus `MULH`/`MULHSU` sign-correction subtractors loses on Tang Nano 20K, because the extra 32-bit correction/mux logic on the ALU hot path costs more than carrying the multiplier sign bit through the DSP.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r5s1 (improvement, +12.75%): On `bench`, a one-entry posted store buffer that only posts main-memory stores and blocks only younger memory ops turns random `io_dmemReady` store backpressure into background drain time, recovering CoreMark IPC without paying the IF/ID timing cost of front-end control changes.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r5s2 (regression, +0.93%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table already has enough capacity for CoreMark's hot sequential PCs; adding 2-way set associativity with dual tag checks, way select, and MRU replacement costs more than the low-bit conflict misses it removes.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r6s0 (improvement, +3.63%): On `bench`, carving ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and load/store address generation onto a dedicated EX carry-chain path pays because CoreMark is dominated by simple add/address ops, so keeping them out of the ALU's wide logic/shift/RV32M mux and fanout improves timing without front-end control changes.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r6s1 (regression, -11.69%): On `bench`, extending the posted store buffer with MEM-stage aligned-address/byte-mask overlap checks, load forwarding, and load-vs-drain arbitration lost overall fitness because the extra compare/mask/mux logic sat on the hot memory path, dropping Fmax from 151.3 MHz to 133.4 MHz while CoreMark did not expose enough independent load-behind-store overlap to pay it back.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r6s2 (regression, +1.63%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table should stay a single sequential `pc+4` candidate; adding a second JAL/backward-branch control-target slot only helps on rare first-post-redirect `io_imemReady` stalls, while the extra immediate-decode, target-add, and second replay-hit path in `if_stage` costs more than it saves.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r7s0 (regression, -19.89%): On `bench`, the profitable EX split stops at the add/address fast path; adding op-class gating like `use_alu_result`, zeroed ALU operands for BRANCH/JAL/JALR, and `is_m_op ? a/b : 0` around the shared RV32M cone puts extra decode/mux logic back on the hot EX path and drops Fmax far more than the cold-cone isolation helps.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r7s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On `bench`, the posted-store win appears depth-sensitive on Tang Nano 20K: growing the one-entry main-memory store buffer into a two-entry in-order FIFO with `store_buf_full` backpressure and queued-drain muxing pushed MEM/backpressure routing past place-and-route limits before any CoreMark gain could be measured.\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[]},\"title\":\"cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778831089958,\"end\":1778831089967}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_002158f8e59d6cd7016a06cef1dda8819ba98b8c09daeaffbb\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2a986123001mvlPcHsAIvM3rE\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d56809b4ffeXcDEq9kevYdM7a\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2a983853001yTruenG9SNCZxJ\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:923:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778831089991,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d56809b4ffeXcDEq9kevYdM7a\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_uNUxRppakDTvhKX9hEZsXCGc\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 11 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 3: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Add a small redirect-aware fetch queue\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front-end is tightly coupled to `io_imemReady`: any cycle with\\\\ninstruction-memory backpressure immediately becomes a decode bubble because\\\\nIF emits a NOP and the PC stops. That is especially expensive here because\\\\nthe fitness run injects random iStalls by design, so front-end elasticity is\\\\nworth more than a tiny local logic cleanup.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front-end is tightly coupled to `io_imemReady`: any cycle with\\\\ninstruction-memory backpressure immediately becomes a decode bubble because\\\\nIF emits a NOP and the PC stops. That is especially expensive here because\\\\nthe fitness run injects random iStalls by design, so front-end elasticity is\\\\nworth more than a tiny local logic cleanup.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single-slot combinational fetch path with a small speculative\\\\nfetch queue in `if_stage`, likely 2-4 entries deep, that buffers `{pc,\\\\ninstr}` pairs when `io_imemReady` is high and feeds ID from the queue when\\\\nthe backend is ready. On redirects, flush younger queued instructions and\\\\nrestart fetch at the resolved target; on load-use or dmem stalls, preserve\\\\nthe buffered instructions instead of re-fetching them. This is a structural\\\\nfront-end change, not a predictor: it should raise CoreMark iterations/cycle\\\\nunder the orchestrator's random stall model with only modest area/timing\\\\ncost and without changing the single-retire RVFI contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Refactor IF into a small prefetch FIFO/skid-buffer front-end with redirect-aware invalidation and queue-based instruction delivery.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Change front-end stall logic to depend on fetch-queue availability plus backend hazards instead of bubbling immediately on every `!imemReady`.\\\"}, {\\\"...\\n  Line 6: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Operand-Stage-Aware Load Interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current hazard unit stalls on any raw `rs1`/`rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not actually read that field yet\\\\nor only needs it later as store data in MEM. That conservative policy burns\\\\navoidable one-cycle bubbles in CoreMark's dense load/address/store code, and\\\\nthe recent `bench` regressions already showed that extra front-end machinery\\\\nis a worse trade here than tightening the backend interlock.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current hazard unit stalls on any raw `rs1`/`rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not actually read that field yet\\\\nor only needs it later as store data in MEM. That conservative policy burns\\\\navoidable one-cycle bubbles in CoreMark's dense load/address/store code, and\\\\nthe recent `bench` regressions already showed that extra front-end machinery\\\\nis a worse trade here than tightening the backend interlock.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework the load-use interlock around the stage where an operand is actually\\\\nconsumed. Add decode metadata for real `rs1`/`rs2` usage, treat STORE `rs2`\\\\nas a late MEM-stage consumer instead of an EX-stage consumer, and add a\\\\nWB-to-MEM store-data bypass so `load -> store` pairs with an independent\\\\naddress can proceed without the textbook bubble. This should recover\\\\nCoreMark IPC from both false-positive load stalls and true-but-late\\\\nstore-data dependencies, while keeping load data off the EX ALU/branch path\\\\nand avoiding another fetch-path timing penalty.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add decoded source-usage and late-store-data control bits to the pipeline bundles so hazards can distinguish EX-stage operands from MEM-stage-only store data.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/decoder.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Classify which legal ins...\\n  Line 9: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Exact load-use interlock with late store-data bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The fetch side already has a winning replay mechanism, so the strongest\\\\nremaining structural gain is more likely in the backend. `hazard_unit.sv`\\\\nstill stalls on any raw `if_id_rs1` or `if_id_rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not consume that field in EX or\\\\nonly needs store data later in MEM. CoreMark's dense mix of loads,\\\\nimmediate arithmetic, address generation, and stores makes those\\\\nconservative one-cycle bubbles expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The fetch side already has a winning replay mechanism, so the strongest\\\\nremaining structural gain is more likely in the backend. `hazard_unit.sv`\\\\nstill stalls on any raw `if_id_rs1` or `if_id_rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not consume that field in EX or\\\\nonly needs store data later in MEM. CoreMark's dense mix of loads,\\\\nimmediate arithmetic, address generation, and stores makes those\\\\nconservative one-cycle bubbles expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework the load-use interlock around true operand-consumption timing instead\\\\nof raw register-field matches. Decode exact legal `rs1` and `rs2` usage for\\\\neach instruction class, keep the current conservative stall policy for\\\\nillegal or otherwise unclassified encodings, and treat STORE `rs2` as a\\\\nMEM-stage consumer with a dedicated WB-to-MEM store-data bypass. To avoid\\\\nthe prior broken attempt's RVFI bug, carry the final architecturally\\\\nconsumed store-data value into retirement so `rvfi_rs2_rdata`,\\\\n`rvfi_mem_wdata`, and the actual dmem write all agree. This should remove\\\\nfalse bubbles from OP-IMM/LOAD/JALR/LUI/AUIPC/FENCE paths and eliminate the\\\\nmandatory `load -> store(rs2)` stall without putting load data on the EX\\\\nALU, branch-compare, or JALR critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"...\\n  Line 12: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"ID-stage exact control resolution\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best low-risk fetch and\\\\nload-use gains: it replays hot sequential instructions in IF and forwards\\\\nzero-wait loads from MEM to EX. The remaining visible IPC loss is that every\\\\ntaken BRANCH, JAL, and JALR still resolves in EX after the ID/EX register,\\\\nwhich forces extra wrong-path bubbles on CoreMark's hot loop backedges and\\\\nhelper calls.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best low-risk fetch and\\\\nload-use gains: it replays hot sequential instructions in IF and forwards\\\\nzero-wait loads from MEM to EX. The remaining visible IPC loss is that every\\\\ntaken BRANCH, JAL, and JALR still resolves in EX after the ID/EX register,\\\\nwhich forces extra wrong-path bubbles on CoreMark's hot loop backedges and\\\\nhelper calls.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move exact control-flow resolution one stage earlier by adding a dedicated\\\\nID-stage branch/jump unit. JAL can redirect immediately from `pc+imm`; BRANCH\\\\nand JALR should compare/add in ID using the regfile value plus targeted\\\\nbypasses from EX/MEM and MEM/WB, while conservatively stalling only when an\\\\noperand is still unavailable from an in-flight load. Carry the final\\\\nforwarded source values into the pipeline state so RVFI operand snapshots stay\\\\naligned with the values the control instruction actually consumed, and mark\\\\nID-resolved control ops so EX does not issue a duplicate redirect a cycle\\\\nlater. This keeps the existing single-retire contract and replay frontend,\\\\nbut should cut the taken-control penalty by roughly one cycle without adding\\\\nIF-side predictor state or target-table complexity.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the pipeline bundles with control-resolution metadata so ID-resolved BRANCH/JAL/JALR instru...\\n  Line 15: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-entry posted store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry posted-store buffer that captures a store's aligned address,\\\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible metadata when the store\\\\nreaches MEM. If the external dmem port stalls, retire the store from the buffer\\\\nimmediately, keep draining the buffered write in the background, and allow\\\\nyounger non-memory instructions to keep flowing. Only stall when a younger\\\\nload or store reaches MEM while the buffer is still occupied, preserving\\\\nin-order memory semantics without reintroducing load-use or control-path\\\\ncomplexity. This should recover CoreMark IPC from random dmem backpressure on\\\\nstore-heavy regions while avoiding the IF/ID critical-path regressions seen in\\\\nearlier structural experiments.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the MEM-stage/retirement bundles with store-buffer bookkeepin...\\n  Line 18: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Alias-aware load bypass around posted stores\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing posted store buffer, but make it address-aware instead of\\\\nblocking all younger memory ops. When a younger load reaches MEM while a\\\\nmain-memory store is buffered, compare the load's aligned word address and\\\\nbyte mask against the buffered store: if there is no overlap, let the load use\\\\nthe dmem port immediately and drain the buffered store later; if the load is\\\\nfully covered by the buffered bytes, forward from the buffer; only stall on\\\\ntrue alias or partial-overlap cases, and keep younger stores conservative.\\\\nThis is a backend-only structural change that compounds with the winning\\\\nposted-store buffer while avoiding the IF/ID timing regressions seen in prior\\\\nstructural attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add store-buffer alias checking, demand-load-vs-drain arbitration, and buffered-store forwarding for fully covered younger loads.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the blank...\\n  Line 21: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r7s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Two-entry posted store FIFO\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single posted-store slot with a tiny two-entry in-order store\\\\nFIFO that can absorb a short burst of normal-RAM stores while older buffered\\\\nwrites drain in the background. Keep the current conservative memory-order\\\\nrule that younger loads still wait until the FIFO is empty, and avoid the\\\\nlosing alias-compare/forwarding path entirely; the gain should come from\\\\nletting consecutive stores and following non-memory instructions retire\\\\nacross multi-cycle `io_dmemReady` stalls without touching IF timing or the\\\\nEX critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/store_queue.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a tiny two-entry in-order posted-store FIFO with enqueue/dequeue logic and head/tail status for MEM arbitration.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single buffered-store slot with the FIFO so a postable store can enqueue behind an older buffered store while preserving the existing conservative load ordering and RVFI store reporting.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"de...\\n  Line 24: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve direct JAL in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow early-redirect path for legal `JAL` instructions in ID while\\\\nleaving BRANCH and JALR resolution in EX. `JAL` needs only `pc + imm`, so it\\\\ncan redirect one stage earlier without the operand-bypass, compare, and\\\\nload-interlock machinery that made full ID-stage control resolution collapse\\\\nFmax. Carry an `early_redirected`/resolved-next-PC bit with the instruction so\\\\nEX suppresses the duplicate redirect but still produces the normal `rd = pc+4`\\\\nwriteback and RVFI retirement record. This should shave a bubble from hot\\\\ndirect calls and unconditional jumps in CoreMark while keeping the replay\\\\nfrontend, posted store buffer, and hot EX datapath essentially unchanged.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the decode/pipeline control bundles with a bit that marks a JAL as already redirected before EX, plus any resolved next-PC metadata needed to suppress duplicate redirects later.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JALs in decode, compute `pc + imm`, and...\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve canonical RET in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing ID redirect path to cover canonical aligned returns,\\\\ne.g. `jalr x0, x1, 0` and `jalr x0, x5, 0`, when the return-address\\\\nregister is already architecturally available from the regfile/WB path. If\\\\nan older in-flight instruction still owns that source register, simply fall\\\\nback to the current EX-stage JALR path instead of adding the full ID-stage\\\\nbypass/interlock machinery that regressed earlier. This reuses the existing\\\\n`id_redirect` plumbing, carries an \\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through\\\\nthe pipeline so EX suppresses the duplicate redirect, and should cut\\\\nanother hot function-return bubble in CoreMark with much lower timing risk\\\\nthan general early JALR/BRANCH resolution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the existing `jal_early_redirect` pipeline bit into an ID-resolved jump marker that can cover both direct `JAL` and narrow early-return `JALR` cases.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"descr...\\n  Line 30: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drain-and-replace posted store slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing single posted-store slot, but let it dequeue the older\\\\nbuffered store to dmem and capture one younger postable store in the same\\\\ncycle. In practice this means teaching `mem_stage` to drain and replace the\\\\nslot when `dmem_ready` comes back, and teaching `hazard_unit` not to stall\\\\nan EX/MEM store that can retire into the just-freed slot while still keeping\\\\nyounger loads and non-postable stores conservative. This should recover some\\\\nof the store-burst benefit the two-entry FIFO targeted, without adding\\\\nanother queue entry, alias-compare logic, or front-end timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Allow the one-entry posted store buffer to drain an older buffered RAM store and capture a younger postable store in the same cycle, ...\\n  Line 33: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Replay-backed one-slot IF decoupler\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Turn the registered replay candidate into a true one-slot IF decoupler. When\\\\nthe current PC already matches a valid replay entry, let decode consume the\\\\ncached `{pc,instr}` while the live imem port fetches the next sequential PC and\\\\nrefreshes the lookahead slot/table in the same cycle. Keep the current\\\\nredirect/flush rules, keep all state local to `if_stage`, and do not add IF-side\\\\nbranch decode, target tables, or a multi-entry queue. This should raise\\\\nCoreMark iterations/cycle by converting hot replay hits into useful prefetch\\\\nbandwidth, recovering more random iStall loss than the fallback-only replay\\\\npath without the timing cost that sank broader fetch-queue and control-predictor\\\\nattempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"des...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":11,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778831089971,\"end\":1778831089990}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_002158f8e59d6cd7016a06cef1ddb8819b93bc0da63c72beb9\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2a986127001xN3uMt2cgCzjQa\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d56809b4ffeXcDEq9kevYdM7a\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2a983853001yTruenG9SNCZxJ\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:924:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778831090136,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d56809b4ffeXcDEq9kevYdM7a\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_j1xKB99uJxnYENnwaOkFAu1a\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1|hyp-20260515-002-r10s1|hyp-20260515-002-r11s1|hyp-20260515-002-r9s1|hyp-20260515-002-r7s1|hyp-20260515-002-r6s1|hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 7 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 15: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-entry posted store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry posted-store buffer that captures a store's aligned address,\\\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible metadata when the store\\\\nreaches MEM. If the external dmem port stalls, retire the store from the buffer\\\\nimmediately, keep draining the buffered write in the background, and allow\\\\nyounger non-memory instructions to keep flowing. Only stall when a younger\\\\nload or store reaches MEM while the buffer is still occupied, preserving\\\\nin-order memory semantics without reintroducing load-use or control-path\\\\ncomplexity. This should recover CoreMark IPC from random dmem backpressure on\\\\nstore-heavy regions while avoiding the IF/ID critical-path regressions seen in\\\\nearlier structural experiments.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the MEM-stage/retirement bundles with store-buffer bookkeepin...\\n  Line 18: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Alias-aware load bypass around posted stores\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing posted store buffer, but make it address-aware instead of\\\\nblocking all younger memory ops. When a younger load reaches MEM while a\\\\nmain-memory store is buffered, compare the load's aligned word address and\\\\nbyte mask against the buffered store: if there is no overlap, let the load use\\\\nthe dmem port immediately and drain the buffered store later; if the load is\\\\nfully covered by the buffered bytes, forward from the buffer; only stall on\\\\ntrue alias or partial-overlap cases, and keep younger stores conservative.\\\\nThis is a backend-only structural change that compounds with the winning\\\\nposted-store buffer while avoiding the IF/ID timing regressions seen in prior\\\\nstructural attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add store-buffer alias checking, demand-load-vs-drain arbitration, and buffered-store forwarding for fully covered younger loads.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the blank...\\n  Line 21: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r7s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Two-entry posted store FIFO\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single posted-store slot with a tiny two-entry in-order store\\\\nFIFO that can absorb a short burst of normal-RAM stores while older buffered\\\\nwrites drain in the background. Keep the current conservative memory-order\\\\nrule that younger loads still wait until the FIFO is empty, and avoid the\\\\nlosing alias-compare/forwarding path entirely; the gain should come from\\\\nletting consecutive stores and following non-memory instructions retire\\\\nacross multi-cycle `io_dmemReady` stalls without touching IF timing or the\\\\nEX critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/store_queue.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a tiny two-entry in-order posted-store FIFO with enqueue/dequeue logic and head/tail status for MEM arbitration.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single buffered-store slot with the FIFO so a postable store can enqueue behind an older buffered store while preserving the existing conservative load ordering and RVFI store reporting.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"de...\\n  Line 24: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve direct JAL in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow early-redirect path for legal `JAL` instructions in ID while\\\\nleaving BRANCH and JALR resolution in EX. `JAL` needs only `pc + imm`, so it\\\\ncan redirect one stage earlier without the operand-bypass, compare, and\\\\nload-interlock machinery that made full ID-stage control resolution collapse\\\\nFmax. Carry an `early_redirected`/resolved-next-PC bit with the instruction so\\\\nEX suppresses the duplicate redirect but still produces the normal `rd = pc+4`\\\\nwriteback and RVFI retirement record. This should shave a bubble from hot\\\\ndirect calls and unconditional jumps in CoreMark while keeping the replay\\\\nfrontend, posted store buffer, and hot EX datapath essentially unchanged.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the decode/pipeline control bundles with a bit that marks a JAL as already redirected before EX, plus any resolved next-PC metadata needed to suppress duplicate redirects later.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JALs in decode, compute `pc + imm`, and...\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve canonical RET in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JAL