lback-only replay\\\\npath without the timing cost that sank broader fetch-queue and control-predictor\\\\nattempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"des...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":7,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1|hyp-20260515-002-r10s1|hyp-20260515-002-r11s1|hyp-20260515-002-r9s1|hyp-20260515-002-r7s1|hyp-20260515-002-r6s1|hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778831090119,\"end\":1778831090134}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_002158f8e59d6cd7016a06cef1ddbc819baa3d8b884ff5a4c8\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2a9861c0001ninLaEdpakv3Po\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d56809b4ffeXcDEq9kevYdM7a\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2a983853001yTruenG9SNCZxJ\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:929:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778831225455,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d56809b4ffeXcDEq9kevYdM7a\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_1BAvupFkjA44mHpB6oonIyVw\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"id_redirect|jal_early_redirect\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 3 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 24: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve direct JAL in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow early-redirect path for legal `JAL` instructions in ID while\\\\nleaving BRANCH and JALR resolution in EX. `JAL` needs only `pc + imm`, so it\\\\ncan redirect one stage earlier without the operand-bypass, compare, and\\\\nload-interlock machinery that made full ID-stage control resolution collapse\\\\nFmax. Carry an `early_redirected`/resolved-next-PC bit with the instruction so\\\\nEX suppresses the duplicate redirect but still produces the normal `rd = pc+4`\\\\nwriteback and RVFI retirement record. This should shave a bubble from hot\\\\ndirect calls and unconditional jumps in CoreMark while keeping the replay\\\\nfrontend, posted store buffer, and hot EX datapath essentially unchanged.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the decode/pipeline control bundles with a bit that marks a JAL as already redirected before EX, plus any resolved next-PC metadata needed to suppress duplicate redirects later.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JALs in decode, compute `pc + imm`, and...\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve canonical RET in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing ID redirect path to cover canonical aligned returns,\\\\ne.g. `jalr x0, x1, 0` and `jalr x0, x5, 0`, when the return-address\\\\nregister is already architecturally available from the regfile/WB path. If\\\\nan older in-flight instruction still owns that source register, simply fall\\\\nback to the current EX-stage JALR path instead of adding the full ID-stage\\\\nbypass/interlock machinery that regressed earlier. This reuses the existing\\\\n`id_redirect` plumbing, carries an \\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through\\\\nthe pipeline so EX suppresses the duplicate redirect, and should cut\\\\nanother hot function-return bubble in CoreMark with much lower timing risk\\\\nthan general early JALR/BRANCH resolution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the existing `jal_early_redirect` pipeline bit into an ID-resolved jump marker that can cover both direct `JAL` and narrow early-return `JALR` cases.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"descr...\\n  Line 31: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r10s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Seed replay from ID-resolved JAL targets\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest `bench` predictor result is still the tiny registered replay\\\\ntable, and predictor attempts that widened IF control, added target decode,\\\\nor carried prediction metadata into later stages consistently lost fitness.\\\\nAfter the recent ID-stage `JAL` win, the next control-side blind spot is the\\\\nvery first fetch at a direct-call target: the target PC is now known exactly\\\\none cycle early, but `if_stage` still drops replay state on `id_redirect`, so\\\\na warmed callee entry point bubbles whenever `io_imemReady=0` on that first\\\\ntarget cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest `bench` predictor result is still the tiny registered replay\\\\ntable, and predictor attempts that widened IF control, added target decode,\\\\nor carried prediction metadata into later stages consistently lost fitness.\\\\nAfter the recent ID-stage `JAL` win, the next control-side blind spot is the\\\\nvery first fetch at a direct-call target: the target PC is now known exactly\\\\none cycle early, but `if_stage` still drops replay state on `id_redirect`, so\\\\na warmed callee entry point bubbles whenever `io_imemReady=0` on that first\\\\ntarget cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the existing `{pc,instr}` replay table to seed the next-cycle replay\\\\ncandidate from an exact `id_redirect_target` when decode resolves a legal\\\\naligned `JAL`. On a live ID redirect, `if_stage` would probe the existing\\\\nreplay table with the JAL target and, on a hit, load `replay_valid_q`,\\\\n`replay_pc_q`, and `replay_instr_q` for the upcoming target fetch instead of\\\\nunconditionally clearing them. Keep EX-stage redirect handling, sequential\\\\nreplay fill, and `fetch_available = imem_ready || replay_hit` unchanged, so\\\\nthis stays in the proven replay-family design space: no IF-side immediate\\\\ndecode, no new predicted-PC metadata through ID/EX, and no broader branch or\\\\nreturn speculation. The pay...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":3,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"id_redirect|jal_early_redirect\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778831225439,\"end\":1778831225454}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0e5bb251b1a61a30016a06cf79281c819886e259e0ad1a1909\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2a9a71e3001qOYFbpR9MzUt8M\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d56809b4ffeXcDEq9kevYdM7a\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2a98631f001B0wLiIiMvsI7se\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:971:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778832658537,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d54fefe4ffeAIZkrcLO9DLQdX\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_GnWl86bnRSVRbvl5pKGtpkLC\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":400},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Moving DIV/REM into a multicycle EX micro-unit is not a local ALU swap; if stall/freeze logic does not hold the full EX instruction context and suppress side effects until completion, riscv-formal will start failing even simple ADD/SUB/reg checks from corrupted EX/MEM retirement state.\\n2: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r1s1 (regression, -3.89%): A tiny sequential fetch queue is not enough by itself to beat random `io_imemReady` stalls on this front end; with only 1-instruction-per-cycle fetch bandwidth and frequent redirect flushes, the queue rarely gets far enough ahead of decode to repay its extra control.\\n3: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r1s2 (regression, -1.94%): On `bench`, a zero-table IF predictor for backward B-type branches and direct JALs still loses overall fitness when it adds immediate-decode, target-add, and `predicted_taken`/`predicted_target` bookkeeping to the fetch path, because the front-end cost outweighs the CoreMark loop/call bubbles it removes.\\n4: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r2s0 (regression, +7.86%): On `bench`, moving cold DIV/REM ops off the ALU can still lose when the precise fix adds hot-path interlock machinery like `ex_long_busy`, full EX-context shadow registers, and EX/MEM bubble control, because CoreMark rarely executes divides and does not repay the added control cost.\\n5: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r2s1 (broken, n/a): Stage-aware load/store interlocks must move the architectural operand snapshot with the true consumer stage too; if STORE `rs2` is satisfied by a late WB->MEM bypass but retirement still reports the old EX-captured `rs2_val`, riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs2_rdata` on load->store pairs.\\n6: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r2s2 (improvement, +11.75%): On `bench`, a registered `{pc,instr}` replay table that banks the next `pc+4` hit can hide random `io_imemReady` stalls better than a plain fetch queue or decode-time branch predictor, because CoreMark revisits a small hot sequential PC set and the replay path stays off the IF critical path.\\n7: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r3s0 (improvement, +5.14%): On `bench`, a MEM-to-EX load bypass is worth more than a mandatory load-use bubble when `io_dmemReady` commonly returns zero-wait data and CoreMark presents many short load-consumer chains, provided the forwarded value still flows through the normal EX path so RVFI operand snapshots stay aligned.\\n8: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): Exact `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2_*` load-use gating must retime RVFI source snapshots to the operand's true consumer stage for both sources, not just STORE data, or riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs1_rdata`/`rvfi_rs2_rdata` when forwarding changes the architecturally consumed value.\\n9: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r3s2 (broken, n/a): Extending the successful replay table into a learned next-PC predictor can exhaust `riscv-formal`'s wall-clock budget even without IF-side decode, because threading full `predicted_next_pc`/`predicted_valid` metadata and per-entry successor state through IF/ID/EX forces every instruction proof to reason about extra 32-bit control-flow state and EX mispredict comparisons.\\n10: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.78%): On `bench`, parallel `mul_ss`/`mul_uu`/`mul_su` datapaths are wasted hot-path area for RV32M; collapsing `MUL*` onto one sign-selectable 33x33 product preserves single-cycle behavior while reducing ALU routing pressure enough to lift Tang Nano 20K fitness.\\n11: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (regression, -59.23%): On `bench`, exact ID-stage BRANCH/JAL/JALR resolution is too timing-expensive; pulling compare/target-add plus EX/MEM/MEM/WB operand-bypass and load-interlock logic into decode collapsed Fmax to 52 MHz, so CoreMark's taken-control bubbles are cheaper than widening the front-end critical path.\\n12: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (regression, +0.61%): On `bench`, replaying warmed EX `redirect_target` instructions from the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` table is too narrow a win to pay for extra redirect-hit lookup/arming logic, because it only helps when `io_imemReady` stalls on the first post-redirect cycle while the original `pc+4` replay already captures the hotter sequential locality.\\n13: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r5s0 (regression, +9.14%): On `bench`, replacing the shared sign-selectable 33x33 `MUL*` datapath with a 32x32 product plus `MULH`/`MULHSU` sign-correction subtractors loses on Tang Nano 20K, because the extra 32-bit correction/mux logic on the ALU hot path costs more than carrying the multiplier sign bit through the DSP.\\n14: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r5s1 (improvement, +12.75%): On `bench`, a one-entry posted store buffer that only posts main-memory stores and blocks only younger memory ops turns random `io_dmemReady` store backpressure into background drain time, recovering CoreMark IPC without paying the IF/ID timing cost of front-end control changes.\\n15: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r5s2 (regression, +0.93%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table already has enough capacity for CoreMark's hot sequential PCs; adding 2-way set associativity with dual tag checks, way select, and MRU replacement costs more than the low-bit conflict misses it removes.\\n16: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r6s0 (improvement, +3.63%): On `bench`, carving ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and load/store address generation onto a dedicated EX carry-chain path pays because CoreMark is dominated by simple add/address ops, so keeping them out of the ALU's wide logic/shift/RV32M mux and fanout improves timing without front-end control changes.\\n17: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r6s1 (regression, -11.69%): On `bench`, extending the posted store buffer with MEM-stage aligned-address/byte-mask overlap checks, load forwarding, and load-vs-drain arbitration lost overall fitness because the extra compare/mask/mux logic sat on the hot memory path, dropping Fmax from 151.3 MHz to 133.4 MHz while CoreMark did not expose enough independent load-behind-store overlap to pay it back.\\n18: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r6s2 (regression, +1.63%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table should stay a single sequential `pc+4` candidate; adding a second JAL/backward-branch control-target slot only helps on rare first-post-redirect `io_imemReady` stalls, while the extra immediate-decode, target-add, and second replay-hit path in `if_stage` costs more than it saves.\\n19: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r7s0 (regression, -19.89%): On `bench`, the profitable EX split stops at the add/address fast path; adding op-class gating like `use_alu_result`, zeroed ALU operands for BRANCH/JAL/JALR, and `is_m_op ? a/b : 0` around the shared RV32M cone puts extra decode/mux logic back on the hot EX path and drops Fmax far more than the cold-cone isolation helps.\\n20: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r7s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On `bench`, the posted-store win appears depth-sensitive on Tang Nano 20K: growing the one-entry main-memory store buffer into a two-entry in-order FIFO with `store_buf_full` backpressure and queued-drain muxing pushed MEM/backpressure routing past place-and-route limits before any CoreMark gain could be measured.\\n21: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r7s2 (regression, +0.00%): On `bench`, low-bit conflict misses in the 8-entry sequential `{pc,instr}` replay table are not a meaningful limiter; even a one-entry fully-associative victim spill/check in `if_stage` failed to beat the plain direct-mapped design, so extra replay-hit selection state is already past CoreMark's payoff point.\\n22: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r8s0 (regression, -14.36%): On `bench`, peeling aligned `LW`/`SW` onto a dedicated MEM fast path is not worthwhile when the generic width logic already makes word accesses the default identity/`4'b1111` case; hoisting `mem_mask`/store formatting into EX and threading `mem_word_op` through the pipe adds ALU-result fanout and stage metadata without enough hot-path relief to repay it.\\n23: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r8s1 (improvement, +31.31%): On `bench`, decode-stage control fixes should isolate the cheapest unconditional case: resolving legal aligned `JAL` in ID and carrying a `jal_early_redirect` bit to suppress the duplicate EX redirect removes CoreMark's hot direct-call/jump bubble without dragging BRANCH/JALR compare, bypass, and load-interlock logic onto the front-end critical path.\\n24: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r8s2 (regression, -4.11%): On `bench`, redirect-target replay is opportunity-limited rather than lookup-limited; even a separate one-cycle landing-pad `{pc,instr}` cache in `if_stage` only pays on repeated redirects whose very first fetch later stalls on `io_imemReady=0`, which is rarer than the locality already captured by the sequential `pc+4` replay.\\n25: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r9s0 (regression, -11.24%): On `bench`, precomputing and registering MEM/WB `wb_data` is not a free timing win if the change merely shifts that value's fanout into same-cycle WB-to-regfile/ID read-bypass compare+mux logic; backend writeback cleanup must reduce total hot-path routing, not just move the `mem_to_reg` select earlier.\\n26: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r9s1 (regression, -13.86%): On `bench`, decode-time redirect wins stop at plain `JAL`; even a narrow canonical `ret` fast path (`jalr x0, x1/x5, 0`) with `rs1_busy` fallback adds enough decode-side dependency compare and target-select logic to cost more Fmax than CoreMark's return bubbles repay.\\n27: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r9s2 (regression, -64.82%): On `bench`, the canonical-return loss is not just an `rs1_busy` decode hazard: even a tiny 4-entry `ras_stack` that predicts only `JAL x1`/`ret` and carries `early_redirect_target` into EX for repair adds enough speculative call/return bookkeeping to lose more Fmax than CoreMark's return bubbles repay.\\n28: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r10s0 (broken, n/a): On `bench`, the `reg_file` write-first/WB-to-ID same-cycle read path is part of the backend correctness contract, not free timing junk; moving that bypass behind decode-gated `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` logic can miss real dependencies and break selftest.\\n29: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r10s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On `bench`, the posted-store budget is not just buffer depth: feeding `dmem_ready` into same-cycle drain-and-replace control in both `mem_stage` and `hazard_unit` was enough to blow Tang Nano 20K placement, so future store-burst work must avoid combinational refill/backpressure feedback.\\n30: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r10s2 (regression, -35.73%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table should stay dedicated to the sequential `pc+4` stream; repointing `replay_valid_q`/`replay_pc_q` at an exact ID-resolved `JAL` target burns the hotter fallthrough reuse and adds redirect-target selection to `if_stage` for a first-target-stall corner CoreMark rarely needs.\\n31: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r11s0 (broken, n/a): On `bench`, unused-source cleanup must not rewrite the shared `id_stage` `rs*_addr`/regfile-read path, because this repo's cosim/RVFI contract snapshots raw instruction `rs1`/`rs2` fields and pre-write register contents even for operands the ISA ignores, so `uses_rs*` normalization to `x0` needs a separate trace view or breaks selftest.\\n32: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r11s2 (regression, -51.23%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table only pays when a hit is pre-banked into the one-cycle replay candidate; adding same-cycle current-PC tag-compare and fallback muxing to salvage warm entries after flush/redirect clears puts too much logic back on the fetch critical path for a rare stall corner.\\n33: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r12s0 (regression, -3.37%): On `bench`, a tiny 32x32 2R1W register file is too small and semantically constrained for LUTRAM-plus-valid-bit conversion to pay; preserving write-first bypass and reset-to-zero semantics with `reg_valid`/read-zero gating adds muxing on the hot WB-to-ID read path that can cost more Fmax than removing the data-array reset saves.\\n34: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r12s1 (regression, -11.10%): On `bench`, decode-time redirect wins really stop at source-free `JAL`; even an exact one-source zero-compare B-type fast path guarded only by `older_id_ex`/`older_ex_mem` pending checks still puts live register-read dependency compares and branch-condition logic on `id_stage`, costing more Fmax than the taken-branch bubble repays.\\n35: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r12s2 (regression, -49.77%): On `bench`, decode-time redirect wins truly stop at unconditional `JAL`; even a source-free static backward-B-type predictor that only checks `imm[31]`, legal/aligned target, and carries an `id_ctrl_redirect` mispredict-repair bit into EX adds enough front-end control plumbing that CoreMark's hot loop backedges still do not repay the Fmax loss.\\n\\n(End of file - total 35 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Moving DIV/REM into a multicycle EX micro-unit is not a local ALU swap; if stall/freeze logic does not hold the full EX instruction context and suppress side effects until completion, riscv-formal will start failing even simple ADD/SUB/reg checks from corrupted EX/MEM retirement state.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r1s1 (regression, -3.89%): A tiny sequential fetch queue is not enough by itself to beat random `io_imemReady` stalls on this front end; with only 1-instruction-per-cycle fetch bandwidth and frequent redirect flushes, the queue rarely gets far enough ahead of decode to repay its extra control.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r1s2 (regression, -1.94%): On `bench`, a zero-table IF predictor for backward B-type branches and direct JALs still loses overall fitness when it adds immediate-decode, target-add, and `predicted_taken`/`predicted_target` bookkeeping to the fetch path, because the front-end cost outweighs the CoreMark loop/call bubbles it removes.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r2s0 (regression, +7.86%): On `bench`, moving cold DIV/REM ops off the ALU can still lose when the precise fix adds hot-path interlock machinery like `ex_long_busy`, full EX-context shadow registers, and EX/MEM bubble control, because CoreMark rarely executes divides and does not repay the added control cost.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r2s1 (broken, n/a): Stage-aware load/store interlocks must move the architectural operand snapshot with the true consumer stage too; if STORE `rs2` is satisfied by a late WB->MEM bypass but retirement still reports the old EX-captured `rs2_val`, riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs2_rdata` on load->store pairs.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r2s2 (improvement, +11.75%): On `bench`, a registered `{pc,instr}` replay table that banks the next `pc+4` hit can hide random `io_imemReady` stalls better than a plain fetch queue or decode-time branch predictor, because CoreMark revisits a small hot sequential PC set and the replay path stays off the IF critical path.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r3s0 (improvement, +5.14%): On `bench`, a MEM-to-EX load bypass is worth more than a mandatory load-use bubble when `io_dmemReady` commonly returns zero-wait data and CoreMark presents many short load-consumer chains, provided the forwarded value still flows through the normal EX path so RVFI operand snapshots stay aligned.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): Exact `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2_*` load-use gating must retime RVFI source snapshots to the operand's true consumer stage for both sources, not just STORE data, or riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs1_rdata`/`rvfi_rs2_rdata` when forwarding changes the architecturally consumed value.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r3s2 (broken, n/a): Extending the successful replay table into a learned next-PC predictor can exhaust `riscv-formal`'s wall-clock budget even without IF-side decode, because threading full `predicted_next_pc`/`predicted_valid` metadata and per-entry successor state through IF/ID/EX forces every instruction proof to reason about extra 32-bit control-flow state and EX mispredict comparisons.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.78%): On `bench`, parallel `mul_ss`/`mul_uu`/`mul_su` datapaths are wasted hot-path area for RV32M; collapsing `MUL*` onto one sign-selectable 33x33 product preserves single-cycle behavior while reducing ALU routing pressure enough to lift Tang Nano 20K fitness.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (regression, -59.23%): On `bench`, exact ID-stage BRANCH/JAL/JALR resolution is too timing-expensive; pulling compare/target-add plus EX/MEM/MEM/WB operand-bypass and load-interlock logic into decode collapsed Fmax to 52 MHz, so CoreMark's taken-control bubbles are cheaper than widening the front-end critical path.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (regression, +0.61%): On `bench`, replaying warmed EX `redirect_target` instructions from the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` table is too narrow a win to pay for extra redirect-hit lookup/arming logic, because it only helps when `io_imemReady` stalls on the first post-redirect cycle while the original `pc+4` replay already captures the hotter sequential locality.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r5s0 (regression, +9.14%): On `bench`, replacing the shared sign-selectable 33x33 `MUL*` datapath with a 32x32 product plus `MULH`/`MULHSU` sign-correction subtractors loses on Tang Nano 20K, because the extra 32-bit correction/mux logic on the ALU hot path costs more than carrying the multiplier sign bit through the DSP.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r5s1 (improvement, +12.75%): On `bench`, a one-entry posted store buffer that only posts main-memory stores and blocks only younger memory ops turns random `io_dmemReady` store backpressure into background drain time, recovering CoreMark IPC without paying the IF/ID timing cost of front-end control changes.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r5s2 (regression, +0.93%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table already has enough capacity for CoreMark's hot sequential PCs; adding 2-way set associativity with dual tag checks, way select, and MRU replacement costs more than the low-bit conflict misses it removes.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r6s0 (improvement, +3.63%): On `bench`, carving ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and load/store address generation onto a dedicated EX carry-chain path pays because CoreMark is dominated by simple add/address ops, so keeping them out of the ALU's wide logic/shift/RV32M mux and fanout improves timing without front-end control changes.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r6s1 (regression, -11.69%): On `bench`, extending the posted store buffer with MEM-stage aligned-address/byte-mask overlap checks, load forwarding, and load-vs-drain arbitration lost overall fitness because the extra compare/mask/mux logic sat on the hot memory path, dropping Fmax from 151.3 MHz to 133.4 MHz while CoreMark did not expose enough independent load-behind-store overlap to pay it back.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r6s2 (regression, +1.63%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table should stay a single sequential `pc+4` candidate; adding a second JAL/backward-branch control-target slot only helps on rare first-post-redirect `io_imemReady` stalls, while the extra immediate-decode, target-add, and second replay-hit path in `if_stage` costs more than it saves.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r7s0 (regression, -19.89%): On `bench`, the profitable EX split stops at the add/address fast path; adding op-class gating like `use_alu_result`, zeroed ALU operands for BRANCH/JAL/JALR, and `is_m_op ? a/b : 0` around the shared RV32M cone puts extra decode/mux logic back on the hot EX path and drops Fmax far more than the cold-cone isolation helps.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r7s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On `bench`, the posted-store win appears depth-sensitive on Tang Nano 20K: growing the one-entry main-memory store buffer into a two-entry in-order FIFO with `store_buf_full` backpressure and queued-drain muxing pushed MEM/backpressure routing past place-and-route limits before any CoreMark gain could be measured.\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[]},\"title\":\"cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778832658494,\"end\":1778832658535}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0ab5283c03d9832d016a06d51255f4819bb0d15fe3c25481e9\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2ab05029001x3I3iwP7LwWfS3\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d54fefe4ffeAIZkrcLO9DLQdX\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2ab010a8001IhZVrpC5wCFEH2\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:972:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778832658622,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d54fefe4ffeAIZkrcLO9DLQdX\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_6Z8XE7WkHGw0A8qPDOIvZq9B\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|category:\\\\s*structural|structural\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 15 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 2: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Move DIV/REM off the fast ALU path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current EX-stage ALU carries fully combinational divide and remainder\\\\nhardware even though CoreMark spends very little time in DIV/REM. That rare\\\\nlogic still taxes timing closure and routing on the Tang Nano 20K, and the\\\\nbench core is already close to its LUT budget.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current EX-stage ALU carries fully combinational divide and remainder\\\\nhardware even though CoreMark spends very little time in DIV/REM. That rare\\\\nlogic still taxes timing closure and routing on the Tang Nano 20K, and the\\\\nbench core is already close to its LUT budget.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single-cycle DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU datapath with a small iterative\\\\ndivider/remainder micro-unit, while keeping the common integer ALU and MUL\\\\npath single-cycle. EX would launch the divider on DIV/REM ops, freeze the\\\\nfront of the pipeline until completion, and then retire normally with the\\\\nsame RV32M corner-case semantics. This should remove a rarely useful critical\\\\npath from normal cycles, improve Fmax and placement slack, and cost little\\\\nCoreMark/MHz because CoreMark is dominated by adds, loads, stores, branches,\\\\nand plain MUL rather than division. Using an 8-cycle worst-case divider keeps\\\\nthe added stall bounded enough to stay compatible with the existing formal\\\\ndepth budget.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/div_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a compact iterative signed/unsigned divider-remainder unit with RV32M divide-by-zero and overflow behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the combinational DIV/REM datapath from the fast ALU path and keep common arithmetic, shifts, compares, and MUL results single-cycle.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU, launch the divider, hold EX state while it runs, and only write EX/MEM when a...\\n  Line 3: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Add a small redirect-aware fetch queue\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front-end is tightly coupled to `io_imemReady`: any cycle with\\\\ninstruction-memory backpressure immediately becomes a decode bubble because\\\\nIF emits a NOP and the PC stops. That is especially expensive here because\\\\nthe fitness run injects random iStalls by design, so front-end elasticity is\\\\nworth more than a tiny local logic cleanup.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front-end is tightly coupled to `io_imemReady`: any cycle with\\\\ninstruction-memory backpressure immediately becomes a decode bubble because\\\\nIF emits a NOP and the PC stops. That is especially expensive here because\\\\nthe fitness run injects random iStalls by design, so front-end elasticity is\\\\nworth more than a tiny local logic cleanup.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single-slot combinational fetch path with a small speculative\\\\nfetch queue in `if_stage`, likely 2-4 entries deep, that buffers `{pc,\\\\ninstr}` pairs when `io_imemReady` is high and feeds ID from the queue when\\\\nthe backend is ready. On redirects, flush younger queued instructions and\\\\nrestart fetch at the resolved target; on load-use or dmem stalls, preserve\\\\nthe buffered instructions instead of re-fetching them. This is a structural\\\\nfront-end change, not a predictor: it should raise CoreMark iterations/cycle\\\\nunder the orchestrator's random stall model with only modest area/timing\\\\ncost and without changing the single-retire RVFI contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Refactor IF into a small prefetch FIFO/skid-buffer front-end with redirect-aware invalidation and queue-based instruction delivery.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Change front-end stall logic to depend on fetch-queue availability plus backend hazards instead of bubbling immediately on every `!imemReady`.\\\"}, {\\\"...\\n  Line 6: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Operand-Stage-Aware Load Interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current hazard unit stalls on any raw `rs1`/`rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not actually read that field yet\\\\nor only needs it later as store data in MEM. That conservative policy burns\\\\navoidable one-cycle bubbles in CoreMark's dense load/address/store code, and\\\\nthe recent `bench` regressions already showed that extra front-end machinery\\\\nis a worse trade here than tightening the backend interlock.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current hazard unit stalls on any raw `rs1`/`rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not actually read that field yet\\\\nor only needs it later as store data in MEM. That conservative policy burns\\\\navoidable one-cycle bubbles in CoreMark's dense load/address/store code, and\\\\nthe recent `bench` regressions already showed that extra front-end machinery\\\\nis a worse trade here than tightening the backend interlock.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework the load-use interlock around the stage where an operand is actually\\\\nconsumed. Add decode metadata for real `rs1`/`rs2` usage, treat STORE `rs2`\\\\nas a late MEM-stage consumer instead of an EX-stage consumer, and add a\\\\nWB-to-MEM store-data bypass so `load -> store` pairs with an independent\\\\naddress can proceed without the textbook bubble. This should recover\\\\nCoreMark IPC from both false-positive load stalls and true-but-late\\\\nstore-data dependencies, while keeping load data off the EX ALU/branch path\\\\nand avoiding another fetch-path timing penalty.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add decoded source-usage and late-store-data control bits to the pipeline bundles so hazards can distinguish EX-stage operands from MEM-stage-only store data.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/decoder.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Classify which legal ins...\\n  Line 7: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r2s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Registered I-Fetch Replay Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench frontend turns every `!io_imemReady` cycle into an\\\\nimmediate decode bubble, and the last structural fetch-queue attempt showed\\\\nthat simply buffering sequential fetches is not enough to get far enough\\\\nahead under the tournament's random ~22% iStall model. CoreMark repeatedly\\\\nrevisits a compact hot set of loop and helper PCs, so reusing already-fetched\\\\ninstructions is a stronger predictor bet than adding more branch decode or\\\\ntarget-add logic to the IF critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench frontend turns every `!io_imemReady` cycle into an\\\\nimmediate decode bubble, and the last structural fetch-queue attempt showed\\\\nthat simply buffering sequential fetches is not enough to get far enough\\\\nahead under the tournament's random ~22% iStall model. CoreMark repeatedly\\\\nrevisits a compact hot set of loop and helper PCs, so reusing already-fetched\\\\ninstructions is a stronger predictor bet than adding more branch decode or\\\\ntarget-add logic to the IF critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a small registered lookahead instruction replay predictor in `if_stage`.\\\\nOn each architecturally live fetch, fill a direct-mapped `{pc tag, instr}`\\\\ntable and, in parallel, look up the next fetch PC and register a one-cycle-\\\\nahead replay candidate. When `io_imemReady` later drops and the current PC\\\\nmatches a valid candidate, feed the cached instruction to decode and report\\\\nfetch-ready instead of injecting a bubble. Keep the normal ready-cycle path\\\\nlive-imem-first, invalidate replay candidates on redirect/flush, and avoid\\\\nfilling the table with killed wrong-path fetches. This should recover a\\\\nmeaningful fraction of random iStall loss while avoiding the immediate-decode\\\\nand predicted-target bookkeeping that made the earlier static branch/JAL\\\\npredictor regress on `bench`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6...\\n  Line 9: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Exact load-use interlock with late store-data bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The fetch side already has a winning replay mechanism, so the strongest\\\\nremaining structural gain is more likely in the backend. `hazard_unit.sv`\\\\nstill stalls on any raw `if_id_rs1` or `if_id_rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not consume that field in EX or\\\\nonly needs store data later in MEM. CoreMark's dense mix of loads,\\\\nimmediate arithmetic, address generation, and stores makes those\\\\nconservative one-cycle bubbles expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The fetch side already has a winning replay mechanism, so the strongest\\\\nremaining structural gain is more likely in the backend. `hazard_unit.sv`\\\\nstill stalls on any raw `if_id_rs1` or `if_id_rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not consume that field in EX or\\\\nonly needs store data later in MEM. CoreMark's dense mix of loads,\\\\nimmediate arithmetic, address generation, and stores makes those\\\\nconservative one-cycle bubbles expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework the load-use interlock around true operand-consumption timing instead\\\\nof raw register-field matches. Decode exact legal `rs1` and `rs2` usage for\\\\neach instruction class, keep the current conservative stall policy for\\\\nillegal or otherwise unclassified encodings, and treat STORE `rs2` as a\\\\nMEM-stage consumer with a dedicated WB-to-MEM store-data bypass. To avoid\\\\nthe prior broken attempt's RVFI bug, carry the final architecturally\\\\nconsumed store-data value into retirement so `rvfi_rs2_rdata`,\\\\n`rvfi_mem_wdata`, and the actual dmem write all agree. This should remove\\\\nfalse bubbles from OP-IMM/LOAD/JALR/LUI/AUIPC/FENCE paths and eliminate the\\\\nmandatory `load -> store(rs2)` stall without putting load data on the EX\\\\nALU, branch-compare, or JALR critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"...\\n  Line 12: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"ID-stage exact control resolution\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best low-risk fetch and\\\\nload-use gains: it replays hot sequential instructions in IF and forwards\\\\nzero-wait loads from MEM to EX. The remaining visible IPC loss is that every\\\\ntaken BRANCH, JAL, and JALR still resolves in EX after the ID/EX register,\\\\nwhich forces extra wrong-path bubbles on CoreMark's hot loop backedges and\\\\nhelper calls.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best low-risk fetch and\\\\nload-use gains: it replays hot sequential instructions in IF and forwards\\\\nzero-wait loads from MEM to EX. The remaining visible IPC loss is that every\\\\ntaken BRANCH, JAL, and JALR still resolves in EX after the ID/EX register,\\\\nwhich forces extra wrong-path bubbles on CoreMark's hot loop backedges and\\\\nhelper calls.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move exact control-flow resolution one stage earlier by adding a dedicated\\\\nID-stage branch/jump unit. JAL can redirect immediately from `pc+imm`; BRANCH\\\\nand JALR should compare/add in ID using the regfile value plus targeted\\\\nbypasses from EX/MEM and MEM/WB, while conservatively stalling only when an\\\\noperand is still unavailable from an in-flight load. Carry the final\\\\nforwarded source values into the pipeline state so RVFI operand snapshots stay\\\\naligned with the values the control instruction actually consumed, and mark\\\\nID-resolved control ops so EX does not issue a duplicate redirect a cycle\\\\nlater. This keeps the existing single-retire contract and replay frontend,\\\\nbut should cut the taken-control penalty by roughly one cycle without adding\\\\nIF-side predictor state or target-table complexity.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the pipeline bundles with control-resolution metadata so ID-resolved BRANCH/JAL/JALR instru...\\n  Line 15: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-entry posted store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry posted-store buffer that captures a store's aligned address,\\\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible metadata when the store\\\\nreaches MEM. If the external dmem port stalls, retire the store from the buffer\\\\nimmediately, keep draining the buffered write in the background, and allow\\\\nyounger non-memory instructions to keep flowing. Only stall when a younger\\\\nload or store reaches MEM while the buffer is still occupied, preserving\\\\nin-order memory semantics without reintroducing load-use or control-path\\\\ncomplexity. This should recover CoreMark IPC from random dmem backpressure on\\\\nstore-heavy regions while avoiding the IF/ID critical-path regressions seen in\\\\nearlier structural experiments.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the MEM-stage/retirement bundles with store-buffer bookkeepin...\\n  Line 18: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Alias-aware load bypass around posted stores\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing posted store buffer, but make it address-aware instead of\\\\nblocking all younger memory ops. When a younger load reaches MEM while a\\\\nmain-memory store is buffered, compare the load's aligned word address and\\\\nbyte mask against the buffered store: if there is no overlap, let the load use\\\\nthe dmem port immediately and drain the buffered store later; if the load is\\\\nfully covered by the buffered bytes, forward from the buffer; only stall on\\\\ntrue alias or partial-overlap cases, and keep younger stores conservative.\\\\nThis is a backend-only structural change that compounds with the winning\\\\nposted-store buffer while avoiding the IF/ID timing regressions seen in prior\\\\nstructural attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add store-buffer alias checking, demand-load-vs-drain arbitration, and buffered-store forwarding for fully covered younger loads.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the blank...\\n  Line 21: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r7s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Two-entry posted store FIFO\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single posted-store slot with a tiny two-entry in-order store\\\\nFIFO that can absorb a short burst of normal-RAM stores while older buffered\\\\nwrites drain in the background. Keep the current conservative memory-order\\\\nrule that younger loads still wait until the FIFO is empty, and avoid the\\\\nlosing alias-compare/forwarding path entirely; the gain should come from\\\\nletting consecutive stores and following non-memory instructions retire\\\\nacross multi-cycle `io_dmemReady` stalls without touching IF timing or the\\\\nEX critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/store_queue.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a tiny two-entry in-order posted-store FIFO with enqueue/dequeue logic and head/tail status for MEM arbitration.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single buffered-store slot with the FIFO so a postable store can enqueue behind an older buffered store while preserving the existing conservative load ordering and RVFI store reporting.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"de...\\n  Line 24: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve direct JAL in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow early-redirect path for legal `JAL` instructions in ID while\\\\nleaving BRANCH and JALR resolution in EX. `JAL` needs only `pc + imm`, so it\\\\ncan redirect one stage earlier without the operand-bypass, compare, and\\\\nload-interlock machinery that made full ID-stage control resolution collapse\\\\nFmax. Carry an `early_redirected`/resolved-next-PC bit with the instruction so\\\\nEX suppresses the duplicate redirect but still produces the normal `rd = pc+4`\\\\nwriteback and RVFI retirement record. This should shave a bubble from hot\\\\ndirect calls and unconditional jumps in CoreMark while keeping the replay\\\\nfrontend, posted store buffer, and hot EX datapath essentially unchanged.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the decode/pipeline control bundles with a bit that marks a JAL as already redirected before EX, plus any resolved next-PC metadata needed to suppress duplicate redirects later.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JALs in decode, compute `pc + imm`, and...\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve canonical RET in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing ID redirect path to cover canonical aligned returns,\\\\ne.g. `jalr x0, x1, 0` and `jalr x0, x5, 0`, when the return-address\\\\nregister is already architecturally available from the regfile/WB path. If\\\\nan older in-flight instruction still owns that source register, simply fall\\\\nback to the current EX-stage JALR path instead of adding the full ID-stage\\\\nbypass/interlock machinery that regressed earlier. This reuses the existing\\\\n`id_redirect` plumbing, carries an \\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through\\\\nthe pipeline so EX suppresses the duplicate redirect, and should cut\\\\nanother hot function-return bubble in CoreMark with much lower timing risk\\\\nthan general early JALR/BRANCH resolution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the existing `jal_early_redirect` pipeline bit into an ID-resolved jump marker that can cover both direct `JAL` and narrow early-return `JALR` cases.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"descr...\\n  Line 28: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Canonical return-address stack predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` already found its best predictor win in the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay path, and the recent structural JAL improvement removed the cheapest\\\\nunconditional jump bubble. The remaining hot control penalty is canonical\\\\nfunction return traffic: `ret`-shaped `JALR` instructions still wait for EX,\\\\nwhile broader IF-side branch prediction, wider replay tables, and redirect\\\\nlanding-pad schemes all failed to repay their timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` already found its best predictor win in the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay path, and the recent structural JAL improvement removed the cheapest\\\\nunconditional jump bubble. The remaining hot control penalty is canonical\\\\nfunction return traffic: `ret`-shaped `JALR` instructions still wait for EX,\\\\nwhile broader IF-side branch prediction, wider replay tables, and redirect\\\\nlanding-pad schemes all failed to repay their timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a very small return-address stack that predicts only the common ABI call\\\\nand return pattern instead of general branch targets. Push `pc+4` for direct\\\\ncalls that write `x1`, use the top entry to early-redirect only canonical\\\\n`ret`/`jalr x0, 0(x1)` in ID, and carry the predicted target forward so EX\\\\ncan suppress the duplicate redirect on a hit or repair the path on a rare\\\\nmismatch. This stays much narrower than the failed branch/JAL predictors: it\\\\ndoes not widen the replay table, does not add BTB-style target RAM, and only\\\\nadds prediction metadata to the small subset of return instructions. CoreMark\\\\nis call/return heavy enough that shaving the return bubble should improve IPC\\\\nwhile keeping Fmax risk materially lower than earlier full-control schemes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the ID/EX bundle with narrow early...\\n  Line 30: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drain-and-replace posted store slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing single posted-store slot, but let it dequeue the older\\\\nbuffered store to dmem and capture one younger postable store in the same\\\\ncycle. In practice this means teaching `mem_stage` to drain and replace the\\\\nslot when `dmem_ready` comes back, and teaching `hazard_unit` not to stall\\\\nan EX/MEM store that can retire into the just-freed slot while still keeping\\\\nyounger loads and non-postable stores conservative. This should recover some\\\\nof the store-burst benefit the two-entry FIFO targeted, without adding\\\\nanother queue entry, alias-compare logic, or front-end timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Allow the one-entry posted store buffer to drain an older buffered RAM store and capture a younger postable store in the same cycle, ...\\n  Line 33: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Replay-backed one-slot IF decoupler\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Turn the registered replay candidate into a true one-slot IF decoupler. When\\\\nthe current PC already matches a valid replay entry, let decode consume the\\\\ncached `{pc,instr}` while the live imem port fetches the next sequential PC and\\\\nrefreshes the lookahead slot/table in the same cycle. Keep the current\\\\nredirect/flush rules, keep all state local to `if_stage`, and do not add IF-side\\\\nbranch decode, target tables, or a multi-entry queue. This should raise\\\\nCoreMark iterations/cycle by converting hot replay hits into useful prefetch\\\\nbandwidth, recovering more random iStall loss than the fallback-only replay\\\\npath without the timing cost that sank broader fetch-queue and control-predictor\\\\nattempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"des...\\n  Line 36: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r12s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve ready branch-to-zero in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already harvested the cheapest broad structural wins: sequential IF\\\\nreplay, MEM-to-EX load forwarding, a one-entry posted store buffer, and an\\\\nexact early `JAL` redirect. The next visible IPC loss is still the taken\\\\nbranch bubble, but prior history shows that full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR\\\\nresolution collapses Fmax once decode has to do general bypass and\\\\ninterlock work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already harvested the cheapest broad structural wins: sequential IF\\\\nreplay, MEM-to-EX load forwarding, a one-entry posted store buffer, and an\\\\nexact early `JAL` redirect. The next visible IPC loss is still the taken\\\\nbranch bubble, but prior history shows that full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR\\\\nresolution collapses Fmax once decode has to do general bypass and\\\\ninterlock work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the existing `id_redirect` path for a much narrower exact control case:\\\\nlegal, aligned branch-against-zero instructions whose single live source is\\\\nalready architecturally ready in the regfile/WB path. Detect `BEQ/BNE` and\\\\nsigned zero-compare branch pseudos (`BLT/BGE` with `rs2 = x0`) in\\\\n`id_stage`, guard them with a cheap \\\\\\\"rs1 not pending in ID/EX or EX/MEM\\\\\\\"\\\\ncheck, and redirect taken cases one stage earlier while falling back to the\\\\nunchanged EX-stage branch path for everything else. Carry a generic\\\\n\\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through `id_ex_t` so EX suppresses duplicate\\\\nredirects but still recomputes `pc_next` and trap behavior, keeping RVFI\\\\nsource snapshots correct because the early path only fires when decode is\\\\nalready reading the final architecturally consumed operand.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the current JAL-only early-redirect marker in `id_ex_t` into a generic ID-resolved-control bit that can also tag ...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":15,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|category:\\\\s*structural|structural\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778832658601,\"end\":1778832658621}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0ab5283c03d9832d016a06d5125600819bbd0f821cb9c55051\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2ab05052001R1S2QghmvsA6ln\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d54fefe4ffeAIZkrcLO9DLQdX\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2ab010a8001IhZVrpC5wCFEH2\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:977:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778832672667,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d54fefe4ffeAIZkrcLO9DLQdX\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_4YfbEWOXVJU2A8T958vRRelt\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\",\"offset\":13,\"limit\":26},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n13: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Redirect-aware replay target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` frontend already proved that a tiny registered replay\\\\npredictor can recover a meaningful slice of random `io_imemReady` stalls\\\\nwithout hurting the IF critical path. Its remaining blind spot is taken\\\\ncontrol flow: every EX redirect clears the replay candidate, so hot loop\\\\nbackedges and direct-call targets lose replay coverage exactly where\\\\nCoreMark revisits the same PCs most often.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` frontend already proved that a tiny registered replay\\\\npredictor can recover a meaningful slice of random `io_imemReady` stalls\\\\nwithout hurting the IF critical path. Its remaining blind spot is taken\\\\ncontrol flow: every EX redirect clears the replay candidate, so hot loop\\\\nbackedges and direct-call targets lose replay coverage exactly where\\\\nCoreMark revisits the same PCs most often.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing replay-table predictor inside `if_stage` so redirects\\\\npre-arm replay for the resolved target instead of unconditionally clearing\\\\nthe candidate. On a redirect, look up `redirect_target` in the existing\\\\n`{pc,instr}` table and bank that target instruction for the next cycle when\\\\nit is already cached; keep all prediction state local to IF and continue to\\\\nemit a bubble on the redirect cycle itself. This should preserve the timing\\\\nadvantage of the current replay design, improve post-redirect fetch\\\\navailability on hot loops and helper calls, and avoid the formal-state and\\\\nbookkeeping costs that broke the learned next-PC predictor.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Teach the replay predictor to bank a redirect-target replay candidate from the existing table, instead of always invalidating replay state on redirect, while keeping all predictor state local to IF.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n14: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r5s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"DSP-friendly corrected shared multiplier\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` RTL already recovered the bigger IPC wins, so the\\\\nnext micro-opt is most likely another Fmax-and-LUT cleanup in the hot ALU\\\\ndatapath. `rtl/alu.sv` now shares `MUL*` through one 33x33 signed product,\\\\nwhich was a good first step, but it still widens both operands by a sign bit\\\\nand routes a 66-bit product even for plain `MUL` and `MULHU`. On the Tang\\\\nNano 20K, trimming that extra width and glue logic is attractive because the\\\\ncore is still above its LUT target and the previous multiplier cleanup already\\\\nproved this region is fitness-sensitive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` RTL already recovered the bigger IPC wins, so the\\\\nnext micro-opt is most likely another Fmax-and-LUT cleanup in the hot ALU\\\\ndatapath. `rtl/alu.sv` now shares `MUL*` through one 33x33 signed product,\\\\nwhich was a good first step, but it still widens both operands by a sign bit\\\\nand routes a 66-bit product even for plain `MUL` and `MULHU`. On the Tang\\\\nNano 20K, trimming that extra width and glue logic is attractive because the\\\\ncore is still above its LUT target and the previous multiplier cleanup already\\\\nproved this region is fitness-sensitive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the current sign-extended 33x33 shared multiply with a DSP-friendlier\\\\n32x32 unsigned product plus small signed-high correction terms for `MULH` and\\\\n`MULHSU`. `MUL` can still take the low 32 bits directly, `MULHU` can use the\\\\nraw high half, and the signed variants can derive their high halves by\\\\nsubtracting the operand-dependent correction terms instead of carrying an\\\\nextra sign bit through the full multiplier. This keeps all `MUL*` operations\\\\nsingle-cycle, avoids the control risk of a multicycle M-unit, preserves the\\\\nexisting DIV/REM and ALTOPS behavior, and should further reduce multiplier\\\\nrouting pressure and LUT glue without giving back ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n15: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-entry posted store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry posted-store buffer that captures a store's aligned address,\\\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible metadata when the store\\\\nreaches MEM. If the external dmem port stalls, retire the store from the buffer\\\\nimmediately, keep draining the buffered write in the background, and allow\\\\nyounger non-memory instructions to keep flowing. Only stall when a younger\\\\nload or store reaches MEM while the buffer is still occupied, preserving\\\\nin-order memory semantics without reintroducing load-use or control-path\\\\ncomplexity. This should recover CoreMark IPC from random dmem backpressure on\\\\nstore-heavy regions while avoiding the IF/ID critical-path regressions seen in\\\\nearlier structural experiments.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the MEM-stage/retirement bundles with store-buffer bookkeepin... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n16: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r5s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Conflict-tolerant replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` frontend already gets real value from the tiny\\\\nsequential replay predictor, which means the safest remaining predictor win\\\\nis probably higher replay hit rate rather than deeper control speculation.\\\\nToday the table is only 8 entries and direct-mapped, so hot CoreMark PCs\\\\nthat alias on the same low index bits can evict each other and turn later\\\\nrandom `io_imemReady=0` cycles back into bubbles.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` frontend already gets real value from the tiny\\\\nsequential replay predictor, which means the safest remaining predictor win\\\\nis probably higher replay hit rate rather than deeper control speculation.\\\\nToday the table is only 8 entries and direct-mapped, so hot CoreMark PCs\\\\nthat alias on the same low index bits can evict each other and turn later\\\\nrandom `io_imemReady=0` cycles back into bubbles.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the direct-mapped 8-entry `{pc,instr}` replay table in `if_stage`\\\\nwith a small conflict-tolerant structure, preferably 2-way set-associative\\\\nwith a simple per-set MRU or round-robin replacement bit, while keeping the\\\\nexisting one-cycle-ahead replay candidate scheme and all predictor state\\\\nlocal to IF. The key point is that `fetch_available` still depends only on\\\\n`imem_ready || replay_hit`; the wider table lookup is only used to bank the\\\\nnext cycle's candidate, so this should improve replay coverage under random\\\\niStalls without reintroducing the IF decode, EX-visible prediction metadata,\\\\nor formal-state growth that hurt earlier predictor attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the direct-mapped replay table with a small conflict-tolerant replay predictor, keeping the live-fetch-first behavior and the current redirect/flush safety rules.\\\"}, {\\\"fi... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n17: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r6s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"EX fast-path add/address bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest safe `bench` gains have come from local backend changes that\\\\nrecovered IPC or reduced hot-path routing without widening the front-end\\\\ncontrol logic. After the replay cache, MEM-to-EX load bypass, and posted\\\\nstore buffer, the next likely win is Fmax: the common EX add/address path\\\\nstill goes through the same mixed-function ALU block that also carries\\\\nshifts, compares, the shared multiplier, and combinational DIV/REM logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest safe `bench` gains have come from local backend changes that\\\\nrecovered IPC or reduced hot-path routing without widening the front-end\\\\ncontrol logic. After the replay cache, MEM-to-EX load bypass, and posted\\\\nstore buffer, the next likely win is Fmax: the common EX add/address path\\\\nstill goes through the same mixed-function ALU block that also carries\\\\nshifts, compares, the shared multiplier, and combinational DIV/REM logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split execute into a dedicated carry-chain fast path for the common\\\\nADD-class work and a side path for rarer logic/shift/M-extension work.\\\\n`ex_stage.sv` would detect plain ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and load/store address\\\\ngeneration and take a direct 32-bit adder result, while `alu.sv` is\\\\nrefactored so those hot operations no longer share the same wide output mux\\\\nand multiplier/divider fanout as the rare RV32M cases. This preserves the\\\\ncurrent single-cycle semantics and RVFI contract, avoids the control-heavy\\\\nmulticycle DIV/REM regressions, and should improve Tang Nano 20K timing on\\\\nthe instructions CoreMark executes most often.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a direct fast-path adder select for ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and memory address generation so common EX results bypass the mixed-function ALU result path.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"des... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n18: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Alias-aware load bypass around posted stores\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing posted store buffer, but make it address-aware instead of\\\\nblocking all younger memory ops. When a younger load reaches MEM while a\\\\nmain-memory store is buffered, compare the load's aligned word address and\\\\nbyte mask against the buffered store: if there is no overlap, let the load use\\\\nthe dmem port immediately and drain the buffered store later; if the load is\\\\nfully covered by the buffered bytes, forward from the buffer; only stall on\\\\ntrue alias or partial-overlap cases, and keep younger stores conservative.\\\\nThis is a backend-only structural change that compounds with the winning\\\\nposted-store buffer while avoiding the IF/ID timing regressions seen in prior\\\\nstructural attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add store-buffer alias checking, demand-load-vs-drain arbitration, and buffered-store forwarding for fully covered younger loads.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the blank... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n19: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r6s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Dual-candidate control-aware replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The winning predictor on `bench` is the tiny IF replay table, not earlier\\\\nbranch resolution or heavier control speculation. Recent failures showed that\\\\nadding decode-to-PC steering, prediction metadata, or wider replay RAM hurts\\\\neither Fmax or formal cost, yet the current single replay candidate still\\\\ngoes blind across hot taken backedges and direct-call targets because it only\\\\ntracks the sequential `pc+4` path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The winning predictor on `bench` is the tiny IF replay table, not earlier\\\\nbranch resolution or heavier control speculation. Recent failures showed that\\\\nadding decode-to-PC steering, prediction metadata, or wider replay RAM hurts\\\\neither Fmax or formal cost, yet the current single replay candidate still\\\\ngoes blind across hot taken backedges and direct-call targets because it only\\\\ntracks the sequential `pc+4` path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing replay-table frontend and EX redirect contract, but add a\\\\nsecond replay candidate slot dedicated to likely control successors. When a\\\\nlive fetch sees a direct JAL or an obvious backward B-type loop branch,\\\\n`if_stage` does a decode-light target calculation off the fetched instruction,\\\\nlooks that target up in the existing `{pc,instr}` replay table, and banks it\\\\nas a control-target candidate while the normal sequential `pc+4` candidate\\\\nstays unchanged. Fetch still walks `pc+4` until EX redirects, so there is no\\\\nnew predicted-PC state in IF/ID/EX; the win comes from having the first\\\\npost-redirect target instruction already replayable if `io_imemReady` drops.\\\\nThis aims at the same hot loop/call locality as prior control predictors, but\\\\nstays off the live fetch critical path and avoids the metadata/formal blow-up\\\\nthat hurt the earlier static and learned next-PC attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"mediu... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n20: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r7s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"EX operand-isolated M/control side paths\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent winning `bench` changes came from trimming hot backend cones, not from\\\\nadding more speculative state. `ex_stage.sv` still routes BRANCH/JAL/JALR through\\\\nthe generic ALU plumbing even though branches only need compare/target logic and\\\\njumps retire `pc+4`, while `alu.sv` always builds the multiplier/divider operand\\\\ncone around live `a`/`b`. On Tang Nano 20K, that extra fanout and muxing is a\\\\nplausible remaining Fmax tax after the add/address fast path already landed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent winning `bench` changes came from trimming hot backend cones, not from\\\\nadding more speculative state. `ex_stage.sv` still routes BRANCH/JAL/JALR through\\\\nthe generic ALU plumbing even though branches only need compare/target logic and\\\\njumps retire `pc+4`, while `alu.sv` always builds the multiplier/divider operand\\\\ncone around live `a`/`b`. On Tang Nano 20K, that extra fanout and muxing is a\\\\nplausible remaining Fmax tax after the add/address fast path already landed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the current single-cycle semantics, but split execute into operation-class\\\\nside paths so only the relevant cone sees hot operands. In `ex_stage.sv`, treat\\\\nBRANCH/JAL/JALR as control-only ops that bypass the generic ALU result path, and\\\\nin `alu.sv` split simple logic/shift/compare work from a dedicated M-extension\\\\nsubpath so MUL/DIV/REM operand extension and result selection are only active for\\\\n`ALU_M*` ops. This is a narrower follow-on to the successful fast-add bypass: it\\\\nremoves more unnecessary mux/fanout from the common EX datapath without\\\\nreintroducing the multicycle divider control risk that already failed on `bench`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Classify control-only operations and bypass the generic ALU for BRANCH/JAL/JA... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n21: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r7s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Two-entry posted store FIFO\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single posted-store slot with a tiny two-entry in-order store\\\\nFIFO that can absorb a short burst of normal-RAM stores while older buffered\\\\nwrites drain in the background. Keep the current conservative memory-order\\\\nrule that younger loads still wait until the FIFO is empty, and avoid the\\\\nlosing alias-compare/forwarding path entirely; the gain should come from\\\\nletting consecutive stores and following non-memory instructions retire\\\\nacross multi-cycle `io_dmemReady` stalls without touching IF timing or the\\\\nEX critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/store_queue.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a tiny two-entry in-order posted-store FIFO with enqueue/dequeue logic and head/tail status for MEM arbitration.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single buffered-store slot with the FIFO so a postable store can enqueue behind an older buffered store while preserving the existing conservative load ordering and RVFI store reporting.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"de... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n22: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r7s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Victim-slot replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest predictor result on `bench` is still the tiny sequential replay\\\\ntable in `if_stage`, while every attempt that steered PC early or added wider\\\\ncontrol bookkeeping either regressed badly or only recovered a very small\\\\nbenefit. The remaining plausible predictor win is therefore not deeper branch\\\\nspeculation, but a cheaper way to recover replay-table conflict misses than\\\\nthe prior 2-way and control-target variants.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest predictor result on `bench` is still the tiny sequential replay\\\\ntable in `if_stage`, while every attempt that steered PC early or added wider\\\\ncontrol bookkeeping either regressed badly or only recovered a very small\\\\nbenefit. The remaining plausible predictor win is therefore not deeper branch\\\\nspeculation, but a cheaper way to recover replay-table conflict misses than\\\\nthe prior 2-way and control-target variants.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing 8-entry direct-mapped `{pc,instr}` replay table and the\\\\ncurrent one-cycle-ahead candidate scheme, but add a single fully-associative\\\\nvictim entry that captures only the most recently evicted replay line. On a\\\\nlive fetch that would overwrite a different valid PC in the direct-mapped\\\\nslot, spill that old `{pc,instr}` pair into the victim entry; when banking the\\\\nnext `pc+4` replay candidate, check the indexed slot first and then the victim\\\\nentry before giving up. This directly targets the low-bit alias pattern that\\\\nmotivated the earlier set-associative attempt, but avoids dual-way lookup,\\\\nMRU state, EX-visible prediction metadata, target-add logic, or any change to\\\\n`fetch_available = imem_ready || replay_hit`. If CoreMark's hot sequential PCs\\\\nmostly bounce between a few colliding indices, a one-entry victim should claw\\\\nback some replay hits at materially lower timing cost than widening the main\\\\ntable.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n23: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r8s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Aligned LW/SW MEM fast path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the biggest front-end and\\\\nEX-stage wins, so the next plausible limiter is the MEM-stage cone. Today\\\\n`mem_stage.sv` handles byte/halfword extraction, sign extension, byte-mask\\\\ngeneration, store-data replication, posted-store capture, and same-cycle\\\\nMEM-to-EX load bypass in one place even though CoreMark spends most of its\\\\ntimed region on aligned word loads and stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the biggest front-end and\\\\nEX-stage wins, so the next plausible limiter is the MEM-stage cone. Today\\\\n`mem_stage.sv` handles byte/halfword extraction, sign extension, byte-mask\\\\ngeneration, store-data replication, posted-store capture, and same-cycle\\\\nMEM-to-EX load bypass in one place even though CoreMark spends most of its\\\\ntimed region on aligned word loads and stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split common aligned `LW`/`SW` traffic onto a dedicated memory fast path and\\\\nleave byte/halfword formatting on a rarer side path. Precompute store-lane\\\\nmask and replicated store data as soon as the effective address and\\\\nforwarded store operand are known, then let aligned word loads forward raw\\\\n`dmem_rdata` directly to EX without passing through the generic\\\\nshift-and-sign-extend mux. Byte/halfword cases, misalignment traps, posted\\\\nstores, and RVFI masks still stay exact, but the hot CoreMark path loses\\\\nunnecessary lane-steering logic on both the dmem output cone and the\\\\nMEM-to-EX load-bypass cone. Because this removes logic from the common\\\\nmemory path instead of adding alias checks or deeper queues, it is a better\\\\nfit for Tang Nano 20K than the prior memory-side regressions.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the EX/MEM bundle with preformatted store-byte-lane metadata a... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n24: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve direct JAL in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow early-redirect path for legal `JAL` instructions in ID while\\\\nleaving BRANCH and JALR resolution in EX. `JAL` needs only `pc + imm`, so it\\\\ncan redirect one stage earlier without the operand-bypass, compare, and\\\\nload-interlock machinery that made full ID-stage control resolution collapse\\\\nFmax. Carry an `early_redirected`/resolved-next-PC bit with the instruction so\\\\nEX suppresses the duplicate redirect but still produces the normal `rd = pc+4`\\\\nwriteback and RVFI retirement record. This should shave a bubble from hot\\\\ndirect calls and unconditional jumps in CoreMark while keeping the replay\\\\nfrontend, posted store buffer, and hot EX datapath essentially unchanged.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the decode/pipeline control bundles with a bit that marks a JAL as already redirected before EX, plus any resolved next-PC metadata needed to suppress duplicate redirects later.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JALs in decode, compute `pc + imm`, and... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n25: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r8s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Redirect landing-pad replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The existing `bench` predictor win came from the tiny sequential replay path,\\\\nwhile heavier control speculation, wider replay tables, and IF-side target\\\\ndecode all regressed. The remaining frontend blind spot is the first fetch\\\\nafter a taken redirect: hot loop headers and helper-call targets are revisited\\\\noften, but the current replay candidate is cleared exactly when those PCs\\\\nbecome useful again.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The existing `bench` predictor win came from the tiny sequential replay path,\\\\nwhile heavier control speculation, wider replay tables, and IF-side target\\\\ndecode all regressed. The remaining frontend blind spot is the first fetch\\\\nafter a taken redirect: hot loop headers and helper-call targets are revisited\\\\noften, but the current replay candidate is cleared exactly when those PCs\\\\nbecome useful again.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny redirect-target landing-pad cache inside `if_stage`, trained only\\\\nfrom successful post-redirect fetches. When EX redirects, remember the target\\\\nPC for one cycle; if the first fetch at that target later sees `io_imemReady=0`\\\\nand the landing-pad cache has a matching `{pc, instr}` entry, serve that\\\\ncached instruction instead of bubbling. Keep the existing sequential replay\\\\ntable unchanged for the common `pc+4` case so the new logic only touches the\\\\nnarrow post-redirect path, and let the normal replay candidate take over again\\\\nafter the landing-pad instruction issues. Compared with the prior redirect-\\\\ntarget replay attempt, this avoids an always-on lookup into the general replay\\\\ntable and removes dependence on that table still holding the target line,\\\\nwhich should make the control-side replay win cheaper in both timing and\\\\nrouting.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a tiny redirect-tar... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n26: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r9s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Retimed MEM/WB writeback path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core has already harvested the largest IPC wins,\\\\nso the next realistic gain is another Fmax cleanup on a path exercised by\\\\nalmost every dependent instruction. Today the final `rd` value is still\\\\nselected late in `wb_stage` and then fanned out into MEM/WB forwarding,\\\\nregfile writeback, same-cycle decode read bypass, and RVFI `rd_wdata`.\\\\nThat keeps an avoidable mux and extra routing on a very hot backend path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core has already harvested the largest IPC wins,\\\\nso the next realistic gain is another Fmax cleanup on a path exercised by\\\\nalmost every dependent instruction. Today the final `rd` value is still\\\\nselected late in `wb_stage` and then fanned out into MEM/WB forwarding,\\\\nregfile writeback, same-cycle decode read bypass, and RVFI `rd_wdata`.\\\\nThat keeps an avoidable mux and extra routing on a very hot backend path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Register the final writeback word at the MEM/WB boundary instead of\\\\nrecomputing it after the MEM/WB flop. `mem_stage.sv` can already see both\\\\n`alu_result` and the formed load data, so it can write a single `wb_data`\\\\nfield into `mem_wb_t`; downstream logic would then forward, write the\\\\nregfile, and drive RVFI from that registered value directly. Pair that with\\\\na simpler regfile/read-bypass arrangement so the MEM/WB-to-ID/EX dependency\\\\npath no longer pays the `mem_to_reg` select plus duplicated write-first\\\\nmuxing on every read port. This stays fully in-order, preserves the current\\\\nsingle-cycle load-use behavior and RVFI contract, and is a more local timing\\\\noptimization than the control-side and memory-side experiments that already\\\\nregressed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a registered final writeback-data field to `mem_wb_t` so... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve canonical RET in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing ID redirect path to cover canonical aligned returns,\\\\ne.g. `jalr x0, x1, 0` and `jalr x0, x5, 0`, when the return-address\\\\nregister is already architecturally available from the regfile/WB path. If\\\\nan older in-flight instruction still owns that source register, simply fall\\\\nback to the current EX-stage JALR path instead of adding the full ID-stage\\\\nbypass/interlock machinery that regressed earlier. This reuses the existing\\\\n`id_redirect` plumbing, carries an \\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through\\\\nthe pipeline so EX suppresses the duplicate redirect, and should cut\\\\nanother hot function-return bubble in CoreMark with much lower timing risk\\\\nthan general early JALR/BRANCH resolution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the existing `jal_early_redirect` pipeline bit into an ID-resolved jump marker that can cover both direct `JAL` and narrow early-return `JALR` cases.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"descr... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n28: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Canonical return-address stack predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` already found its best predictor win in the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay path, and the recent structural JAL improvement removed the cheapest\\\\nunconditional jump bubble. The remaining hot control penalty is canonical\\\\nfunction return traffic: `ret`-shaped `JALR` instructions still wait for EX,\\\\nwhile broader IF-side branch prediction, wider replay tables, and redirect\\\\nlanding-pad schemes all failed to repay their timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` already found its best predictor win in the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay path, and the recent structural JAL improvement removed the cheapest\\\\nunconditional jump bubble. The remaining hot control penalty is canonical\\\\nfunction return traffic: `ret`-shaped `JALR` instructions still wait for EX,\\\\nwhile broader IF-side branch prediction, wider replay tables, and redirect\\\\nlanding-pad schemes all failed to repay their timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a very small return-address stack that predicts only the common ABI call\\\\nand return pattern instead of general branch targets. Push `pc+4` for direct\\\\ncalls that write `x1`, use the top entry to early-redirect only canonical\\\\n`ret`/`jalr x0, 0(x1)` in ID, and carry the predicted target forward so EX\\\\ncan suppress the duplicate redirect on a hit or repair the path on a rare\\\\nmismatch. This stays much narrower than the failed branch/JAL predictors: it\\\\ndoes not widen the replay table, does not add BTB-style target RAM, and only\\\\nadds prediction metadata to the small subset of return instructions. CoreMark\\\\nis call/return heavy enough that shaving the return bubble should improve IPC\\\\nwhile keeping Fmax risk materially lower than earlier full-control schemes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the ID/EX bundle with narrow early... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n29: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r10s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-gated operand bypass cleanup\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent writeback-retime regression showed that the remaining backend\\\\nlimiter is not just where `wb_data` is selected, but the always-on\\\\nWB-to-ID read-bypass compares and muxes that sit on both regfile read\\\\nports every cycle. On this core, many hot CoreMark instructions\\\\n(`OP-IMM`, `LOAD`, `JALR`, `AUIPC`, `LUI`, `JAL`) do not consume `rs2`,\\\\nand some consume neither source, yet the current datapath still drives raw\\\\ninstruction bitfields through regfile read, bypass, and forwarding logic.\\\\nTrimming that unnecessary operand work is a plausible remaining Fmax win\\\\nthat stays off the fragile IF/control paths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent writeback-retime regression showed that the remaining backend\\\\nlimiter is not just where `wb_data` is selected, but the always-on\\\\nWB-to-ID read-bypass compares and muxes that sit on both regfile read\\\\nports every cycle. On this core, many hot CoreMark instructions\\\\n(`OP-IMM`, `LOAD`, `JALR`, `AUIPC`, `LUI`, `JAL`) do not consume `rs2`,\\\\nand some consume neither source, yet the current datapath still drives raw\\\\ninstruction bitfields through regfile read, bypass, and forwarding logic.\\\\nTrimming that unnecessary operand work is a plausible remaining Fmax win\\\\nthat stays off the fragile IF/control paths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add exact source-usage classification in decode and use it to normalize\\\\nunused source operands to `x0` before they enter the hot read/bypass path.\\\\nThen simplify the register-file/writeback interaction so same-cycle WB\\\\nbypass is only applied to architecturally live operands, ideally by moving\\\\nthe compare+mux out of the generic regfile read ports and into the decode\\\\nstage after source-use is known. Forwarding logic should see zeroed unused\\\\nsource addresses, and retirement should carry the architecturally consumed\\\\nsource values so RVFI stays exact; illegal or otherwise uncl... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n30: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drain-and-replace posted store slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing single posted-store slot, but let it dequeue the older\\\\nbuffered store to dmem and capture one younger postable store in the same\\\\ncycle. In practice this means teaching `mem_stage` to drain and replace the\\\\nslot when `dmem_ready` comes back, and teaching `hazard_unit` not to stall\\\\nan EX/MEM store that can retire into the just-freed slot while still keeping\\\\nyounger loads and non-postable stores conservative. This should recover some\\\\nof the store-burst benefit the two-entry FIFO targeted, without adding\\\\nanother queue entry, alias-compare logic, or front-end timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Allow the one-entry posted store buffer to drain an older buffered RAM store and capture a younger postable store in the same cycle, ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n31: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r10s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Seed replay from ID-resolved JAL targets\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest `bench` predictor result is still the tiny registered replay\\\\ntable, and predictor attempts that widened IF control, added target decode,\\\\nor carried prediction metadata into later stages consistently lost fitness.\\\\nAfter the recent ID-stage `JAL` win, the next control-side blind spot is the\\\\nvery first fetch at a direct-call target: the target PC is now known exactly\\\\none cycle early, but `if_stage` still drops replay state on `id_redirect`, so\\\\na warmed callee entry point bubbles whenever `io_imemReady=0` on that first\\\\ntarget cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest `bench` predictor result is still the tiny registered replay\\\\ntable, and predictor attempts that widened IF control, added target decode,\\\\nor carried prediction metadata into later stages consistently lost fitness.\\\\nAfter the recent ID-stage `JAL` win, the next control-side blind spot is the\\\\nvery first fetch at a direct-call target: the target PC is now known exactly\\\\none cycle early, but `if_stage` still drops replay state on `id_redirect`, so\\\\na warmed callee entry point bubbles whenever `io_imemReady=0` on that first\\\\ntarget cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the existing `{pc,instr}` replay table to seed the next-cycle replay\\\\ncandidate from an exact `id_redirect_target` when decode resolves a legal\\\\naligned `JAL`. On a live ID redirect, `if_stage` would probe the existing\\\\nreplay table with the JAL target and, on a hit, load `replay_valid_q`,\\\\n`replay_pc_q`, and `replay_instr_q` for the upcoming target fetch instead of\\\\nunconditionally clearing them. Keep EX-stage redirect handling, sequential\\\\nreplay fill, and `fetch_available = imem_ready || replay_hit` unchanged, so\\\\nthis stays in the proven replay-family design space: no IF-side immediate\\\\ndecode, no new predicted-PC metadata through ID/EX, and no broader branch or\\\\nreturn speculation. The pay... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n32: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r11s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Normalize unused legal source reads\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The biggest `bench` gains have come from narrow backend cleanups that cut\\\\nhot-path routing without widening IF or control logic. After the JAL-in-ID,\\\\nposted-store, and add-fast-path wins, the remaining backend tax is that ID\\\\nstill drives raw instruction bitfields into the regfile, same-cycle WB read\\\\nbypass, and EX forwarding compares even for legal instructions that do not\\\\narchitecturally read `rs1` and/or `rs2`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The biggest `bench` gains have come from narrow backend cleanups that cut\\\\nhot-path routing without widening IF or control logic. After the JAL-in-ID,\\\\nposted-store, and add-fast-path wins, the remaining backend tax is that ID\\\\nstill drives raw instruction bitfields into the regfile, same-cycle WB read\\\\nbypass, and EX forwarding compares even for legal instructions that do not\\\\narchitecturally read `rs1` and/or `rs2`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add exact `uses_rs1` / `uses_rs2` classification for legal instructions, and\\\\nuse it only to normalize definitely-unused source addresses to `x0` before\\\\nthey enter the hot read/bypass path. Keep the existing write-first regfile\\\\ncontract intact and do not move bypassing out of `reg_file`; the key change\\\\nis to stop reading and comparing random immediate bits on opclasses like\\\\n`LUI`, `AUIPC`, `JAL`, `FENCE`, `EBREAK`, and the `rs2`-unused I-type ops.\\\\nFor safety, illegal or otherwise unvalidated encodings should stay\\\\nconservative, with source-use bits only cleared inside positively validated\\\\ndecoder arms. This should shrink the always-on WB-to-ID compare/mux work and\\\\nthe downstream forwarding compare fanout while staying off the fragile IF,\\\\nbranch, and memory-control paths that already regressed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/decoder.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Emit exact `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` bits... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n33: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Replay-backed one-slot IF decoupler\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Turn the registered replay candidate into a true one-slot IF decoupler. When\\\\nthe current PC already matches a valid replay entry, let decode consume the\\\\ncached `{pc,instr}` while the live imem port fetches the next sequential PC and\\\\nrefreshes the lookahead slot/table in the same cycle. Keep the current\\\\nredirect/flush rules, keep all state local to `if_stage`, and do not add IF-side\\\\nbranch decode, target tables, or a multi-entry queue. This should raise\\\\nCoreMark iterations/cycle by converting hot replay hits into useful prefetch\\\\nbandwidth, recovering more random iStall loss than the fallback-only replay\\\\npath without the timing cost that sank broader fetch-queue and control-predictor\\\\nattempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"des... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n34: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r11s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Warm-table current-PC replay fallback\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The winning predictor on `bench` is still the tiny sequential replay table,\\\\nbut it only helps when the next-PC candidate was banked in the prior cycle.\\\\nAfter redirects, flushes, or other candidate-clearing events, the frontend\\\\ncan still bubble on a warm PC even though the replay table already holds the\\\\ncorrect instruction for that address.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The winning predictor on `bench` is still the tiny sequential replay table,\\\\nbut it only helps when the next-PC candidate was banked in the prior cycle.\\\\nAfter redirects, flushes, or other candidate-clearing events, the frontend\\\\ncan still bubble on a warm PC even though the replay table already holds the\\\\ncorrect instruction for that address.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing one-cycle replay candidate and all current redirect rules,\\\\nbut add a cheaper fallback path inside `if_stage`: when `io_imemReady=0` and\\\\nthe registered replay candidate misses, probe the existing direct-mapped\\\\n`{pc,instr}` table for the current PC and serve that cached instruction on a\\\\nhit. This does not steer fetch to a predicted target, does not add branch or\\\\nreturn metadata, and does not widen the table; it only lets the frontend\\\\nexploit already-warm entries when `replay_valid_q` was intentionally cleared.\\\\nThat specifically targets the current post-redirect and post-flush blind spot\\\\nwith less timing risk than target-add logic, second candidate slots, or wider\\\\nassociative replay structures.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a current-PC fallback hit path that reuses the existing replay table when `imem_ready=0` and the registered replay candidate is invalid or mismatched, while preserving the existing redirect, flush, and sequential candidate behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"des... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n35: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r12s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"LUTRAM-backed reset-valid register file\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current backend still carries a 32x32 flop-based register file with a\\\\nfull synchronous clear loop. On FPGA that reset fanout and FF routing sit on\\\\na hot path touched by decode reads, write-first bypass, and EX dependencies,\\\\nand they likely block the synthesis patterns that would infer cheaper\\\\ndistributed RAM.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current backend still carries a 32x32 flop-based register file with a\\\\nfull synchronous clear loop. On FPGA that reset fanout and FF routing sit on\\\\na hot path touched by decode reads, write-first bypass, and EX dependencies,\\\\nand they likely block the synthesis patterns that would infer cheaper\\\\ndistributed RAM.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Re-implement `rtl/reg_file.sv` as an FPGA-oriented 2-read/1-write register\\\\nfile that keeps x0 hardwired and same-cycle write-first bypass, but removes\\\\nthe per-word storage reset from the 1024-bit data array. Preserve the\\\\nexisting \\\\\\\"reset clears all architectural registers\\\\\\\" behavior with a small\\\\nper-register valid bitmap or equivalent epoch tag that resets cheaply and\\\\nforces unread entries to zero until rewritten. This should let\\\\nYosys/nextpnr map the data array to LUTRAM or other denser memory resources,\\\\ncut reset routing and FF pressure, and shorten the hot WB-to-ID/read path\\\\nwithout touching IF control, memory ordering, or RVFI source snapshots.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/reg_file.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the flop-array plus full synchronous clear with an FPGA-friendly inferred memory implementation, preserving x0 hardwiring and write-first bypass while using a small reset-valid structure to keep architectural reset-to-zero semantics.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_reg_file.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the regfile unit tests to verify that reset still makes all architectural reads return z... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n36: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r12s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve ready branch-to-zero in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already harvested the cheapest broad structural wins: sequential IF\\\\nreplay, MEM-to-EX load forwarding, a one-entry posted store buffer, and an\\\\nexact early `JAL` redirect. The next visible IPC loss is still the taken\\\\nbranch bubble, but prior history shows that full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR\\\\nresolution collapses Fmax once decode has to do general bypass and\\\\ninterlock work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already harvested the cheapest broad structural wins: sequential IF\\\\nreplay, MEM-to-EX load forwarding, a one-entry posted store buffer, and an\\\\nexact early `JAL` redirect. The next visible IPC loss is still the taken\\\\nbranch bubble, but prior history shows that full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR\\\\nresolution collapses Fmax once decode has to do general bypass and\\\\ninterlock work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the existing `id_redirect` path for a much narrower exact control case:\\\\nlegal, aligned branch-against-zero instructions whose single live source is\\\\nalready architecturally ready in the regfile/WB path. Detect `BEQ/BNE` and\\\\nsigned zero-compare branch pseudos (`BLT/BGE` with `rs2 = x0`) in\\\\n`id_stage`, guard them with a cheap \\\\\\\"rs1 not pending in ID/EX or EX/MEM\\\\\\\"\\\\ncheck, and redirect taken cases one stage earlier while falling back to the\\\\nunchanged EX-stage branch path for everything else. Carry a generic\\\\n\\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through `id_ex_t` so EX suppresses duplicate\\\\nredirects but still recomputes `pc_next` and trap behavior, keeping RVFI\\\\nsource snapshots correct because the early path only fires when decode is\\\\nalready reading the final architecturally consumed operand.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the current JAL-only early-redirect marker in `id_ex_t` into a generic ID-resolved-control bit that can also tag ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n37: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r12s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Predict backward branches in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already found its best front-end wins by keeping speculation narrow:\\\\nthe sequential replay table helps with random iStalls, and the current best\\\\nsource moved only unconditional `JAL` into ID. The biggest remaining control\\\\nbubble is still the hot loop backedge branch, but the prior full ID-stage\\\\nBRANCH/JALR resolution failed because decode-side compare, bypass, and\\\\nload-interlock logic were too timing-expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already found its best front-end wins by keeping speculation narrow:\\\\nthe sequential replay table helps with random iStalls, and the current best\\\\nsource moved only unconditional `JAL` into ID. The biggest remaining control\\\\nbubble is still the hot loop backedge branch, but the prior full ID-stage\\\\nBRANCH/JALR resolution failed because decode-side compare, bypass, and\\\\nload-interlock logic were too timing-expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing ID redirect path from \\\\\\\"exact early JAL\\\\\\\" to a static\\\\nbackward-branch predictor that redirects only legal, aligned B-type\\\\ninstructions with a negative immediate. This keeps prediction out of IF,\\\\nreuses the already-cheap `pc + imm` work that ID does for JAL, and avoids\\\\nthe operand-bypass/compare machinery that sank exact early branch\\\\nresolution. EX still computes the real branch condition and target, but it\\\\nonly repairs mispredicted loop exits by redirecting back to `pc+4`; correct\\\\ntaken backedges simply suppress the duplicate EX redirect. That targets a\\\\nhot CoreMark penalty with much lower Fmax risk than IF-side branch target\\\\nprediction, RAS state, or full decode-time BRANCH/JALR resolution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the existing JAL-only early-redirect metadata in `id_ex_t` into a generic ID-predicted-control bit for J... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n\\n(End of file - total 37 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Redirect-aware replay target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` frontend already proved that a tiny registered replay\\\\npredictor can recover a meaningful slice of random `io_imemReady` stalls\\\\nwithout hurting the IF critical path. Its remaining blind spot is taken\\\\ncontrol flow: every EX redirect clears the replay candidate, so hot loop\\\\nbackedges and direct-call targets lose replay coverage exactly where\\\\nCoreMark revisits the same PCs most often.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` frontend already proved that a tiny registered replay\\\\npredictor can recover a meaningful slice of random `io_imemReady` stalls\\\\nwithout hurting the IF critical path. Its remaining blind spot is taken\\\\ncontrol flow: every EX redirect clears the replay candidate, so hot loop\\\\nbackedges and direct-call targets lose replay coverage exactly where\\\\nCoreMark revisits the same PCs most often.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing replay-table predictor inside `if_stage` so redirects\\\\npre-arm replay for the resolved target instead of unconditionally clearing\\\\nthe candidate. On a redirect, look up `redirect_target` in the existing\\\\n`{pc,instr}` table and bank that target instruction for the next cycle when\\\\nit is already cached; keep all prediction state local to IF and continue to\\\\nemit a bubble on the redirect cycle itself. This should preserve the timing\\\\nadvantage of the current replay design, improve post-redirect fetch\\\\navailability on hot loops and helper calls, and avoid the formal-state and\\\\nbookkeeping costs that broke the learned next-PC predictor.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Teach the replay predictor to bank a redirect-target replay candidate from the existing table, instead of always invalidating replay state on redirect, while keeping all predictor state local to IF.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r5s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"DSP-friendly corrected shared multiplier\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` RTL already recovered the bigger IPC wins, so the\\\\nnext micro-opt is most likely another Fmax-and-LUT cleanup in the hot ALU\\\\ndatapath. `rtl/alu.sv` now shares `MUL*` through one 33x33 signed product,\\\\nwhich was a good first step, but it still widens both operands by a sign bit\\\\nand routes a 66-bit product even for plain `MUL` and `MULHU`. On the Tang\\\\nNano 20K, trimming that extra width and glue logic is attractive because the\\\\ncore is still above its LUT target and the previous multiplier cleanup already\\\\nproved this region is fitness-sensitive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` RTL already recovered the bigger IPC wins, so the\\\\nnext micro-opt is most likely another Fmax-and-LUT cleanup in the hot ALU\\\\ndatapath. `rtl/alu.sv` now shares `MUL*` through one 33x33 signed product,\\\\nwhich was a good first step, but it still widens both operands by a sign bit\\\\nand routes a 66-bit product even for plain `MUL` and `MULHU`. On the Tang\\\\nNano 20K, trimming that extra width and glue logic is attractive because the\\\\ncore is still above its LUT target and the previous multiplier cleanup already\\\\nproved this region is fitness-sensitive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the current sign-extended 33x33 shared multiply with a DSP-friendlier\\\\n32x32 unsigned product plus small signed-high correction terms for `MULH` and\\\\n`MULHSU`. `MUL` can still take the low 32 bits directly, `MULHU` can use the\\\\nraw high half, and the signed variants can derive their high halves by\\\\nsubtracting the operand-dependent correction terms instead of carrying an\\\\nextra sign bit through the full multiplier. This keeps all `MUL*` operations\\\\nsingle-cycle, avoids the control risk of a multicycle M-unit, preserves the\\\\nexisting DIV/REM and ALTOPS behavior, and should further reduce multiplier\\\\nrouting pressure and LUT glue without giving back ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-entry posted store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry posted-store buffer that captures a store's aligned address,\\\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible metadata when the store\\\\nreaches MEM. If the external dmem port stalls, retire the store from the buffer\\\\nimmediately, keep draining the buffered write in the background, and allow\\\\nyounger non-memory instructions to keep flowing. Only stall when a younger\\\\nload or store reaches MEM while the buffer is still occupied, preserving\\\\nin-order memory semantics without reintroducing load-use or control-path\\\\ncomplexity. This should recover CoreMark IPC from random dmem backpressure on\\\\nstore-heavy regions while avoiding the IF/ID critical-path regressions seen in\\\\nearlier structural experiments.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the MEM-stage/retirement bundles with store-buffer bookkeepin... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r5s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Conflict-tolerant replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` frontend already gets real value from the tiny\\\\nsequential replay predictor, which means the safest remaining predictor win\\\\nis probably higher replay hit rate rather than deeper control speculation.\\\\nToday the table is only 8 entries and direct-mapped, so hot CoreMark PCs\\\\nthat alias on the same low index bits can evict each other and turn later\\\\nrandom `io_imemReady=0` cycles back into bubbles.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` frontend already gets real value from the tiny\\\\nsequential replay predictor, which means the safest remaining predictor win\\\\nis probably higher replay hit rate rather than deeper control speculation.\\\\nToday the table is only 8 entries and direct-mapped, so hot CoreMark PCs\\\\nthat alias on the same low index bits can evict each other and turn later\\\\nrandom `io_imemReady=0` cycles back into bubbles.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the direct-mapped 8-entry `{pc,instr}` replay table in `if_stage`\\\\nwith a small conflict-tolerant structure, preferably 2-way set-associative\\\\nwith a simple per-set MRU or round-robin replacement bit, while keeping the\\\\nexisting one-cycle-ahead replay candidate scheme and all predictor state\\\\nlocal to IF. The key point is that `fetch_available` still depends only on\\\\n`imem_ready || replay_hit`; the wider table lookup is only used to bank the\\\\nnext cycle's candidate, so this should improve replay coverage under random\\\\niStalls without reintroducing the IF decode, EX-visible prediction metadata,\\\\nor formal-state growth that hurt earlier predictor attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the direct-mapped replay table with a small conflict-tolerant replay predictor, keeping the live-fetch-first behavior and the current redirect/flush safety rules.\\\"}, {\\\"fi... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r6s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"EX fast-path add/address bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest safe `bench` gains have come from local backend changes that\\\\nrecovered IPC or reduced hot-path routing without widening the front-end\\\\ncontrol logic. After the replay cache, MEM-to-EX load bypass, and posted\\\\nstore buffer, the next likely win is Fmax: the common EX add/address path\\\\nstill goes through the same mixed-function ALU block that also carries\\\\nshifts, compares, the shared multiplier, and combinational DIV/REM logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest safe `bench` gains have come from local backend changes that\\\\nrecovered IPC or reduced hot-path routing without widening the front-end\\\\ncontrol logic. After the replay cache, MEM-to-EX load bypass, and posted\\\\nstore buffer, the next likely win is Fmax: the common EX add/address path\\\\nstill goes through the same mixed-function ALU block that also carries\\\\nshifts, compares, the shared multiplier, and combinational DIV/REM logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split execute into a dedicated carry-chain fast path for the common\\\\nADD-class work and a side path for rarer logic/shift/M-extension work.\\\\n`ex_stage.sv` would detect plain ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and load/store address\\\\ngeneration and take a direct 32-bit adder result, while `alu.sv` is\\\\nrefactored so those hot operations no longer share the same wide output mux\\\\nand multiplier/divider fanout as the rare RV32M cases. This preserves the\\\\ncurrent single-cycle semantics and RVFI contract, avoids the control-heavy\\\\nmulticycle DIV/REM regressions, and should improve Tang Nano 20K timing on\\\\nthe instructions CoreMark executes most often.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a direct fast-path adder select for ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and memory address generation so common EX results bypass the mixed-function ALU result path.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"des... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Alias-aware load bypass around posted stores\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing posted store buffer, but make it address-aware instead of\\\\nblocking all younger memory ops. When a younger load reaches MEM while a\\\\nmain-memory store is buffered, compare the load's aligned word address and\\\\nbyte mask against the buffered store: if there is no overlap, let the load use\\\\nthe dmem port immediately and drain the buffered store later; if the load is\\\\nfully covered by the buffered bytes, forward from the buffer; only stall on\\\\ntrue alias or partial-overlap cases, and keep younger stores conservative.\\\\nThis is a backend-only structural change that compounds with the winning\\\\nposted-store buffer while avoiding the IF/ID timing regressions seen in prior\\\\nstructural attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add store-buffer alias checking, demand-load-vs-drain arbitration, and buffered-store forwarding for fully covered younger loads.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the blank... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r6s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Dual-candidate control-aware replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The winning predictor on `bench` is the tiny IF replay table, not earlier\\\\nbranch resolution or heavier control speculation. Recent failures showed that\\\\nadding decode-to-PC steering, prediction metadata, or wider replay RAM hurts\\\\neither Fmax or formal cost, yet the current single replay candidate still\\\\ngoes blind across hot taken backedges and direct-call targets because it only\\\\ntracks the sequential `pc+4` path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The winning predictor on `bench` is the tiny IF replay table, not earlier\\\\nbranch resolution or heavier control speculation. Recent failures showed that\\\\nadding decode-to-PC steering, prediction metadata, or wider replay RAM hurts\\\\neither Fmax or formal cost, yet the current single replay candidate still\\\\ngoes blind across hot taken backedges and direct-call targets because it only\\\\ntracks the sequential `pc+4` path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing replay-table frontend and EX redirect contract, but add a\\\\nsecond replay candidate slot dedicated to likely control successors. When a\\\\nlive fetch sees a direct JAL or an obvious backward B-type loop branch,\\\\n`if_stage` does a decode-light target calculation off the fetched instruction,\\\\nlooks that target up in the existing `{pc,instr}` replay table, and banks it\\\\nas a control-target candidate while the normal sequential `pc+4` candidate\\\\nstays unchanged. Fetch still walks `pc+4` until EX redirects, so there is no\\\\nnew predicted-PC state in IF/ID/EX; the win comes from having the first\\\\npost-redirect target instruction already replayable if `io_imemReady` drops.\\\\nThis aims at the same hot loop/call locality as prior control predictors, but\\\\nstays off the live fetch critical path and avoids the metadata/formal blow-up\\\\nthat hurt the earlier static and learned next-PC attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"mediu... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r7s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"EX operand-isolated M/control side paths\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent winning `bench` changes came from trimming hot backend cones, not from\\\\nadding more speculative state. `ex_stage.sv` still routes BRANCH/JAL/JALR through\\\\nthe generic ALU plumbing even though branches only need compare/target logic and\\\\njumps retire `pc+4`, while `alu.sv` always builds the multiplier/divider operand\\\\ncone around live `a`/`b`. On Tang Nano 20K, that extra fanout and muxing is a\\\\nplausible remaining Fmax tax after the add/address fast path already landed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent winning `bench` changes came from trimming hot backend cones, not from\\\\nadding more speculative state. `ex_stage.sv` still routes BRANCH/JAL/JALR through\\\\nthe generic ALU plumbing even though branches only need compare/target logic and\\\\njumps retire `pc+4`, while `alu.sv` always builds the multiplier/divider operand\\\\ncone around live `a`/`b`. On Tang Nano 20K, that extra fanout and muxing is a\\\\nplausible remaining Fmax tax after the add/address fast path already landed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the current single-cycle semantics, but split execute into operation-class\\\\nside paths so only the relevant cone sees hot operands. In `ex_stage.sv`, treat\\\\nBRANCH/JAL/JALR as control-only ops that bypass the generic ALU result path, and\\\\nin `alu.sv` split simple logic/shift/compare work from a dedicated M-extension\\\\nsubpath so MUL/DIV/REM operand extension and result selection are only active for\\\\n`ALU_M*` ops. This is a narrower follow-on to the successful fast-add bypass: it\\\\nremoves more unnecessary mux/fanout from the common EX datapath without\\\\nreintroducing the multicycle divider control risk that already failed on `bench`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Classify control-only operations and bypass the generic ALU for BRANCH/JAL/JA... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r7s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Two-entry posted store FIFO\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single posted-store slot with a tiny two-entry in-order store\\\\nFIFO that can absorb a short burst of normal-RAM stores while older buffered\\\\nwrites drain in the background. Keep the current conservative memory-order\\\\nrule that younger loads still wait until the FIFO is empty, and avoid the\\\\nlosing alias-compare/forwarding path entirely; the gain should come from\\\\nletting consecutive stores and following non-memory instructions retire\\\\nacross multi-cycle `io_dmemReady` stalls without touching IF timing or the\\\\nEX critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/store_queue.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a tiny two-entry in-order posted-store FIFO with enqueue/dequeue logic and head/tail status for MEM arbitration.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single buffered-store slot with the FIFO so a postable store can enqueue behind an older buffered store while preserving the existing conservative load ordering and RVFI store reporting.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"de... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r7s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Victim-slot replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest predictor result on `bench` is still the tiny sequential replay\\\\ntable in `if_stage`, while every attempt that steered PC early or added wider\\\\ncontrol bookkeeping either regressed badly or only recovered a very small\\\\nbenefit. The remaining plausible predictor win is therefore not deeper branch\\\\nspeculation, but a cheaper way to recover replay-table conflict misses than\\\\nthe prior 2-way and control-target variants.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest predictor result on `bench` is still the tiny sequential replay\\\\ntable in `if_stage`, while every attempt that steered PC early or added wider\\\\ncontrol bookkeeping either regressed badly or only recovered a very small\\\\nbenefit. The remaining plausible predictor win is therefore not deeper branch\\\\nspeculation, but a cheaper way to recover replay-table conflict misses than\\\\nthe prior 2-way and control-target variants.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing 8-entry direct-mapped `{pc,instr}` replay table and the\\\\ncurrent one-cycle-ahead candidate scheme, but add a single fully-associative\\\\nvictim entry that captures only the most recently evicted replay line. On a\\\\nlive fetch that would overwrite a different valid PC in the direct-mapped\\\\nslot, spill that old `{pc,instr}` pair into the victim entry; when banking the\\\\nnext `pc+4` replay candidate, check the indexed slot first and then the victim\\\\nentry before giving up. This directly targets the low-bit alias pattern that\\\\nmotivated the earlier set-associative attempt, but avoids dual-way lookup,\\\\nMRU state, EX-visible prediction metadata, target-add logic, or any change to\\\\n`fetch_available = imem_ready || replay_hit`. If CoreMark's hot sequential PCs\\\\nmostly bounce between a few colliding indices, a one-entry victim should claw\\\\nback some replay hits at materially lower timing cost than widening the main\\\\ntable.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r8s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Aligned LW/SW MEM fast path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the biggest front-end and\\\\nEX-stage wins, so the next plausible limiter is the MEM-stage cone. Today\\\\n`mem_stage.sv` handles byte/halfword extraction, sign extension, byte-mask\\\\ngeneration, store-data replication, posted-store capture, and same-cycle\\\\nMEM-to-EX load bypass in one place even though CoreMark spends most of its\\\\ntimed region on aligned word loads and stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the biggest front-end and\\\\nEX-stage wins, so the next plausible limiter is the MEM-stage cone. Today\\\\n`mem_stage.sv` handles byte/halfword extraction, sign extension, byte-mask\\\\ngeneration, store-data replication, posted-store capture, and same-cycle\\\\nMEM-to-EX load bypass in one place even though CoreMark spends most of its\\\\ntimed region on aligned word loads and stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split common aligned `LW`/`SW` traffic onto a dedicated memory fast path and\\\\nleave byte/halfword formatting on a rarer side path. Precompute store-lane\\\\nmask and replicated store data as soon as the effective address and\\\\nforwarded store operand are known, then let aligned word loads forward raw\\\\n`dmem_rdata` directly to EX without passing through the generic\\\\nshift-and-sign-extend mux. Byte/halfword cases, misalignment traps, posted\\\\nstores, and RVFI masks still stay exact, but the hot CoreMark path loses\\\\nunnecessary lane-steering logic on both the dmem output cone and the\\\\nMEM-to-EX load-bypass cone. Because this removes logic from the common\\\\nmemory path instead of adding alias checks or deeper queues, it is a better\\\\nfit for Tang Nano 20K than the prior memory-side regressions.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the EX/MEM bundle with preformatted store-byte-lane metadata a... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve direct JAL in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow early-redirect path for legal `JAL` instructions in ID while\\\\nleaving BRANCH and JALR resolution in EX. `JAL` needs only `pc + imm`, so it\\\\ncan redirect one stage earlier without the operand-bypass, compare, and\\\\nload-interlock machinery that made full ID-stage control resolution collapse\\\\nFmax. Carry an `early_redirected`/resolved-next-PC bit with the instruction so\\\\nEX suppresses the duplicate redirect but still produces the normal `rd = pc+4`\\\\nwriteback and RVFI retirement record. This should shave a bubble from hot\\\\ndirect calls and unconditional jumps in CoreMark while keeping the replay\\\\nfrontend, posted store buffer, and hot EX datapath essentially unchanged.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the decode/pipeline control bundles with a bit that marks a JAL as already redirected before EX, plus any resolved next-PC metadata needed to suppress duplicate redirects later.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JALs in decode, compute `pc + imm`, and... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r8s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Redirect landing-pad replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The existing `bench` predictor win came from the tiny sequential replay path,\\\\nwhile heavier control speculation, wider replay tables, and IF-side target\\\\ndecode all regressed. The remaining frontend blind spot is the first fetch\\\\nafter a taken redirect: hot loop headers and helper-call targets are revisited\\\\noften, but the current replay candidate is cleared exactly when those PCs\\\\nbecome useful again.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The existing `bench` predictor win came from the tiny sequential replay path,\\\\nwhile heavier control speculation, wider replay tables, and IF-side target\\\\ndecode all regressed. The remaining frontend blind spot is the first fetch\\\\nafter a taken redirect: hot loop headers and helper-call targets are revisited\\\\noften, but the current replay candidate is cleared exactly when those PCs\\\\nbecome useful again.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny redirect-target landing-pad cache inside `if_stage`, trained only\\\\nfrom successful post-redirect fetches. When EX redirects, remember the target\\\\nPC for one cycle; if the first fetch at that target later sees `io_imemReady=0`\\\\nand the landing-pad cache has a matching `{pc, instr}` entry, serve that\\\\ncached instruction instead of bubbling. Keep the existing sequential replay\\\\ntable unchanged for the common `pc+4` case so the new logic only touches the\\\\nnarrow post-redirect path, and let the normal replay candidate take over again\\\\nafter the landing-pad instruction issues. Compared with the prior redirect-\\\\ntarget replay attempt, this avoids an always-on lookup into the general replay\\\\ntable and removes dependence on that table still holding the target line,\\\\nwhich should make the control-side replay win cheaper in both timing and\\\\nrouting.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a tiny redirect-tar... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r9s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Retimed MEM/WB writeback path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core has already harvested the largest IPC wins,\\\\nso the next realistic gain is another Fmax cleanup on a path exercised by\\\\nalmost every dependent instruction. Today the final `rd` value is still\\\\nselected late in `wb_stage` and then fanned out into MEM/WB forwarding,\\\\nregfile writeback, same-cycle decode read bypass, and RVFI `rd_wdata`.\\\\nThat keeps an avoidable mux and extra routing on a very hot backend path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core has already harvested the largest IPC wins,\\\\nso the next realistic gain is another Fmax cleanup on a path exercised by\\\\nalmost every dependent instruction. Today the final `rd` value is still\\\\nselected late in `wb_stage` and then fanned out into MEM/WB forwarding,\\\\nregfile writeback, same-cycle decode read bypass, and RVFI `rd_wdata`.\\\\nThat keeps an avoidable mux and extra routing on a very hot backend path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Register the final writeback word at the MEM/WB boundary instead of\\\\nrecomputing it after the MEM/WB flop. `mem_stage.sv` can already see both\\\\n`alu_result` and the formed load data, so it can write a single `wb_data`\\\\nfield into `mem_wb_t`; downstream logic would then forward, write the\\\\nregfile, and drive RVFI from that registered value directly. Pair that with\\\\na simpler regfile/read-bypass arrangement so the MEM/WB-to-ID/EX dependency\\\\npath no longer pays the `mem_to_reg` select plus duplicated write-first\\\\nmuxing on every read port. This stays fully in-order, preserves the current\\\\nsingle-cycle load-use behavior and RVFI contract, and is a more local timing\\\\noptimization than the control-side and memory-side experiments that already\\\\nregressed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a registered final writeback-data field to `mem_wb_t` so... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve canonical RET in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing ID redirect path to cover canonical aligned returns,\\\\ne.g. `jalr x0, x1, 0` and `jalr x0, x5, 0`, when the return-address\\\\nregister is already architecturally available from the regfile/WB path. If\\\\nan older in-flight instruction still owns that source register, simply fall\\\\nback to the current EX-stage JALR path instead of adding the full ID-stage\\\\nbypass/interlock machinery that regressed earlier. This reuses the existing\\\\n`id_redirect` plumbing, carries an \\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through\\\\nthe pipeline so EX suppresses the duplicate redirect, and should cut\\\\nanother hot function-return bubble in CoreMark with much lower timing risk\\\\nthan general early JALR/BRANCH resolution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the existing `jal_early_redirect` pipeline bit into an ID-resolved jump marker that can cover both direct `JAL` and narrow early-return `JALR` cases.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"descr... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Canonical return-address stack predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` already found its best predictor win in the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay path, and the recent structural JAL improvement removed the cheapest\\\\nunconditional jump bubble. The remaining hot control penalty is canonical\\\\nfunction return traffic: `ret`-shaped `JALR` instructions still wait for EX,\\\\nwhile broader IF-side branch prediction, wider replay tables, and redirect\\\\nlanding-pad schemes all failed to repay their timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` already found its best predictor win in the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay path, and the recent structural JAL improvement removed the cheapest\\\\nunconditional jump bubble. The remaining hot control penalty is canonical\\\\nfunction return traffic: `ret`-shaped `JALR` instructions still wait for EX,\\\\nwhile broader IF-side branch prediction, wider replay tables, and redirect\\\\nlanding-pad schemes all failed to repay their timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a very small return-address stack that predicts only the common ABI call\\\\nand return pattern instead of general branch targets. Push `pc+4` for direct\\\\ncalls that write `x1`, use the top entry to early-redirect only canonical\\\\n`ret`/`jalr x0, 0(x1)` in ID, and carry the predicted target forward so EX\\\\ncan suppress the duplicate redirect on a hit or repair the path on a rare\\\\nmismatch. This stays much narrower than the failed branch/JAL predictors: it\\\\ndoes not widen the replay table, does not add BTB-style target RAM, and only\\\\nadds prediction metadata to the small subset of return instructions. CoreMark\\\\nis call/return heavy enough that shaving the return bubble should improve IPC\\\\nwhile keeping Fmax risk materially lower than earlier full-control schemes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the ID/EX bundle with narrow early... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r10s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-gated operand bypass cleanup\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent writeback-retime regression showed that the remaining backend\\\\nlimiter is not just where `wb_data` is selected, but the always-on\\\\nWB-to-ID read-bypass compares and muxes that sit on both regfile read\\\\nports every cycle. On this core, many hot CoreMark instructions\\\\n(`OP-IMM`, `LOAD`, `JALR`, `AUIPC`, `LUI`, `JAL`) do not consume `rs2`,\\\\nand some consume neither source, yet the current datapath still drives raw\\\\ninstruction bitfields through regfile read, bypass, and forwarding logic.\\\\nTrimming that unnecessary operand work is a plausible remaining Fmax win\\\\nthat stays off the fragile IF/control paths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent writeback-retime regression showed that the remaining backend\\\\nlimiter is not just where `wb_data` is selected, but the always-on\\\\nWB-to-ID read-bypass compares and muxes that sit on both regfile read\\\\nports every cycle. On this core, many hot CoreMark instructions\\\\n(`OP-IMM`, `LOAD`, `JALR`, `AUIPC`, `LUI`, `JAL`) do not consume `rs2`,\\\\nand some consume neither source, yet the current datapath still drives raw\\\\ninstruction bitfields through regfile read, bypass, and forwarding logic.\\\\nTrimming that unnecessary operand work is a plausible remaining Fmax win\\\\nthat stays off the fragile IF/control paths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add exact source-usage classification in decode and use it to normalize\\\\nunused source operands to `x0` before they enter the hot read/bypass path.\\\\nThen simplify the register-file/writeback interaction so same-cycle WB\\\\nbypass is only applied to architecturally live operands, ideally by moving\\\\nthe compare+mux out of the generic regfile read ports and into the decode\\\\nstage after source-use is known. Forwarding logic should see zeroed unused\\\\nsource addresses, and retirement should carry the architecturally consumed\\\\nsource values so RVFI stays exact; illegal or otherwise uncl... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drain-and-replace posted store slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing single posted-store slot, but let it dequeue the older\\\\nbuffered store to dmem and capture one younger postable store in the same\\\\ncycle. In practice this means teaching `mem_stage` to drain and replace the\\\\nslot when `dmem_ready` comes back, and teaching `hazard_unit` not to stall\\\\nan EX/MEM store that can retire into the just-freed slot while still keeping\\\\nyounger loads and non-postable stores conservative. This should recover some\\\\nof the store-burst benefit the two-entry FIFO targeted, without adding\\\\nanother queue entry, alias-compare logic, or front-end timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Allow the one-entry posted store buffer to drain an older buffered RAM store and capture a younger postable store in the same cycle, ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r10s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Seed replay from ID-resolved JAL targets\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest `bench` predictor result is still the tiny registered replay\\\\ntable, and predictor attempts that widened IF control, added target decode,\\\\nor carried prediction metadata into later stages consistently lost fitness.\\\\nAfter the recent ID-stage `JAL` win, the next control-side blind spot is the\\\\nvery first fetch at a direct-call target: the target PC is now known exactly\\\\none cycle early, but `if_stage` still drops replay state on `id_redirect`, so\\\\na warmed callee entry point bubbles whenever `io_imemReady=0` on that first\\\\ntarget cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest `bench` predictor result is still the tiny registered replay\\\\ntable, and predictor attempts that widened IF control, added target decode,\\\\nor carried prediction metadata into later stages consistently lost fitness.\\\\nAfter the recent ID-stage `JAL` win, the next control-side blind spot is the\\\\nvery first fetch at a direct-call target: the target PC is now known exactly\\\\none cycle early, but `if_stage` still drops replay state on `id_redirect`, so\\\\na warmed callee entry point bubbles whenever `io_imemReady=0` on that first\\\\ntarget cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the existing `{pc,instr}` replay table to seed the next-cycle replay\\\\ncandidate from an exact `id_redirect_target` when decode resolves a legal\\\\naligned `JAL`. On a live ID redirect, `if_stage` would probe the existing\\\\nreplay table with the JAL target and, on a hit, load `replay_valid_q`,\\\\n`replay_pc_q`, and `replay_instr_q` for the upcoming target fetch instead of\\\\nunconditionally clearing them. Keep EX-stage redirect handling, sequential\\\\nreplay fill, and `fetch_available = imem_ready || replay_hit` unchanged, so\\\\nthis stays in the proven replay-family design space: no IF-side immediate\\\\ndecode, no new predicted-PC metadata through ID/EX, and no broader branch or\\\\nreturn speculation. The pay... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r11s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Normalize unused legal source reads\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The biggest `bench` gains have come from narrow backend cleanups that cut\\\\nhot-path routing without widening IF or control logic. After the JAL-in-ID,\\\\nposted-store, and add-fast-path wins, the remaining backend tax is that ID\\\\nstill drives raw instruction bitfields into the regfile, same-cycle WB read\\\\nbypass, and EX forwarding compares even for legal instructions that do not\\\\narchitecturally read `rs1` and/or `rs2`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The biggest `bench` gains have come from narrow backend cleanups that cut\\\\nhot-path routing without widening IF or control logic. After the JAL-in-ID,\\\\nposted-store, and add-fast-path wins, the remaining backend tax is that ID\\\\nstill drives raw instruction bitfields into the regfile, same-cycle WB read\\\\nbypass, and EX forwarding compares even for legal instructions that do not\\\\narchitecturally read `rs1` and/or `rs2`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add exact `uses_rs1` / `uses_rs2` classification for legal instructions, and\\\\nuse it only to normalize definitely-unused source addresses to `x0` before\\\\nthey enter the hot read/bypass path. Keep the existing write-first regfile\\\\ncontract intact and do not move bypassing out of `reg_file`; the key change\\\\nis to stop reading and comparing random immediate bits on opclasses like\\\\n`LUI`, `AUIPC`, `JAL`, `FENCE`, `EBREAK`, and the `rs2`-unused I-type ops.\\\\nFor safety, illegal or otherwise unvalidated encodings should stay\\\\nconservative, with source-use bits only cleared inside positively validated\\\\ndecoder arms. This should shrink the always-on WB-to-ID compare/mux work and\\\\nthe downstream forwarding compare fanout while staying off the fragile IF,\\\\nbranch, and memory-control paths that already regressed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/decoder.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Emit exact `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` bits... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[]},\"title\":\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778832672656,\"end\":1778832672665}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_09e32757a6c179af016a06d52051488198a05914b547288fb9\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2ab0869b001l2RVxOQBZQ5Fsa\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d54fefe4ffeAIZkrcLO9DLQdX\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2ab0524b001VwQ1oMEx1tqolm\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:1008:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778834548141,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d53321f1ffebzeyg5JxRb9p3u\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_lMjHhObuhhdTxvjEaKAz8ScZ\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":220},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Moving DIV/REM into a multicycle EX micro-unit is not a local ALU swap; if stall/freeze logic does not hold the full EX instruction context and suppress side effects until completion, riscv-formal will start failing even simple ADD/SUB/reg checks from corrupted EX/MEM retirement state.\\n2: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r1s1 (regression, -3.89%): A tiny sequential fetch queue is not enough by itself to beat random `io_imemReady` stalls on this front end; with only 1-instruction-per-cycle fetch bandwidth and frequent redirect flushes, the queue rarely gets far enough ahead of decode to repay its extra control.\\n3: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r1s2 (regression, -1.94%): On `bench`, a zero-table IF predictor for backward B-type branches and direct JALs still loses overall fitness when it adds immediate-decode, target-add, and `predicted_taken`/`predicted_target` bookkeeping to the fetch path, because the front-end cost outweighs the CoreMark loop/call bubbles it removes.\\n4: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r2s0 (regression, +7.86%): On `bench`, moving cold DIV/REM ops off the ALU can still lose when the precise fix adds hot-path interlock machinery like `ex_long_busy`, full EX-context shadow registers, and EX/MEM bubble control, because CoreMark rarely executes divides and does not repay the added control cost.\\n5: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r2s1 (broken, n/a): Stage-aware load/store interlocks must move the architectural operand snapshot with the true consumer stage too; if STORE `rs2` is satisfied by a late WB->MEM bypass but retirement still reports the old EX-captured `rs2_val`, riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs2_rdata` on load->store pairs.\\n6: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r2s2 (improvement, +11.75%): On `bench`, a registered `{pc,instr}` replay table that banks the next `pc+4` hit can hide random `io_imemReady` stalls better than a plain fetch queue or decode-time branch predictor, because CoreMark revisits a small hot sequential PC set and the replay path stays off the IF critical path.\\n7: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r3s0 (improvement, +5.14%): On `bench`, a MEM-to-EX load bypass is worth more than a mandatory load-use bubble when `io_dmemReady` commonly returns zero-wait data and CoreMark presents many short load-consumer chains, provided the forwarded value still flows through the normal EX path so RVFI operand snapshots stay aligned.\\n8: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): Exact `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2_*` load-use gating must retime RVFI source snapshots to the operand's true consumer stage for both sources, not just STORE data, or riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs1_rdata`/`rvfi_rs2_rdata` when forwarding changes the architecturally consumed value.\\n9: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r3s2 (broken, n/a): Extending the successful replay table into a learned next-PC predictor can exhaust `riscv-formal`'s wall-clock budget even without IF-side decode, because threading full `predicted_next_pc`/`predicted_valid` metadata and per-entry successor state through IF/ID/EX forces every instruction proof to reason about extra 32-bit control-flow state and EX mispredict comparisons.\\n10: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.78%): On `bench`, parallel `mul_ss`/`mul_uu`/`mul_su` datapaths are wasted hot-path area for RV32M; collapsing `MUL*` onto one sign-selectable 33x33 product preserves single-cycle behavior while reducing ALU routing pressure enough to lift Tang Nano 20K fitness.\\n11: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (regression, -59.23%): On `bench`, exact ID-stage BRANCH/JAL/JALR resolution is too timing-expensive; pulling compare/target-add plus EX/MEM/MEM/WB operand-bypass and load-interlock logic into decode collapsed Fmax to 52 MHz, so CoreMark's taken-control bubbles are cheaper than widening the front-end critical path.\\n12: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (regression, +0.61%): On `bench`, replaying warmed EX `redirect_target` instructions from the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` table is too narrow a win to pay for extra redirect-hit lookup/arming logic, because it only helps when `io_imemReady` stalls on the first post-redirect cycle while the original `pc+4` replay already captures the hotter sequential locality.\\n13: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r5s0 (regression, +9.14%): On `bench`, replacing the shared sign-selectable 33x33 `MUL*` datapath with a 32x32 product plus `MULH`/`MULHSU` sign-correction subtractors loses on Tang Nano 20K, because the extra 32-bit correction/mux logic on the ALU hot path costs more than carrying the multiplier sign bit through the DSP.\\n14: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r5s1 (improvement, +12.75%): On `bench`, a one-entry posted store buffer that only posts main-memory stores and blocks only younger memory ops turns random `io_dmemReady` store backpressure into background drain time, recovering CoreMark IPC without paying the IF/ID timing cost of front-end control changes.\\n15: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r5s2 (regression, +0.93%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table already has enough capacity for CoreMark's hot sequential PCs; adding 2-way set associativity with dual tag checks, way select, and MRU replacement costs more than the low-bit conflict misses it removes.\\n16: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r6s0 (improvement, +3.63%): On `bench`, carving ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and load/store address generation onto a dedicated EX carry-chain path pays because CoreMark is dominated by simple add/address ops, so keeping them out of the ALU's wide logic/shift/RV32M mux and fanout improves timing without front-end control changes.\\n17: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r6s1 (regression, -11.69%): On `bench`, extending the posted store buffer with MEM-stage aligned-address/byte-mask overlap checks, load forwarding, and load-vs-drain arbitration lost overall fitness because the extra compare/mask/mux logic sat on the hot memory path, dropping Fmax from 151.3 MHz to 133.4 MHz while CoreMark did not expose enough independent load-behind-store overlap to pay it back.\\n18: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r6s2 (regression, +1.63%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table should stay a single sequential `pc+4` candidate; adding a second JAL/backward-branch control-target slot only helps on rare first-post-redirect `io_imemReady` stalls, while the extra immediate-decode, target-add, and second replay-hit path in `if_stage` costs more than it saves.\\n19: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r7s0 (regression, -19.89%): On `bench`, the profitable EX split stops at the add/address fast path; adding op-class gating like `use_alu_result`, zeroed ALU operands for BRANCH/JAL/JALR, and `is_m_op ? a/b : 0` around the shared RV32M cone puts extra decode/mux logic back on the hot EX path and drops Fmax far more than the cold-cone isolation helps.\\n20: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r7s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On `bench`, the posted-store win appears depth-sensitive on Tang Nano 20K: growing the one-entry main-memory store buffer into a two-entry in-order FIFO with `store_buf_full` backpressure and queued-drain muxing pushed MEM/backpressure routing past place-and-route limits before any CoreMark gain could be measured.\\n21: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r7s2 (regression, +0.00%): On `bench`, low-bit conflict misses in the 8-entry sequential `{pc,instr}` replay table are not a meaningful limiter; even a one-entry fully-associative victim spill/check in `if_stage` failed to beat the plain direct-mapped design, so extra replay-hit selection state is already past CoreMark's payoff point.\\n22: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r8s0 (regression, -14.36%): On `bench`, peeling aligned `LW`/`SW` onto a dedicated MEM fast path is not worthwhile when the generic width logic already makes word accesses the default identity/`4'b1111` case; hoisting `mem_mask`/store formatting into EX and threading `mem_word_op` through the pipe adds ALU-result fanout and stage metadata without enough hot-path relief to repay it.\\n23: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r8s1 (improvement, +31.31%): On `bench`, decode-stage control fixes should isolate the cheapest unconditional case: resolving legal aligned `JAL` in ID and carrying a `jal_early_redirect` bit to suppress the duplicate EX redirect removes CoreMark's hot direct-call/jump bubble without dragging BRANCH/JALR compare, bypass, and load-interlock logic onto the front-end critical path.\\n24: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r8s2 (regression, -4.11%): On `bench`, redirect-target replay is opportunity-limited rather than lookup-limited; even a separate one-cycle landing-pad `{pc,instr}` cache in `if_stage` only pays on repeated redirects whose very first fetch later stalls on `io_imemReady=0`, which is rarer than the locality already captured by the sequential `pc+4` replay.\\n25: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r9s0 (regression, -11.24%): On `bench`, precomputing and registering MEM/WB `wb_data` is not a free timing win if the change merely shifts that value's fanout into same-cycle WB-to-regfile/ID read-bypass compare+mux logic; backend writeback cleanup must reduce total hot-path routing, not just move the `mem_to_reg` select earlier.\\n26: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r9s1 (regression, -13.86%): On `bench`, decode-time redirect wins stop at plain `JAL`; even a narrow canonical `ret` fast path (`jalr x0, x1/x5, 0`) with `rs1_busy` fallback adds enough decode-side dependency compare and target-select logic to cost more Fmax than CoreMark's return bubbles repay.\\n27: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r9s2 (regression, -64.82%): On `bench`, the canonical-return loss is not just an `rs1_busy` decode hazard: even a tiny 4-entry `ras_stack` that predicts only `JAL x1`/`ret` and carries `early_redirect_target` into EX for repair adds enough speculative call/return bookkeeping to lose more Fmax than CoreMark's return bubbles repay.\\n28: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r10s0 (broken, n/a): On `bench`, the `reg_file` write-first/WB-to-ID same-cycle read path is part of the backend correctness contract, not free timing junk; moving that bypass behind decode-gated `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` logic can miss real dependencies and break selftest.\\n29: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r10s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On `bench`, the posted-store budget is not just buffer depth: feeding `dmem_ready` into same-cycle drain-and-replace control in both `mem_stage` and `hazard_unit` was enough to blow Tang Nano 20K placement, so future store-burst work must avoid combinational refill/backpressure feedback.\\n30: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r10s2 (regression, -35.73%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table should stay dedicated to the sequential `pc+4` stream; repointing `replay_valid_q`/`replay_pc_q` at an exact ID-resolved `JAL` target burns the hotter fallthrough reuse and adds redirect-target selection to `if_stage` for a first-target-stall corner CoreMark rarely needs.\\n31: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r11s0 (broken, n/a): On `bench`, unused-source cleanup must not rewrite the shared `id_stage` `rs*_addr`/regfile-read path, because this repo's cosim/RVFI contract snapshots raw instruction `rs1`/`rs2` fields and pre-write register contents even for operands the ISA ignores, so `uses_rs*` normalization to `x0` needs a separate trace view or breaks selftest.\\n32: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r11s2 (regression, -51.23%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table only pays when a hit is pre-banked into the one-cycle replay candidate; adding same-cycle current-PC tag-compare and fallback muxing to salvage warm entries after flush/redirect clears puts too much logic back on the fetch critical path for a rare stall corner.\\n33: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r12s0 (regression, -3.37%): On `bench`, a tiny 32x32 2R1W register file is too small and semantically constrained for LUTRAM-plus-valid-bit conversion to pay; preserving write-first bypass and reset-to-zero semantics with `reg_valid`/read-zero gating adds muxing on the hot WB-to-ID read path that can cost more Fmax than removing the data-array reset saves.\\n34: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r12s1 (regression, -11.10%): On `bench`, decode-time redirect wins really stop at source-free `JAL`; even an exact one-source zero-compare B-type fast path guarded only by `older_id_ex`/`older_ex_mem` pending checks still puts live register-read dependency compares and branch-condition logic on `id_stage`, costing more Fmax than the taken-branch bubble repays.\\n35: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r12s2 (regression, -49.77%): On `bench`, decode-time redirect wins truly stop at unconditional `JAL`; even a source-free static backward-B-type predictor that only checks `imm[31]`, legal/aligned target, and carries an `id_ctrl_redirect` mispredict-repair bit into EX adds enough front-end control plumbing that CoreMark's hot loop backedges still do not repay the Fmax loss.\\n36: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r13s0 (regression, -30.85%): On `bench`, downstream-only exact `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` gating is not a free backend cleanup when RVFI must still see ungated source forwarding; splitting `forward_unit`/`ex_stage` into gated EX selects plus separate `rvfi_fwd_rs*_sel` paths duplicates hot compare/mux logic and costs more Fmax than the pruned unused-operand matches save.\\n\\n(End of file - total 36 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Moving DIV/REM into a multicycle EX micro-unit is not a local ALU swap; if stall/freeze logic does not hold the full EX instruction context and suppress side effects until completion, riscv-formal will start failing even simple ADD/SUB/reg checks from corrupted EX/MEM retirement state.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r1s1 (regression, -3.89%): A tiny sequential fetch queue is not enough by itself to beat random `io_imemReady` stalls on this front end; with only 1-instruction-per-cycle fetch bandwidth and frequent redirect flushes, the queue rarely gets far enough ahead of decode to repay its extra control.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r1s2 (regression, -1.94%): On `bench`, a zero-table IF predictor for backward B-type branches and direct JALs still loses overall fitness when it adds immediate-decode, target-add, and `predicted_taken`/`predicted_target` bookkeeping to the fetch path, because the front-end cost outweighs the CoreMark loop/call bubbles it removes.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r2s0 (regression, +7.86%): On `bench`, moving cold DIV/REM ops off the ALU can still lose when the precise fix adds hot-path interlock machinery like `ex_long_busy`, full EX-context shadow registers, and EX/MEM bubble control, because CoreMark rarely executes divides and does not repay the added control cost.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r2s1 (broken, n/a): Stage-aware load/store interlocks must move the architectural operand snapshot with the true consumer stage too; if STORE `rs2` is satisfied by a late WB->MEM bypass but retirement still reports the old EX-captured `rs2_val`, riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs2_rdata` on load->store pairs.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r2s2 (improvement, +11.75%): On `bench`, a registered `{pc,instr}` replay table that banks the next `pc+4` hit can hide random `io_imemReady` stalls better than a plain fetch queue or decode-time branch predictor, because CoreMark revisits a small hot sequential PC set and the replay path stays off the IF critical path.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r3s0 (improvement, +5.14%): On `bench`, a MEM-to-EX load bypass is worth more than a mandatory load-use bubble when `io_dmemReady` commonly returns zero-wait data and CoreMark presents many short load-consumer chains, provided the forwarded value still flows through the normal EX path so RVFI operand snapshots stay aligned.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): Exact `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2_*` load-use gating must retime RVFI source snapshots to the operand's true consumer stage for both sources, not just STORE data, or riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs1_rdata`/`rvfi_rs2_rdata` when forwarding changes the architecturally consumed value.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r3s2 (broken, n/a): Extending the successful replay table into a learned next-PC predictor can exhaust `riscv-formal`'s wall-clock budget even without IF-side decode, because threading full `predicted_next_pc`/`predicted_valid` metadata and per-entry successor state through IF/ID/EX forces every instruction proof to reason about extra 32-bit control-flow state and EX mispredict comparisons.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.78%): On `bench`, parallel `mul_ss`/`mul_uu`/`mul_su` datapaths are wasted hot-path area for RV32M; collapsing `MUL*` onto one sign-selectable 33x33 product preserves single-cycle behavior while reducing ALU routing pressure enough to lift Tang Nano 20K fitness.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (regression, -59.23%): On `bench`, exact ID-stage BRANCH/JAL/JALR resolution is too timing-expensive; pulling compare/target-add plus EX/MEM/MEM/WB operand-bypass and load-interlock logic into decode collapsed Fmax to 52 MHz, so CoreMark's taken-control bubbles are cheaper than widening the front-end critical path.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (regression, +0.61%): On `bench`, replaying warmed EX `redirect_target` instructions from the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` table is too narrow a win to pay for extra redirect-hit lookup/arming logic, because it only helps when `io_imemReady` stalls on the first post-redirect cycle while the original `pc+4` replay already captures the hotter sequential locality.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r5s0 (regression, +9.14%): On `bench`, replacing the shared sign-selectable 33x33 `MUL*` datapath with a 32x32 product plus `MULH`/`MULHSU` sign-correction subtractors loses on Tang Nano 20K, because the extra 32-bit correction/mux logic on the ALU hot path costs more than carrying the multiplier sign bit through the DSP.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r5s1 (improvement, +12.75%): On `bench`, a one-entry posted store buffer that only posts main-memory stores and blocks only younger memory ops turns random `io_dmemReady` store backpressure into background drain time, recovering CoreMark IPC without paying the IF/ID timing cost of front-end control changes.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r5s2 (regression, +0.93%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table already has enough capacity for CoreMark's hot sequential PCs; adding 2-way set associativity with dual tag checks, way select, and MRU replacement costs more than the low-bit conflict misses it removes.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r6s0 (improvement, +3.63%): On `bench`, carving ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and load/store address generation onto a dedicated EX carry-chain path pays because CoreMark is dominated by simple add/address ops, so keeping them out of the ALU's wide logic/shift/RV32M mux and fanout improves timing without front-end control changes.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r6s1 (regression, -11.69%): On `bench`, extending the posted store buffer with MEM-stage aligned-address/byte-mask overlap checks, load forwarding, and load-vs-drain arbitration lost overall fitness because the extra compare/mask/mux logic sat on the hot memory path, dropping Fmax from 151.3 MHz to 133.4 MHz while CoreMark did not expose enough independent load-behind-store overlap to pay it back.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r6s2 (regression, +1.63%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table should stay a single sequential `pc+4` candidate; adding a second JAL/backward-branch control-target slot only helps on rare first-post-redirect `io_imemReady` stalls, while the extra immediate-decode, target-add, and second replay-hit path in `if_stage` costs more than it saves.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r7s0 (regression, -19.89%): On `bench`, the profitable EX split stops at the add/address fast path; adding op-class gating like `use_alu_result`, zeroed ALU operands for BRANCH/JAL/JALR, and `is_m_op ? a/b : 0` around the shared RV32M cone puts extra decode/mux logic back on the hot EX path and drops Fmax far more than the cold-cone isolation helps.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r7s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On `bench`, the posted-store win appears depth-sensitive on Tang Nano 20K: growing the one-entry main-memory store buffer into a two-entry in-order FIFO with `store_buf_full` backpressure and queued-drain muxing pushed MEM/backpressure routing past place-and-route limits before any CoreMark gain could be measured.\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[]},\"title\":\"cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778834548115,\"end\":1778834548140}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0c2f3d1936e9163b016a06dc73e3ac819889061e853ec9157f\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2acd255e0010fkYZJ3p6eoWoy\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d53321f1ffebzeyg5JxRb9p3u\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2accdfc6001bv54fT7w6rOQcw\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:1014:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778834555370,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d53321f1ffebzeyg5JxRb9p3u\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_BG84pIGGy394KIgEBGkDafIF\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"\\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\"\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 13 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 3: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Add a small redirect-aware fetch queue\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front-end is tightly coupled to `io_imemReady`: any cycle with\\\\ninstruction-memory backpressure immediately becomes a decode bubble because\\\\nIF emits a NOP and the PC stops. That is especially expensive here because\\\\nthe fitness run injects random iStalls by design, so front-end elasticity is\\\\nworth more than a tiny local logic cleanup.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front-end is tightly coupled to `io_imemReady`: any cycle with\\\\ninstruction-memory backpressure immediately becomes a decode bubble because\\\\nIF emits a NOP and the PC stops. That is especially expensive here because\\\\nthe fitness run injects random iStalls by design, so front-end elasticity is\\\\nworth more than a tiny local logic cleanup.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single-slot combinational fetch path with a small speculative\\\\nfetch queue in `if_stage`, likely 2-4 entries deep, that buffers `{pc,\\\\ninstr}` pairs when `io_imemReady` is high and feeds ID from the queue when\\\\nthe backend is ready. On redirects, flush younger queued instructions and\\\\nrestart fetch at the resolved target; on load-use or dmem stalls, preserve\\\\nthe buffered instructions instead of re-fetching them. This is a structural\\\\nfront-end change, not a predictor: it should raise CoreMark iterations/cycle\\\\nunder the orchestrator's random stall model with only modest area/timing\\\\ncost and without changing the single-retire RVFI contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Refactor IF into a small prefetch FIFO/skid-buffer front-end with redirect-aware invalidation and queue-based instruction delivery.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Change front-end stall logic to depend on fetch-queue availability plus backend hazards instead of bubbling immediately on every `!imemReady`.\\\"}, {\\\"...\\n  Line 6: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Operand-Stage-Aware Load Interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current hazard unit stalls on any raw `rs1`/`rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not actually read that field yet\\\\nor only needs it later as store data in MEM. That conservative policy burns\\\\navoidable one-cycle bubbles in CoreMark's dense load/address/store code, and\\\\nthe recent `bench` regressions already showed that extra front-end machinery\\\\nis a worse trade here than tightening the backend interlock.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current hazard unit stalls on any raw `rs1`/`rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not actually read that field yet\\\\nor only needs it later as store data in MEM. That conservative policy burns\\\\navoidable one-cycle bubbles in CoreMark's dense load/address/store code, and\\\\nthe recent `bench` regressions already showed that extra front-end machinery\\\\nis a worse trade here than tightening the backend interlock.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework the load-use interlock around the stage where an operand is actually\\\\nconsumed. Add decode metadata for real `rs1`/`rs2` usage, treat STORE `rs2`\\\\nas a late MEM-stage consumer instead of an EX-stage consumer, and add a\\\\nWB-to-MEM store-data bypass so `load -> store` pairs with an independent\\\\naddress can proceed without the textbook bubble. This should recover\\\\nCoreMark IPC from both false-positive load stalls and true-but-late\\\\nstore-data dependencies, while keeping load data off the EX ALU/branch path\\\\nand avoiding another fetch-path timing penalty.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add decoded source-usage and late-store-data control bits to the pipeline bundles so hazards can distinguish EX-stage operands from MEM-stage-only store data.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/decoder.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Classify which legal ins...\\n  Line 9: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Exact load-use interlock with late store-data bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The fetch side already has a winning replay mechanism, so the strongest\\\\nremaining structural gain is more likely in the backend. `hazard_unit.sv`\\\\nstill stalls on any raw `if_id_rs1` or `if_id_rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not consume that field in EX or\\\\nonly needs store data later in MEM. CoreMark's dense mix of loads,\\\\nimmediate arithmetic, address generation, and stores makes those\\\\nconservative one-cycle bubbles expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The fetch side already has a winning replay mechanism, so the strongest\\\\nremaining structural gain is more likely in the backend. `hazard_unit.sv`\\\\nstill stalls on any raw `if_id_rs1` or `if_id_rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not consume that field in EX or\\\\nonly needs store data later in MEM. CoreMark's dense mix of loads,\\\\nimmediate arithmetic, address generation, and stores makes those\\\\nconservative one-cycle bubbles expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework the load-use interlock around true operand-consumption timing instead\\\\nof raw register-field matches. Decode exact legal `rs1` and `rs2` usage for\\\\neach instruction class, keep the current conservative stall policy for\\\\nillegal or otherwise unclassified encodings, and treat STORE `rs2` as a\\\\nMEM-stage consumer with a dedicated WB-to-MEM store-data bypass. To avoid\\\\nthe prior broken attempt's RVFI bug, carry the final architecturally\\\\nconsumed store-data value into retirement so `rvfi_rs2_rdata`,\\\\n`rvfi_mem_wdata`, and the actual dmem write all agree. This should remove\\\\nfalse bubbles from OP-IMM/LOAD/JALR/LUI/AUIPC/FENCE paths and eliminate the\\\\nmandatory `load -> store(rs2)` stall without putting load data on the EX\\\\nALU, branch-compare, or JALR critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"...\\n  Line 12: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"ID-stage exact control resolution\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best low-risk fetch and\\\\nload-use gains: it replays hot sequential instructions in IF and forwards\\\\nzero-wait loads from MEM to EX. The remaining visible IPC loss is that every\\\\ntaken BRANCH, JAL, and JALR still resolves in EX after the ID/EX register,\\\\nwhich forces extra wrong-path bubbles on CoreMark's hot loop backedges and\\\\nhelper calls.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best low-risk fetch and\\\\nload-use gains: it replays hot sequential instructions in IF and forwards\\\\nzero-wait loads from MEM to EX. The remaining visible IPC loss is that every\\\\ntaken BRANCH, JAL, and JALR still resolves in EX after the ID/EX register,\\\\nwhich forces extra wrong-path bubbles on CoreMark's hot loop backedges and\\\\nhelper calls.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move exact control-flow resolution one stage earlier by adding a dedicated\\\\nID-stage branch/jump unit. JAL can redirect immediately from `pc+imm`; BRANCH\\\\nand JALR should compare/add in ID using the regfile value plus targeted\\\\nbypasses from EX/MEM and MEM/WB, while conservatively stalling only when an\\\\noperand is still unavailable from an in-flight load. Carry the final\\\\nforwarded source values into the pipeline state so RVFI operand snapshots stay\\\\naligned with the values the control instruction actually consumed, and mark\\\\nID-resolved control ops so EX does not issue a duplicate redirect a cycle\\\\nlater. This keeps the existing single-retire contract and replay frontend,\\\\nbut should cut the taken-control penalty by roughly one cycle without adding\\\\nIF-side predictor state or target-table complexity.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the pipeline bundles with control-resolution metadata so ID-resolved BRANCH/JAL/JALR instru...\\n  Line 15: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-entry posted store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry posted-store buffer that captures a store's aligned address,\\\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible metadata when the store\\\\nreaches MEM. If the external dmem port stalls, retire the store from the buffer\\\\nimmediately, keep draining the buffered write in the background, and allow\\\\nyounger non-memory instructions to keep flowing. Only stall when a younger\\\\nload or store reaches MEM while the buffer is still occupied, preserving\\\\nin-order memory semantics without reintroducing load-use or control-path\\\\ncomplexity. This should recover CoreMark IPC from random dmem backpressure on\\\\nstore-heavy regions while avoiding the IF/ID critical-path regressions seen in\\\\nearlier structural experiments.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the MEM-stage/retirement bundles with store-buffer bookkeepin...\\n  Line 18: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Alias-aware load bypass around posted stores\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing posted store buffer, but make it address-aware instead of\\\\nblocking all younger memory ops. When a younger load reaches MEM while a\\\\nmain-memory store is buffered, compare the load's aligned word address and\\\\nbyte mask against the buffered store: if there is no overlap, let the load use\\\\nthe dmem port immediately and drain the buffered store later; if the load is\\\\nfully covered by the buffered bytes, forward from the buffer; only stall on\\\\ntrue alias or partial-overlap cases, and keep younger stores conservative.\\\\nThis is a backend-only structural change that compounds with the winning\\\\nposted-store buffer while avoiding the IF/ID timing regressions seen in prior\\\\nstructural attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add store-buffer alias checking, demand-load-vs-drain arbitration, and buffered-store forwarding for fully covered younger loads.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the blank...\\n  Line 21: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r7s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Two-entry posted store FIFO\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single posted-store slot with a tiny two-entry in-order store\\\\nFIFO that can absorb a short burst of normal-RAM stores while older buffered\\\\nwrites drain in the background. Keep the current conservative memory-order\\\\nrule that younger loads still wait until the FIFO is empty, and avoid the\\\\nlosing alias-compare/forwarding path entirely; the gain should come from\\\\nletting consecutive stores and following non-memory instructions retire\\\\nacross multi-cycle `io_dmemReady` stalls without touching IF timing or the\\\\nEX critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/store_queue.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a tiny two-entry in-order posted-store FIFO with enqueue/dequeue logic and head/tail status for MEM arbitration.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single buffered-store slot with the FIFO so a postable store can enqueue behind an older buffered store while preserving the existing conservative load ordering and RVFI store reporting.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"de...\\n  Line 24: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve direct JAL in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow early-redirect path for legal `JAL` instructions in ID while\\\\nleaving BRANCH and JALR resolution in EX. `JAL` needs only `pc + imm`, so it\\\\ncan redirect one stage earlier without the operand-bypass, compare, and\\\\nload-interlock machinery that made full ID-stage control resolution collapse\\\\nFmax. Carry an `early_redirected`/resolved-next-PC bit with the instruction so\\\\nEX suppresses the duplicate redirect but still produces the normal `rd = pc+4`\\\\nwriteback and RVFI retirement record. This should shave a bubble from hot\\\\ndirect calls and unconditional jumps in CoreMark while keeping the replay\\\\nfrontend, posted store buffer, and hot EX datapath essentially unchanged.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the decode/pipeline control bundles with a bit that marks a JAL as already redirected before EX, plus any resolved next-PC metadata needed to suppress duplicate redirects later.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JALs in decode, compute `pc + imm`, and...\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve canonical RET in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing ID redirect path to cover canonical aligned returns,\\\\ne.g. `jalr x0, x1, 0` and `jalr x0, x5, 0`, when the return-address\\\\nregister is already architecturally available from the regfile/WB path. If\\\\nan older in-flight instruction still owns that source register, simply fall\\\\nback to the current EX-stage JALR path instead of adding the full ID-stage\\\\nbypass/interlock machinery that regressed earlier. This reuses the existing\\\\n`id_redirect` plumbing, carries an \\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through\\\\nthe pipeline so EX suppresses the duplicate redirect, and should cut\\\\nanother hot function-return bubble in CoreMark with much lower timing risk\\\\nthan general early JALR/BRANCH resolution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the existing `jal_early_redirect` pipeline bit into an ID-resolved jump marker that can cover both direct `JAL` and narrow early-return `JALR` cases.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"descr...\\n  Line 30: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drain-and-replace posted store slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing single posted-store slot, but let it dequeue the older\\\\nbuffered store to dmem and capture one younger postable store in the same\\\\ncycle. In practice this means teaching `mem_stage` to drain and replace the\\\\nslot when `dmem_ready` comes back, and teaching `hazard_unit` not to stall\\\\nan EX/MEM store that can retire into the just-freed slot while still keeping\\\\nyounger loads and non-postable stores conservative. This should recover some\\\\nof the store-burst benefit the two-entry FIFO targeted, without adding\\\\nanother queue entry, alias-compare logic, or front-end timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Allow the one-entry posted store buffer to drain an older buffered RAM store and capture a younger postable store in the same cycle, ...\\n  Line 33: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Replay-backed one-slot IF decoupler\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Turn the registered replay candidate into a true one-slot IF decoupler. When\\\\nthe current PC already matches a valid replay entry, let decode consume the\\\\ncached `{pc,instr}` while the live imem port fetches the next sequential PC and\\\\nrefreshes the lookahead slot/table in the same cycle. Keep the current\\\\nredirect/flush rules, keep all state local to `if_stage`, and do not add IF-side\\\\nbranch decode, target tables, or a multi-entry queue. This should raise\\\\nCoreMark iterations/cycle by converting hot replay hits into useful prefetch\\\\nbandwidth, recovering more random iStall loss than the fallback-only replay\\\\npath without the timing cost that sank broader fetch-queue and control-predictor\\\\nattempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"des...\\n  Line 36: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r12s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve ready branch-to-zero in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already harvested the cheapest broad structural wins: sequential IF\\\\nreplay, MEM-to-EX load forwarding, a one-entry posted store buffer, and an\\\\nexact early `JAL` redirect. The next visible IPC loss is still the taken\\\\nbranch bubble, but prior history shows that full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR\\\\nresolution collapses Fmax once decode has to do general bypass and\\\\ninterlock work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already harvested the cheapest broad structural wins: sequential IF\\\\nreplay, MEM-to-EX load forwarding, a one-entry posted store buffer, and an\\\\nexact early `JAL` redirect. The next visible IPC loss is still the taken\\\\nbranch bubble, but prior history shows that full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR\\\\nresolution collapses Fmax once decode has to do general bypass and\\\\ninterlock work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the existing `id_redirect` path for a much narrower exact control case:\\\\nlegal, aligned branch-against-zero instructions whose single live source is\\\\nalready architecturally ready in the regfile/WB path. Detect `BEQ/BNE` and\\\\nsigned zero-compare branch pseudos (`BLT/BGE` with `rs2 = x0`) in\\\\n`id_stage`, guard them with a cheap \\\\\\\"rs1 not pending in ID/EX or EX/MEM\\\\\\\"\\\\ncheck, and redirect taken cases one stage earlier while falling back to the\\\\nunchanged EX-stage branch path for everything else. Carry a generic\\\\n\\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through `id_ex_t` so EX suppresses duplicate\\\\nredirects but still recomputes `pc_next` and trap behavior, keeping RVFI\\\\nsource snapshots correct because the early path only fires when decode is\\\\nalready reading the final architecturally consumed operand.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the current JAL-only early-redirect marker in `id_ex_t` into a generic ID-resolved-control bit that can also tag ...\\n  Line 39: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r13s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Mem-stall replay prefetch slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current winning frontend only learns future instructions from cycles where\\\\ndecode itself is advancing. When MEM-side backpressure freezes the pipe, the\\\\ninstruction port usually sits idle even though those cycles could have been\\\\nused to seed the next sequential replay hit. Earlier broad fetch queues lost\\\\nbecause they had to run ahead every cycle; the remaining structural opening is\\\\na narrower overlap of dmem-stall time with harmless prefetch work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current winning frontend only learns future instructions from cycles where\\\\ndecode itself is advancing. When MEM-side backpressure freezes the pipe, the\\\\ninstruction port usually sits idle even though those cycles could have been\\\\nused to seed the next sequential replay hit. Earlier broad fetch queues lost\\\\nbecause they had to run ahead every cycle; the remaining structural opening is\\\\na narrower overlap of dmem-stall time with harmless prefetch work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Teach `if_stage` to treat backend-only MEM stalls as prefetch opportunities\\\\ninstead of total frontend dead time. While architectural PC and IF->ID state\\\\nstay frozen, drive a shadow sequential fetch for the held `pc+4` (or a tiny\\\\none-slot skid successor) and bank the returned `{pc,instr}` into the existing\\\\nreplay candidate/table; when the stall clears, the normal path resumes and\\\\nthe next instruction is already replayable if `io_imemReady` drops. This\\\\ncompounds with the existing replay-table and posted-store wins, avoids\\\\ndecode-time branch/JALR logic, and is narrower than the failed always-on\\\\nfetch queue because it only adds work on cycles that were already lost to\\\\ndmem backpressure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a backend-stall prefetch/skid path that keeps architectural IF output frozen...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":13,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"\\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\"\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778834555354,\"end\":1778834555369}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_01265be87262ec97016a06dc7b2d84819989e3afd9b21ac035\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2acd41d8001655A6TwZPi0gPe\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d53321f1ffebzeyg5JxRb9p3u\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2acd27fb0018PJRE8axt4sxkx\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:1015:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778834555450,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d53321f1ffebzeyg5JxRb9p3u\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_3XLCUWdqYLy0NYpsCLljJ6XU\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"\\\"outcome\\\": \\\"(improvement|regression|broken|placement_failed)\\\"\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 40 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 1: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"baseline-bench-a2dc641\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Baseline retest for cores/bench\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 282.82, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 0.0, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 0.0, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 127.03, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491485, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 9563, \\\"ff\\\": 1866, \\\"seeds\\\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-05-15T01:37:19.464500Z\\\", \\\"round_id\\\": 0, \\\"slot\\\": 0}\\n\\n  Line 2: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Move DIV/REM off the fast ALU path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current EX-stage ALU carries fully combinational divide and remainder\\\\nhardware even though CoreMark spends very little time in DIV/REM. That rare\\\\nlogic still taxes timing closure and routing on the Tang Nano 20K, and the\\\\nbench core is already close to its LUT budget.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current EX-stage ALU carries fully combinational divide and remainder\\\\nhardware even though CoreMark spends very little time in DIV/REM. That rare\\\\nlogic still taxes timing closure and routing on the Tang Nano 20K, and the\\\\nbench core is already close to its LUT budget.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single-cycle DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU datapath with a small iterative\\\\ndivider/remainder micro-unit, while keeping the common integer ALU and MUL\\\\npath single-cycle. EX would launch the divider on DIV/REM ops, freeze the\\\\nfront of the pipeline until completion, and then retire normally with the\\\\nsame RV32M corner-case semantics. This should remove a rarely useful critical\\\\npath from normal cycles, improve Fmax and placement slack, and cost little\\\\nCoreMark/MHz because CoreMark is dominated by adds, loads, stores, branches,\\\\nand plain MUL rather than division. Using an 8-cycle worst-case divider keeps\\\\nthe added stall bounded enough to stay compatible with the existing formal\\\\ndepth budget.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/div_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a compact iterative signed/unsigned divider-remainder unit with RV32M divide-by-zero and overflow behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the combinational DIV/REM datapath from the fast ALU path and keep common arithmetic, shifts, compares, and MUL results single-cycle.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU, launch the divider, hold EX state while it runs, and only write EX/MEM when a...\\n  Line 3: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Add a small redirect-aware fetch queue\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front-end is tightly coupled to `io_imemReady`: any cycle with\\\\ninstruction-memory backpressure immediately becomes a decode bubble because\\\\nIF emits a NOP and the PC stops. That is especially expensive here because\\\\nthe fitness run injects random iStalls by design, so front-end elasticity is\\\\nworth more than a tiny local logic cleanup.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front-end is tightly coupled to `io_imemReady`: any cycle with\\\\ninstruction-memory backpressure immediately becomes a decode bubble because\\\\nIF emits a NOP and the PC stops. That is especially expensive here because\\\\nthe fitness run injects random iStalls by design, so front-end elasticity is\\\\nworth more than a tiny local logic cleanup.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single-slot combinational fetch path with a small speculative\\\\nfetch queue in `if_stage`, likely 2-4 entries deep, that buffers `{pc,\\\\ninstr}` pairs when `io_imemReady` is high and feeds ID from the queue when\\\\nthe backend is ready. On redirects, flush younger queued instructions and\\\\nrestart fetch at the resolved target; on load-use or dmem stalls, preserve\\\\nthe buffered instructions instead of re-fetching them. This is a structural\\\\nfront-end change, not a predictor: it should raise CoreMark iterations/cycle\\\\nunder the orchestrator's random stall model with only modest area/timing\\\\ncost and without changing the single-retire RVFI contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Refactor IF into a small prefetch FIFO/skid-buffer front-end with redirect-aware invalidation and queue-based instruction delivery.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Change front-end stall logic to depend on fetch-queue availability plus backend hazards instead of bubbling immediately on every `!imemReady`.\\\"}, {\\\"...\\n  Line 4: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r1s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Static IF branch and JAL predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` still fetches strictly `pc+4` until EX resolves every branch\\\\nand jump, so each taken loop backedge and direct call burns wrong-path\\\\nbubbles. The bench core is already close to its LUT budget, so the best\\\\npredictor bet is a zero-table frontend change that improves CoreMark control\\\\nflow without adding BTB, RAS, or counter-RAM state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` still fetches strictly `pc+4` until EX resolves every branch\\\\nand jump, so each taken loop backedge and direct call burns wrong-path\\\\nbubbles. The bench core is already close to its LUT budget, so the best\\\\npredictor bet is a zero-table frontend change that improves CoreMark control\\\\nflow without adding BTB, RAS, or counter-RAM state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Port the lightweight static control predictor shape that already proved out\\\\nin `cores/v1`: predict aligned backward B-type branches taken and aligned\\\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, then carry `predicted_taken` and\\\\n`predicted_target` through the frontend so EX redirects only on mispredict.\\\\nLeave JALR resolved in EX. This should recover much of CoreMark's loop and\\\\ndirect-call control loss while staying much cheaper than learned branch\\\\ntables or return predictors, which are more likely to hurt Fmax on this\\\\nLUT-tight bench core.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX bundles with predicted_taken and predicted_target metadata so EX can validate the frontend prediction against the resolved next PC.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a decode-light static predictor that recognizes legal backward B-type branches and direct JALs, computes aligned predicted targets, and selects the predicted next PC only when fetch is not being stalled or flushed.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description...\\n  Line 5: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Precise cold DIV/REM interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench ALU keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same combinational\\\\nEX-stage result path as ADD, address generation, branch resolution, and MUL.\\\\nThat divider logic is cold in CoreMark's timed region but still taxes Fmax on\\\\nthe Tang Nano 20K. The prior broken attempt showed the performance idea is\\\\nstill attractive, but it must be implemented as a precise long-op interlock\\\\nthat preserves the full EX instruction context until completion.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench ALU keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same combinational\\\\nEX-stage result path as ADD, address generation, branch resolution, and MUL.\\\\nThat divider logic is cold in CoreMark's timed region but still taxes Fmax on\\\\nthe Tang Nano 20K. The prior broken attempt showed the performance idea is\\\\nstill attractive, but it must be implemented as a precise long-op interlock\\\\nthat preserves the full EX instruction context until completion.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move only DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a small iterative `div_unit` and leave the\\\\ncommon RV32I operations plus MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing one-cycle\\\\npath. When a divide-class instruction reaches EX, snapshot the full decoded\\\\ninstruction context and the forwarded operands into shadow registers, assert\\\\nan `ex_long_busy` interlock, bubble EX/MEM while the divider runs, and emit\\\\nexactly one EX/MEM result when the unit completes. This removes the cold\\\\ndivider from the universal ALU path, should improve Fmax with negligible\\\\nCoreMark IPC loss, and directly addresses the earlier failure mode of stale\\\\nEX/MEM retirement state leaking through during a multi-cycle operation.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 10, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/div_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add an iterative divider/remainder unit with exact RV32M divide-by-zero, overflow, and ALTOPS behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/a...\\n  Line 6: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Operand-Stage-Aware Load Interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current hazard unit stalls on any raw `rs1`/`rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not actually read that field yet\\\\nor only needs it later as store data in MEM. That conservative policy burns\\\\navoidable one-cycle bubbles in CoreMark's dense load/address/store code, and\\\\nthe recent `bench` regressions already showed that extra front-end machinery\\\\nis a worse trade here than tightening the backend interlock.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current hazard unit stalls on any raw `rs1`/`rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not actually read that field yet\\\\nor only needs it later as store data in MEM. That conservative policy burns\\\\navoidable one-cycle bubbles in CoreMark's dense load/address/store code, and\\\\nthe recent `bench` regressions already showed that extra front-end machinery\\\\nis a worse trade here than tightening the backend interlock.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework the load-use interlock around the stage where an operand is actually\\\\nconsumed. Add decode metadata for real `rs1`/`rs2` usage, treat STORE `rs2`\\\\nas a late MEM-stage consumer instead of an EX-stage consumer, and add a\\\\nWB-to-MEM store-data bypass so `load -> store` pairs with an independent\\\\naddress can proceed without the textbook bubble. This should recover\\\\nCoreMark IPC from both false-positive load stalls and true-but-late\\\\nstore-data dependencies, while keeping load data off the EX ALU/branch path\\\\nand avoiding another fetch-path timing penalty.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add decoded source-usage and late-store-data control bits to the pipeline bundles so hazards can distinguish EX-stage operands from MEM-stage-only store data.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/decoder.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Classify which legal ins...\\n  Line 7: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r2s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Registered I-Fetch Replay Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench frontend turns every `!io_imemReady` cycle into an\\\\nimmediate decode bubble, and the last structural fetch-queue attempt showed\\\\nthat simply buffering sequential fetches is not enough to get far enough\\\\nahead under the tournament's random ~22% iStall model. CoreMark repeatedly\\\\nrevisits a compact hot set of loop and helper PCs, so reusing already-fetched\\\\ninstructions is a stronger predictor bet than adding more branch decode or\\\\ntarget-add logic to the IF critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench frontend turns every `!io_imemReady` cycle into an\\\\nimmediate decode bubble, and the last structural fetch-queue attempt showed\\\\nthat simply buffering sequential fetches is not enough to get far enough\\\\nahead under the tournament's random ~22% iStall model. CoreMark repeatedly\\\\nrevisits a compact hot set of loop and helper PCs, so reusing already-fetched\\\\ninstructions is a stronger predictor bet than adding more branch decode or\\\\ntarget-add logic to the IF critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a small registered lookahead instruction replay predictor in `if_stage`.\\\\nOn each architecturally live fetch, fill a direct-mapped `{pc tag, instr}`\\\\ntable and, in parallel, look up the next fetch PC and register a one-cycle-\\\\nahead replay candidate. When `io_imemReady` later drops and the current PC\\\\nmatches a valid candidate, feed the cached instruction to decode and report\\\\nfetch-ready instead of injecting a bubble. Keep the normal ready-cycle path\\\\nlive-imem-first, invalidate replay candidates on redirect/flush, and avoid\\\\nfilling the table with killed wrong-path fetches. This should recover a\\\\nmeaningful fraction of random iStall loss while avoiding the immediate-decode\\\\nand predicted-target bookkeeping that made the earlier static branch/JAL\\\\npredictor regress on `bench`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6...\\n  Line 8: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r3s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"MEM-to-EX load bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench core still pays a mandatory one-cycle bubble after every\\\\nload-to-use dependency even though the memory interface is zero-wait when\\\\n`io_dmemReady=1` and the loaded word is already formed in MEM that same\\\\ncycle. CoreMark is rich in short load-consumer chains, so recovering those\\\\nbubbles should improve iterations/cycle without adding another speculative\\\\nfrontend structure or touching the cold DIV/REM path again.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench core still pays a mandatory one-cycle bubble after every\\\\nload-to-use dependency even though the memory interface is zero-wait when\\\\n`io_dmemReady=1` and the loaded word is already formed in MEM that same\\\\ncycle. CoreMark is rich in short load-consumer chains, so recovering those\\\\nbubbles should improve iterations/cycle without adding another speculative\\\\nfrontend structure or touching the cold DIV/REM path again.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a direct MEM-to-EX forwarding path for load results and relax the\\\\nhazard unit so a consumer can advance behind a load when the value will be\\\\navailable from MEM in the next cycle. This keeps the existing 5-stage\\\\npipeline and RVFI retirement boundary, but replaces the textbook load-use\\\\ninterlock with a late bypass that can feed ALU ops, branches, JALR, and\\\\nstore data from the current MEM-stage load result instead of forcing a\\\\nbubble. Compared with the earlier broken stage-aware store interlock, this\\\\nis more local: the forwarded operand still flows through EX and is captured\\\\ninto the normal pipeline state, so RVFI operand snapshots stay aligned with\\\\nthe real consumer datapath.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 7, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire a new MEM-stage load-result forwarding signal from `mem_stage` into EX/forwarding control and update the top-level hazard connections for the n...\\n  Line 9: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Exact load-use interlock with late store-data bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The fetch side already has a winning replay mechanism, so the strongest\\\\nremaining structural gain is more likely in the backend. `hazard_unit.sv`\\\\nstill stalls on any raw `if_id_rs1` or `if_id_rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not consume that field in EX or\\\\nonly needs store data later in MEM. CoreMark's dense mix of loads,\\\\nimmediate arithmetic, address generation, and stores makes those\\\\nconservative one-cycle bubbles expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The fetch side already has a winning replay mechanism, so the strongest\\\\nremaining structural gain is more likely in the backend. `hazard_unit.sv`\\\\nstill stalls on any raw `if_id_rs1` or `if_id_rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not consume that field in EX or\\\\nonly needs store data later in MEM. CoreMark's dense mix of loads,\\\\nimmediate arithmetic, address generation, and stores makes those\\\\nconservative one-cycle bubbles expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework the load-use interlock around true operand-consumption timing instead\\\\nof raw register-field matches. Decode exact legal `rs1` and `rs2` usage for\\\\neach instruction class, keep the current conservative stall policy for\\\\nillegal or otherwise unclassified encodings, and treat STORE `rs2` as a\\\\nMEM-stage consumer with a dedicated WB-to-MEM store-data bypass. To avoid\\\\nthe prior broken attempt's RVFI bug, carry the final architecturally\\\\nconsumed store-data value into retirement so `rvfi_rs2_rdata`,\\\\n`rvfi_mem_wdata`, and the actual dmem write all agree. This should remove\\\\nfalse bubbles from OP-IMM/LOAD/JALR/LUI/AUIPC/FENCE paths and eliminate the\\\\nmandatory `load -> store(rs2)` stall without putting load data on the EX\\\\nALU, branch-compare, or JALR critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"...\\n  Line 10: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r3s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Replay-Trained Next-PC Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` result already proved that a tiny replay predictor can\\\\nclaw back a meaningful fraction of the tournament's random iStall loss without\\\\nputting decode or target-add logic on the IF critical path. What it still does\\\\nnot address is the compulsory redirect/flush cost on hot taken loop backedges\\\\nand direct calls, which CoreMark executes repeatedly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` result already proved that a tiny replay predictor can\\\\nclaw back a meaningful fraction of the tournament's random iStall loss without\\\\nputting decode or target-add logic on the IF critical path. What it still does\\\\nnot address is the compulsory redirect/flush cost on hot taken loop backedges\\\\nand direct calls, which CoreMark executes repeatedly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing replay-table frontend into a learned next-PC predictor that\\\\nis trained by EX with the actual resolved successor of taken BRANCH and direct\\\\nJAL instructions. Each table entry would keep the fetched instruction plus a\\\\nsmall predicted-successor field, letting IF steer to a previously seen target\\\\non a PC hit and carry that prediction through the pipe so EX only redirects on\\\\nmispredict. This keeps the successful replay-table structure, avoids immediate\\\\ndecode and target-add work in IF, and should recover stable loop/call bubbles\\\\nthat the current sequential-only replay predictor still pays every time.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add predicted-control metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles so EX can validate a learned next-PC prediction against the resolved successor.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Upgrade the replay table into a tiny direct-mapped next-PC predictor that stores hot control-flow successors and can steer fetch to a ...\\n  Line 11: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r4s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Shared signedness-selectable multiplier\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core has already recovered the largest obvious IPC\\\\nlosses from random iStalls and load-use bubbles, so the next micro-opt is\\\\nmore likely to come from reducing hot-path ALU area and routing pressure.\\\\nToday `rtl/alu.sv` builds three separate 64-bit multiply datapaths\\\\n(`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`, `mul_su`) just to cover the four `MUL*` variants.\\\\nThat is expensive on a LUT-tight Tang Nano 20K and does not buy extra IPC,\\\\nbecause only one multiply flavor is needed per instruction.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core has already recovered the largest obvious IPC\\\\nlosses from random iStalls and load-use bubbles, so the next micro-opt is\\\\nmore likely to come from reducing hot-path ALU area and routing pressure.\\\\nToday `rtl/alu.sv` builds three separate 64-bit multiply datapaths\\\\n(`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`, `mul_su`) just to cover the four `MUL*` variants.\\\\nThat is expensive on a LUT-tight Tang Nano 20K and does not buy extra IPC,\\\\nbecause only one multiply flavor is needed per instruction.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the three parallel multiply datapaths with one shared\\\\nsignedness-selectable multiplier that derives all `MUL`, `MULH`, `MULHU`,\\\\nand `MULHSU` results from a single op-conditioned operand-extension path.\\\\nA 33x33-style sign-extended product is enough to cover the high-half signed\\\\ncombinations while still yielding the correct low 32 bits for plain `MUL`.\\\\nThis keeps multiply single-cycle, avoids the control and formal risk of a\\\\nmulticycle M-unit, preserves the existing DIV/REM behavior, and should cut\\\\nLUT/DSP glue plus route congestion enough to lift Fmax without giving back\\\\nthe IPC gains from the recent replay and load-bypass wins.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Collapse `mul_ss`/`mul_uu`/`mul_su` into ...\\n  Line 12: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"ID-stage exact control resolution\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best low-risk fetch and\\\\nload-use gains: it replays hot sequential instructions in IF and forwards\\\\nzero-wait loads from MEM to EX. The remaining visible IPC loss is that every\\\\ntaken BRANCH, JAL, and JALR still resolves in EX after the ID/EX register,\\\\nwhich forces extra wrong-path bubbles on CoreMark's hot loop backedges and\\\\nhelper calls.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best low-risk fetch and\\\\nload-use gains: it replays hot sequential instructions in IF and forwards\\\\nzero-wait loads from MEM to EX. The remaining visible IPC loss is that every\\\\ntaken BRANCH, JAL, and JALR still resolves in EX after the ID/EX register,\\\\nwhich forces extra wrong-path bubbles on CoreMark's hot loop backedges and\\\\nhelper calls.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move exact control-flow resolution one stage earlier by adding a dedicated\\\\nID-stage branch/jump unit. JAL can redirect immediately from `pc+imm`; BRANCH\\\\nand JALR should compare/add in ID using the regfile value plus targeted\\\\nbypasses from EX/MEM and MEM/WB, while conservatively stalling only when an\\\\noperand is still unavailable from an in-flight load. Carry the final\\\\nforwarded source values into the pipeline state so RVFI operand snapshots stay\\\\naligned with the values the control instruction actually consumed, and mark\\\\nID-resolved control ops so EX does not issue a duplicate redirect a cycle\\\\nlater. This keeps the existing single-retire contract and replay frontend,\\\\nbut should cut the taken-control penalty by roughly one cycle without adding\\\\nIF-side predictor state or target-table complexity.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the pipeline bundles with control-resolution metadata so ID-resolved BRANCH/JAL/JALR instru...\\n  Line 13: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Redirect-aware replay target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` frontend already proved that a tiny registered replay\\\\npredictor can recover a meaningful slice of random `io_imemReady` stalls\\\\nwithout hurting the IF critical path. Its remaining blind spot is taken\\\\ncontrol flow: every EX redirect clears the replay candidate, so hot loop\\\\nbackedges and direct-call targets lose replay coverage exactly where\\\\nCoreMark revisits the same PCs most often.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` frontend already proved that a tiny registered replay\\\\npredictor can recover a meaningful slice of random `io_imemReady` stalls\\\\nwithout hurting the IF critical path. Its remaining blind spot is taken\\\\ncontrol flow: every EX redirect clears the replay candidate, so hot loop\\\\nbackedges and direct-call targets lose replay coverage exactly where\\\\nCoreMark revisits the same PCs most often.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing replay-table predictor inside `if_stage` so redirects\\\\npre-arm replay for the resolved target instead of unconditionally clearing\\\\nthe candidate. On a redirect, look up `redirect_target` in the existing\\\\n`{pc,instr}` table and bank that target instruction for the next cycle when\\\\nit is already cached; keep all prediction state local to IF and continue to\\\\nemit a bubble on the redirect cycle itself. This should preserve the timing\\\\nadvantage of the current replay design, improve post-redirect fetch\\\\navailability on hot loops and helper calls, and avoid the formal-state and\\\\nbookkeeping costs that broke the learned next-PC predictor.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Teach the replay predictor to bank a redirect-target replay candidate from the existing table, instead of always invalidating replay state on redirect, while keeping all predictor state local to IF.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/...\\n  Line 14: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r5s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"DSP-friendly corrected shared multiplier\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` RTL already recovered the bigger IPC wins, so the\\\\nnext micro-opt is most likely another Fmax-and-LUT cleanup in the hot ALU\\\\ndatapath. `rtl/alu.sv` now shares `MUL*` through one 33x33 signed product,\\\\nwhich was a good first step, but it still widens both operands by a sign bit\\\\nand routes a 66-bit product even for plain `MUL` and `MULHU`. On the Tang\\\\nNano 20K, trimming that extra width and glue logic is attractive because the\\\\ncore is still above its LUT target and the previous multiplier cleanup already\\\\nproved this region is fitness-sensitive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` RTL already recovered the bigger IPC wins, so the\\\\nnext micro-opt is most likely another Fmax-and-LUT cleanup in the hot ALU\\\\ndatapath. `rtl/alu.sv` now shares `MUL*` through one 33x33 signed product,\\\\nwhich was a good first step, but it still widens both operands by a sign bit\\\\nand routes a 66-bit product even for plain `MUL` and `MULHU`. On the Tang\\\\nNano 20K, trimming that extra width and glue logic is attractive because the\\\\ncore is still above its LUT target and the previous multiplier cleanup already\\\\nproved this region is fitness-sensitive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the current sign-extended 33x33 shared multiply with a DSP-friendlier\\\\n32x32 unsigned product plus small signed-high correction terms for `MULH` and\\\\n`MULHSU`. `MUL` can still take the low 32 bits directly, `MULHU` can use the\\\\nraw high half, and the signed variants can derive their high halves by\\\\nsubtracting the operand-dependent correction terms instead of carrying an\\\\nextra sign bit through the full multiplier. This keeps all `MUL*` operations\\\\nsingle-cycle, avoids the control risk of a multicycle M-unit, preserves the\\\\nexisting DIV/REM and ALTOPS behavior, and should further reduce multiplier\\\\nrouting pressure and LUT glue without giving back ...\\n  Line 15: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-entry posted store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry posted-store buffer that captures a store's aligned address,\\\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible metadata when the store\\\\nreaches MEM. If the external dmem port stalls, retire the store from the buffer\\\\nimmediately, keep draining the buffered write in the background, and allow\\\\nyounger non-memory instructions to keep flowing. Only stall when a younger\\\\nload or store reaches MEM while the buffer is still occupied, preserving\\\\nin-order memory semantics without reintroducing load-use or control-path\\\\ncomplexity. This should recover CoreMark IPC from random dmem backpressure on\\\\nstore-heavy regions while avoiding the IF/ID critical-path regressions seen in\\\\nearlier structural experiments.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the MEM-stage/retirement bundles with store-buffer bookkeepin...\\n  Line 16: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r5s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Conflict-tolerant replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` frontend already gets real value from the tiny\\\\nsequential replay predictor, which means the safest remaining predictor win\\\\nis probably higher replay hit rate rather than deeper control speculation.\\\\nToday the table is only 8 entries and direct-mapped, so hot CoreMark PCs\\\\nthat alias on the same low index bits can evict each other and turn later\\\\nrandom `io_imemReady=0` cycles back into bubbles.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` frontend already gets real value from the tiny\\\\nsequential replay predictor, which means the safest remaining predictor win\\\\nis probably higher replay hit rate rather than deeper control speculation.\\\\nToday the table is only 8 entries and direct-mapped, so hot CoreMark PCs\\\\nthat alias on the same low index bits can evict each other and turn later\\\\nrandom `io_imemReady=0` cycles back into bubbles.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the direct-mapped 8-entry `{pc,instr}` replay table in `if_stage`\\\\nwith a small conflict-tolerant structure, preferably 2-way set-associative\\\\nwith a simple per-set MRU or round-robin replacement bit, while keeping the\\\\nexisting one-cycle-ahead replay candidate scheme and all predictor state\\\\nlocal to IF. The key point is that `fetch_available` still depends only on\\\\n`imem_ready || replay_hit`; the wider table lookup is only used to bank the\\\\nnext cycle's candidate, so this should improve replay coverage under random\\\\niStalls without reintroducing the IF decode, EX-visible prediction metadata,\\\\nor formal-state growth that hurt earlier predictor attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the direct-mapped replay table with a small conflict-tolerant replay predictor, keeping the live-fetch-first behavior and the current redirect/flush safety rules.\\\"}, {\\\"fi...\\n  Line 17: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r6s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"EX fast-path add/address bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest safe `bench` gains have come from local backend changes that\\\\nrecovered IPC or reduced hot-path routing without widening the front-end\\\\ncontrol logic. After the replay cache, MEM-to-EX load bypass, and posted\\\\nstore buffer, the next likely win is Fmax: the common EX add/address path\\\\nstill goes through the same mixed-function ALU block that also carries\\\\nshifts, compares, the shared multiplier, and combinational DIV/REM logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest safe `bench` gains have come from local backend changes that\\\\nrecovered IPC or reduced hot-path routing without widening the front-end\\\\ncontrol logic. After the replay cache, MEM-to-EX load bypass, and posted\\\\nstore buffer, the next likely win is Fmax: the common EX add/address path\\\\nstill goes through the same mixed-function ALU block that also carries\\\\nshifts, compares, the shared multiplier, and combinational DIV/REM logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split execute into a dedicated carry-chain fast path for the common\\\\nADD-class work and a side path for rarer logic/shift/M-extension work.\\\\n`ex_stage.sv` would detect plain ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and load/store address\\\\ngeneration and take a direct 32-bit adder result, while `alu.sv` is\\\\nrefactored so those hot operations no longer share the same wide output mux\\\\nand multiplier/divider fanout as the rare RV32M cases. This preserves the\\\\ncurrent single-cycle semantics and RVFI contract, avoids the control-heavy\\\\nmulticycle DIV/REM regressions, and should improve Tang Nano 20K timing on\\\\nthe instructions CoreMark executes most often.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a direct fast-path adder select for ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and memory address generation so common EX results bypass the mixed-function ALU result path.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"des...\\n  Line 18: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Alias-aware load bypass around posted stores\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing posted store buffer, but make it address-aware instead of\\\\nblocking all younger memory ops. When a younger load reaches MEM while a\\\\nmain-memory store is buffered, compare the load's aligned word address and\\\\nbyte mask against the buffered store: if there is no overlap, let the load use\\\\nthe dmem port immediately and drain the buffered store later; if the load is\\\\nfully covered by the buffered bytes, forward from the buffer; only stall on\\\\ntrue alias or partial-overlap cases, and keep younger stores conservative.\\\\nThis is a backend-only structural change that compounds with the winning\\\\nposted-store buffer while avoiding the IF/ID timing regressions seen in prior\\\\nstructural attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add store-buffer alias checking, demand-load-vs-drain arbitration, and buffered-store forwarding for fully covered younger loads.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the blank...\\n  Line 19: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r6s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Dual-candidate control-aware replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The winning predictor on `bench` is the tiny IF replay table, not earlier\\\\nbranch resolution or heavier control speculation. Recent failures showed that\\\\nadding decode-to-PC steering, prediction metadata, or wider replay RAM hurts\\\\neither Fmax or formal cost, yet the current single replay candidate still\\\\ngoes blind across hot taken backedges and direct-call targets because it only\\\\ntracks the sequential `pc+4` path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The winning predictor on `bench` is the tiny IF replay table, not earlier\\\\nbranch resolution or heavier control speculation. Recent failures showed that\\\\nadding decode-to-PC steering, prediction metadata, or wider replay RAM hurts\\\\neither Fmax or formal cost, yet the current single replay candidate still\\\\ngoes blind across hot taken backedges and direct-call targets because it only\\\\ntracks the sequential `pc+4` path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing replay-table frontend and EX redirect contract, but add a\\\\nsecond replay candidate slot dedicated to likely control successors. When a\\\\nlive fetch sees a direct JAL or an obvious backward B-type loop branch,\\\\n`if_stage` does a decode-light target calculation off the fetched instruction,\\\\nlooks that target up in the existing `{pc,instr}` replay table, and banks it\\\\nas a control-target candidate while the normal sequential `pc+4` candidate\\\\nstays unchanged. Fetch still walks `pc+4` until EX redirects, so there is no\\\\nnew predicted-PC state in IF/ID/EX; the win comes from having the first\\\\npost-redirect target instruction already replayable if `io_imemReady` drops.\\\\nThis aims at the same hot loop/call locality as prior control predictors, but\\\\nstays off the live fetch critical path and avoids the metadata/formal blow-up\\\\nthat hurt the earlier static and learned next-PC attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"mediu...\\n  Line 20: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r7s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"EX operand-isolated M/control side paths\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent winning `bench` changes came from trimming hot backend cones, not from\\\\nadding more speculative state. `ex_stage.sv` still routes BRANCH/JAL/JALR through\\\\nthe generic ALU plumbing even though branches only need compare/target logic and\\\\njumps retire `pc+4`, while `alu.sv` always builds the multiplier/divider operand\\\\ncone around live `a`/`b`. On Tang Nano 20K, that extra fanout and muxing is a\\\\nplausible remaining Fmax tax after the add/address fast path already landed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent winning `bench` changes came from trimming hot backend cones, not from\\\\nadding more speculative state. `ex_stage.sv` still routes BRANCH/JAL/JALR through\\\\nthe generic ALU plumbing even though branches only need compare/target logic and\\\\njumps retire `pc+4`, while `alu.sv` always builds the multiplier/divider operand\\\\ncone around live `a`/`b`. On Tang Nano 20K, that extra fanout and muxing is a\\\\nplausible remaining Fmax tax after the add/address fast path already landed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the current single-cycle semantics, but split execute into operation-class\\\\nside paths so only the relevant cone sees hot operands. In `ex_stage.sv`, treat\\\\nBRANCH/JAL/JALR as control-only ops that bypass the generic ALU result path, and\\\\nin `alu.sv` split simple logic/shift/compare work from a dedicated M-extension\\\\nsubpath so MUL/DIV/REM operand extension and result selection are only active for\\\\n`ALU_M*` ops. This is a narrower follow-on to the successful fast-add bypass: it\\\\nremoves more unnecessary mux/fanout from the common EX datapath without\\\\nreintroducing the multicycle divider control risk that already failed on `bench`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Classify control-only operations and bypass the generic ALU for BRANCH/JAL/JA...\\n  Line 21: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r7s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Two-entry posted store FIFO\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single posted-store slot with a tiny two-entry in-order store\\\\nFIFO that can absorb a short burst of normal-RAM stores while older buffered\\\\nwrites drain in the background. Keep the current conservative memory-order\\\\nrule that younger loads still wait until the FIFO is empty, and avoid the\\\\nlosing alias-compare/forwarding path entirely; the gain should come from\\\\nletting consecutive stores and following non-memory instructions retire\\\\nacross multi-cycle `io_dmemReady` stalls without touching IF timing or the\\\\nEX critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/store_queue.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a tiny two-entry in-order posted-store FIFO with enqueue/dequeue logic and head/tail status for MEM arbitration.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single buffered-store slot with the FIFO so a postable store can enqueue behind an older buffered store while preserving the existing conservative load ordering and RVFI store reporting.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"de...\\n  Line 22: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r7s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Victim-slot replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest predictor result on `bench` is still the tiny sequential replay\\\\ntable in `if_stage`, while every attempt that steered PC early or added wider\\\\ncontrol bookkeeping either regressed badly or only recovered a very small\\\\nbenefit. The remaining plausible predictor win is therefore not deeper branch\\\\nspeculation, but a cheaper way to recover replay-table conflict misses than\\\\nthe prior 2-way and control-target variants.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest predictor result on `bench` is still the tiny sequential replay\\\\ntable in `if_stage`, while every attempt that steered PC early or added wider\\\\ncontrol bookkeeping either regressed badly or only recovered a very small\\\\nbenefit. The remaining plausible predictor win is therefore not deeper branch\\\\nspeculation, but a cheaper way to recover replay-table conflict misses than\\\\nthe prior 2-way and control-target variants.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing 8-entry direct-mapped `{pc,instr}` replay table and the\\\\ncurrent one-cycle-ahead candidate scheme, but add a single fully-associative\\\\nvictim entry that captures only the most recently evicted replay line. On a\\\\nlive fetch that would overwrite a different valid PC in the direct-mapped\\\\nslot, spill that old `{pc,instr}` pair into the victim entry; when banking the\\\\nnext `pc+4` replay candidate, check the indexed slot first and then the victim\\\\nentry before giving up. This directly targets the low-bit alias pattern that\\\\nmotivated the earlier set-associative attempt, but avoids dual-way lookup,\\\\nMRU state, EX-visible prediction metadata, target-add logic, or any change to\\\\n`fetch_available = imem_ready || replay_hit`. If CoreMark's hot sequential PCs\\\\nmostly bounce between a few colliding indices, a one-entry victim should claw\\\\nback some replay hits at materially lower timing cost than widening the main\\\\ntable.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {...\\n  Line 23: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r8s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Aligned LW/SW MEM fast path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the biggest front-end and\\\\nEX-stage wins, so the next plausible limiter is the MEM-stage cone. Today\\\\n`mem_stage.sv` handles byte/halfword extraction, sign extension, byte-mask\\\\ngeneration, store-data replication, posted-store capture, and same-cycle\\\\nMEM-to-EX load bypass in one place even though CoreMark spends most of its\\\\ntimed region on aligned word loads and stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the biggest front-end and\\\\nEX-stage wins, so the next plausible limiter is the MEM-stage cone. Today\\\\n`mem_stage.sv` handles byte/halfword extraction, sign extension, byte-mask\\\\ngeneration, store-data replication, posted-store capture, and same-cycle\\\\nMEM-to-EX load bypass in one place even though CoreMark spends most of its\\\\ntimed region on aligned word loads and stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split common aligned `LW`/`SW` traffic onto a dedicated memory fast path and\\\\nleave byte/halfword formatting on a rarer side path. Precompute store-lane\\\\nmask and replicated store data as soon as the effective address and\\\\nforwarded store operand are known, then let aligned word loads forward raw\\\\n`dmem_rdata` directly to EX without passing through the generic\\\\nshift-and-sign-extend mux. Byte/halfword cases, misalignment traps, posted\\\\nstores, and RVFI masks still stay exact, but the hot CoreMark path loses\\\\nunnecessary lane-steering logic on both the dmem output cone and the\\\\nMEM-to-EX load-bypass cone. Because this removes logic from the common\\\\nmemory path instead of adding alias checks or deeper queues, it is a better\\\\nfit for Tang Nano 20K than the prior memory-side regressions.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the EX/MEM bundle with preformatted store-byte-lane metadata a...\\n  Line 24: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve direct JAL in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow early-redirect path for legal `JAL` instructions in ID while\\\\nleaving BRANCH and JALR resolution in EX. `JAL` needs only `pc + imm`, so it\\\\ncan redirect one stage earlier without the operand-bypass, compare, and\\\\nload-interlock machinery that made full ID-stage control resolution collapse\\\\nFmax. Carry an `early_redirected`/resolved-next-PC bit with the instruction so\\\\nEX suppresses the duplicate redirect but still produces the normal `rd = pc+4`\\\\nwriteback and RVFI retirement record. This should shave a bubble from hot\\\\ndirect calls and unconditional jumps in CoreMark while keeping the replay\\\\nfrontend, posted store buffer, and hot EX datapath essentially unchanged.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the decode/pipeline control bundles with a bit that marks a JAL as already redirected before EX, plus any resolved next-PC metadata needed to suppress duplicate redirects later.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JALs in decode, compute `pc + imm`, and...\\n  Line 25: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r8s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Redirect landing-pad replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The existing `bench` predictor win came from the tiny sequential replay path,\\\\nwhile heavier control speculation, wider replay tables, and IF-side target\\\\ndecode all regressed. The remaining frontend blind spot is the first fetch\\\\nafter a taken redirect: hot loop headers and helper-call targets are revisited\\\\noften, but the current replay candidate is cleared exactly when those PCs\\\\nbecome useful again.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The existing `bench` predictor win came from the tiny sequential replay path,\\\\nwhile heavier control speculation, wider replay tables, and IF-side target\\\\ndecode all regressed. The remaining frontend blind spot is the first fetch\\\\nafter a taken redirect: hot loop headers and helper-call targets are revisited\\\\noften, but the current replay candidate is cleared exactly when those PCs\\\\nbecome useful again.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny redirect-target landing-pad cache inside `if_stage`, trained only\\\\nfrom successful post-redirect fetches. When EX redirects, remember the target\\\\nPC for one cycle; if the first fetch at that target later sees `io_imemReady=0`\\\\nand the landing-pad cache has a matching `{pc, instr}` entry, serve that\\\\ncached instruction instead of bubbling. Keep the existing sequential replay\\\\ntable unchanged for the common `pc+4` case so the new logic only touches the\\\\nnarrow post-redirect path, and let the normal replay candidate take over again\\\\nafter the landing-pad instruction issues. Compared with the prior redirect-\\\\ntarget replay attempt, this avoids an always-on lookup into the general replay\\\\ntable and removes dependence on that table still holding the target line,\\\\nwhich should make the control-side replay win cheaper in both timing and\\\\nrouting.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a tiny redirect-tar...\\n  Line 26: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r9s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Retimed MEM/WB writeback path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core has already harvested the largest IPC wins,\\\\nso the next realistic gain is another Fmax cleanup on a path exercised by\\\\nalmost every dependent instruction. Today the final `rd` value is still\\\\nselected late in `wb_stage` and then fanned out into MEM/WB forwarding,\\\\nregfile writeback, same-cycle decode read bypass, and RVFI `rd_wdata`.\\\\nThat keeps an avoidable mux and extra routing on a very hot backend path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core has already harvested the largest IPC wins,\\\\nso the next realistic gain is another Fmax cleanup on a path exercised by\\\\nalmost every dependent instruction. Today the final `rd` value is still\\\\nselected late in `wb_stage` and then fanned out into MEM/WB forwarding,\\\\nregfile writeback, same-cycle decode read bypass, and RVFI `rd_wdata`.\\\\nThat keeps an avoidable mux and extra routing on a very hot backend path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Register the final writeback word at the MEM/WB boundary instead of\\\\nrecomputing it after the MEM/WB flop. `mem_stage.sv` can already see both\\\\n`alu_result` and the formed load data, so it can write a single `wb_data`\\\\nfield into `mem_wb_t`; downstream logic would then forward, write the\\\\nregfile, and drive RVFI from that registered value directly. Pair that with\\\\na simpler regfile/read-bypass arrangement so the MEM/WB-to-ID/EX dependency\\\\npath no longer pays the `mem_to_reg` select plus duplicated write-first\\\\nmuxing on every read port. This stays fully in-order, preserves the current\\\\nsingle-cycle load-use behavior and RVFI contract, and is a more local timing\\\\noptimization than the control-side and memory-side experiments that already\\\\nregressed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a registered final writeback-data field to `mem_wb_t` so...\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve canonical RET in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing ID redirect path to cover canonical aligned returns,\\\\ne.g. `jalr x0, x1, 0` and `jalr x0, x5, 0`, when the return-address\\\\nregister is already architecturally available from the regfile/WB path. If\\\\nan older in-flight instruction still owns that source register, simply fall\\\\nback to the current EX-stage JALR path instead of adding the full ID-stage\\\\nbypass/interlock machinery that regressed earlier. This reuses the existing\\\\n`id_redirect` plumbing, carries an \\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through\\\\nthe pipeline so EX suppresses the duplicate redirect, and should cut\\\\nanother hot function-return bubble in CoreMark with much lower timing risk\\\\nthan general early JALR/BRANCH resolution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the existing `jal_early_redirect` pipeline bit into an ID-resolved jump marker that can cover both direct `JAL` and narrow early-return `JALR` cases.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"descr...\\n  Line 28: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Canonical return-address stack predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` already found its best predictor win in the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay path, and the recent structural JAL improvement removed the cheapest\\\\nunconditional jump bubble. The remaining hot control penalty is canonical\\\\nfunction return traffic: `ret`-shaped `JALR` instructions still wait for EX,\\\\nwhile broader IF-side branch prediction, wider replay tables, and redirect\\\\nlanding-pad schemes all failed to repay their timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` already found its best predictor win in the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay path, and the recent structural JAL improvement removed the cheapest\\\\nunconditional jump bubble. The remaining hot control penalty is canonical\\\\nfunction return traffic: `ret`-shaped `JALR` instructions still wait for EX,\\\\nwhile broader IF-side branch prediction, wider replay tables, and redirect\\\\nlanding-pad schemes all failed to repay their timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a very small return-address stack that predicts only the common ABI call\\\\nand return pattern instead of general branch targets. Push `pc+4` for direct\\\\ncalls that write `x1`, use the top entry to early-redirect only canonical\\\\n`ret`/`jalr x0, 0(x1)` in ID, and carry the predicted target forward so EX\\\\ncan suppress the duplicate redirect on a hit or repair the path on a rare\\\\nmismatch. This stays much narrower than the failed branch/JAL predictors: it\\\\ndoes not widen the replay table, does not add BTB-style target RAM, and only\\\\nadds prediction metadata to the small subset of return instructions. CoreMark\\\\nis call/return heavy enough that shaving the return bubble should improve IPC\\\\nwhile keeping Fmax risk materially lower than earlier full-control schemes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the ID/EX bundle with narrow early...\\n  Line 29: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r10s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-gated operand bypass cleanup\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent writeback-retime regression showed that the remaining backend\\\\nlimiter is not just where `wb_data` is selected, but the always-on\\\\nWB-to-ID read-bypass compares and muxes that sit on both regfile read\\\\nports every cycle. On this core, many hot CoreMark instructions\\\\n(`OP-IMM`, `LOAD`, `JALR`, `AUIPC`, `LUI`, `JAL`) do not consume `rs2`,\\\\nand some consume neither source, yet the current datapath still drives raw\\\\ninstruction bitfields through regfile read, bypass, and forwarding logic.\\\\nTrimming that unnecessary operand work is a plausible remaining Fmax win\\\\nthat stays off the fragile IF/control paths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent writeback-retime regression showed that the remaining backend\\\\nlimiter is not just where `wb_data` is selected, but the always-on\\\\nWB-to-ID read-bypass compares and muxes that sit on both regfile read\\\\nports every cycle. On this core, many hot CoreMark instructions\\\\n(`OP-IMM`, `LOAD`, `JALR`, `AUIPC`, `LUI`, `JAL`) do not consume `rs2`,\\\\nand some consume neither source, yet the current datapath still drives raw\\\\ninstruction bitfields through regfile read, bypass, and forwarding logic.\\\\nTrimming that unnecessary operand work is a plausible remaining Fmax win\\\\nthat stays off the fragile IF/control paths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add exact source-usage classification in decode and use it to normalize\\\\nunused source operands to `x0` before they enter the hot read/bypass path.\\\\nThen simplify the register-file/writeback interaction so same-cycle WB\\\\nbypass is only applied to architecturally live operands, ideally by moving\\\\nthe compare+mux out of the generic regfile read ports and into the decode\\\\nstage after source-use is known. Forwarding logic should see zeroed unused\\\\nsource addresses, and retirement should carry the architecturally consumed\\\\nsource values so RVFI stays exact; illegal or otherwise uncl...\\n  Line 30: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drain-and-replace posted store slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing single posted-store slot, but let it dequeue the older\\\\nbuffered store to dmem and capture one younger postable store in the same\\\\ncycle. In practice this means teaching `mem_stage` to drain and replace the\\\\nslot when `dmem_ready` comes back, and teaching `hazard_unit` not to stall\\\\nan EX/MEM store that can retire into the just-freed slot while still keeping\\\\nyounger loads and non-postable stores conservative. This should recover some\\\\nof the store-burst benefit the two-entry FIFO targeted, without adding\\\\nanother queue entry, alias-compare logic, or front-end timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Allow the one-entry posted store buffer to drain an older buffered RAM store and capture a younger postable store in the same cycle, ...\\n  Line 31: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r10s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Seed replay from ID-resolved JAL targets\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest `bench` predictor result is still the tiny registered replay\\\\ntable, and predictor attempts that widened IF control, added target decode,\\\\nor carried prediction metadata into later stages consistently lost fitness.\\\\nAfter the recent ID-stage `JAL` win, the next control-side blind spot is the\\\\nvery first fetch at a direct-call target: the target PC is now known exactly\\\\none cycle early, but `if_stage` still drops replay state on `id_redirect`, so\\\\na warmed callee entry point bubbles whenever `io_imemReady=0` on that first\\\\ntarget cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest `bench` predictor result is still the tiny registered replay\\\\ntable, and predictor attempts that widened IF control, added target decode,\\\\nor carried prediction metadata into later stages consistently lost fitness.\\\\nAfter the recent ID-stage `JAL` win, the next control-side blind spot is the\\\\nvery first fetch at a direct-call target: the target PC is now known exactly\\\\none cycle early, but `if_stage` still drops replay state on `id_redirect`, so\\\\na warmed callee entry point bubbles whenever `io_imemReady=0` on that first\\\\ntarget cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the existing `{pc,instr}` replay table to seed the next-cycle replay\\\\ncandidate from an exact `id_redirect_target` when decode resolves a legal\\\\naligned `JAL`. On a live ID redirect, `if_stage` would probe the existing\\\\nreplay table with the JAL target and, on a hit, load `replay_valid_q`,\\\\n`replay_pc_q`, and `replay_instr_q` for the upcoming target fetch instead of\\\\nunconditionally clearing them. Keep EX-stage redirect handling, sequential\\\\nreplay fill, and `fetch_available = imem_ready || replay_hit` unchanged, so\\\\nthis stays in the proven replay-family design space: no IF-side immediate\\\\ndecode, no new predicted-PC metadata through ID/EX, and no broader branch or\\\\nreturn speculation. The pay...\\n  Line 32: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r11s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Normalize unused legal source reads\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The biggest `bench` gains have come from narrow backend cleanups that cut\\\\nhot-path routing without widening IF or control logic. After the JAL-in-ID,\\\\nposted-store, and add-fast-path wins, the remaining backend tax is that ID\\\\nstill drives raw instruction bitfields into the regfile, same-cycle WB read\\\\nbypass, and EX forwarding compares even for legal instructions that do not\\\\narchitecturally read `rs1` and/or `rs2`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The biggest `bench` gains have come from narrow backend cleanups that cut\\\\nhot-path routing without widening IF or control logic. After the JAL-in-ID,\\\\nposted-store, and add-fast-path wins, the remaining backend tax is that ID\\\\nstill drives raw instruction bitfields into the regfile, same-cycle WB read\\\\nbypass, and EX forwarding compares even for legal instructions that do not\\\\narchitecturally read `rs1` and/or `rs2`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add exact `uses_rs1` / `uses_rs2` classification for legal instructions, and\\\\nuse it only to normalize definitely-unused source addresses to `x0` before\\\\nthey enter the hot read/bypass path. Keep the existing write-first regfile\\\\ncontract intact and do not move bypassing out of `reg_file`; the key change\\\\nis to stop reading and comparing random immediate bits on opclasses like\\\\n`LUI`, `AUIPC`, `JAL`, `FENCE`, `EBREAK`, and the `rs2`-unused I-type ops.\\\\nFor safety, illegal or otherwise unvalidated encodings should stay\\\\nconservative, with source-use bits only cleared inside positively validated\\\\ndecoder arms. This should shrink the always-on WB-to-ID compare/mux work and\\\\nthe downstream forwarding compare fanout while staying off the fragile IF,\\\\nbranch, and memory-control paths that already regressed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/decoder.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Emit exact `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` bits...\\n  Line 33: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Replay-backed one-slot IF decoupler\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Turn the registered replay candidate into a true one-slot IF decoupler. When\\\\nthe current PC already matches a valid replay entry, let decode consume the\\\\ncached `{pc,instr}` while the live imem port fetches the next sequential PC and\\\\nrefreshes the lookahead slot/table in the same cycle. Keep the current\\\\nredirect/flush rules, keep all state local to `if_stage`, and do not add IF-side\\\\nbranch decode, target tables, or a multi-entry queue. This should raise\\\\nCoreMark iterations/cycle by converting hot replay hits into useful prefetch\\\\nbandwidth, recovering more random iStall loss than the fallback-only replay\\\\npath without the timing cost that sank broader fetch-queue and control-predictor\\\\nattempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"des...\\n  Line 34: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r11s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Warm-table current-PC replay fallback\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The winning predictor on `bench` is still the tiny sequential replay table,\\\\nbut it only helps when the next-PC candidate was banked in the prior cycle.\\\\nAfter redirects, flushes, or other candidate-clearing events, the frontend\\\\ncan still bubble on a warm PC even though the replay table already holds the\\\\ncorrect instruction for that address.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The winning predictor on `bench` is still the tiny sequential replay table,\\\\nbut it only helps when the next-PC candidate was banked in the prior cycle.\\\\nAfter redirects, flushes, or other candidate-clearing events, the frontend\\\\ncan still bubble on a warm PC even though the replay table already holds the\\\\ncorrect instruction for that address.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing one-cycle replay candidate and all current redirect rules,\\\\nbut add a cheaper fallback path inside `if_stage`: when `io_imemReady=0` and\\\\nthe registered replay candidate misses, probe the existing direct-mapped\\\\n`{pc,instr}` table for the current PC and serve that cached instruction on a\\\\nhit. This does not steer fetch to a predicted target, does not add branch or\\\\nreturn metadata, and does not widen the table; it only lets the frontend\\\\nexploit already-warm entries when `replay_valid_q` was intentionally cleared.\\\\nThat specifically targets the current post-redirect and post-flush blind spot\\\\nwith less timing risk than target-add logic, second candidate slots, or wider\\\\nassociative replay structures.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a current-PC fallback hit path that reuses the existing replay table when `imem_ready=0` and the registered replay candidate is invalid or mismatched, while preserving the existing redirect, flush, and sequential candidate behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"des...\\n  Line 35: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r12s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"LUTRAM-backed reset-valid register file\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current backend still carries a 32x32 flop-based register file with a\\\\nfull synchronous clear loop. On FPGA that reset fanout and FF routing sit on\\\\na hot path touched by decode reads, write-first bypass, and EX dependencies,\\\\nand they likely block the synthesis patterns that would infer cheaper\\\\ndistributed RAM.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current backend still carries a 32x32 flop-based register file with a\\\\nfull synchronous clear loop. On FPGA that reset fanout and FF routing sit on\\\\na hot path touched by decode reads, write-first bypass, and EX dependencies,\\\\nand they likely block the synthesis patterns that would infer cheaper\\\\ndistributed RAM.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Re-implement `rtl/reg_file.sv` as an FPGA-oriented 2-read/1-write register\\\\nfile that keeps x0 hardwired and same-cycle write-first bypass, but removes\\\\nthe per-word storage reset from the 1024-bit data array. Preserve the\\\\nexisting \\\\\\\"reset clears all architectural registers\\\\\\\" behavior with a small\\\\nper-register valid bitmap or equivalent epoch tag that resets cheaply and\\\\nforces unread entries to zero until rewritten. This should let\\\\nYosys/nextpnr map the data array to LUTRAM or other denser memory resources,\\\\ncut reset routing and FF pressure, and shorten the hot WB-to-ID/read path\\\\nwithout touching IF control, memory ordering, or RVFI source snapshots.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/reg_file.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the flop-array plus full synchronous clear with an FPGA-friendly inferred memory implementation, preserving x0 hardwiring and write-first bypass while using a small reset-valid structure to keep architectural reset-to-zero semantics.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_reg_file.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the regfile unit tests to verify that reset still makes all architectural reads return z...\\n  Line 36: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r12s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve ready branch-to-zero in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already harvested the cheapest broad structural wins: sequential IF\\\\nreplay, MEM-to-EX load forwarding, a one-entry posted store buffer, and an\\\\nexact early `JAL` redirect. The next visible IPC loss is still the taken\\\\nbranch bubble, but prior history shows that full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR\\\\nresolution collapses Fmax once decode has to do general bypass and\\\\ninterlock work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already harvested the cheapest broad structural wins: sequential IF\\\\nreplay, MEM-to-EX load forwarding, a one-entry posted store buffer, and an\\\\nexact early `JAL` redirect. The next visible IPC loss is still the taken\\\\nbranch bubble, but prior history shows that full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR\\\\nresolution collapses Fmax once decode has to do general bypass and\\\\ninterlock work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the existing `id_redirect` path for a much narrower exact control case:\\\\nlegal, aligned branch-against-zero instructions whose single live source is\\\\nalready architecturally ready in the regfile/WB path. Detect `BEQ/BNE` and\\\\nsigned zero-compare branch pseudos (`BLT/BGE` with `rs2 = x0`) in\\\\n`id_stage`, guard them with a cheap \\\\\\\"rs1 not pending in ID/EX or EX/MEM\\\\\\\"\\\\ncheck, and redirect taken cases one stage earlier while falling back to the\\\\nunchanged EX-stage branch path for everything else. Carry a generic\\\\n\\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through `id_ex_t` so EX suppresses duplicate\\\\nredirects but still recomputes `pc_next` and trap behavior, keeping RVFI\\\\nsource snapshots correct because the early path only fires when decode is\\\\nalready reading the final architecturally consumed operand.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the current JAL-only early-redirect marker in `id_ex_t` into a generic ID-resolved-control bit that can also tag ...\\n  Line 37: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r12s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Predict backward branches in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already found its best front-end wins by keeping speculation narrow:\\\\nthe sequential replay table helps with random iStalls, and the current best\\\\nsource moved only unconditional `JAL` into ID. The biggest remaining control\\\\nbubble is still the hot loop backedge branch, but the prior full ID-stage\\\\nBRANCH/JALR resolution failed because decode-side compare, bypass, and\\\\nload-interlock logic were too timing-expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already found its best front-end wins by keeping speculation narrow:\\\\nthe sequential replay table helps with random iStalls, and the current best\\\\nsource moved only unconditional `JAL` into ID. The biggest remaining control\\\\nbubble is still the hot loop backedge branch, but the prior full ID-stage\\\\nBRANCH/JALR resolution failed because decode-side compare, bypass, and\\\\nload-interlock logic were too timing-expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing ID redirect path from \\\\\\\"exact early JAL\\\\\\\" to a static\\\\nbackward-branch predictor that redirects only legal, aligned B-type\\\\ninstructions with a negative immediate. This keeps prediction out of IF,\\\\nreuses the already-cheap `pc + imm` work that ID does for JAL, and avoids\\\\nthe operand-bypass/compare machinery that sank exact early branch\\\\nresolution. EX still computes the real branch condition and target, but it\\\\nonly repairs mispredicted loop exits by redirecting back to `pc+4`; correct\\\\ntaken backedges simply suppress the duplicate EX redirect. That targets a\\\\nhot CoreMark penalty with much lower Fmax risk than IF-side branch target\\\\nprediction, RAS state, or full decode-time BRANCH/JALR resolution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the existing JAL-only early-redirect metadata in `id_ex_t` into a generic ID-predicted-control bit for J...\\n  Line 38: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r13s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Downstream-only source-use gating\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The remaining plausible `bench` gains are backend timing cleanups, not more\\\\nfront-end speculation or memory-control machinery. Two recent micro-opt tries\\\\nalready pointed at the same hotspot: unused `rs1`/`rs2` fields still drive\\\\nforwarding and bypass compares on hot OP-IMM, LOAD, JAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE,\\\\nand EBREAK traffic. Those tries failed because they touched the raw regfile\\\\nread path and RVFI-visible source view, which this core's correctness contract\\\\ntreats as architectural.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The remaining plausible `bench` gains are backend timing cleanups, not more\\\\nfront-end speculation or memory-control machinery. Two recent micro-opt tries\\\\nalready pointed at the same hotspot: unused `rs1`/`rs2` fields still drive\\\\nforwarding and bypass compares on hot OP-IMM, LOAD, JAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE,\\\\nand EBREAK traffic. Those tries failed because they touched the raw regfile\\\\nread path and RVFI-visible source view, which this core's correctness contract\\\\ntreats as architectural.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add exact legal `uses_rs1` / `uses_rs2` bits in decode, but apply them only\\\\nafter the raw regfile read has already happened. Keep `reg_file.sv`, the raw\\\\n`rs1_addr`/`rs2_addr` wiring, same-cycle write-first behavior, and RVFI source\\\\nsnapshots unchanged. Instead, carry conservative source-use bits through the\\\\nexisting control bundle and use them only to suppress downstream EX forwarding\\\\ncompare trees and select logic for definitely-unused operands. Default the new\\\\nbits to \\\\\\\"used\\\\\\\" and only clear them inside validated decoder arms, so illegal\\\\nencodings remain conservative. This targets the same backend fanout that the\\\\nbroken normalization attempts were aiming at, but avoids reopening their\\\\nselftest/RVFI failure mode by leaving the architectural source-read view intact.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\"...\\n  Line 39: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r13s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Mem-stall replay prefetch slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current winning frontend only learns future instructions from cycles where\\\\ndecode itself is advancing. When MEM-side backpressure freezes the pipe, the\\\\ninstruction port usually sits idle even though those cycles could have been\\\\nused to seed the next sequential replay hit. Earlier broad fetch queues lost\\\\nbecause they had to run ahead every cycle; the remaining structural opening is\\\\na narrower overlap of dmem-stall time with harmless prefetch work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current winning frontend only learns future instructions from cycles where\\\\ndecode itself is advancing. When MEM-side backpressure freezes the pipe, the\\\\ninstruction port usually sits idle even though those cycles could have been\\\\nused to seed the next sequential replay hit. Earlier broad fetch queues lost\\\\nbecause they had to run ahead every cycle; the remaining structural opening is\\\\na narrower overlap of dmem-stall time with harmless prefetch work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Teach `if_stage` to treat backend-only MEM stalls as prefetch opportunities\\\\ninstead of total frontend dead time. While architectural PC and IF->ID state\\\\nstay frozen, drive a shadow sequential fetch for the held `pc+4` (or a tiny\\\\none-slot skid successor) and bank the returned `{pc,instr}` into the existing\\\\nreplay candidate/table; when the stall clears, the normal path resumes and\\\\nthe next instruction is already replayable if `io_imemReady` drops. This\\\\ncompounds with the existing replay-table and posted-store wins, avoids\\\\ndecode-time branch/JALR logic, and is narrower than the failed always-on\\\\nfetch queue because it only adds work on cycles that were already lost to\\\\ndmem backpressure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a backend-stall prefetch/skid path that keeps architectural IF output frozen...\\n  Line 40: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r13s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Protect hot replay lines from conflict eviction\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The only predictor family that has helped `bench` is the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay table in `if_stage`; broader control prediction, target seeding,\\\\ncurrent-PC fallback, and associative widening all regressed. One remaining\\\\nweakness of the current direct-mapped design is that every architecturally\\\\nlive fetch overwrites its indexed slot immediately, so a one-off colliding PC\\\\ncan evict a line that already proved useful on prior replay hits.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The only predictor family that has helped `bench` is the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay table in `if_stage`; broader control prediction, target seeding,\\\\ncurrent-PC fallback, and associative widening all regressed. One remaining\\\\nweakness of the current direct-mapped design is that every architecturally\\\\nlive fetch overwrites its indexed slot immediately, so a one-off colliding PC\\\\ncan evict a line that already proved useful on prior replay hits.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing `fetch_available = imem_ready || replay_hit` contract, the\\\\none-cycle `pc+4` replay candidate, and all redirect behavior unchanged, but\\\\nadd a tiny usefulness predictor to replay-table replacement. Give each replay\\\\nentry a one-bit \\\\\\\"hot\\\\\\\" or second-chance marker that is set only after that\\\\nentry successfully serves a replay hit; on a later colliding fill from a\\\\ndifferent PC, do not immediately evict a marked-hot line, and instead require\\\\na second conflict before replacement. This stays entirely inside\\\\n`rtl/if_stage.sv`, adds no extra tag-compare path to `fetch_available`, and\\\\navoids the timing cost of extra ways, victim lookups, target caches, or\\\\ndecode-time control prediction while still targeting replay misses caused by\\\\ncold conflict pollution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a per-entr...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":40,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"\\\"outcome\\\": \\\"(improvement|regression|broken|placement_failed)\\\"\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778834555433,\"end\":1778834555448}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_01265be87262ec97016a06dc7b2da081998a39a6c2c5d4ff83\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2acd41db001IlE2K1TmtcPq7I\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d53321f1ffebzeyg5JxRb9p3u\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2acd27fb0018PJRE8axt4sxkx\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:1020:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778834575012,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d53321f1ffebzeyg5JxRb9p3u\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_hmh5ikzPl645XFfrrld9rffU\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"drain-and-replace|one-slot IF decoupler|mem-stall replay prefetch|canonical RET|branch-to-zero\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 4 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve canonical RET in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing ID redirect path to cover canonical aligned returns,\\\\ne.g. `jalr x0, x1, 0` and `jalr x0, x5, 0`, when the return-address\\\\nregister is already architecturally available from the regfile/WB path. If\\\\nan older in-flight instruction still owns that source register, simply fall\\\\nback to the current EX-stage JALR path instead of adding the full ID-stage\\\\nbypass/interlock machinery that regressed earlier. This reuses the existing\\\\n`id_redirect` plumbing, carries an \\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through\\\\nthe pipeline so EX suppresses the duplicate redirect, and should cut\\\\nanother hot function-return bubble in CoreMark with much lower timing risk\\\\nthan general early JALR/BRANCH resolution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the existing `jal_early_redirect` pipeline bit into an ID-resolved jump marker that can cover both direct `JAL` and narrow early-return `JALR` cases.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"descr...\\n  Line 30: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drain-and-replace posted store slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing single posted-store slot, but let it dequeue the older\\\\nbuffered store to dmem and capture one younger postable store in the same\\\\ncycle. In practice this means teaching `mem_stage` to drain and replace the\\\\nslot when `dmem_ready` comes back, and teaching `hazard_unit` not to stall\\\\nan EX/MEM store that can retire into the just-freed slot while still keeping\\\\nyounger loads and non-postable stores conservative. This should recover some\\\\nof the store-burst benefit the two-entry FIFO targeted, without adding\\\\nanother queue entry, alias-compare logic, or front-end timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Allow the one-entry posted store buffer to drain an older buffered RAM store and capture a younger postable store in the same cycle, ...\\n  Line 33: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Replay-backed one-slot IF decoupler\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Turn the registered replay candidate into a true one-slot IF decoupler. When\\\\nthe current PC already matches a valid replay entry, let decode consume the\\\\ncached `{pc,instr}` while the live imem port fetches the next sequential PC and\\\\nrefreshes the lookahead slot/table in the same cycle. Keep the current\\\\nredirect/flush rules, keep all state local to `if_stage`, and do not add IF-side\\\\nbranch decode, target tables, or a multi-entry queue. This should raise\\\\nCoreMark iterations/cycle by converting hot replay hits into useful prefetch\\\\nbandwidth, recovering more random iStall loss than the fallback-only replay\\\\npath without the timing cost that sank broader fetch-queue and control-predictor\\\\nattempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"des...\\n  Line 36: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r12s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve ready branch-to-zero in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already harvested the cheapest broad structural wins: sequential IF\\\\nreplay, MEM-to-EX load forwarding, a one-entry posted store buffer, and an\\\\nexact early `JAL` redirect. The next visible IPC loss is still the taken\\\\nbranch bubble, but prior history shows that full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR\\\\nresolution collapses Fmax once decode has to do general bypass and\\\\ninterlock work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already harvested the cheapest broad structural wins: sequential IF\\\\nreplay, MEM-to-EX load forwarding, a one-entry posted store buffer, and an\\\\nexact early `JAL` redirect. The next visible IPC loss is still the taken\\\\nbranch bubble, but prior history shows that full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR\\\\nresolution collapses Fmax once decode has to do general bypass and\\\\ninterlock work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the existing `id_redirect` path for a much narrower exact control case:\\\\nlegal, aligned branch-against-zero instructions whose single live source is\\\\nalready architecturally ready in the regfile/WB path. Detect `BEQ/BNE` and\\\\nsigned zero-compare branch pseudos (`BLT/BGE` with `rs2 = x0`) in\\\\n`id_stage`, guard them with a cheap \\\\\\\"rs1 not pending in ID/EX or EX/MEM\\\\\\\"\\\\ncheck, and redirect taken cases one stage earlier while falling back to the\\\\nunchanged EX-stage branch path for everything else. Carry a generic\\\\n\\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through `id_ex_t` so EX suppresses duplicate\\\\nredirects but still recomputes `pc_next` and trap behavior, keeping RVFI\\\\nsource snapshots correct because the early path only fires when decode is\\\\nalready reading the final architecturally consumed operand.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the current JAL-only early-redirect marker in `id_ex_t` into a generic ID-resolved-control bit that can also tag ...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":4,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"drain-and-replace|one-slot IF decoupler|mem-stall replay prefetch|canonical RET|branch-to-zero\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778834574986,\"end\":1778834575011}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0a35396f5fe60fd6016a06dc8ede4c8198ae3601fc5783b7fc\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2acd8e88001jgqr04R75IdwIu\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d53321f1ffebzeyg5JxRb9p3u\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2acd43c4001qTxQbShRd5csKD\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:1047:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778836137625,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d51ad624ffe3QWHb1QQRha61G\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_CqIlfjK2sCTWy28GHPERH653\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":400},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Moving DIV/REM into a multicycle EX micro-unit is not a local ALU swap; if stall/freeze logic does not hold the full EX instruction context and suppress side effects until completion, riscv-formal will start failing even simple ADD/SUB/reg checks from corrupted EX/MEM retirement state.\\n2: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r1s1 (regression, -3.89%): A tiny sequential fetch queue is not enough by itself to beat random `io_imemReady` stalls on this front end; with only 1-instruction-per-cycle fetch bandwidth and frequent redirect flushes, the queue rarely gets far enough ahead of decode to repay its extra control.\\n3: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r1s2 (regression, -1.94%): On `bench`, a zero-table IF predictor for backward B-type branches and direct JALs still loses overall fitness when it adds immediate-decode, target-add, and `predicted_taken`/`predicted_target` bookkeeping to the fetch path, because the front-end cost outweighs the CoreMark loop/call bubbles it removes.\\n4: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r2s0 (regression, +7.86%): On `bench`, moving cold DIV/REM ops off the ALU can still lose when the precise fix adds hot-path interlock machinery like `ex_long_busy`, full EX-context shadow registers, and EX/MEM bubble control, because CoreMark rarely executes divides and does not repay the added control cost.\\n5: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r2s1 (broken, n/a): Stage-aware load/store interlocks must move the architectural operand snapshot with the true consumer stage too; if STORE `rs2` is satisfied by a late WB->MEM bypass but retirement still reports the old EX-captured `rs2_val`, riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs2_rdata` on load->store pairs.\\n6: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r2s2 (improvement, +11.75%): On `bench`, a registered `{pc,instr}` replay table that banks the next `pc+4` hit can hide random `io_imemReady` stalls better than a plain fetch queue or decode-time branch predictor, because CoreMark revisits a small hot sequential PC set and the replay path stays off the IF critical path.\\n7: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r3s0 (improvement, +5.14%): On `bench`, a MEM-to-EX load bypass is worth more than a mandatory load-use bubble when `io_dmemReady` commonly returns zero-wait data and CoreMark presents many short load-consumer chains, provided the forwarded value still flows through the normal EX path so RVFI operand snapshots stay aligned.\\n8: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): Exact `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2_*` load-use gating must retime RVFI source snapshots to the operand's true consumer stage for both sources, not just STORE data, or riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs1_rdata`/`rvfi_rs2_rdata` when forwarding changes the architecturally consumed value.\\n9: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r3s2 (broken, n/a): Extending the successful replay table into a learned next-PC predictor can exhaust `riscv-formal`'s wall-clock budget even without IF-side decode, because threading full `predicted_next_pc`/`predicted_valid` metadata and per-entry successor state through IF/ID/EX forces every instruction proof to reason about extra 32-bit control-flow state and EX mispredict comparisons.\\n10: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.78%): On `bench`, parallel `mul_ss`/`mul_uu`/`mul_su` datapaths are wasted hot-path area for RV32M; collapsing `MUL*` onto one sign-selectable 33x33 product preserves single-cycle behavior while reducing ALU routing pressure enough to lift Tang Nano 20K fitness.\\n11: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (regression, -59.23%): On `bench`, exact ID-stage BRANCH/JAL/JALR resolution is too timing-expensive; pulling compare/target-add plus EX/MEM/MEM/WB operand-bypass and load-interlock logic into decode collapsed Fmax to 52 MHz, so CoreMark's taken-control bubbles are cheaper than widening the front-end critical path.\\n12: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (regression, +0.61%): On `bench`, replaying warmed EX `redirect_target` instructions from the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` table is too narrow a win to pay for extra redirect-hit lookup/arming logic, because it only helps when `io_imemReady` stalls on the first post-redirect cycle while the original `pc+4` replay already captures the hotter sequential locality.\\n13: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r5s0 (regression, +9.14%): On `bench`, replacing the shared sign-selectable 33x33 `MUL*` datapath with a 32x32 product plus `MULH`/`MULHSU` sign-correction subtractors loses on Tang Nano 20K, because the extra 32-bit correction/mux logic on the ALU hot path costs more than carrying the multiplier sign bit through the DSP.\\n14: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r5s1 (improvement, +12.75%): On `bench`, a one-entry posted store buffer that only posts main-memory stores and blocks only younger memory ops turns random `io_dmemReady` store backpressure into background drain time, recovering CoreMark IPC without paying the IF/ID timing cost of front-end control changes.\\n15: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r5s2 (regression, +0.93%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table already has enough capacity for CoreMark's hot sequential PCs; adding 2-way set associativity with dual tag checks, way select, and MRU replacement costs more than the low-bit conflict misses it removes.\\n16: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r6s0 (improvement, +3.63%): On `bench`, carving ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and load/store address generation onto a dedicated EX carry-chain path pays because CoreMark is dominated by simple add/address ops, so keeping them out of the ALU's wide logic/shift/RV32M mux and fanout improves timing without front-end control changes.\\n17: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r6s1 (regression, -11.69%): On `bench`, extending the posted store buffer with MEM-stage aligned-address/byte-mask overlap checks, load forwarding, and load-vs-drain arbitration lost overall fitness because the extra compare/mask/mux logic sat on the hot memory path, dropping Fmax from 151.3 MHz to 133.4 MHz while CoreMark did not expose enough independent load-behind-store overlap to pay it back.\\n18: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r6s2 (regression, +1.63%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table should stay a single sequential `pc+4` candidate; adding a second JAL/backward-branch control-target slot only helps on rare first-post-redirect `io_imemReady` stalls, while the extra immediate-decode, target-add, and second replay-hit path in `if_stage` costs more than it saves.\\n19: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r7s0 (regression, -19.89%): On `bench`, the profitable EX split stops at the add/address fast path; adding op-class gating like `use_alu_result`, zeroed ALU operands for BRANCH/JAL/JALR, and `is_m_op ? a/b : 0` around the shared RV32M cone puts extra decode/mux logic back on the hot EX path and drops Fmax far more than the cold-cone isolation helps.\\n20: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r7s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On `bench`, the posted-store win appears depth-sensitive on Tang Nano 20K: growing the one-entry main-memory store buffer into a two-entry in-order FIFO with `store_buf_full` backpressure and queued-drain muxing pushed MEM/backpressure routing past place-and-route limits before any CoreMark gain could be measured.\\n21: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r7s2 (regression, +0.00%): On `bench`, low-bit conflict misses in the 8-entry sequential `{pc,instr}` replay table are not a meaningful limiter; even a one-entry fully-associative victim spill/check in `if_stage` failed to beat the plain direct-mapped design, so extra replay-hit selection state is already past CoreMark's payoff point.\\n22: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r8s0 (regression, -14.36%): On `bench`, peeling aligned `LW`/`SW` onto a dedicated MEM fast path is not worthwhile when the generic width logic already makes word accesses the default identity/`4'b1111` case; hoisting `mem_mask`/store formatting into EX and threading `mem_word_op` through the pipe adds ALU-result fanout and stage metadata without enough hot-path relief to repay it.\\n23: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r8s1 (improvement, +31.31%): On `bench`, decode-stage control fixes should isolate the cheapest unconditional case: resolving legal aligned `JAL` in ID and carrying a `jal_early_redirect` bit to suppress the duplicate EX redirect removes CoreMark's hot direct-call/jump bubble without dragging BRANCH/JALR compare, bypass, and load-interlock logic onto the front-end critical path.\\n24: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r8s2 (regression, -4.11%): On `bench`, redirect-target replay is opportunity-limited rather than lookup-limited; even a separate one-cycle landing-pad `{pc,instr}` cache in `if_stage` only pays on repeated redirects whose very first fetch later stalls on `io_imemReady=0`, which is rarer than the locality already captured by the sequential `pc+4` replay.\\n25: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r9s0 (regression, -11.24%): On `bench`, precomputing and registering MEM/WB `wb_data` is not a free timing win if the change merely shifts that value's fanout into same-cycle WB-to-regfile/ID read-bypass compare+mux logic; backend writeback cleanup must reduce total hot-path routing, not just move the `mem_to_reg` select earlier.\\n26: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r9s1 (regression, -13.86%): On `bench`, decode-time redirect wins stop at plain `JAL`; even a narrow canonical `ret` fast path (`jalr x0, x1/x5, 0`) with `rs1_busy` fallback adds enough decode-side dependency compare and target-select logic to cost more Fmax than CoreMark's return bubbles repay.\\n27: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r9s2 (regression, -64.82%): On `bench`, the canonical-return loss is not just an `rs1_busy` decode hazard: even a tiny 4-entry `ras_stack` that predicts only `JAL x1`/`ret` and carries `early_redirect_target` into EX for repair adds enough speculative call/return bookkeeping to lose more Fmax than CoreMark's return bubbles repay.\\n28: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r10s0 (broken, n/a): On `bench`, the `reg_file` write-first/WB-to-ID same-cycle read path is part of the backend correctness contract, not free timing junk; moving that bypass behind decode-gated `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` logic can miss real dependencies and break selftest.\\n29: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r10s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On `bench`, the posted-store budget is not just buffer depth: feeding `dmem_ready` into same-cycle drain-and-replace control in both `mem_stage` and `hazard_unit` was enough to blow Tang Nano 20K placement, so future store-burst work must avoid combinational refill/backpressure feedback.\\n30: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r10s2 (regression, -35.73%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table should stay dedicated to the sequential `pc+4` stream; repointing `replay_valid_q`/`replay_pc_q` at an exact ID-resolved `JAL` target burns the hotter fallthrough reuse and adds redirect-target selection to `if_stage` for a first-target-stall corner CoreMark rarely needs.\\n31: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r11s0 (broken, n/a): On `bench`, unused-source cleanup must not rewrite the shared `id_stage` `rs*_addr`/regfile-read path, because this repo's cosim/RVFI contract snapshots raw instruction `rs1`/`rs2` fields and pre-write register contents even for operands the ISA ignores, so `uses_rs*` normalization to `x0` needs a separate trace view or breaks selftest.\\n32: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r11s2 (regression, -51.23%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table only pays when a hit is pre-banked into the one-cycle replay candidate; adding same-cycle current-PC tag-compare and fallback muxing to salvage warm entries after flush/redirect clears puts too much logic back on the fetch critical path for a rare stall corner.\\n33: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r12s0 (regression, -3.37%): On `bench`, a tiny 32x32 2R1W register file is too small and semantically constrained for LUTRAM-plus-valid-bit conversion to pay; preserving write-first bypass and reset-to-zero semantics with `reg_valid`/read-zero gating adds muxing on the hot WB-to-ID read path that can cost more Fmax than removing the data-array reset saves.\\n34: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r12s1 (regression, -11.10%): On `bench`, decode-time redirect wins really stop at source-free `JAL`; even an exact one-source zero-compare B-type fast path guarded only by `older_id_ex`/`older_ex_mem` pending checks still puts live register-read dependency compares and branch-condition logic on `id_stage`, costing more Fmax than the taken-branch bubble repays.\\n35: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r12s2 (regression, -49.77%): On `bench`, decode-time redirect wins truly stop at unconditional `JAL`; even a source-free static backward-B-type predictor that only checks `imm[31]`, legal/aligned target, and carries an `id_ctrl_redirect` mispredict-repair bit into EX adds enough front-end control plumbing that CoreMark's hot loop backedges still do not repay the Fmax loss.\\n36: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r13s0 (regression, -30.85%): On `bench`, downstream-only exact `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` gating is not a free backend cleanup when RVFI must still see ungated source forwarding; splitting `forward_unit`/`ex_stage` into gated EX selects plus separate `rvfi_fwd_rs*_sel` paths duplicates hot compare/mux logic and costs more Fmax than the pruned unused-operand matches save.\\n37: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r14s0 (regression, -28.58%): On `bench`, collapsing `DIV`/`DIVU`/`REM`/`REMU` into a sign-corrected unsigned-magnitude flow is not automatically a resource win; expressing the refactor as shared absolute-value logic plus parallel `/` and `%` operators can leave two wide cold arithmetic cones and extra fixup muxing in `alu.sv`, which CoreMark's rare divides do not repay.\\n38: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r14s1 (broken, n/a): On `bench`, execute-under-load is not a small extension of the posted-store buffer; once a stalled load moves into a `pending_load` slot and a younger op can complete into a shadow result, the core effectively needs mini-ROB-grade bookkeeping beyond a simple `rd` scoreboard to keep long CoreMark execution coherent under random `io_dmemReady` stalls.\\n39: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r14s2 (broken, n/a): On `bench`, a \\\"non-architectural\\\" MEM-stall shadow prefetch is not actually invisible on the single shared IF port; once `if_stage` drives `imem_addr` to held `pc+4` while `pc`/IF->ID stay frozen, the external fetch stream no longer matches architectural frontend state and long CoreMark cosim runs can desynchronize.\\n\\n(End of file - total 39 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Moving DIV/REM into a multicycle EX micro-unit is not a local ALU swap; if stall/freeze logic does not hold the full EX instruction context and suppress side effects until completion, riscv-formal will start failing even simple ADD/SUB/reg checks from corrupted EX/MEM retirement state.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r1s1 (regression, -3.89%): A tiny sequential fetch queue is not enough by itself to beat random `io_imemReady` stalls on this front end; with only 1-instruction-per-cycle fetch bandwidth and frequent redirect flushes, the queue rarely gets far enough ahead of decode to repay its extra control.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r1s2 (regression, -1.94%): On `bench`, a zero-table IF predictor for backward B-type branches and direct JALs still loses overall fitness when it adds immediate-decode, target-add, and `predicted_taken`/`predicted_target` bookkeeping to the fetch path, because the front-end cost outweighs the CoreMark loop/call bubbles it removes.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r2s0 (regression, +7.86%): On `bench`, moving cold DIV/REM ops off the ALU can still lose when the precise fix adds hot-path interlock machinery like `ex_long_busy`, full EX-context shadow registers, and EX/MEM bubble control, because CoreMark rarely executes divides and does not repay the added control cost.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r2s1 (broken, n/a): Stage-aware load/store interlocks must move the architectural operand snapshot with the true consumer stage too; if STORE `rs2` is satisfied by a late WB->MEM bypass but retirement still reports the old EX-captured `rs2_val`, riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs2_rdata` on load->store pairs.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r2s2 (improvement, +11.75%): On `bench`, a registered `{pc,instr}` replay table that banks the next `pc+4` hit can hide random `io_imemReady` stalls better than a plain fetch queue or decode-time branch predictor, because CoreMark revisits a small hot sequential PC set and the replay path stays off the IF critical path.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r3s0 (improvement, +5.14%): On `bench`, a MEM-to-EX load bypass is worth more than a mandatory load-use bubble when `io_dmemReady` commonly returns zero-wait data and CoreMark presents many short load-consumer chains, provided the forwarded value still flows through the normal EX path so RVFI operand snapshots stay aligned.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r3s1 (broken, n/a): Exact `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2_*` load-use gating must retime RVFI source snapshots to the operand's true consumer stage for both sources, not just STORE data, or riscv-formal's `reg` check will catch stale `rvfi_rs1_rdata`/`rvfi_rs2_rdata` when forwarding changes the architecturally consumed value.\\n- 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r3s2 (broken, n/a): Extending the successful replay table into a learned next-PC predictor can exhaust `riscv-formal`'s wall-clock budget even without IF-side decode, because threading full `predicted_next_pc`/`predicted_valid` metadata and per-entry successor state through IF/ID/EX forces every instruction proof to reason about extra 32-bit control-flow state and EX mispredict comparisons.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.78%): On `bench`, parallel `mul_ss`/`mul_uu`/`mul_su` datapaths are wasted hot-path area for RV32M; collapsing `MUL*` onto one sign-selectable 33x33 product preserves single-cycle behavior while reducing ALU routing pressure enough to lift Tang Nano 20K fitness.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (regression, -59.23%): On `bench`, exact ID-stage BRANCH/JAL/JALR resolution is too timing-expensive; pulling compare/target-add plus EX/MEM/MEM/WB operand-bypass and load-interlock logic into decode collapsed Fmax to 52 MHz, so CoreMark's taken-control bubbles are cheaper than widening the front-end critical path.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (regression, +0.61%): On `bench`, replaying warmed EX `redirect_target` instructions from the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` table is too narrow a win to pay for extra redirect-hit lookup/arming logic, because it only helps when `io_imemReady` stalls on the first post-redirect cycle while the original `pc+4` replay already captures the hotter sequential locality.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r5s0 (regression, +9.14%): On `bench`, replacing the shared sign-selectable 33x33 `MUL*` datapath with a 32x32 product plus `MULH`/`MULHSU` sign-correction subtractors loses on Tang Nano 20K, because the extra 32-bit correction/mux logic on the ALU hot path costs more than carrying the multiplier sign bit through the DSP.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r5s1 (improvement, +12.75%): On `bench`, a one-entry posted store buffer that only posts main-memory stores and blocks only younger memory ops turns random `io_dmemReady` store backpressure into background drain time, recovering CoreMark IPC without paying the IF/ID timing cost of front-end control changes.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r5s2 (regression, +0.93%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table already has enough capacity for CoreMark's hot sequential PCs; adding 2-way set associativity with dual tag checks, way select, and MRU replacement costs more than the low-bit conflict misses it removes.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r6s0 (improvement, +3.63%): On `bench`, carving ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and load/store address generation onto a dedicated EX carry-chain path pays because CoreMark is dominated by simple add/address ops, so keeping them out of the ALU's wide logic/shift/RV32M mux and fanout improves timing without front-end control changes.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r6s1 (regression, -11.69%): On `bench`, extending the posted store buffer with MEM-stage aligned-address/byte-mask overlap checks, load forwarding, and load-vs-drain arbitration lost overall fitness because the extra compare/mask/mux logic sat on the hot memory path, dropping Fmax from 151.3 MHz to 133.4 MHz while CoreMark did not expose enough independent load-behind-store overlap to pay it back.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r6s2 (regression, +1.63%): On `bench`, the tiny IF `{pc,instr}` replay table should stay a single sequential `pc+4` candidate; adding a second JAL/backward-branch control-target slot only helps on rare first-post-redirect `io_imemReady` stalls, while the extra immediate-decode, target-add, and second replay-hit path in `if_stage` costs more than it saves.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r7s0 (regression, -19.89%): On `bench`, the profitable EX split stops at the add/address fast path; adding op-class gating like `use_alu_result`, zeroed ALU operands for BRANCH/JAL/JALR, and `is_m_op ? a/b : 0` around the shared RV32M cone puts extra decode/mux logic back on the hot EX path and drops Fmax far more than the cold-cone isolation helps.\\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r7s1 (placement_failed, n/a): On `bench`, the posted-store win appears depth-sensitive on Tang Nano 20K: growing the one-entry main-memory store buffer into a two-entry in-order FIFO with `store_buf_full` backpressure and queued-drain muxing pushed MEM/backpressure routing past place-and-route limits before any CoreMark gain could be measured.\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[]},\"title\":\"cores/bench/LESSONS.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778836137609,\"end\":1778836137624}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0b2a6c38c0b369ed016a06e2a97a8c819a8496bc2bf0f26dd7\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2ae56672001QgO7t45XIvRyDB\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d51ad624ffe3QWHb1QQRha61G\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2ae52a72001Q2N37N4JxxwUck\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:1054:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778836149315,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d51ad624ffe3QWHb1QQRha61G\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_0D6n6oMntkxt0OTkjzYjk687\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1|hyp-20260515-002-r5s1|structural|shadow|store buffer|replay|branch|jal|load|scoreboard|pipeline|stage\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"*.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 41 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 2: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Move DIV/REM off the fast ALU path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current EX-stage ALU carries fully combinational divide and remainder\\\\nhardware even though CoreMark spends very little time in DIV/REM. That rare\\\\nlogic still taxes timing closure and routing on the Tang Nano 20K, and the\\\\nbench core is already close to its LUT budget.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current EX-stage ALU carries fully combinational divide and remainder\\\\nhardware even though CoreMark spends very little time in DIV/REM. That rare\\\\nlogic still taxes timing closure and routing on the Tang Nano 20K, and the\\\\nbench core is already close to its LUT budget.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single-cycle DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU datapath with a small iterative\\\\ndivider/remainder micro-unit, while keeping the common integer ALU and MUL\\\\npath single-cycle. EX would launch the divider on DIV/REM ops, freeze the\\\\nfront of the pipeline until completion, and then retire normally with the\\\\nsame RV32M corner-case semantics. This should remove a rarely useful critical\\\\npath from normal cycles, improve Fmax and placement slack, and cost little\\\\nCoreMark/MHz because CoreMark is dominated by adds, loads, stores, branches,\\\\nand plain MUL rather than division. Using an 8-cycle worst-case divider keeps\\\\nthe added stall bounded enough to stay compatible with the existing formal\\\\ndepth budget.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/div_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a compact iterative signed/unsigned divider-remainder unit with RV32M divide-by-zero and overflow behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove the combinational DIV/REM datapath from the fast ALU path and keep common arithmetic, shifts, compares, and MUL results single-cycle.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU, launch the divider, hold EX state while it runs, and only write EX/MEM when a...\\n  Line 3: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Add a small redirect-aware fetch queue\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front-end is tightly coupled to `io_imemReady`: any cycle with\\\\ninstruction-memory backpressure immediately becomes a decode bubble because\\\\nIF emits a NOP and the PC stops. That is especially expensive here because\\\\nthe fitness run injects random iStalls by design, so front-end elasticity is\\\\nworth more than a tiny local logic cleanup.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front-end is tightly coupled to `io_imemReady`: any cycle with\\\\ninstruction-memory backpressure immediately becomes a decode bubble because\\\\nIF emits a NOP and the PC stops. That is especially expensive here because\\\\nthe fitness run injects random iStalls by design, so front-end elasticity is\\\\nworth more than a tiny local logic cleanup.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single-slot combinational fetch path with a small speculative\\\\nfetch queue in `if_stage`, likely 2-4 entries deep, that buffers `{pc,\\\\ninstr}` pairs when `io_imemReady` is high and feeds ID from the queue when\\\\nthe backend is ready. On redirects, flush younger queued instructions and\\\\nrestart fetch at the resolved target; on load-use or dmem stalls, preserve\\\\nthe buffered instructions instead of re-fetching them. This is a structural\\\\nfront-end change, not a predictor: it should raise CoreMark iterations/cycle\\\\nunder the orchestrator's random stall model with only modest area/timing\\\\ncost and without changing the single-retire RVFI contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Refactor IF into a small prefetch FIFO/skid-buffer front-end with redirect-aware invalidation and queue-based instruction delivery.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Change front-end stall logic to depend on fetch-queue availability plus backend hazards instead of bubbling immediately on every `!imemReady`.\\\"}, {\\\"...\\n  Line 4: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r1s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Static IF branch and JAL predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` still fetches strictly `pc+4` until EX resolves every branch\\\\nand jump, so each taken loop backedge and direct call burns wrong-path\\\\nbubbles. The bench core is already close to its LUT budget, so the best\\\\npredictor bet is a zero-table frontend change that improves CoreMark control\\\\nflow without adding BTB, RAS, or counter-RAM state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` still fetches strictly `pc+4` until EX resolves every branch\\\\nand jump, so each taken loop backedge and direct call burns wrong-path\\\\nbubbles. The bench core is already close to its LUT budget, so the best\\\\npredictor bet is a zero-table frontend change that improves CoreMark control\\\\nflow without adding BTB, RAS, or counter-RAM state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Port the lightweight static control predictor shape that already proved out\\\\nin `cores/v1`: predict aligned backward B-type branches taken and aligned\\\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, then carry `predicted_taken` and\\\\n`predicted_target` through the frontend so EX redirects only on mispredict.\\\\nLeave JALR resolved in EX. This should recover much of CoreMark's loop and\\\\ndirect-call control loss while staying much cheaper than learned branch\\\\ntables or return predictors, which are more likely to hurt Fmax on this\\\\nLUT-tight bench core.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX bundles with predicted_taken and predicted_target metadata so EX can validate the frontend prediction against the resolved next PC.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a decode-light static predictor that recognizes legal backward B-type branches and direct JALs, computes aligned predicted targets, and selects the predicted next PC only when fetch is not being stalled or flushed.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description...\\n  Line 5: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Precise cold DIV/REM interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench ALU keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same combinational\\\\nEX-stage result path as ADD, address generation, branch resolution, and MUL.\\\\nThat divider logic is cold in CoreMark's timed region but still taxes Fmax on\\\\nthe Tang Nano 20K. The prior broken attempt showed the performance idea is\\\\nstill attractive, but it must be implemented as a precise long-op interlock\\\\nthat preserves the full EX instruction context until completion.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench ALU keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same combinational\\\\nEX-stage result path as ADD, address generation, branch resolution, and MUL.\\\\nThat divider logic is cold in CoreMark's timed region but still taxes Fmax on\\\\nthe Tang Nano 20K. The prior broken attempt showed the performance idea is\\\\nstill attractive, but it must be implemented as a precise long-op interlock\\\\nthat preserves the full EX instruction context until completion.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move only DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU into a small iterative `div_unit` and leave the\\\\ncommon RV32I operations plus MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU on the existing one-cycle\\\\npath. When a divide-class instruction reaches EX, snapshot the full decoded\\\\ninstruction context and the forwarded operands into shadow registers, assert\\\\nan `ex_long_busy` interlock, bubble EX/MEM while the divider runs, and emit\\\\nexactly one EX/MEM result when the unit completes. This removes the cold\\\\ndivider from the universal ALU path, should improve Fmax with negligible\\\\nCoreMark IPC loss, and directly addresses the earlier failure mode of stale\\\\nEX/MEM retirement state leaking through during a multi-cycle operation.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 10, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/div_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add an iterative divider/remainder unit with exact RV32M divide-by-zero, overflow, and ALTOPS behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/a...\\n  Line 6: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Operand-Stage-Aware Load Interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current hazard unit stalls on any raw `rs1`/`rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not actually read that field yet\\\\nor only needs it later as store data in MEM. That conservative policy burns\\\\navoidable one-cycle bubbles in CoreMark's dense load/address/store code, and\\\\nthe recent `bench` regressions already showed that extra front-end machinery\\\\nis a worse trade here than tightening the backend interlock.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current hazard unit stalls on any raw `rs1`/`rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not actually read that field yet\\\\nor only needs it later as store data in MEM. That conservative policy burns\\\\navoidable one-cycle bubbles in CoreMark's dense load/address/store code, and\\\\nthe recent `bench` regressions already showed that extra front-end machinery\\\\nis a worse trade here than tightening the backend interlock.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework the load-use interlock around the stage where an operand is actually\\\\nconsumed. Add decode metadata for real `rs1`/`rs2` usage, treat STORE `rs2`\\\\nas a late MEM-stage consumer instead of an EX-stage consumer, and add a\\\\nWB-to-MEM store-data bypass so `load -> store` pairs with an independent\\\\naddress can proceed without the textbook bubble. This should recover\\\\nCoreMark IPC from both false-positive load stalls and true-but-late\\\\nstore-data dependencies, while keeping load data off the EX ALU/branch path\\\\nand avoiding another fetch-path timing penalty.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add decoded source-usage and late-store-data control bits to the pipeline bundles so hazards can distinguish EX-stage operands from MEM-stage-only store data.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/decoder.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Classify which legal ins...\\n  Line 7: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r2s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Registered I-Fetch Replay Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench frontend turns every `!io_imemReady` cycle into an\\\\nimmediate decode bubble, and the last structural fetch-queue attempt showed\\\\nthat simply buffering sequential fetches is not enough to get far enough\\\\nahead under the tournament's random ~22% iStall model. CoreMark repeatedly\\\\nrevisits a compact hot set of loop and helper PCs, so reusing already-fetched\\\\ninstructions is a stronger predictor bet than adding more branch decode or\\\\ntarget-add logic to the IF critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench frontend turns every `!io_imemReady` cycle into an\\\\nimmediate decode bubble, and the last structural fetch-queue attempt showed\\\\nthat simply buffering sequential fetches is not enough to get far enough\\\\nahead under the tournament's random ~22% iStall model. CoreMark repeatedly\\\\nrevisits a compact hot set of loop and helper PCs, so reusing already-fetched\\\\ninstructions is a stronger predictor bet than adding more branch decode or\\\\ntarget-add logic to the IF critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a small registered lookahead instruction replay predictor in `if_stage`.\\\\nOn each architecturally live fetch, fill a direct-mapped `{pc tag, instr}`\\\\ntable and, in parallel, look up the next fetch PC and register a one-cycle-\\\\nahead replay candidate. When `io_imemReady` later drops and the current PC\\\\nmatches a valid candidate, feed the cached instruction to decode and report\\\\nfetch-ready instead of injecting a bubble. Keep the normal ready-cycle path\\\\nlive-imem-first, invalidate replay candidates on redirect/flush, and avoid\\\\nfilling the table with killed wrong-path fetches. This should recover a\\\\nmeaningful fraction of random iStall loss while avoiding the immediate-decode\\\\nand predicted-target bookkeeping that made the earlier static branch/JAL\\\\npredictor regress on `bench`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6...\\n  Line 8: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r3s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"MEM-to-EX load bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench core still pays a mandatory one-cycle bubble after every\\\\nload-to-use dependency even though the memory interface is zero-wait when\\\\n`io_dmemReady=1` and the loaded word is already formed in MEM that same\\\\ncycle. CoreMark is rich in short load-consumer chains, so recovering those\\\\nbubbles should improve iterations/cycle without adding another speculative\\\\nfrontend structure or touching the cold DIV/REM path again.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench core still pays a mandatory one-cycle bubble after every\\\\nload-to-use dependency even though the memory interface is zero-wait when\\\\n`io_dmemReady=1` and the loaded word is already formed in MEM that same\\\\ncycle. CoreMark is rich in short load-consumer chains, so recovering those\\\\nbubbles should improve iterations/cycle without adding another speculative\\\\nfrontend structure or touching the cold DIV/REM path again.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a direct MEM-to-EX forwarding path for load results and relax the\\\\nhazard unit so a consumer can advance behind a load when the value will be\\\\navailable from MEM in the next cycle. This keeps the existing 5-stage\\\\npipeline and RVFI retirement boundary, but replaces the textbook load-use\\\\ninterlock with a late bypass that can feed ALU ops, branches, JALR, and\\\\nstore data from the current MEM-stage load result instead of forcing a\\\\nbubble. Compared with the earlier broken stage-aware store interlock, this\\\\nis more local: the forwarded operand still flows through EX and is captured\\\\ninto the normal pipeline state, so RVFI operand snapshots stay aligned with\\\\nthe real consumer datapath.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 7, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire a new MEM-stage load-result forwarding signal from `mem_stage` into EX/forwarding control and update the top-level hazard connections for the n...\\n  Line 9: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Exact load-use interlock with late store-data bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The fetch side already has a winning replay mechanism, so the strongest\\\\nremaining structural gain is more likely in the backend. `hazard_unit.sv`\\\\nstill stalls on any raw `if_id_rs1` or `if_id_rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not consume that field in EX or\\\\nonly needs store data later in MEM. CoreMark's dense mix of loads,\\\\nimmediate arithmetic, address generation, and stores makes those\\\\nconservative one-cycle bubbles expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The fetch side already has a winning replay mechanism, so the strongest\\\\nremaining structural gain is more likely in the backend. `hazard_unit.sv`\\\\nstill stalls on any raw `if_id_rs1` or `if_id_rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not consume that field in EX or\\\\nonly needs store data later in MEM. CoreMark's dense mix of loads,\\\\nimmediate arithmetic, address generation, and stores makes those\\\\nconservative one-cycle bubbles expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework the load-use interlock around true operand-consumption timing instead\\\\nof raw register-field matches. Decode exact legal `rs1` and `rs2` usage for\\\\neach instruction class, keep the current conservative stall policy for\\\\nillegal or otherwise unclassified encodings, and treat STORE `rs2` as a\\\\nMEM-stage consumer with a dedicated WB-to-MEM store-data bypass. To avoid\\\\nthe prior broken attempt's RVFI bug, carry the final architecturally\\\\nconsumed store-data value into retirement so `rvfi_rs2_rdata`,\\\\n`rvfi_mem_wdata`, and the actual dmem write all agree. This should remove\\\\nfalse bubbles from OP-IMM/LOAD/JALR/LUI/AUIPC/FENCE paths and eliminate the\\\\nmandatory `load -> store(rs2)` stall without putting load data on the EX\\\\nALU, branch-compare, or JALR critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"...\\n  Line 10: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r3s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Replay-Trained Next-PC Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` result already proved that a tiny replay predictor can\\\\nclaw back a meaningful fraction of the tournament's random iStall loss without\\\\nputting decode or target-add logic on the IF critical path. What it still does\\\\nnot address is the compulsory redirect/flush cost on hot taken loop backedges\\\\nand direct calls, which CoreMark executes repeatedly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` result already proved that a tiny replay predictor can\\\\nclaw back a meaningful fraction of the tournament's random iStall loss without\\\\nputting decode or target-add logic on the IF critical path. What it still does\\\\nnot address is the compulsory redirect/flush cost on hot taken loop backedges\\\\nand direct calls, which CoreMark executes repeatedly.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing replay-table frontend into a learned next-PC predictor that\\\\nis trained by EX with the actual resolved successor of taken BRANCH and direct\\\\nJAL instructions. Each table entry would keep the fetched instruction plus a\\\\nsmall predicted-successor field, letting IF steer to a previously seen target\\\\non a PC hit and carry that prediction through the pipe so EX only redirects on\\\\nmispredict. This keeps the successful replay-table structure, avoids immediate\\\\ndecode and target-add work in IF, and should recover stable loop/call bubbles\\\\nthat the current sequential-only replay predictor still pays every time.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add predicted-control metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles so EX can validate a learned next-PC prediction against the resolved successor.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Upgrade the replay table into a tiny direct-mapped next-PC predictor that stores hot control-flow successors and can steer fetch to a ...\\n  Line 11: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r4s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Shared signedness-selectable multiplier\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core has already recovered the largest obvious IPC\\\\nlosses from random iStalls and load-use bubbles, so the next micro-opt is\\\\nmore likely to come from reducing hot-path ALU area and routing pressure.\\\\nToday `rtl/alu.sv` builds three separate 64-bit multiply datapaths\\\\n(`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`, `mul_su`) just to cover the four `MUL*` variants.\\\\nThat is expensive on a LUT-tight Tang Nano 20K and does not buy extra IPC,\\\\nbecause only one multiply flavor is needed per instruction.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core has already recovered the largest obvious IPC\\\\nlosses from random iStalls and load-use bubbles, so the next micro-opt is\\\\nmore likely to come from reducing hot-path ALU area and routing pressure.\\\\nToday `rtl/alu.sv` builds three separate 64-bit multiply datapaths\\\\n(`mul_ss`, `mul_uu`, `mul_su`) just to cover the four `MUL*` variants.\\\\nThat is expensive on a LUT-tight Tang Nano 20K and does not buy extra IPC,\\\\nbecause only one multiply flavor is needed per instruction.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the three parallel multiply datapaths with one shared\\\\nsignedness-selectable multiplier that derives all `MUL`, `MULH`, `MULHU`,\\\\nand `MULHSU` results from a single op-conditioned operand-extension path.\\\\nA 33x33-style sign-extended product is enough to cover the high-half signed\\\\ncombinations while still yielding the correct low 32 bits for plain `MUL`.\\\\nThis keeps multiply single-cycle, avoids the control and formal risk of a\\\\nmulticycle M-unit, preserves the existing DIV/REM behavior, and should cut\\\\nLUT/DSP glue plus route congestion enough to lift Fmax without giving back\\\\nthe IPC gains from the recent replay and load-bypass wins.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Collapse `mul_ss`/`mul_uu`/`mul_su` into ...\\n  Line 12: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"ID-stage exact control resolution\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best low-risk fetch and\\\\nload-use gains: it replays hot sequential instructions in IF and forwards\\\\nzero-wait loads from MEM to EX. The remaining visible IPC loss is that every\\\\ntaken BRANCH, JAL, and JALR still resolves in EX after the ID/EX register,\\\\nwhich forces extra wrong-path bubbles on CoreMark's hot loop backedges and\\\\nhelper calls.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best low-risk fetch and\\\\nload-use gains: it replays hot sequential instructions in IF and forwards\\\\nzero-wait loads from MEM to EX. The remaining visible IPC loss is that every\\\\ntaken BRANCH, JAL, and JALR still resolves in EX after the ID/EX register,\\\\nwhich forces extra wrong-path bubbles on CoreMark's hot loop backedges and\\\\nhelper calls.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move exact control-flow resolution one stage earlier by adding a dedicated\\\\nID-stage branch/jump unit. JAL can redirect immediately from `pc+imm`; BRANCH\\\\nand JALR should compare/add in ID using the regfile value plus targeted\\\\nbypasses from EX/MEM and MEM/WB, while conservatively stalling only when an\\\\noperand is still unavailable from an in-flight load. Carry the final\\\\nforwarded source values into the pipeline state so RVFI operand snapshots stay\\\\naligned with the values the control instruction actually consumed, and mark\\\\nID-resolved control ops so EX does not issue a duplicate redirect a cycle\\\\nlater. This keeps the existing single-retire contract and replay frontend,\\\\nbut should cut the taken-control penalty by roughly one cycle without adding\\\\nIF-side predictor state or target-table complexity.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the pipeline bundles with control-resolution metadata so ID-resolved BRANCH/JAL/JALR instru...\\n  Line 13: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Redirect-aware replay target predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` frontend already proved that a tiny registered replay\\\\npredictor can recover a meaningful slice of random `io_imemReady` stalls\\\\nwithout hurting the IF critical path. Its remaining blind spot is taken\\\\ncontrol flow: every EX redirect clears the replay candidate, so hot loop\\\\nbackedges and direct-call targets lose replay coverage exactly where\\\\nCoreMark revisits the same PCs most often.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` frontend already proved that a tiny registered replay\\\\npredictor can recover a meaningful slice of random `io_imemReady` stalls\\\\nwithout hurting the IF critical path. Its remaining blind spot is taken\\\\ncontrol flow: every EX redirect clears the replay candidate, so hot loop\\\\nbackedges and direct-call targets lose replay coverage exactly where\\\\nCoreMark revisits the same PCs most often.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing replay-table predictor inside `if_stage` so redirects\\\\npre-arm replay for the resolved target instead of unconditionally clearing\\\\nthe candidate. On a redirect, look up `redirect_target` in the existing\\\\n`{pc,instr}` table and bank that target instruction for the next cycle when\\\\nit is already cached; keep all prediction state local to IF and continue to\\\\nemit a bubble on the redirect cycle itself. This should preserve the timing\\\\nadvantage of the current replay design, improve post-redirect fetch\\\\navailability on hot loops and helper calls, and avoid the formal-state and\\\\nbookkeeping costs that broke the learned next-PC predictor.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Teach the replay predictor to bank a redirect-target replay candidate from the existing table, instead of always invalidating replay state on redirect, while keeping all predictor state local to IF.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/...\\n  Line 14: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r5s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"DSP-friendly corrected shared multiplier\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` RTL already recovered the bigger IPC wins, so the\\\\nnext micro-opt is most likely another Fmax-and-LUT cleanup in the hot ALU\\\\ndatapath. `rtl/alu.sv` now shares `MUL*` through one 33x33 signed product,\\\\nwhich was a good first step, but it still widens both operands by a sign bit\\\\nand routes a 66-bit product even for plain `MUL` and `MULHU`. On the Tang\\\\nNano 20K, trimming that extra width and glue logic is attractive because the\\\\ncore is still above its LUT target and the previous multiplier cleanup already\\\\nproved this region is fitness-sensitive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` RTL already recovered the bigger IPC wins, so the\\\\nnext micro-opt is most likely another Fmax-and-LUT cleanup in the hot ALU\\\\ndatapath. `rtl/alu.sv` now shares `MUL*` through one 33x33 signed product,\\\\nwhich was a good first step, but it still widens both operands by a sign bit\\\\nand routes a 66-bit product even for plain `MUL` and `MULHU`. On the Tang\\\\nNano 20K, trimming that extra width and glue logic is attractive because the\\\\ncore is still above its LUT target and the previous multiplier cleanup already\\\\nproved this region is fitness-sensitive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the current sign-extended 33x33 shared multiply with a DSP-friendlier\\\\n32x32 unsigned product plus small signed-high correction terms for `MULH` and\\\\n`MULHSU`. `MUL` can still take the low 32 bits directly, `MULHU` can use the\\\\nraw high half, and the signed variants can derive their high halves by\\\\nsubtracting the operand-dependent correction terms instead of carrying an\\\\nextra sign bit through the full multiplier. This keeps all `MUL*` operations\\\\nsingle-cycle, avoids the control risk of a multicycle M-unit, preserves the\\\\nexisting DIV/REM and ALTOPS behavior, and should further reduce multiplier\\\\nrouting pressure and LUT glue without giving back ...\\n  Line 15: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-entry posted store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry posted-store buffer that captures a store's aligned address,\\\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible metadata when the store\\\\nreaches MEM. If the external dmem port stalls, retire the store from the buffer\\\\nimmediately, keep draining the buffered write in the background, and allow\\\\nyounger non-memory instructions to keep flowing. Only stall when a younger\\\\nload or store reaches MEM while the buffer is still occupied, preserving\\\\nin-order memory semantics without reintroducing load-use or control-path\\\\ncomplexity. This should recover CoreMark IPC from random dmem backpressure on\\\\nstore-heavy regions while avoiding the IF/ID critical-path regressions seen in\\\\nearlier structural experiments.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the MEM-stage/retirement bundles with store-buffer bookkeepin...\\n  Line 16: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r5s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Conflict-tolerant replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` frontend already gets real value from the tiny\\\\nsequential replay predictor, which means the safest remaining predictor win\\\\nis probably higher replay hit rate rather than deeper control speculation.\\\\nToday the table is only 8 entries and direct-mapped, so hot CoreMark PCs\\\\nthat alias on the same low index bits can evict each other and turn later\\\\nrandom `io_imemReady=0` cycles back into bubbles.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` frontend already gets real value from the tiny\\\\nsequential replay predictor, which means the safest remaining predictor win\\\\nis probably higher replay hit rate rather than deeper control speculation.\\\\nToday the table is only 8 entries and direct-mapped, so hot CoreMark PCs\\\\nthat alias on the same low index bits can evict each other and turn later\\\\nrandom `io_imemReady=0` cycles back into bubbles.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the direct-mapped 8-entry `{pc,instr}` replay table in `if_stage`\\\\nwith a small conflict-tolerant structure, preferably 2-way set-associative\\\\nwith a simple per-set MRU or round-robin replacement bit, while keeping the\\\\nexisting one-cycle-ahead replay candidate scheme and all predictor state\\\\nlocal to IF. The key point is that `fetch_available` still depends only on\\\\n`imem_ready || replay_hit`; the wider table lookup is only used to bank the\\\\nnext cycle's candidate, so this should improve replay coverage under random\\\\niStalls without reintroducing the IF decode, EX-visible prediction metadata,\\\\nor formal-state growth that hurt earlier predictor attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the direct-mapped replay table with a small conflict-tolerant replay predictor, keeping the live-fetch-first behavior and the current redirect/flush safety rules.\\\"}, {\\\"fi...\\n  Line 17: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r6s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"EX fast-path add/address bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest safe `bench` gains have come from local backend changes that\\\\nrecovered IPC or reduced hot-path routing without widening the front-end\\\\ncontrol logic. After the replay cache, MEM-to-EX load bypass, and posted\\\\nstore buffer, the next likely win is Fmax: the common EX add/address path\\\\nstill goes through the same mixed-function ALU block that also carries\\\\nshifts, compares, the shared multiplier, and combinational DIV/REM logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The largest safe `bench` gains have come from local backend changes that\\\\nrecovered IPC or reduced hot-path routing without widening the front-end\\\\ncontrol logic. After the replay cache, MEM-to-EX load bypass, and posted\\\\nstore buffer, the next likely win is Fmax: the common EX add/address path\\\\nstill goes through the same mixed-function ALU block that also carries\\\\nshifts, compares, the shared multiplier, and combinational DIV/REM logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split execute into a dedicated carry-chain fast path for the common\\\\nADD-class work and a side path for rarer logic/shift/M-extension work.\\\\n`ex_stage.sv` would detect plain ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and load/store address\\\\ngeneration and take a direct 32-bit adder result, while `alu.sv` is\\\\nrefactored so those hot operations no longer share the same wide output mux\\\\nand multiplier/divider fanout as the rare RV32M cases. This preserves the\\\\ncurrent single-cycle semantics and RVFI contract, avoids the control-heavy\\\\nmulticycle DIV/REM regressions, and should improve Tang Nano 20K timing on\\\\nthe instructions CoreMark executes most often.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a direct fast-path adder select for ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and memory address generation so common EX results bypass the mixed-function ALU result path.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"des...\\n  Line 18: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Alias-aware load bypass around posted stores\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing posted store buffer, but make it address-aware instead of\\\\nblocking all younger memory ops. When a younger load reaches MEM while a\\\\nmain-memory store is buffered, compare the load's aligned word address and\\\\nbyte mask against the buffered store: if there is no overlap, let the load use\\\\nthe dmem port immediately and drain the buffered store later; if the load is\\\\nfully covered by the buffered bytes, forward from the buffer; only stall on\\\\ntrue alias or partial-overlap cases, and keep younger stores conservative.\\\\nThis is a backend-only structural change that compounds with the winning\\\\nposted-store buffer while avoiding the IF/ID timing regressions seen in prior\\\\nstructural attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add store-buffer alias checking, demand-load-vs-drain arbitration, and buffered-store forwarding for fully covered younger loads.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the blank...\\n  Line 19: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r6s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Dual-candidate control-aware replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The winning predictor on `bench` is the tiny IF replay table, not earlier\\\\nbranch resolution or heavier control speculation. Recent failures showed that\\\\nadding decode-to-PC steering, prediction metadata, or wider replay RAM hurts\\\\neither Fmax or formal cost, yet the current single replay candidate still\\\\ngoes blind across hot taken backedges and direct-call targets because it only\\\\ntracks the sequential `pc+4` path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The winning predictor on `bench` is the tiny IF replay table, not earlier\\\\nbranch resolution or heavier control speculation. Recent failures showed that\\\\nadding decode-to-PC steering, prediction metadata, or wider replay RAM hurts\\\\neither Fmax or formal cost, yet the current single replay candidate still\\\\ngoes blind across hot taken backedges and direct-call targets because it only\\\\ntracks the sequential `pc+4` path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing replay-table frontend and EX redirect contract, but add a\\\\nsecond replay candidate slot dedicated to likely control successors. When a\\\\nlive fetch sees a direct JAL or an obvious backward B-type loop branch,\\\\n`if_stage` does a decode-light target calculation off the fetched instruction,\\\\nlooks that target up in the existing `{pc,instr}` replay table, and banks it\\\\nas a control-target candidate while the normal sequential `pc+4` candidate\\\\nstays unchanged. Fetch still walks `pc+4` until EX redirects, so there is no\\\\nnew predicted-PC state in IF/ID/EX; the win comes from having the first\\\\npost-redirect target instruction already replayable if `io_imemReady` drops.\\\\nThis aims at the same hot loop/call locality as prior control predictors, but\\\\nstays off the live fetch critical path and avoids the metadata/formal blow-up\\\\nthat hurt the earlier static and learned next-PC attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"mediu...\\n  Line 20: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r7s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"EX operand-isolated M/control side paths\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent winning `bench` changes came from trimming hot backend cones, not from\\\\nadding more speculative state. `ex_stage.sv` still routes BRANCH/JAL/JALR through\\\\nthe generic ALU plumbing even though branches only need compare/target logic and\\\\njumps retire `pc+4`, while `alu.sv` always builds the multiplier/divider operand\\\\ncone around live `a`/`b`. On Tang Nano 20K, that extra fanout and muxing is a\\\\nplausible remaining Fmax tax after the add/address fast path already landed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent winning `bench` changes came from trimming hot backend cones, not from\\\\nadding more speculative state. `ex_stage.sv` still routes BRANCH/JAL/JALR through\\\\nthe generic ALU plumbing even though branches only need compare/target logic and\\\\njumps retire `pc+4`, while `alu.sv` always builds the multiplier/divider operand\\\\ncone around live `a`/`b`. On Tang Nano 20K, that extra fanout and muxing is a\\\\nplausible remaining Fmax tax after the add/address fast path already landed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the current single-cycle semantics, but split execute into operation-class\\\\nside paths so only the relevant cone sees hot operands. In `ex_stage.sv`, treat\\\\nBRANCH/JAL/JALR as control-only ops that bypass the generic ALU result path, and\\\\nin `alu.sv` split simple logic/shift/compare work from a dedicated M-extension\\\\nsubpath so MUL/DIV/REM operand extension and result selection are only active for\\\\n`ALU_M*` ops. This is a narrower follow-on to the successful fast-add bypass: it\\\\nremoves more unnecessary mux/fanout from the common EX datapath without\\\\nreintroducing the multicycle divider control risk that already failed on `bench`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Classify control-only operations and bypass the generic ALU for BRANCH/JAL/JA...\\n  Line 21: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r7s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Two-entry posted store FIFO\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single posted-store slot with a tiny two-entry in-order store\\\\nFIFO that can absorb a short burst of normal-RAM stores while older buffered\\\\nwrites drain in the background. Keep the current conservative memory-order\\\\nrule that younger loads still wait until the FIFO is empty, and avoid the\\\\nlosing alias-compare/forwarding path entirely; the gain should come from\\\\nletting consecutive stores and following non-memory instructions retire\\\\nacross multi-cycle `io_dmemReady` stalls without touching IF timing or the\\\\nEX critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/store_queue.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a tiny two-entry in-order posted-store FIFO with enqueue/dequeue logic and head/tail status for MEM arbitration.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single buffered-store slot with the FIFO so a postable store can enqueue behind an older buffered store while preserving the existing conservative load ordering and RVFI store reporting.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"de...\\n  Line 22: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r7s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Victim-slot replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest predictor result on `bench` is still the tiny sequential replay\\\\ntable in `if_stage`, while every attempt that steered PC early or added wider\\\\ncontrol bookkeeping either regressed badly or only recovered a very small\\\\nbenefit. The remaining plausible predictor win is therefore not deeper branch\\\\nspeculation, but a cheaper way to recover replay-table conflict misses than\\\\nthe prior 2-way and control-target variants.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest predictor result on `bench` is still the tiny sequential replay\\\\ntable in `if_stage`, while every attempt that steered PC early or added wider\\\\ncontrol bookkeeping either regressed badly or only recovered a very small\\\\nbenefit. The remaining plausible predictor win is therefore not deeper branch\\\\nspeculation, but a cheaper way to recover replay-table conflict misses than\\\\nthe prior 2-way and control-target variants.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing 8-entry direct-mapped `{pc,instr}` replay table and the\\\\ncurrent one-cycle-ahead candidate scheme, but add a single fully-associative\\\\nvictim entry that captures only the most recently evicted replay line. On a\\\\nlive fetch that would overwrite a different valid PC in the direct-mapped\\\\nslot, spill that old `{pc,instr}` pair into the victim entry; when banking the\\\\nnext `pc+4` replay candidate, check the indexed slot first and then the victim\\\\nentry before giving up. This directly targets the low-bit alias pattern that\\\\nmotivated the earlier set-associative attempt, but avoids dual-way lookup,\\\\nMRU state, EX-visible prediction metadata, target-add logic, or any change to\\\\n`fetch_available = imem_ready || replay_hit`. If CoreMark's hot sequential PCs\\\\nmostly bounce between a few colliding indices, a one-entry victim should claw\\\\nback some replay hits at materially lower timing cost than widening the main\\\\ntable.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {...\\n  Line 23: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r8s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Aligned LW/SW MEM fast path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the biggest front-end and\\\\nEX-stage wins, so the next plausible limiter is the MEM-stage cone. Today\\\\n`mem_stage.sv` handles byte/halfword extraction, sign extension, byte-mask\\\\ngeneration, store-data replication, posted-store capture, and same-cycle\\\\nMEM-to-EX load bypass in one place even though CoreMark spends most of its\\\\ntimed region on aligned word loads and stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the biggest front-end and\\\\nEX-stage wins, so the next plausible limiter is the MEM-stage cone. Today\\\\n`mem_stage.sv` handles byte/halfword extraction, sign extension, byte-mask\\\\ngeneration, store-data replication, posted-store capture, and same-cycle\\\\nMEM-to-EX load bypass in one place even though CoreMark spends most of its\\\\ntimed region on aligned word loads and stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split common aligned `LW`/`SW` traffic onto a dedicated memory fast path and\\\\nleave byte/halfword formatting on a rarer side path. Precompute store-lane\\\\nmask and replicated store data as soon as the effective address and\\\\nforwarded store operand are known, then let aligned word loads forward raw\\\\n`dmem_rdata` directly to EX without passing through the generic\\\\nshift-and-sign-extend mux. Byte/halfword cases, misalignment traps, posted\\\\nstores, and RVFI masks still stay exact, but the hot CoreMark path loses\\\\nunnecessary lane-steering logic on both the dmem output cone and the\\\\nMEM-to-EX load-bypass cone. Because this removes logic from the common\\\\nmemory path instead of adding alias checks or deeper queues, it is a better\\\\nfit for Tang Nano 20K than the prior memory-side regressions.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the EX/MEM bundle with preformatted store-byte-lane metadata a...\\n  Line 24: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve direct JAL in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow early-redirect path for legal `JAL` instructions in ID while\\\\nleaving BRANCH and JALR resolution in EX. `JAL` needs only `pc + imm`, so it\\\\ncan redirect one stage earlier without the operand-bypass, compare, and\\\\nload-interlock machinery that made full ID-stage control resolution collapse\\\\nFmax. Carry an `early_redirected`/resolved-next-PC bit with the instruction so\\\\nEX suppresses the duplicate redirect but still produces the normal `rd = pc+4`\\\\nwriteback and RVFI retirement record. This should shave a bubble from hot\\\\ndirect calls and unconditional jumps in CoreMark while keeping the replay\\\\nfrontend, posted store buffer, and hot EX datapath essentially unchanged.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the decode/pipeline control bundles with a bit that marks a JAL as already redirected before EX, plus any resolved next-PC metadata needed to suppress duplicate redirects later.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JALs in decode, compute `pc + imm`, and...\\n  Line 25: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r8s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Redirect landing-pad replay predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The existing `bench` predictor win came from the tiny sequential replay path,\\\\nwhile heavier control speculation, wider replay tables, and IF-side target\\\\ndecode all regressed. The remaining frontend blind spot is the first fetch\\\\nafter a taken redirect: hot loop headers and helper-call targets are revisited\\\\noften, but the current replay candidate is cleared exactly when those PCs\\\\nbecome useful again.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The existing `bench` predictor win came from the tiny sequential replay path,\\\\nwhile heavier control speculation, wider replay tables, and IF-side target\\\\ndecode all regressed. The remaining frontend blind spot is the first fetch\\\\nafter a taken redirect: hot loop headers and helper-call targets are revisited\\\\noften, but the current replay candidate is cleared exactly when those PCs\\\\nbecome useful again.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny redirect-target landing-pad cache inside `if_stage`, trained only\\\\nfrom successful post-redirect fetches. When EX redirects, remember the target\\\\nPC for one cycle; if the first fetch at that target later sees `io_imemReady=0`\\\\nand the landing-pad cache has a matching `{pc, instr}` entry, serve that\\\\ncached instruction instead of bubbling. Keep the existing sequential replay\\\\ntable unchanged for the common `pc+4` case so the new logic only touches the\\\\nnarrow post-redirect path, and let the normal replay candidate take over again\\\\nafter the landing-pad instruction issues. Compared with the prior redirect-\\\\ntarget replay attempt, this avoids an always-on lookup into the general replay\\\\ntable and removes dependence on that table still holding the target line,\\\\nwhich should make the control-side replay win cheaper in both timing and\\\\nrouting.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a tiny redirect-tar...\\n  Line 26: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r9s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Retimed MEM/WB writeback path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core has already harvested the largest IPC wins,\\\\nso the next realistic gain is another Fmax cleanup on a path exercised by\\\\nalmost every dependent instruction. Today the final `rd` value is still\\\\nselected late in `wb_stage` and then fanned out into MEM/WB forwarding,\\\\nregfile writeback, same-cycle decode read bypass, and RVFI `rd_wdata`.\\\\nThat keeps an avoidable mux and extra routing on a very hot backend path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core has already harvested the largest IPC wins,\\\\nso the next realistic gain is another Fmax cleanup on a path exercised by\\\\nalmost every dependent instruction. Today the final `rd` value is still\\\\nselected late in `wb_stage` and then fanned out into MEM/WB forwarding,\\\\nregfile writeback, same-cycle decode read bypass, and RVFI `rd_wdata`.\\\\nThat keeps an avoidable mux and extra routing on a very hot backend path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Register the final writeback word at the MEM/WB boundary instead of\\\\nrecomputing it after the MEM/WB flop. `mem_stage.sv` can already see both\\\\n`alu_result` and the formed load data, so it can write a single `wb_data`\\\\nfield into `mem_wb_t`; downstream logic would then forward, write the\\\\nregfile, and drive RVFI from that registered value directly. Pair that with\\\\na simpler regfile/read-bypass arrangement so the MEM/WB-to-ID/EX dependency\\\\npath no longer pays the `mem_to_reg` select plus duplicated write-first\\\\nmuxing on every read port. This stays fully in-order, preserves the current\\\\nsingle-cycle load-use behavior and RVFI contract, and is a more local timing\\\\noptimization than the control-side and memory-side experiments that already\\\\nregressed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a registered final writeback-data field to `mem_wb_t` so...\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve canonical RET in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing ID redirect path to cover canonical aligned returns,\\\\ne.g. `jalr x0, x1, 0` and `jalr x0, x5, 0`, when the return-address\\\\nregister is already architecturally available from the regfile/WB path. If\\\\nan older in-flight instruction still owns that source register, simply fall\\\\nback to the current EX-stage JALR path instead of adding the full ID-stage\\\\nbypass/interlock machinery that regressed earlier. This reuses the existing\\\\n`id_redirect` plumbing, carries an \\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through\\\\nthe pipeline so EX suppresses the duplicate redirect, and should cut\\\\nanother hot function-return bubble in CoreMark with much lower timing risk\\\\nthan general early JALR/BRANCH resolution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the existing `jal_early_redirect` pipeline bit into an ID-resolved jump marker that can cover both direct `JAL` and narrow early-return `JALR` cases.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"descr...\\n  Line 28: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Canonical return-address stack predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` already found its best predictor win in the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay path, and the recent structural JAL improvement removed the cheapest\\\\nunconditional jump bubble. The remaining hot control penalty is canonical\\\\nfunction return traffic: `ret`-shaped `JALR` instructions still wait for EX,\\\\nwhile broader IF-side branch prediction, wider replay tables, and redirect\\\\nlanding-pad schemes all failed to repay their timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`cores/bench` already found its best predictor win in the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay path, and the recent structural JAL improvement removed the cheapest\\\\nunconditional jump bubble. The remaining hot control penalty is canonical\\\\nfunction return traffic: `ret`-shaped `JALR` instructions still wait for EX,\\\\nwhile broader IF-side branch prediction, wider replay tables, and redirect\\\\nlanding-pad schemes all failed to repay their timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a very small return-address stack that predicts only the common ABI call\\\\nand return pattern instead of general branch targets. Push `pc+4` for direct\\\\ncalls that write `x1`, use the top entry to early-redirect only canonical\\\\n`ret`/`jalr x0, 0(x1)` in ID, and carry the predicted target forward so EX\\\\ncan suppress the duplicate redirect on a hit or repair the path on a rare\\\\nmismatch. This stays much narrower than the failed branch/JAL predictors: it\\\\ndoes not widen the replay table, does not add BTB-style target RAM, and only\\\\nadds prediction metadata to the small subset of return instructions. CoreMark\\\\nis call/return heavy enough that shaving the return bubble should improve IPC\\\\nwhile keeping Fmax risk materially lower than earlier full-control schemes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the ID/EX bundle with narrow early...\\n  Line 29: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r10s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-gated operand bypass cleanup\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent writeback-retime regression showed that the remaining backend\\\\nlimiter is not just where `wb_data` is selected, but the always-on\\\\nWB-to-ID read-bypass compares and muxes that sit on both regfile read\\\\nports every cycle. On this core, many hot CoreMark instructions\\\\n(`OP-IMM`, `LOAD`, `JALR`, `AUIPC`, `LUI`, `JAL`) do not consume `rs2`,\\\\nand some consume neither source, yet the current datapath still drives raw\\\\ninstruction bitfields through regfile read, bypass, and forwarding logic.\\\\nTrimming that unnecessary operand work is a plausible remaining Fmax win\\\\nthat stays off the fragile IF/control paths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent writeback-retime regression showed that the remaining backend\\\\nlimiter is not just where `wb_data` is selected, but the always-on\\\\nWB-to-ID read-bypass compares and muxes that sit on both regfile read\\\\nports every cycle. On this core, many hot CoreMark instructions\\\\n(`OP-IMM`, `LOAD`, `JALR`, `AUIPC`, `LUI`, `JAL`) do not consume `rs2`,\\\\nand some consume neither source, yet the current datapath still drives raw\\\\ninstruction bitfields through regfile read, bypass, and forwarding logic.\\\\nTrimming that unnecessary operand work is a plausible remaining Fmax win\\\\nthat stays off the fragile IF/control paths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add exact source-usage classification in decode and use it to normalize\\\\nunused source operands to `x0` before they enter the hot read/bypass path.\\\\nThen simplify the register-file/writeback interaction so same-cycle WB\\\\nbypass is only applied to architecturally live operands, ideally by moving\\\\nthe compare+mux out of the generic regfile read ports and into the decode\\\\nstage after source-use is known. Forwarding logic should see zeroed unused\\\\nsource addresses, and retirement should carry the architecturally consumed\\\\nsource values so RVFI stays exact; illegal or otherwise uncl...\\n  Line 30: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drain-and-replace posted store slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing single posted-store slot, but let it dequeue the older\\\\nbuffered store to dmem and capture one younger postable store in the same\\\\ncycle. In practice this means teaching `mem_stage` to drain and replace the\\\\nslot when `dmem_ready` comes back, and teaching `hazard_unit` not to stall\\\\nan EX/MEM store that can retire into the just-freed slot while still keeping\\\\nyounger loads and non-postable stores conservative. This should recover some\\\\nof the store-burst benefit the two-entry FIFO targeted, without adding\\\\nanother queue entry, alias-compare logic, or front-end timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Allow the one-entry posted store buffer to drain an older buffered RAM store and capture a younger postable store in the same cycle, ...\\n  Line 31: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r10s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Seed replay from ID-resolved JAL targets\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest `bench` predictor result is still the tiny registered replay\\\\ntable, and predictor attempts that widened IF control, added target decode,\\\\nor carried prediction metadata into later stages consistently lost fitness.\\\\nAfter the recent ID-stage `JAL` win, the next control-side blind spot is the\\\\nvery first fetch at a direct-call target: the target PC is now known exactly\\\\none cycle early, but `if_stage` still drops replay state on `id_redirect`, so\\\\na warmed callee entry point bubbles whenever `io_imemReady=0` on that first\\\\ntarget cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest `bench` predictor result is still the tiny registered replay\\\\ntable, and predictor attempts that widened IF control, added target decode,\\\\nor carried prediction metadata into later stages consistently lost fitness.\\\\nAfter the recent ID-stage `JAL` win, the next control-side blind spot is the\\\\nvery first fetch at a direct-call target: the target PC is now known exactly\\\\none cycle early, but `if_stage` still drops replay state on `id_redirect`, so\\\\na warmed callee entry point bubbles whenever `io_imemReady=0` on that first\\\\ntarget cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the existing `{pc,instr}` replay table to seed the next-cycle replay\\\\ncandidate from an exact `id_redirect_target` when decode resolves a legal\\\\naligned `JAL`. On a live ID redirect, `if_stage` would probe the existing\\\\nreplay table with the JAL target and, on a hit, load `replay_valid_q`,\\\\n`replay_pc_q`, and `replay_instr_q` for the upcoming target fetch instead of\\\\nunconditionally clearing them. Keep EX-stage redirect handling, sequential\\\\nreplay fill, and `fetch_available = imem_ready || replay_hit` unchanged, so\\\\nthis stays in the proven replay-family design space: no IF-side immediate\\\\ndecode, no new predicted-PC metadata through ID/EX, and no broader branch or\\\\nreturn speculation. The pay...\\n  Line 32: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r11s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Normalize unused legal source reads\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The biggest `bench` gains have come from narrow backend cleanups that cut\\\\nhot-path routing without widening IF or control logic. After the JAL-in-ID,\\\\nposted-store, and add-fast-path wins, the remaining backend tax is that ID\\\\nstill drives raw instruction bitfields into the regfile, same-cycle WB read\\\\nbypass, and EX forwarding compares even for legal instructions that do not\\\\narchitecturally read `rs1` and/or `rs2`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The biggest `bench` gains have come from narrow backend cleanups that cut\\\\nhot-path routing without widening IF or control logic. After the JAL-in-ID,\\\\nposted-store, and add-fast-path wins, the remaining backend tax is that ID\\\\nstill drives raw instruction bitfields into the regfile, same-cycle WB read\\\\nbypass, and EX forwarding compares even for legal instructions that do not\\\\narchitecturally read `rs1` and/or `rs2`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add exact `uses_rs1` / `uses_rs2` classification for legal instructions, and\\\\nuse it only to normalize definitely-unused source addresses to `x0` before\\\\nthey enter the hot read/bypass path. Keep the existing write-first regfile\\\\ncontract intact and do not move bypassing out of `reg_file`; the key change\\\\nis to stop reading and comparing random immediate bits on opclasses like\\\\n`LUI`, `AUIPC`, `JAL`, `FENCE`, `EBREAK`, and the `rs2`-unused I-type ops.\\\\nFor safety, illegal or otherwise unvalidated encodings should stay\\\\nconservative, with source-use bits only cleared inside positively validated\\\\ndecoder arms. This should shrink the always-on WB-to-ID compare/mux work and\\\\nthe downstream forwarding compare fanout while staying off the fragile IF,\\\\nbranch, and memory-control paths that already regressed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/decoder.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Emit exact `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` bits...\\n  Line 33: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Replay-backed one-slot IF decoupler\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Turn the registered replay candidate into a true one-slot IF decoupler. When\\\\nthe current PC already matches a valid replay entry, let decode consume the\\\\ncached `{pc,instr}` while the live imem port fetches the next sequential PC and\\\\nrefreshes the lookahead slot/table in the same cycle. Keep the current\\\\nredirect/flush rules, keep all state local to `if_stage`, and do not add IF-side\\\\nbranch decode, target tables, or a multi-entry queue. This should raise\\\\nCoreMark iterations/cycle by converting hot replay hits into useful prefetch\\\\nbandwidth, recovering more random iStall loss than the fallback-only replay\\\\npath without the timing cost that sank broader fetch-queue and control-predictor\\\\nattempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"des...\\n  Line 34: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r11s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Warm-table current-PC replay fallback\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The winning predictor on `bench` is still the tiny sequential replay table,\\\\nbut it only helps when the next-PC candidate was banked in the prior cycle.\\\\nAfter redirects, flushes, or other candidate-clearing events, the frontend\\\\ncan still bubble on a warm PC even though the replay table already holds the\\\\ncorrect instruction for that address.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The winning predictor on `bench` is still the tiny sequential replay table,\\\\nbut it only helps when the next-PC candidate was banked in the prior cycle.\\\\nAfter redirects, flushes, or other candidate-clearing events, the frontend\\\\ncan still bubble on a warm PC even though the replay table already holds the\\\\ncorrect instruction for that address.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing one-cycle replay candidate and all current redirect rules,\\\\nbut add a cheaper fallback path inside `if_stage`: when `io_imemReady=0` and\\\\nthe registered replay candidate misses, probe the existing direct-mapped\\\\n`{pc,instr}` table for the current PC and serve that cached instruction on a\\\\nhit. This does not steer fetch to a predicted target, does not add branch or\\\\nreturn metadata, and does not widen the table; it only lets the frontend\\\\nexploit already-warm entries when `replay_valid_q` was intentionally cleared.\\\\nThat specifically targets the current post-redirect and post-flush blind spot\\\\nwith less timing risk than target-add logic, second candidate slots, or wider\\\\nassociative replay structures.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a current-PC fallback hit path that reuses the existing replay table when `imem_ready=0` and the registered replay candidate is invalid or mismatched, while preserving the existing redirect, flush, and sequential candidate behavior.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"des...\\n  Line 36: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r12s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve ready branch-to-zero in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already harvested the cheapest broad structural wins: sequential IF\\\\nreplay, MEM-to-EX load forwarding, a one-entry posted store buffer, and an\\\\nexact early `JAL` redirect. The next visible IPC loss is still the taken\\\\nbranch bubble, but prior history shows that full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR\\\\nresolution collapses Fmax once decode has to do general bypass and\\\\ninterlock work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already harvested the cheapest broad structural wins: sequential IF\\\\nreplay, MEM-to-EX load forwarding, a one-entry posted store buffer, and an\\\\nexact early `JAL` redirect. The next visible IPC loss is still the taken\\\\nbranch bubble, but prior history shows that full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR\\\\nresolution collapses Fmax once decode has to do general bypass and\\\\ninterlock work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the existing `id_redirect` path for a much narrower exact control case:\\\\nlegal, aligned branch-against-zero instructions whose single live source is\\\\nalready architecturally ready in the regfile/WB path. Detect `BEQ/BNE` and\\\\nsigned zero-compare branch pseudos (`BLT/BGE` with `rs2 = x0`) in\\\\n`id_stage`, guard them with a cheap \\\\\\\"rs1 not pending in ID/EX or EX/MEM\\\\\\\"\\\\ncheck, and redirect taken cases one stage earlier while falling back to the\\\\nunchanged EX-stage branch path for everything else. Carry a generic\\\\n\\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through `id_ex_t` so EX suppresses duplicate\\\\nredirects but still recomputes `pc_next` and trap behavior, keeping RVFI\\\\nsource snapshots correct because the early path only fires when decode is\\\\nalready reading the final architecturally consumed operand.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the current JAL-only early-redirect marker in `id_ex_t` into a generic ID-resolved-control bit that can also tag ...\\n  Line 37: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r12s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Predict backward branches in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already found its best front-end wins by keeping speculation narrow:\\\\nthe sequential replay table helps with random iStalls, and the current best\\\\nsource moved only unconditional `JAL` into ID. The biggest remaining control\\\\nbubble is still the hot loop backedge branch, but the prior full ID-stage\\\\nBRANCH/JALR resolution failed because decode-side compare, bypass, and\\\\nload-interlock logic were too timing-expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already found its best front-end wins by keeping speculation narrow:\\\\nthe sequential replay table helps with random iStalls, and the current best\\\\nsource moved only unconditional `JAL` into ID. The biggest remaining control\\\\nbubble is still the hot loop backedge branch, but the prior full ID-stage\\\\nBRANCH/JALR resolution failed because decode-side compare, bypass, and\\\\nload-interlock logic were too timing-expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing ID redirect path from \\\\\\\"exact early JAL\\\\\\\" to a static\\\\nbackward-branch predictor that redirects only legal, aligned B-type\\\\ninstructions with a negative immediate. This keeps prediction out of IF,\\\\nreuses the already-cheap `pc + imm` work that ID does for JAL, and avoids\\\\nthe operand-bypass/compare machinery that sank exact early branch\\\\nresolution. EX still computes the real branch condition and target, but it\\\\nonly repairs mispredicted loop exits by redirecting back to `pc+4`; correct\\\\ntaken backedges simply suppress the duplicate EX redirect. That targets a\\\\nhot CoreMark penalty with much lower Fmax risk than IF-side branch target\\\\nprediction, RAS state, or full decode-time BRANCH/JALR resolution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the existing JAL-only early-redirect metadata in `id_ex_t` into a generic ID-predicted-control bit for J...\\n  Line 38: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r13s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Downstream-only source-use gating\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The remaining plausible `bench` gains are backend timing cleanups, not more\\\\nfront-end speculation or memory-control machinery. Two recent micro-opt tries\\\\nalready pointed at the same hotspot: unused `rs1`/`rs2` fields still drive\\\\nforwarding and bypass compares on hot OP-IMM, LOAD, JAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE,\\\\nand EBREAK traffic. Those tries failed because they touched the raw regfile\\\\nread path and RVFI-visible source view, which this core's correctness contract\\\\ntreats as architectural.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The remaining plausible `bench` gains are backend timing cleanups, not more\\\\nfront-end speculation or memory-control machinery. Two recent micro-opt tries\\\\nalready pointed at the same hotspot: unused `rs1`/`rs2` fields still drive\\\\nforwarding and bypass compares on hot OP-IMM, LOAD, JAL, LUI, AUIPC, FENCE,\\\\nand EBREAK traffic. Those tries failed because they touched the raw regfile\\\\nread path and RVFI-visible source view, which this core's correctness contract\\\\ntreats as architectural.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add exact legal `uses_rs1` / `uses_rs2` bits in decode, but apply them only\\\\nafter the raw regfile read has already happened. Keep `reg_file.sv`, the raw\\\\n`rs1_addr`/`rs2_addr` wiring, same-cycle write-first behavior, and RVFI source\\\\nsnapshots unchanged. Instead, carry conservative source-use bits through the\\\\nexisting control bundle and use them only to suppress downstream EX forwarding\\\\ncompare trees and select logic for definitely-unused operands. Default the new\\\\nbits to \\\\\\\"used\\\\\\\" and only clear them inside validated decoder arms, so illegal\\\\nencodings remain conservative. This targets the same backend fanout that the\\\\nbroken normalization attempts were aiming at, but avoids reopening their\\\\nselftest/RVFI failure mode by leaving the architectural source-read view intact.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\"...\\n  Line 39: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r13s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Mem-stall replay prefetch slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current winning frontend only learns future instructions from cycles where\\\\ndecode itself is advancing. When MEM-side backpressure freezes the pipe, the\\\\ninstruction port usually sits idle even though those cycles could have been\\\\nused to seed the next sequential replay hit. Earlier broad fetch queues lost\\\\nbecause they had to run ahead every cycle; the remaining structural opening is\\\\na narrower overlap of dmem-stall time with harmless prefetch work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current winning frontend only learns future instructions from cycles where\\\\ndecode itself is advancing. When MEM-side backpressure freezes the pipe, the\\\\ninstruction port usually sits idle even though those cycles could have been\\\\nused to seed the next sequential replay hit. Earlier broad fetch queues lost\\\\nbecause they had to run ahead every cycle; the remaining structural opening is\\\\na narrower overlap of dmem-stall time with harmless prefetch work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Teach `if_stage` to treat backend-only MEM stalls as prefetch opportunities\\\\ninstead of total frontend dead time. While architectural PC and IF->ID state\\\\nstay frozen, drive a shadow sequential fetch for the held `pc+4` (or a tiny\\\\none-slot skid successor) and bank the returned `{pc,instr}` into the existing\\\\nreplay candidate/table; when the stall clears, the normal path resumes and\\\\nthe next instruction is already replayable if `io_imemReady` drops. This\\\\ncompounds with the existing replay-table and posted-store wins, avoids\\\\ndecode-time branch/JALR logic, and is narrower than the failed always-on\\\\nfetch queue because it only adds work on cycles that were already lost to\\\\ndmem backpressure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a backend-stall prefetch/skid path that keeps architectural IF output frozen...\\n  Line 40: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r13s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Protect hot replay lines from conflict eviction\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The only predictor family that has helped `bench` is the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay table in `if_stage`; broader control prediction, target seeding,\\\\ncurrent-PC fallback, and associative widening all regressed. One remaining\\\\nweakness of the current direct-mapped design is that every architecturally\\\\nlive fetch overwrites its indexed slot immediately, so a one-off colliding PC\\\\ncan evict a line that already proved useful on prior replay hits.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The only predictor family that has helped `bench` is the tiny sequential\\\\nreplay table in `if_stage`; broader control prediction, target seeding,\\\\ncurrent-PC fallback, and associative widening all regressed. One remaining\\\\nweakness of the current direct-mapped design is that every architecturally\\\\nlive fetch overwrites its indexed slot immediately, so a one-off colliding PC\\\\ncan evict a line that already proved useful on prior replay hits.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing `fetch_available = imem_ready || replay_hit` contract, the\\\\none-cycle `pc+4` replay candidate, and all redirect behavior unchanged, but\\\\nadd a tiny usefulness predictor to replay-table replacement. Give each replay\\\\nentry a one-bit \\\\\\\"hot\\\\\\\" or second-chance marker that is set only after that\\\\nentry successfully serves a replay hit; on a later colliding fill from a\\\\ndifferent PC, do not immediately evict a marked-hot line, and instead require\\\\na second conflict before replacement. This stays entirely inside\\\\n`rtl/if_stage.sv`, adds no extra tag-compare path to `fetch_available`, and\\\\navoids the timing cost of extra ways, victim lookups, target caches, or\\\\ndecode-time control prediction while still targeting replay misses caused by\\\\ncold conflict pollution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a per-entr...\\n  Line 41: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r14s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Shared sign-corrected DIV/REM datapath\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The profitable `bench` micro-opts have been local backend cone cleanups that\\\\npreserved the existing 5-stage machine and RVFI contract. `rtl/alu.sv`\\\\nalready got a measurable win from collapsing `MUL*`, but `DIV`, `DIVU`,\\\\n`REM`, and `REMU` still sit in the ALU as separate real-arithmetic cases.\\\\nOn Tang Nano 20K that likely duplicates a large cold cone and adds routing\\\\npressure even though CoreMark rarely executes divides in its timed region.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The profitable `bench` micro-opts have been local backend cone cleanups that\\\\npreserved the existing 5-stage machine and RVFI contract. `rtl/alu.sv`\\\\nalready got a measurable win from collapsing `MUL*`, but `DIV`, `DIVU`,\\\\n`REM`, and `REMU` still sit in the ALU as separate real-arithmetic cases.\\\\nOn Tang Nano 20K that likely duplicates a large cold cone and adds routing\\\\npressure even though CoreMark rarely executes divides in its timed region.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep divide and remainder single-cycle, but refactor the real-arithmetic\\\\n`DIV*`/`REM*` path onto one shared unsigned-magnitude divider/remainder flow\\\\nplus lightweight sign correction and common corner-case handling. Compute the\\\\nabsolute-value quotient and remainder once, derive the signed results with\\\\npost-processing, and preserve the existing ALTOPS branch, divide-by-zero\\\\nbehavior, and `INT_MIN / -1` semantics. This follows the same successful\\\\npattern as the earlier shared `MUL*` cleanup: reduce cold M-extension area\\\\nand routing without adding the multicycle interlock/control machinery that\\\\nalready broke or regressed on prior DIV/REM experiments.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Collapse `DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU` onto a shared magnitude-based combinational divider path with common special-case logic, while keeping the e...\\n  Line 42: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r14s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-slot execute-under-load shadow buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The structural history on `bench` says the profitable direction is backend\\\\ndecoupling, not wider front-end control: the posted store buffer helped, while\\\\nbroader fetch and decode-time redirect schemes mostly regressed. The biggest\\\\nremaining unhidden stall class is a dmem-backed load, which still freezes the\\\\nwhole machine on any `!io_dmemReady` cycle even though the tournament model's\\\\nrandom backpressure creates many short stalls that could overlap useful younger\\\\nnon-memory work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The structural history on `bench` says the profitable direction is backend\\\\ndecoupling, not wider front-end control: the posted store buffer helped, while\\\\nbroader fetch and decode-time redirect schemes mostly regressed. The biggest\\\\nremaining unhidden stall class is a dmem-backed load, which still freezes the\\\\nwhole machine on any `!io_dmemReady` cycle even though the tournament model's\\\\nrandom backpressure creates many short stalls that could overlap useful younger\\\\nnon-memory work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny execute-under-load mechanism instead of more front-end prediction.\\\\nWhen a load reaches MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, spill that older load's full\\\\nretirement context into a dedicated pending-load slot and scoreboard its `rd`.\\\\nWhile that slot waits for memory, allow exactly one younger independent\\\\nALU/branch/jump instruction to continue through EX into a shadow result slot,\\\\nbut keep younger memory operations and any instruction that reads the pending\\\\nload's destination conservatively stalled. When the load completes, retire it\\\\nfirst, then drain the shadowed younger result in order. This keeps single-retire\\\\nRVFI ordering and in-order memory semantics, avoids putting more logic on the\\\\nIF/ID critical path, and targets the same short random dStall bursts that made\\\\nthe posted store buffer pay off.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_i...\\n  Line 43: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r14s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Backend-stall shadow replay prefetcher\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The only predictor family that has consistently helped `bench` is the tiny\\\\nsequential replay path in `rtl/if_stage.sv`. The failures around branch\\\\nprediction, redirect-target seeding, wider replay lookup, and current-PC\\\\nfallback all point to the same rule: do not put more logic on the live IF\\\\ncritical path. The best remaining predictor opportunity is therefore to warm\\\\nthe existing replay state using cycles that are already lost to backend stalls,\\\\ninstead of adding new control speculation.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The only predictor family that has consistently helped `bench` is the tiny\\\\nsequential replay path in `rtl/if_stage.sv`. The failures around branch\\\\nprediction, redirect-target seeding, wider replay lookup, and current-PC\\\\nfallback all point to the same rule: do not put more logic on the live IF\\\\ncritical path. The best remaining predictor opportunity is therefore to warm\\\\nthe existing replay state using cycles that are already lost to backend stalls,\\\\ninstead of adding new control speculation.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the current `fetch_available = imem_ready || replay_hit` contract, the\\\\none-cycle `pc+4` replay candidate, and all EX/ID redirect behavior unchanged,\\\\nbut add a shadow prefetch mode that runs only while the architectural frontend\\\\nis frozen by a backend MEM stall. In that mode, `if_stage` should leave the\\\\nvisible `pc` and IF->ID payload untouched, drive a non-architectural shadow\\\\nfetch for the held `pc+4`, and train the existing replay table/candidate only\\\\nif that shadow fetch returns. This targets the current replay predictor's main\\\\nblind spot: it only learns future instructions on cycles where decode itself\\\\nadvances. By harvesting otherwise idle MEM-stall cycles, the core should raise\\\\nreplay-hit coverage without adding IF-side branch decode, target RAM, second\\\\nlookup ways, or cross-stage prediction...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":41,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1|hyp-20260515-002-r5s1|structural|shadow|store buffer|replay|branch|jal|load|scoreboard|pipeline|stage\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778836149292,\"end\":1778836149314}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0485f3d2ea88bc34016a06e2b5054c8199a687949405ccb1b2\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2ae593b50019b2eeP7lJGPygx\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d51ad624ffe3QWHb1QQRha61G\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2ae568ab001MjS98lkD4IGwu0\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:1061:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778836169533,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d51ad624ffe3QWHb1QQRha61G\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_BfadMhFcvhwGL6o1p4mLhjyJ\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"\\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\"\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 14 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 3: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Add a small redirect-aware fetch queue\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front-end is tightly coupled to `io_imemReady`: any cycle with\\\\ninstruction-memory backpressure immediately becomes a decode bubble because\\\\nIF emits a NOP and the PC stops. That is especially expensive here because\\\\nthe fitness run injects random iStalls by design, so front-end elasticity is\\\\nworth more than a tiny local logic cleanup.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current front-end is tightly coupled to `io_imemReady`: any cycle with\\\\ninstruction-memory backpressure immediately becomes a decode bubble because\\\\nIF emits a NOP and the PC stops. That is especially expensive here because\\\\nthe fitness run injects random iStalls by design, so front-end elasticity is\\\\nworth more than a tiny local logic cleanup.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single-slot combinational fetch path with a small speculative\\\\nfetch queue in `if_stage`, likely 2-4 entries deep, that buffers `{pc,\\\\ninstr}` pairs when `io_imemReady` is high and feeds ID from the queue when\\\\nthe backend is ready. On redirects, flush younger queued instructions and\\\\nrestart fetch at the resolved target; on load-use or dmem stalls, preserve\\\\nthe buffered instructions instead of re-fetching them. This is a structural\\\\nfront-end change, not a predictor: it should raise CoreMark iterations/cycle\\\\nunder the orchestrator's random stall model with only modest area/timing\\\\ncost and without changing the single-retire RVFI contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Refactor IF into a small prefetch FIFO/skid-buffer front-end with redirect-aware invalidation and queue-based instruction delivery.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Change front-end stall logic to depend on fetch-queue availability plus backend hazards instead of bubbling immediately on every `!imemReady`.\\\"}, {\\\"...\\n  Line 6: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Operand-Stage-Aware Load Interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current hazard unit stalls on any raw `rs1`/`rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not actually read that field yet\\\\nor only needs it later as store data in MEM. That conservative policy burns\\\\navoidable one-cycle bubbles in CoreMark's dense load/address/store code, and\\\\nthe recent `bench` regressions already showed that extra front-end machinery\\\\nis a worse trade here than tightening the backend interlock.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current hazard unit stalls on any raw `rs1`/`rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not actually read that field yet\\\\nor only needs it later as store data in MEM. That conservative policy burns\\\\navoidable one-cycle bubbles in CoreMark's dense load/address/store code, and\\\\nthe recent `bench` regressions already showed that extra front-end machinery\\\\nis a worse trade here than tightening the backend interlock.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework the load-use interlock around the stage where an operand is actually\\\\nconsumed. Add decode metadata for real `rs1`/`rs2` usage, treat STORE `rs2`\\\\nas a late MEM-stage consumer instead of an EX-stage consumer, and add a\\\\nWB-to-MEM store-data bypass so `load -> store` pairs with an independent\\\\naddress can proceed without the textbook bubble. This should recover\\\\nCoreMark IPC from both false-positive load stalls and true-but-late\\\\nstore-data dependencies, while keeping load data off the EX ALU/branch path\\\\nand avoiding another fetch-path timing penalty.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add decoded source-usage and late-store-data control bits to the pipeline bundles so hazards can distinguish EX-stage operands from MEM-stage-only store data.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/decoder.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Classify which legal ins...\\n  Line 9: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Exact load-use interlock with late store-data bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The fetch side already has a winning replay mechanism, so the strongest\\\\nremaining structural gain is more likely in the backend. `hazard_unit.sv`\\\\nstill stalls on any raw `if_id_rs1` or `if_id_rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not consume that field in EX or\\\\nonly needs store data later in MEM. CoreMark's dense mix of loads,\\\\nimmediate arithmetic, address generation, and stores makes those\\\\nconservative one-cycle bubbles expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The fetch side already has a winning replay mechanism, so the strongest\\\\nremaining structural gain is more likely in the backend. `hazard_unit.sv`\\\\nstill stalls on any raw `if_id_rs1` or `if_id_rs2` bitfield match behind a\\\\nload, even when the younger instruction does not consume that field in EX or\\\\nonly needs store data later in MEM. CoreMark's dense mix of loads,\\\\nimmediate arithmetic, address generation, and stores makes those\\\\nconservative one-cycle bubbles expensive.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework the load-use interlock around true operand-consumption timing instead\\\\nof raw register-field matches. Decode exact legal `rs1` and `rs2` usage for\\\\neach instruction class, keep the current conservative stall policy for\\\\nillegal or otherwise unclassified encodings, and treat STORE `rs2` as a\\\\nMEM-stage consumer with a dedicated WB-to-MEM store-data bypass. To avoid\\\\nthe prior broken attempt's RVFI bug, carry the final architecturally\\\\nconsumed store-data value into retirement so `rvfi_rs2_rdata`,\\\\n`rvfi_mem_wdata`, and the actual dmem write all agree. This should remove\\\\nfalse bubbles from OP-IMM/LOAD/JALR/LUI/AUIPC/FENCE paths and eliminate the\\\\nmandatory `load -> store(rs2)` stall without putting load data on the EX\\\\nALU, branch-compare, or JALR critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"...\\n  Line 12: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"ID-stage exact control resolution\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best low-risk fetch and\\\\nload-use gains: it replays hot sequential instructions in IF and forwards\\\\nzero-wait loads from MEM to EX. The remaining visible IPC loss is that every\\\\ntaken BRANCH, JAL, and JALR still resolves in EX after the ID/EX register,\\\\nwhich forces extra wrong-path bubbles on CoreMark's hot loop backedges and\\\\nhelper calls.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best low-risk fetch and\\\\nload-use gains: it replays hot sequential instructions in IF and forwards\\\\nzero-wait loads from MEM to EX. The remaining visible IPC loss is that every\\\\ntaken BRANCH, JAL, and JALR still resolves in EX after the ID/EX register,\\\\nwhich forces extra wrong-path bubbles on CoreMark's hot loop backedges and\\\\nhelper calls.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Move exact control-flow resolution one stage earlier by adding a dedicated\\\\nID-stage branch/jump unit. JAL can redirect immediately from `pc+imm`; BRANCH\\\\nand JALR should compare/add in ID using the regfile value plus targeted\\\\nbypasses from EX/MEM and MEM/WB, while conservatively stalling only when an\\\\noperand is still unavailable from an in-flight load. Carry the final\\\\nforwarded source values into the pipeline state so RVFI operand snapshots stay\\\\naligned with the values the control instruction actually consumed, and mark\\\\nID-resolved control ops so EX does not issue a duplicate redirect a cycle\\\\nlater. This keeps the existing single-retire contract and replay frontend,\\\\nbut should cut the taken-control penalty by roughly one cycle without adding\\\\nIF-side predictor state or target-table complexity.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the pipeline bundles with control-resolution metadata so ID-resolved BRANCH/JAL/JALR instru...\\n  Line 15: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-entry posted store buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current pipeline freezes the whole machine on any store when `io_dmemReady`\\\\ndrops, even though a younger run of ALU, branch, or jump instructions could\\\\nexecute correctly once the store address, write data, and byte mask are already\\\\nknown. Prior structural attempts showed that adding more front-end or ID-stage\\\\ncontrol logic is expensive on this core, so the best remaining structural gain\\\\nis backend decoupling that leaves IF/ID timing alone.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry posted-store buffer that captures a store's aligned address,\\\\nreplicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI-visible metadata when the store\\\\nreaches MEM. If the external dmem port stalls, retire the store from the buffer\\\\nimmediately, keep draining the buffered write in the background, and allow\\\\nyounger non-memory instructions to keep flowing. Only stall when a younger\\\\nload or store reaches MEM while the buffer is still occupied, preserving\\\\nin-order memory semantics without reintroducing load-use or control-path\\\\ncomplexity. This should recover CoreMark IPC from random dmem backpressure on\\\\nstore-heavy regions while avoiding the IF/ID critical-path regressions seen in\\\\nearlier structural experiments.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the MEM-stage/retirement bundles with store-buffer bookkeepin...\\n  Line 18: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Alias-aware load bypass around posted stores\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already gets a real win from the one-entry\\\\nposted store buffer, but it still serializes every younger load behind any\\\\nbuffered main-memory store. CoreMark mixes loads and stores densely, and many\\\\nof those younger loads are independent of the older store, so the blanket\\\\nstall likely leaves IPC on the table even after the successful store-buffer\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing posted store buffer, but make it address-aware instead of\\\\nblocking all younger memory ops. When a younger load reaches MEM while a\\\\nmain-memory store is buffered, compare the load's aligned word address and\\\\nbyte mask against the buffered store: if there is no overlap, let the load use\\\\nthe dmem port immediately and drain the buffered store later; if the load is\\\\nfully covered by the buffered bytes, forward from the buffer; only stall on\\\\ntrue alias or partial-overlap cases, and keep younger stores conservative.\\\\nThis is a backend-only structural change that compounds with the winning\\\\nposted-store buffer while avoiding the IF/ID timing regressions seen in prior\\\\nstructural attempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add store-buffer alias checking, demand-load-vs-drain arbitration, and buffered-store forwarding for fully covered younger loads.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the blank...\\n  Line 21: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r7s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Two-entry posted store FIFO\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the best structural win on `bench`,\\\\nwhich points to backend write decoupling as the profitable structural\\\\ndirection. Today any second main-memory store, or any dmem stall burst longer\\\\nthan one buffered write, collapses back to a full-pipeline block even though\\\\nthe pending work is still an in-order sequence of already-resolved stores.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single posted-store slot with a tiny two-entry in-order store\\\\nFIFO that can absorb a short burst of normal-RAM stores while older buffered\\\\nwrites drain in the background. Keep the current conservative memory-order\\\\nrule that younger loads still wait until the FIFO is empty, and avoid the\\\\nlosing alias-compare/forwarding path entirely; the gain should come from\\\\nletting consecutive stores and following non-memory instructions retire\\\\nacross multi-cycle `io_dmemReady` stalls without touching IF timing or the\\\\nEX critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/store_queue.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a tiny two-entry in-order posted-store FIFO with enqueue/dequeue logic and head/tail status for MEM arbitration.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single buffered-store slot with the FIFO so a postable store can enqueue behind an older buffered store while preserving the existing conservative load ordering and RVFI store reporting.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"de...\\n  Line 24: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve direct JAL in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current `bench` core has already captured the best backend decoupling and\\\\nreplay-table wins, but every unconditional direct call/jump still waits until\\\\nEX to redirect fetch. Prior structural history says full ID-stage\\\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolution is too expensive on this FPGA, so the next control\\\\nimprovement should target only the cheapest unconditional case.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a narrow early-redirect path for legal `JAL` instructions in ID while\\\\nleaving BRANCH and JALR resolution in EX. `JAL` needs only `pc + imm`, so it\\\\ncan redirect one stage earlier without the operand-bypass, compare, and\\\\nload-interlock machinery that made full ID-stage control resolution collapse\\\\nFmax. Carry an `early_redirected`/resolved-next-PC bit with the instruction so\\\\nEX suppresses the duplicate redirect but still produces the normal `rd = pc+4`\\\\nwriteback and RVFI retirement record. This should shave a bubble from hot\\\\ndirect calls and unconditional jumps in CoreMark while keeping the replay\\\\nfrontend, posted store buffer, and hot EX datapath essentially unchanged.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the decode/pipeline control bundles with a bit that marks a JAL as already redirected before EX, plus any resolved next-PC metadata needed to suppress duplicate redirects later.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal JALs in decode, compute `pc + imm`, and...\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve canonical RET in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best `bench` core already removed the cheapest unconditional\\\\ncontrol bubble by redirecting direct `JAL` in ID, but common `ret`\\\\ninstructions still wait until EX. Full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR resolution\\\\nalready proved too timing-expensive on Tang Nano 20K, so the next\\\\nstructural step should target only the hottest JALR subcase that needs no\\\\ncompare logic and usually no bypassing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing ID redirect path to cover canonical aligned returns,\\\\ne.g. `jalr x0, x1, 0` and `jalr x0, x5, 0`, when the return-address\\\\nregister is already architecturally available from the regfile/WB path. If\\\\nan older in-flight instruction still owns that source register, simply fall\\\\nback to the current EX-stage JALR path instead of adding the full ID-stage\\\\nbypass/interlock machinery that regressed earlier. This reuses the existing\\\\n`id_redirect` plumbing, carries an \\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through\\\\nthe pipeline so EX suppresses the duplicate redirect, and should cut\\\\nanother hot function-return bubble in CoreMark with much lower timing risk\\\\nthan general early JALR/BRANCH resolution.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the existing `jal_early_redirect` pipeline bit into an ID-resolved jump marker that can cover both direct `JAL` and narrow early-return `JALR` cases.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"descr...\\n  Line 30: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drain-and-replace posted store slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The one-entry posted store buffer was the strongest structural win on `bench`,\\\\nbut it still behaves like a zero-depth queue at the handoff point: when a\\\\nbuffered store drains, any younger store waiting in EX/MEM burns an extra\\\\ncycle before it can post. The full two-entry FIFO already failed placement\\\\nand alias-aware load bypass hurt timing, so the next backend improvement\\\\nneeds to preserve the current one-slot footprint while removing that\\\\nneedless store-to-store bubble.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing single posted-store slot, but let it dequeue the older\\\\nbuffered store to dmem and capture one younger postable store in the same\\\\ncycle. In practice this means teaching `mem_stage` to drain and replace the\\\\nslot when `dmem_ready` comes back, and teaching `hazard_unit` not to stall\\\\nan EX/MEM store that can retire into the just-freed slot while still keeping\\\\nyounger loads and non-postable stores conservative. This should recover some\\\\nof the store-burst benefit the two-entry FIFO targeted, without adding\\\\nanother queue entry, alias-compare logic, or front-end timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Allow the one-entry posted store buffer to drain an older buffered RAM store and capture a younger postable store in the same cycle, ...\\n  Line 33: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Replay-backed one-slot IF decoupler\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current frontend already has a winning sequential replay mechanism, but it\\\\nonly uses cached instructions as a fallback when `io_imemReady=0`. On a replay\\\\nhit with `io_imemReady=1`, the live memory port still spends the cycle\\\\nrefetching the current instruction instead of getting one step ahead. Prior\\\\nstructural history says a general fetch FIFO is too expensive on this FPGA, so\\\\nthe best remaining front-end bet is a much narrower elasticity change that\\\\nreuses the existing replay state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Turn the registered replay candidate into a true one-slot IF decoupler. When\\\\nthe current PC already matches a valid replay entry, let decode consume the\\\\ncached `{pc,instr}` while the live imem port fetches the next sequential PC and\\\\nrefreshes the lookahead slot/table in the same cycle. Keep the current\\\\nredirect/flush rules, keep all state local to `if_stage`, and do not add IF-side\\\\nbranch decode, target tables, or a multi-entry queue. This should raise\\\\nCoreMark iterations/cycle by converting hot replay hits into useful prefetch\\\\nbandwidth, recovering more random iStall loss than the fallback-only replay\\\\npath without the timing cost that sank broader fetch-queue and control-predictor\\\\nattempts.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"des...\\n  Line 36: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r12s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Resolve ready branch-to-zero in ID\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already harvested the cheapest broad structural wins: sequential IF\\\\nreplay, MEM-to-EX load forwarding, a one-entry posted store buffer, and an\\\\nexact early `JAL` redirect. The next visible IPC loss is still the taken\\\\nbranch bubble, but prior history shows that full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR\\\\nresolution collapses Fmax once decode has to do general bypass and\\\\ninterlock work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"`bench` already harvested the cheapest broad structural wins: sequential IF\\\\nreplay, MEM-to-EX load forwarding, a one-entry posted store buffer, and an\\\\nexact early `JAL` redirect. The next visible IPC loss is still the taken\\\\nbranch bubble, but prior history shows that full ID-stage BRANCH/JALR\\\\nresolution collapses Fmax once decode has to do general bypass and\\\\ninterlock work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the existing `id_redirect` path for a much narrower exact control case:\\\\nlegal, aligned branch-against-zero instructions whose single live source is\\\\nalready architecturally ready in the regfile/WB path. Detect `BEQ/BNE` and\\\\nsigned zero-compare branch pseudos (`BLT/BGE` with `rs2 = x0`) in\\\\n`id_stage`, guard them with a cheap \\\\\\\"rs1 not pending in ID/EX or EX/MEM\\\\\\\"\\\\ncheck, and redirect taken cases one stage earlier while falling back to the\\\\nunchanged EX-stage branch path for everything else. Carry a generic\\\\n\\\\\\\"already redirected in ID\\\\\\\" bit through `id_ex_t` so EX suppresses duplicate\\\\nredirects but still recomputes `pc_next` and trap behavior, keeping RVFI\\\\nsource snapshots correct because the early path only fires when decode is\\\\nalready reading the final architecturally consumed operand.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generalize the current JAL-only early-redirect marker in `id_ex_t` into a generic ID-resolved-control bit that can also tag ...\\n  Line 39: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r13s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Mem-stall replay prefetch slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current winning frontend only learns future instructions from cycles where\\\\ndecode itself is advancing. When MEM-side backpressure freezes the pipe, the\\\\ninstruction port usually sits idle even though those cycles could have been\\\\nused to seed the next sequential replay hit. Earlier broad fetch queues lost\\\\nbecause they had to run ahead every cycle; the remaining structural opening is\\\\na narrower overlap of dmem-stall time with harmless prefetch work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current winning frontend only learns future instructions from cycles where\\\\ndecode itself is advancing. When MEM-side backpressure freezes the pipe, the\\\\ninstruction port usually sits idle even though those cycles could have been\\\\nused to seed the next sequential replay hit. Earlier broad fetch queues lost\\\\nbecause they had to run ahead every cycle; the remaining structural opening is\\\\na narrower overlap of dmem-stall time with harmless prefetch work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Teach `if_stage` to treat backend-only MEM stalls as prefetch opportunities\\\\ninstead of total frontend dead time. While architectural PC and IF->ID state\\\\nstay frozen, drive a shadow sequential fetch for the held `pc+4` (or a tiny\\\\none-slot skid successor) and bank the returned `{pc,instr}` into the existing\\\\nreplay candidate/table; when the stall clears, the normal path resumes and\\\\nthe next instruction is already replayable if `io_imemReady` drops. This\\\\ncompounds with the existing replay-table and posted-store wins, avoids\\\\ndecode-time branch/JALR logic, and is narrower than the failed always-on\\\\nfetch queue because it only adds work on cycles that were already lost to\\\\ndmem backpressure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a backend-stall prefetch/skid path that keeps architectural IF output frozen...\\n  Line 42: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r14s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-slot execute-under-load shadow buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The structural history on `bench` says the profitable direction is backend\\\\ndecoupling, not wider front-end control: the posted store buffer helped, while\\\\nbroader fetch and decode-time redirect schemes mostly regressed. The biggest\\\\nremaining unhidden stall class is a dmem-backed load, which still freezes the\\\\nwhole machine on any `!io_dmemReady` cycle even though the tournament model's\\\\nrandom backpressure creates many short stalls that could overlap useful younger\\\\nnon-memory work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The structural history on `bench` says the profitable direction is backend\\\\ndecoupling, not wider front-end control: the posted store buffer helped, while\\\\nbroader fetch and decode-time redirect schemes mostly regressed. The biggest\\\\nremaining unhidden stall class is a dmem-backed load, which still freezes the\\\\nwhole machine on any `!io_dmemReady` cycle even though the tournament model's\\\\nrandom backpressure creates many short stalls that could overlap useful younger\\\\nnon-memory work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny execute-under-load mechanism instead of more front-end prediction.\\\\nWhen a load reaches MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, spill that older load's full\\\\nretirement context into a dedicated pending-load slot and scoreboard its `rd`.\\\\nWhile that slot waits for memory, allow exactly one younger independent\\\\nALU/branch/jump instruction to continue through EX into a shadow result slot,\\\\nbut keep younger memory operations and any instruction that reads the pending\\\\nload's destination conservatively stalled. When the load completes, retire it\\\\nfirst, then drain the shadowed younger result in order. This keeps single-retire\\\\nRVFI ordering and in-order memory semantics, avoids putting more logic on the\\\\nIF/ID critical path, and targets the same short random dStall bursts that made\\\\nthe posted store buffer pay off.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_i...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":14,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"\\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\"\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778836169509,\"end\":1778836169532}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0c671d98fde17cb6016a06e2c961d4819a9177cfa216ecafb0\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2ae5e323001zjbSsX5xHoi3WH\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d51ad624ffe3QWHb1QQRha61G\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2ae595c40015gZD7zyY8YMBEV\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:1080:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778836257547,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d51ad624ffe3QWHb1QQRha61G\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_IZMEzy1ckeF9dlOtYyUOJP1k\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"auipc|jalr\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/bench/programs\",\"include\":\"*.S\"},\"output\":\"Found 2 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2/bench/programs/selftest.S:\\n  Line 128:     auipc t0, 0                    # t0 = pc; nonzero (text past start)\\n\\n  Line 147:     jalr zero, ra, 0\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":2,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"auipc|jalr\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778836257504,\"end\":1778836257546}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0ab8d07ef0902fe5016a06e3213db08199840dd604c47779f4\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2ae73add001RECd6YyCjd97lm\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d51ad624ffe3QWHb1QQRha61G\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2ae65af2001ScNBUJO3m8JV3r\"}}\nbench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep2/agent.log:1081:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778836257549,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d51ad624ffe3QWHb1QQRha61G\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_8AtDrU7LE3z8VzwWJPy4rlYj\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"auipc|jalr\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep2\",\"include\":\"*.lst\"},\"output\":\"No files found\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":0,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"auipc|ja