reg_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_44_60 at rvfi_reg_check.sv:44.6-44.59 step 20\\nSBY 17:04:15 [reg_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\n\\nFull run log: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-luna-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260710-001-r1s0/formal/last_run-53490.log\\nPer-check logs in: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-luna-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260710-001-r1s0/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-53490/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): A zero-bubble EX/MEM load-data bypass must keep the extracted value, pipeline valid bits, and RVFI metadata aligned while `dmemReady` is low; merely holding the memory stage and suppressing redirects lets dependent instructions observe or retire mismatched register state.\"}\n4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Early static backward-branch prediction\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The bench core resolves conditional branches and jumps in EX, then flushes\\nyounger work and redirects the fetch PC. CoreMark contains many tight loops,\\nso taken backward branches repeatedly pay this avoidable redirect penalty.\\nThe fixture is already close to its LUT budget, making a table-based BTB/BHT\\na riskier tradeoff than a small decode-only predictor.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench core resolves conditional branches and jumps in EX, then flushes\\nyounger work and redirects the fetch PC. CoreMark contains many tight loops,\\nso taken backward branches repeatedly pay this avoidable redirect penalty.\\nThe fixture is already close to its LUT budget, making a table-based BTB/BHT\\na riskier tradeoff than a small decode-only predictor.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add an IF-stage static control-flow predictor that predicts valid backward\\nconditional branches taken and predicts direct JAL instructions to their\\nimmediately computed targets; leave forward branches and JALR unpredicted.\\nCarry the predicted next PC through ID/EX and make EX redirect only when the\\nresolved next PC differs from the prediction, recovering to the actual\\ntarget or fall-through PC on a miss. This should remove most EX redirect\\nbubbles from CoreMark loop branches with little area and timing overhead.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute aligned B/J immediates from the fetched instruction and select a predicted target for backward conditional branches and direct JALs before the EX redirect path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles with prediction-valid/target metadata needed to compare the prediction against the resolved next PC.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the IF-stage prediction metadata alongside the decoded instruction so EX can validate or recover from the prediction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the resolved branch/jump next PC with the carried prediction and issue redirects only on mispredictions, preserving trap and RVFI pc_next semantics.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute aligned B/J immediates from the fetched instruction and select a predicted target for backward conditional branches and direct JALs before the EX redirect path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles with prediction-valid/target metadata needed to compare the prediction against the resolved next PC.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the IF-stage prediction metadata alongside the decoded instruction so EX can validate or recover from the prediction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the resolved branch/jump next PC with the carried prediction and issue redirects only on mispredictions, preserving trap and RVFI pc_next semantics.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 310.56, \"delta_pct\": 9.81, \"vs_baseline\": 9.81, \"fmax_mhz\": 132.29, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10554, \"ff\": 1897, \"seeds\": [128.47, 132.29, 133.98], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T20:04:27.584939Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (improvement, +9.81%): A small IF-stage predictor for backward conditional branches and direct JALs, with prediction metadata carried through ID/EX and EX redirects only on next-PC mismatch, removes most CoreMark loop redirect bubbles without the area cost of a BTB/BHT.\"}\n5:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Share the RV32M multiplier datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current combinational ALU builds three independent 32x32 products for\\nMUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU on every cycle, although each instruction needs\\nonly one product interpretation. This is costly on the Tang Nano 20K, where\\nthe current predictor-enhanced fixture is already above the LUT target and\\nmultiplier routing can also limit Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current combinational ALU builds three independent 32x32 products for\\nMUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU on every cycle, although each instruction needs\\nonly one product interpretation. This is costly on the Tang Nano 20K, where\\nthe current predictor-enhanced fixture is already above the LUT target and\\nmultiplier routing can also limit Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel multiplier expressions with one shared signed\\n33x33 product. Select sign-extension versus zero-extension independently for\\neach operand from the decoded M operation, then use the low or high 32 bits\\nfor the corresponding MUL-family result. A 33-bit sign/zero representation\\npreserves the exact RV32M low and high halves, including MULHSU, while\\nkeeping the existing one-cycle EX latency, divider behavior, forwarding, and\\nRVFI timing unchanged. The reduced multiplier replication should lower LUT\\nusage and routing pressure, improving the product of CoreMark IPC and Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a single sign-controlled 33x33 RV32M multiplier, select its low/high halves for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU, and retain the ALTOPS substitutions plus exact divider semantics.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a single sign-controlled 33x33 RV32M multiplier, select its low/high halves for MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU, and retain the ALTOPS substitutions plus exact divider semantics.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 286.85, \"delta_pct\": -7.63, \"vs_baseline\": 1.42, \"fmax_mhz\": 122.19, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9983, \"ff\": 1898, \"seeds\": [114.12, 134.72, 122.19], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T20:22:24.214983Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 2}\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple long-latency M execution\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core places full-width multiply and divide/remainder\\narithmetic directly in the EX combinational cone. That cone is a poor\\nstructural match for the Tang Nano timing budget: the best run is only\\n132.29 MHz while already using 10554 LUT4s, and the prior structural FIFO\\nattempt showed that extra front-end state can lower Fmax. CoreMark's hot\\npath is predominantly integer and memory work, so isolating infrequent\\ndivide/remainder operations should improve timing without adding bubbles to\\nordinary ALU instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core places full-width multiply and divide/remainder\\narithmetic directly in the EX combinational cone. That cone is a poor\\nstructural match for the Tang Nano timing budget: the best run is only\\n132.29 MHz while already using 10554 LUT4s, and the prior structural FIFO\\nattempt showed that extra front-end state can lower Fmax. CoreMark's hot\\npath is predominantly integer and memory work, so isolating infrequent\\ndivide/remainder operations should improve timing without adding bubbles to\\nordinary ALU instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational divide/remainder portion of the ALU with a shared\\niterative radix-2 M execution unit. A DIV, DIVU, REM, or REMU entering EX\\nholds the in-order pipeline while the unit runs, then writes one RV32M-correct\\nresult through the existing EX/MEM and RVFI paths; ordinary ALU, load/store,\\nbranch, and multiply operations retain their single-cycle path. Add explicit\\nbusy/done control and operand/result registers so the divider is removed from\\nthe normal EX critical path, while preserving the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\\nbehavior and all trap, memory-stall, and retirement ordering invariants.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the normal-path / and % operators for DIV/REM and expose a clean divider request/result interface while retaining the ALTOPS and exact RV32M corner-case behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the iterative M unit as a replay-safe multi-cycle EX operation, holding the instruction and preventing duplicate side effects until its result is ready.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add divider-busy backpressure that freezes younger pipeline state but allows the in-flight divide to complete and retire exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire divider start, busy, and completion signals through the pipeline without changing the external bus or single-channel RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the normal-path / and % operators for DIV/REM and expose a clean divider request/result interface while retaining the ALTOPS and exact RV32M corner-case behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the iterative M unit as a replay-safe multi-cycle EX operation, holding the instruction and preventing duplicate side effects until its result is ready.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add divider-busy backpressure that freezes younger pipeline state but allows the in-flight divide to complete and retire exactly once.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire divider start, busy, and completion signals through the pipeline without changing the external bus or single-channel RVFI contract.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 267.72, \"delta_pct\": -13.79, \"vs_baseline\": -5.34, \"fmax_mhz\": 114.04, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5901, \"ff\": 2227, \"seeds\": [114.04, 122.97, 111.92], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T20:33:22.624502Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (regression, -13.79%): A radix-2 iterative divider that holds the in-order pipeline for its full multi-cycle latency can lose more CoreMark throughput than removing divide operators from the EX cone gains in timing, so divider frequency and stall cost must be measured before coupling DIV/REM to a global EX hold.\"}\n7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Add a tiny dynamic branch history predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current static predictor removes most taken backward-loop redirects, but\\nit still predicts every forward conditional branch as not taken and cannot\\nlearn recurring loop-exit or irregular branch behavior. CoreMark contains\\nrepeated control-flow patterns where a few bits of per-PC history can improve\\nprediction without the LUT and timing cost of a BTB.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current static predictor removes most taken backward-loop redirects, but\\nit still predicts every forward conditional branch as not taken and cannot\\nlearn recurring loop-exit or irregular branch behavior. CoreMark contains\\nrepeated control-flow patterns where a few bits of per-PC history can improve\\nprediction without the LUT and timing cost of a BTB.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small PC-indexed 2-bit saturating branch history table in the IF stage.\\nUse the existing IF-stage B/J immediate target calculation, but consult the\\ntable for every valid conditional branch, with reset counters biased weakly\\ntaken so the existing backward-loop behavior is preserved. Update the\\ncounter at EX resolution for every conditional branch, carrying the existing\\nprediction metadata through the pipeline and redirecting only on a\\nnext-PC mismatch. This should retain the static predictor's loop benefit,\\nreduce forward-branch and repeated loop-exit mispredictions, and add only a\\nfew small registers plus a narrow update path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small PC-indexed 2-bit saturating BHT, use it to choose conditional-branch taken predictions while retaining direct target generation, and update the selected counter from resolved EX branch outcomes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX branch-resolution PC, taken outcome, and update-valid signal into IF so the dynamic predictor learns every resolved conditional branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose a precise conditional-branch predictor update event and outcome at EX resolution without changing redirect or RVFI retirement behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small PC-indexed 2-bit saturating BHT, use it to choose conditional-branch taken predictions while retaining direct target generation, and update the selected counter from resolved EX branch outcomes.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX branch-resolution PC, taken outcome, and update-valid signal into IF so the dynamic predictor learns every resolved conditional branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose a precise conditional-branch predictor update event and outcome at EX resolution without changing redirect or RVFI retirement behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 281.94, \"delta_pct\": -9.22, \"vs_baseline\": -0.31, \"fmax_mhz\": 118.96, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4219411, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10302, \"ff\": 1935, \"seeds\": [121.02, 118.96, 103.07], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T20:29:45.722231Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, -9.22%): A 16-entry BHT indexed only by `pc[5:2]` aliases CoreMark conditional branches, and weak-taken reset can turn the tuned static forward-not-taken behavior into costly early mispredictions, so tiny dynamic predictors need an aliasing-aware initialization and benchmark profile check.\"}\n8:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Share a native-width RV32M multiplier\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor-enhanced bench core has already recovered the important\\nCoreMark control-flow bubbles, but it uses 10554 LUT4s and its ALU still\\ninstantiates three full-width multiplier expressions for the MUL family.\\nThe previous sharing attempt used a 33x33 signed multiplier and reduced\\nFmax substantially, so the next optimization should preserve the FPGA's\\nnative 32x32 mapping rather than widen the arithmetic cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The predictor-enhanced bench core has already recovered the important\\nCoreMark control-flow bubbles, but it uses 10554 LUT4s and its ALU still\\ninstantiates three full-width multiplier expressions for the MUL family.\\nThe previous sharing attempt used a 33x33 signed multiplier and reduced\\nFmax substantially, so the next optimization should preserve the FPGA's\\nnative 32x32 mapping rather than widen the arithmetic cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the parallel signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned\\nproducts with one native 32x32 unsigned product. Use sign-dependent high\\nhalf correction terms to implement MULH and MULHSU exactly, while taking\\nthe unchanged low half for MUL and the unsigned high half for MULHU. Keep\\nthe existing one-cycle EX interface, divider behavior, RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\\nsubstitutions, and RVFI timing unchanged. This should remove redundant\\nmultiplier hardware and routing pressure without the critical-path penalty\\nof the prior 33x33 implementation, improving Fmax and/or fitting margin\\nwhile preserving CoreMark IPC.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Use one native-width unsigned 32x32 product and sign-correction arithmetic for exact MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU results; retain ALTOPS and divider semantics.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Use one native-width unsigned 32x32 product and sign-correction arithmetic for exact MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU results; retain ALTOPS and divider semantics.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 317.32, \"delta_pct\": 2.18, \"vs_baseline\": 12.2, \"fmax_mhz\": 135.17, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10283, \"ff\": 1895, \"seeds\": [135.17, 123.08, 138.24], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T20:49:37.121156Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (improvement, +2.18%): One native-width unsigned `a * b` plus sign-dependent high-half correction terms implements RV32M `MUL`, `MULH`, `MULHU`, and `MULHSU` without the timing penalty of a widened multiplier, preserving CoreMark throughput while reducing redundant multiply hardware.\"}\n9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Add a minimal elastic fetch skid buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current IF stage exposes the instruction bus directly to ID, so any\\ncycle with io_imemReady low flushes the fetched instruction and stalls the\\nfront end. The benchmark injects roughly 22% instruction-bus stalls, making\\nthis coupling an IPC cost. The earlier two-entry prefetch FIFO regressed,\\nsuggesting that a smaller elastic structure is a better timing/area tradeoff\\nfor the already predictor-enhanced core.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current IF stage exposes the instruction bus directly to ID, so any\\ncycle with io_imemReady low flushes the fetched instruction and stalls the\\nfront end. The benchmark injects roughly 22% instruction-bus stalls, making\\nthis coupling an IPC cost. The earlier two-entry prefetch FIFO regressed,\\nsuggesting that a smaller elastic structure is a better timing/area tradeoff\\nfor the already predictor-enhanced core.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Insert a single-entry registered fetch skid buffer between the instruction\\nbus and ID. Capture the fetched instruction, PC, and existing static\\nprediction metadata whenever the bus handshakes, consume the buffered entry\\nwhen ID can advance, and hold it across an imem stall instead of injecting a\\nfront-end bubble. On an EX redirect, invalidate the entry and restart the\\nfetch address at the resolved target; preserve the existing load-use,\\ndmem-stall, RVFI ordering, and predictor-metadata rules. The one-entry\\nelastic path should recover isolated iStall cycles while avoiding the pointer\\nand mux overhead that hurt the prior two-entry FIFO's Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the direct IF/ID coupling with a one-entry ready-aware fetch skid buffer that stores the PC, instruction, validity, and static prediction metadata, and flushes precisely on redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate fetch-buffer fill stalls from back-end pipeline stalls so an imem bus stall preserves a buffered instruction without discarding valid front-end work.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Reconnect the elastic IF output and advance/hold controls while retaining the current static predictor, redirect priority, memory handshakes, and single-channel RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the direct IF/ID coupling with a one-entry ready-aware fetch skid buffer that stores the PC, instruction, validity, and static prediction metadata, and flushes precisely on redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate fetch-buffer fill stalls from back-end pipeline stalls so an imem bus stall preserves a buffered instruction without discarding valid front-end work.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Reconnect the elastic IF output and advance/hold controls while retaining the current static predictor, redirect priority, memory handshakes, and single-channel RVFI contract.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 278.09, \"delta_pct\": -10.46, \"vs_baseline\": -1.67, \"fmax_mhz\": 119.99, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4314735, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10193, \"ff\": 2000, \"seeds\": [113.56, 124.73, 119.99], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T20:54:36.294499Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (regression, -10.46%): A registered one-entry IF skid buffer must preserve empty-slot or simultaneous pop/push bypass behavior and redirect refill latency, or its added fetch bubbles outweigh the isolated imem-stall cycles it hides.\"}\n10:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Add a compact return-address stack predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The successful static predictor removes most backward-loop and direct-JAL\\nredirects, but JALR returns remain unpredicted and still pay the EX resolve\\npenalty on every function return. CoreMark contains frequent call/return\\ntraffic, while the prior 16-entry BHT showed that a general PC-indexed table\\ncan hurt both accuracy through aliasing and Fmax through added IF logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The successful static predictor removes most backward-loop and direct-JAL\\nredirects, but JALR returns remain unpredicted and still pay the EX resolve\\npenalty on every function return. CoreMark contains frequent call/return\\ntraffic, while the prior 16-entry BHT showed that a general PC-indexed table\\ncan hurt both accuracy through aliasing and Fmax through added IF logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small, low-associativity return-address stack (RAS) in IF for JALR\\ninstructions whose source is x1 or x5 and whose destination is x0. Predict\\nthe top stack entry as the next PC, while retaining the existing static\\nbackward-branch and direct-JAL predictions for other control flow. Update\\nthe RAS from resolved EX calls and returns, pushing pc+4 for link-register\\nJAL/JALR calls and popping on returns; use the existing prediction metadata\\nand next-PC comparison so a cold or imperfect RAS recovers through the\\ncurrent redirect path. A 4-entry stack should cover CoreMark's shallow call\\ndepth with substantially less tag-compare and aliasing cost than a BHT/BTB.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact return-address stack, recognize return-form JALR opcodes, and select the stack top as their predicted target while preserving the existing static branch/JAL predictor.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire resolved call/return classification and link addresses from EX into IF so RAS updates occur on the architectural control-flow event rather than arbitrary fetch aliases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose precise JAL/JALR call and return update signals, including the resolved link address, without changing redirect, trap, or RVFI behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact return-address stack, recognize return-form JALR opcodes, and select the stack top as their predicted target while preserving the existing static branch/JAL predictor.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire resolved call/return classification and link addresses from EX into IF so RAS updates occur on the architectural control-flow event rather than arbitrary fetch aliases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose precise JAL/JALR call and return update signals, including the resolved link address, without changing redirect, trap, or RVFI behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 266.64, \"delta_pct\": -14.14, \"vs_baseline\": -5.72, \"fmax_mhz\": 113.21, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4245805, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10460, \"ff\": 2032, \"seeds\": [116.74, 110.53, 113.21], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T20:58:03.456890Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r3s2 (regression, -14.14%): Even a four-entry RAS can regress CoreMark when its combinational 4-way `ras_top` mux and return-form decode sit directly on the IF next-PC path, so return-prediction savings must be weighed against fetch timing before adding predictor state.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
