ream, PC-next values, and register checks disagree even when the EX fallback remains.\"}\n13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Add a tagged loop-exit predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The successful static predictor already removes most taken backward-branch\\nredirects, but it predicts the final iteration of every loop as taken and\\npays an EX redirect when the loop exits. A general 16-entry BHT regressed\\nbecause of aliasing and weak-taken startup behavior, while a RAS regressed\\nFmax by adding a wide IF-stage mux. CoreMark's repeated counted loops make a\\nspecialized, low-aliasing loop predictor a better targeted opportunity.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The successful static predictor already removes most taken backward-branch\\nredirects, but it predicts the final iteration of every loop as taken and\\npays an EX redirect when the loop exits. A general 16-entry BHT regressed\\nbecause of aliasing and weak-taken startup behavior, while a RAS regressed\\nFmax by adding a wide IF-stage mux. CoreMark's repeated counted loops make a\\nspecialized, low-aliasing loop predictor a better targeted opportunity.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small tagged loop predictor for backward conditional branches.\\nKeep the current static backward-taken prediction on a cold or tag-mismatch\\nlookup, then learn a branch's repeated taken sequence and expected exit so a\\nmatching loop PC can predict not-taken on its final iteration. Use exact PC\\ntags and a compact remaining-iterations/confidence state rather than a\\nbroadly aliased BHT, and update entries only from resolved EX branches.\\nCarry the existing prediction metadata and retain the current redirect-on-\\nmismatch recovery, so wrong or changing trip counts remain architecturally\\nharmless while common CoreMark loop exits avoid an extra flush.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny exact-tag loop predictor that overrides the existing backward-taken heuristic only for learned loop exits, with a static-prediction fallback and a narrow lookup path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the resolved backward conditional-branch PC and taken/not-taken outcome as a precise loop-predictor update event without changing redirect or RVFI behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX loop-branch update event into IF and preserve prediction metadata, bus-stall handling, and the existing single-channel RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny exact-tag loop predictor that overrides the existing backward-taken heuristic only for learned loop exits, with a static-prediction fallback and a narrow lookup path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the resolved backward conditional-branch PC and taken/not-taken outcome as a precise loop-predictor update event without changing redirect or RVFI behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX loop-branch update event into IF and preserve prediction metadata, bus-stall handling, and the existing single-channel RVFI contract.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 244.72, \"delta_pct\": -22.88, \"vs_baseline\": -13.47, \"fmax_mhz\": 104.48, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4269318, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 13301, \"ff\": 3141, \"seeds\": [104.48, 100.13, 104.93], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T21:20:20.478409Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r4s2 (regression, -22.88%): A tagged loop predictor that adds IF-stage tag/state lookup to suppress only final-iteration backward-branch redirects can cost more fetch performance than the occasional exit flush it removes, even when exact tags avoid BHT aliasing.\"}\n14:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Zero-bubble zero-wait load-use bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The five-stage pipeline currently inserts a load-use bubble even when the\\ndata bus is zero-wait, which is the normal FPGA bench case and a substantial\\npart of the CoreMark memory/arithmetic stream. A prior unrestricted bypass\\nattempt was broken because it allowed a dependent instruction to advance\\nwhile dmemReady was low. The optimization should therefore be qualified by\\nthe actual ready signal and leave the existing stalled-bus sequencing intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The five-stage pipeline currently inserts a load-use bubble even when the\\ndata bus is zero-wait, which is the normal FPGA bench case and a substantial\\npart of the CoreMark memory/arithmetic stream. A prior unrestricted bypass\\nattempt was broken because it allowed a dependent instruction to advance\\nwhile dmemReady was low. The optimization should therefore be qualified by\\nthe actual ready signal and leave the existing stalled-bus sequencing intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a combinational EX/MEM load-result bypass using the memory stage's\\nalready sign- or zero-extended load value. Suppress the load-use bubble only\\nwhen io_dmemReady is high; in that case the dependent instruction advances\\nimmediately and selects the load value ahead of the normal ALU/MEM-WB\\nforwarding paths. When dmemReady is low, retain the current load-use bubble,\\nEX/MEM hold, MEM/WB valid suppression, and redirect behavior so no dependent\\ninstruction, register write, or RVFI record can observe incomplete memory\\ndata. This preserves the arbitrary-stall correctness contract while removing\\none cycle from each zero-wait load dependency.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify load-use stalling with dmemReady so only zero-wait loads use the new bypass, while preserving the existing dmem-stall and flush precedence.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the current load's extracted sign/zero-extended value as a combinational forwarding source without changing MEM/WB capture or RVFI metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an EX/MEM load-result forwarding selection with priority over the normal EX/MEM ALU and MEM/WB selections.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the ready-qualified memory load bypass into EX while retaining the unchanged external IO and single-channel RVFI contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the forwarded load value for dependent ALU, address, and branch operands, with no bypass use when the memory request is stalled.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Qualify load-use stalling with dmemReady so only zero-wait loads use the new bypass, while preserving the existing dmem-stall and flush precedence.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the current load's extracted sign/zero-extended value as a combinational forwarding source without changing MEM/WB capture or RVFI metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an EX/MEM load-result forwarding selection with priority over the normal EX/MEM ALU and MEM/WB selections.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the ready-qualified memory load bypass into EX while retaining the unchanged external IO and single-channel RVFI contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the forwarded load value for dependent ALU, address, and branch operands, with no bypass use when the memory request is stalled.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 252.64, \"delta_pct\": -20.38, \"vs_baseline\": -10.67, \"fmax_mhz\": 103.34, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4090325, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10254, \"ff\": 1923, \"seeds\": [99.84, 103.34, 105.02], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T21:37:31.609002Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -20.38%): A zero-wait load-use bypass that drives sign/zero-extended `dmem_rdata` through the EX forwarding mux can lengthen the critical path enough to outweigh the bubble it removes, so its CPI win must be measured against the added MEM-to-EX timing cost.\"}\n15:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Retimed two-phase execute pipeline\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current critical path starts at the ID/EX register and traverses the\\nEX forwarding muxes, ALU operand selection, and the 32-bit arithmetic cone\\nbefore reaching EX/MEM.  The successful static predictor already removes\\nmost loop redirect bubbles, while the earlier fetch-buffer and decode-\\nredirect structures lost more Fmax than they gained in IPC.  A physical\\nexecute-stage retiming therefore offers a more direct way to improve the\\nfitness product without adding predictor state or widening the interface.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current critical path starts at the ID/EX register and traverses the\\nEX forwarding muxes, ALU operand selection, and the 32-bit arithmetic cone\\nbefore reaching EX/MEM.  The successful static predictor already removes\\nmost loop redirect bubbles, while the earlier fetch-buffer and decode-\\nredirect structures lost more Fmax than they gained in IPC.  A physical\\nexecute-stage retiming therefore offers a more direct way to improve the\\nfitness product without adding predictor state or widening the interface.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split execute into an operand/forwarding EX1 stage and an arithmetic/control\\nEX2 stage, with a pipeline register between them.  EX1 captures the selected\\nforwarded operands, immediate, PC, control, and prediction metadata; EX2\\nperforms the ALU operation, memory-address calculation, branch or jump\\nresolution, and alignment checks, then drives the existing EX/MEM contract.\\nAdd age-correct EX2-to-EX2 and MEM/WB bypasses and stall/flush control so\\ndependent ALU instructions still sustain one instruction per cycle, while\\nload-use and dmem stalls retain precise RVFI validity and ordering.  The\\nextra register should remove the forwarding/operand mux delay from the\\narithmetic timing path and raise Fmax, with only the already-predicted\\ncontrol-flow cases avoiding an added redirect penalty.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the internal EX1 pipeline bundle needed to carry forwarded operands, control, PC, and static-prediction metadata across the new execute-stage boundary while retaining the existing EX/MEM and RVFI bundle fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Retiming the execute logic into operand-capture EX1 and arithmetic/control EX2 registers, with precise forwarding, branch prediction comparison, misalignment traps, dmem-stall holds, and the unchanged EX/MEM output contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Recompute load-use, redirect flush, and dmem backpressure arbitration for the additional execute stage so bubbles and holds preserve age order and do not duplicate or skip RVFI retirements.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the retimed execute-stage controls and forwarding sources through the existing five-stage top-level datapath without changing the external buses or single-channel RVFI port set.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the internal EX1 pipeline bundle needed to carry forwarded operands, control, PC, and static-prediction metadata across the new execute-stage boundary while retaining the existing EX/MEM and RVFI bundle fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Retiming the execute logic into operand-capture EX1 and arithmetic/control EX2 registers, with precise forwarding, branch prediction comparison, misalignment traps, dmem-stall holds, and the unchanged EX/MEM output contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Recompute load-use, redirect flush, and dmem backpressure arbitration for the additional execute stage so bubbles and holds preserve age order and do not duplicate or skip RVFI retirements.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the retimed execute-stage controls and forwarding sources through the existing five-stage top-level datapath without changing the external buses or single-channel RVFI port set.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 396.37, \"delta_pct\": 24.91, \"vs_baseline\": 40.15, \"fmax_mhz\": 172.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4340155, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10388, \"ff\": 2076, \"seeds\": [179.99, 172.03, 166.97], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T21:45:33.543025Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r5s1 (improvement, +24.91%): Splitting execute into an operand/forwarding EX1 and arithmetic/control EX2 stage can materially improve CoreMark fitness by removing the forwarding muxes from the ALU cone, provided EX2-to-EX1 age-ordered bypasses and precise bubble/hold control preserve dependent ALU, load-use, and dmem-stall ordering.\"}\n16:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Add a tiny exact-tag return target cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The existing static predictor removes most backward-loop and direct-JAL\\nredirects, but indirect JALR returns still resolve in EX and pay a flush.\\nThe prior BHT regressed from aliasing and the four-entry RAS regressed from\\nits IF-stage multiway mux. A very small exact-tag structure can target only\\nrepeated return sites while keeping the fetch lookup narrow.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The existing static predictor removes most backward-loop and direct-JAL\\nredirects, but indirect JALR returns still resolve in EX and pay a flush.\\nThe prior BHT regressed from aliasing and the four-entry RAS regressed from\\nits IF-stage multiway mux. A very small exact-tag structure can target only\\nrepeated return sites while keeping the fetch lookup narrow.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry direct-mapped return-target cache indexed by one PC bit and\\ntagged with the full JALR PC. In IF, recognize only return-form JALR\\ninstructions (rd=x0 and rs1=x1 or x5); on an exact valid-tag hit, predict\\nthe cached target, otherwise retain the current sequential prediction.\\nUpdate the selected entry only when that return resolves in EX, carrying the\\nexisting prediction metadata so the normal mismatch redirect recovers from\\ncold entries and collisions. This should remove recurring JALR return\\nbubbles with less timing and state cost than a stack or general BTB, while\\nexact tags make an alias a harmless miss rather than a wrong-path fetch.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the two-entry exact-tag return-target lookup on the existing IF prediction path, selecting a cached target only for recognized return-form JALR instructions and falling back to the current static prediction otherwise.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose a precise resolved-return update event containing the JALR PC and aligned resolved target, without changing redirect, trap, or RVFI behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX return-target update event into IF and preserve prediction metadata, bus-stall handling, and the single-channel RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the two-entry exact-tag return-target lookup on the existing IF prediction path, selecting a cached target only for recognized return-form JALR instructions and falling back to the current static prediction otherwise.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose a precise resolved-return update event containing the JALR PC and aligned resolved target, without changing redirect, trap, or RVFI behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX return-target update event into IF and preserve prediction metadata, bus-stall handling, and the single-channel RVFI contract.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 249.03, \"delta_pct\": -21.52, \"vs_baseline\": -11.95, \"fmax_mhz\": 105.82, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4249209, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10910, \"ff\": 1997, \"seeds\": [105.33, 105.82, 107.61], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T21:32:35.438089Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 5}\n17:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Share the combinational divide and remainder datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU currently spells out signed and unsigned DIV and REM as separate\\noperators. On the FPGA this can leave redundant quotient/remainder divider\\ncones and wide result-mux routing in the already timing-sensitive EX2\\nstage. The previous iterative-divider experiment showed that adding a\\nmulti-cycle stall is too expensive, so the useful opportunity is to reduce\\nhardware while retaining the existing single-cycle ALU contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU currently spells out signed and unsigned DIV and REM as separate\\noperators. On the FPGA this can leave redundant quotient/remainder divider\\ncones and wide result-mux routing in the already timing-sensitive EX2\\nstage. The previous iterative-divider experiment showed that adding a\\nmulti-cycle stall is too expensive, so the useful opportunity is to reduce\\nhardware while retaining the existing single-cycle ALU contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rewrite the real RV32M division logic around one combinational unsigned\\nmagnitude divider that produces both quotient and remainder, then apply\\noperand-sign correction and the RISC-V divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 rules\\nto select DIV, DIVU, REM, or REMU. Keep the ALTOPS formal substitutions\\nunchanged and preserve combinational latency, so dependent instructions,\\nRVFI ordering, and dmem-stall behavior do not acquire new bubbles. Sharing\\nthe expensive divide work should reduce LUT/routing load and improve Fmax\\nwithout the throughput loss of a globally held iterative unit.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the independent real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU operators with one shared quotient-and-remainder datapath, including exact RV32M signedness, zero-divisor, and overflow handling while retaining the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend ALU coverage for shared-divider signed and unsigned quotient/remainder pairs, divide-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1 overflow, and back-to-back operations to guard the refactor.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the independent real DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU operators with one shared quotient-and-remainder datapath, including exact RV32M signedness, zero-divisor, and overflow handling while retaining the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend ALU coverage for shared-divider signed and unsigned quotient/remainder pairs, divide-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1 overflow, and back-to-back operations to guard the refactor.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"build_failed: yosys synth: ERROR: Latch inferred for signal `\\\\alu.\\\\div_bit' from always_comb process `\\\\alu.$proc$cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv:61$9'.\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (broken, n/a): In Yosys `always_comb`, a module-scoped integer used as a restoring-divider loop iterator is a synthesizable signal, so paths that skip the loop (such as divide-by-zero) must still assign it or the tool infers a latch; keep loop indices local/automatic or fully assigned.\"}\n19:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Shadow-link return predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The existing static predictor already covers backward CoreMark loops and\\ndirect JALs, leaving canonical JALR returns as the main recurring control\\nflow penalty. Prior BHT, loop-table, RAS, and tagged return-cache attempts\\neither aliased or placed expensive lookup and muxing on the IF critical\\npath. A predictor based directly on the architectural link registers can\\ntarget the same return pattern with substantially less state and timing\\noverhead.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The existing static predictor already covers backward CoreMark loops and\\ndirect JALs, leaving canonical JALR returns as the main recurring control\\nflow penalty. Prior BHT, loop-table, RAS, and tagged return-cache attempts\\neither aliased or placed expensive lookup and muxing on the IF critical\\npath. A predictor based directly on the architectural link registers can\\ntarget the same return pattern with substantially less state and timing\\noverhead.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add reset-valid shadow registers for x1 and x5, updated at writeback from\\nthe ordinary architectural write stream. In IF, when the fetched\\ninstruction is the canonical return-form JALR (rd=x0, funct3=0, rs1=x1 or\\nx5), use the corresponding shadow link value plus the decoded immediate as\\nthe predicted target; otherwise retain the current backward-branch and\\ndirect-JAL predictor. Carry the existing prediction metadata unchanged so\\nEX still resolves every return and redirects only on a mismatch. This\\nremoves repeated return bubbles without a tag compare, BHT aliasing, RAS\\nstack mux, or multi-entry target-table lookup; stale or nonstandard link\\nvalues remain harmless because the existing precise redirect recovery\\nremains authoritative.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add two valid shadow link registers and a narrow canonical-return JALR prediction path that uses x1/x5 plus the decoded immediate, while preserving existing static predictions and redirect fallback.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the writeback architectural write-enable, destination, and data to IF so the shadow link registers track committed x1/x5 values without changing the external IO or RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add two valid shadow link registers and a narrow canonical-return JALR prediction path that uses x1/x5 plus the decoded immediate, while preserving existing static predictions and redirect fallback.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the writeback architectural write-enable, destination, and data to IF so the shadow link registers track committed x1/x5 values without changing the external IO or RVFI contract.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 375.45, \"delta_pct\": -5.28, \"vs_baseline\": 32.75, \"fmax_mhz\": 161.92, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4312687, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10577, \"ff\": 2144, \"seeds\": [161.79, 161.92, 173.64], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T21:57:51.602940Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (regression, -5.28%): A writeback-fed x1/x5 shadow predictor can lose CoreMark throughput when return decode, valid compares, and a 32-bit link-plus-immediate adder enter the IF next-PC cone, so return-bubble savings must be weighed against fetch timing and stale-link mispredict redirects.\"}\n21:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Elastic in-order memory backend\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The retimed EX1/EX2 pipeline now has useful independent work available, but\\nthe current MEM control still freezes it whenever io_dmemReady is low for a\\nload or store.  The benchmark injects roughly 22% data-bus stalls, so this\\ncoupling directly reduces CoreMark iterations per cycle.  The previous store\\nbuffer attempt regressed because queue occupancy remained on the global\\ndmem-stall path; the next design should decouple waiting memory requests from\\nyounger non-memory work while retaining strict retirement order.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The retimed EX1/EX2 pipeline now has useful independent work available, but\\nthe current MEM control still freezes it whenever io_dmemReady is low for a\\nload or store.  The benchmark injects roughly 22% data-bus stalls, so this\\ncoupling directly reduces CoreMark iterations per cycle.  The previous store\\nbuffer attempt regressed because queue occupancy remained on the global\\ndmem-stall path; the next design should decouple waiting memory requests from\\nyounger non-memory work while retaining strict retirement order.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small age-ordered completion queue after EX2 together with a dedicated\\nmemory-request slot.  A load or store occupies the memory slot until its\\ndmem handshake completes, while younger non-memory instructions can execute\\nand wait in the completion queue instead of freezing the whole backend.\\nBlock younger memory operations and consumers of a pending load, and apply\\nbackpressure only when the queue is full.  Retire and expose RVFI strictly\\nfrom the oldest queue entry, with queue-aware forwarding for values that are\\ncomplete but not yet retired.  This should hide a meaningful fraction of\\nrandom dmem stalls without adding logic to the IF critical path or changing\\nthe external bus and single-channel RVFI contracts.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the age-ordered completion-entry and pending-memory metadata needed by the elastic backend while preserving the existing RVFI payload fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the memory request slot and completion queue, making the oldest entry the sole WB/RVFI retirement source without changing top-level IO.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate memory-request capture, dmem-ready handshake, and load-response completion so a waiting request no longer freezes unrelated EX work.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the global memory-stall coupling with queue-full, pending-load dependency, and younger-memory ordering controls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add age-correct forwarding from completed-but-unretired queue entries while keeping pending-load results unavailable until the response arrives.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Drive register writes and retirement data from the oldest completed queue entry exactly once, preserving RVFI order and memory metadata.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the age-ordered completion-entry and pending-memory metadata needed by the elastic backend while preserving the existing RVFI payload fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the memory request slot and completion queue, making the oldest entry the sole WB/RVFI retirement source without changing top-level IO.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate memory-request capture, dmem-ready handshake, and load-response completion so a waiting request no longer freezes unrelated EX work.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the global memory-stall coupling with queue-full, pending-load dependency, and younger-memory ordering controls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add age-correct forwarding from completed-but-unretired queue entries while keeping pending-load results unavailable until the response arrives.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Drive register writes and retirement data from the oldest completed queue entry exactly once, preserving RVFI order and memory metadata.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: causal_ch0\\n19:23:02 [cover] summary:   reached cover statement rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__cover_cover_stmts_vh_1_49 at cover_stmts.vh:1.23-1.55 step 20\\nSBY 19:23:02 [cover] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\\nSBY 19:23:02 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:07  Status: passed\\nSBY 19:23:02 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=0)\\nSBY 19:23:02 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\\nSBY 19:23:02 [liveness_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:08 (8)\\nSBY 19:23:02 [liveness_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (